HT_DATA.load("detail", {"vtIzMaLkCaM":{"wy":"HN users repeatedly say it changed how they think about writing, calling it a masterclass useful far beyond academia.","q":{"x":"it's a masterclass in understanding what writing actually is, and what it does for you","i":39688146,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":47580797,"author":"modriano","month":"2026-03","story":"Don't Let AI Write for You","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk arguing writing is the process of thinking","quote":"That talk changed the way I write and made writing a primary tool I reach for when I want to learn something new.","text":"This reminds me of a talk I attended many years ago given by the director of UChicago's writing program (and found a recording of the talk [0]), and his thesis was that writing IS the process of thinking. That talk changed the way I write and made writing a primary tool I reach for when I want to learn something new. Words / language are the great technology we've made for representing ideas, and representing those ideas in the written word enables us to evaluate, edit, and compose those smaller ideas into bigger ideas. Kind of like how teachers would ask for an explanation in my own words, writing down my understanding of something I'd heard or read forced me to really evaluate the idea, focus on the parts I cared about, and record that understanding. Without the writing step, ideas would easily just float through my mind like a phantasm, shapeless and out of focus and useless when I had a tangible need for the idea. I am glad I learned to write (both code and text) long before Claude came online. It would have been very hard to struggle through translating ideas from my head into words and words (back) into ideas in my head if I knew there was an \"Easy button\" I could hit to get something cogent-sounding. I hope a large enough proportion of kids today will still put in the work and won't just end up with a stunted ability to write/think. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42416908,"author":"nazka","month":"2024-12","story":"My PhD advisor rewrote himself in bash (2010)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Valuable for anyone improving communication skills","quote":"It is so good that I would have paid to have access to this video!","text":"If anybody likes this article and wants to know more about the process of writing effective PhD papers they should watch [1]. In fact, anybody who desires to improve their communication skills should watch it. It is so good that I would have paid to have access to this video! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39688146,"author":"learningerrday","month":"2024-03","story":"To write a great essay, think and care deeply (2015)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Masterclass on writing as thinking","quote":"it's a masterclass in understanding what writing actually is, and what it does for you","text":"Larry Mcenerney's lectures on how to write effectively are gold for getting better at writing. There's a lot packed in his lectures, but a big core idea is that you should use writing to help you do your thinking. With complex subject matter, writing is thinking. The thing that we've been taught is writing is more akin to publishing (i.e. the editing you do to share your thoughts with other people after you've finished writing and thinking for yourself). Link to snippets of his talk if you're short on time: [LINK] If you've got more time, you should watch the whole thing, it's a masterclass in understanding what writing actually is, and what it does for you: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31083602,"author":"verdverm","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Advice on Writing Papers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Challenges bad writing advice received so far","quote":"Great video that will definitely help you rethink your approach","text":"Great video that will definitely help you rethink your approach [VIDEO] It challenges the bad advice you've received up to now"},{"id":25218127,"author":"will_pseudonym","month":"2020-11","story":"Writing well","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Resource that changed how commenter thinks about effective writing","quote":"The resource that has spurred me into thinking differently about effective writing","text":"The resource that has spurred me into thinking differently about effective writing was shared on HN, from UChicago Social Sciences' Leadership Lab - The Craft of Writing Effectively. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22629288,"author":"miguelrochefort","month":"2020-03","story":"Not delivering end-user value","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reader-value framing of writing changed commenter's perspective","quote":"I recommend this video to everyone.","text":"This is something I'm just starting to understand after being an idealist/perfectionist for most of my life. YAGNI seemed like a shortsighted dogma. My colleague's design decisions looked like ugly hacks. I preferred something to be well-done than to be useful. According to my MBTI type, I was a perfectionist of idea, not a perfectionist of action/result. I was trying to build a cathedral, not a bazaar. I can attribute my ongoing transformation to a single video about effective writing: [VIDEO] The main takeaway is that text is meant to be read, not written. Providing value to the reader is the only thing that matters. How well you write, how smart you sound, how much knowledge you deliver, doesn't matter as long as the reader gets value from the text. It sounds obvious, but it truly changed my perspective. I recommend this video to everyone."},{"id":42485834,"author":"brudgers","month":"2024-12","story":"Ask HN: How do you improve your writing?","stance":"rec","why":"Offers a helpful perspective on improving writing","quote":"maybe you will find this perspective helpful","text":"Mostly I write HN comments. In part because HN comments are a place to get feedback on my writing craft. Mostly because I frequently feel compelled to write. Anyway maybe you will find this perspective helpful: [VIDEO] Good luck."},{"id":36954977,"author":"andrenotgiant","month":"2023-08","story":"Cut out everything that's not surprising","stance":"rec","why":"Longer lecture on writing for an audience","quote":"Here is a longer lecture on a similar idea from U Chicago Writing Program","text":"Write for an audience, read the other work that audience reads, that will help you get a sense of whether something is going to be surprising to them. Here is a longer lecture on a similar idea from U Chicago Writing Program [VIDEO]"},{"id":32646024,"author":"bradbeam","month":"2022-08","story":"Don’t think to write, write to think","stance":"rec","why":"Introduced idea of writing to think; motivating","quote":"It's definitely been a motivation to try to write more.","text":"First time I heard of using writing to think was in this talk, \"The craft of writing effectively\"[1]. It's definitely been a motivation to try to write more. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31782007,"author":"gnuvince","month":"2022-06","story":"Writing like a pro with Vale and Neovim","stance":"rec","why":"Larry McEnerney talk on writing for reader value","quote":"A post about writing means that I need to plug a couple talks by Larry McEnerney.","text":"A post about writing means that I need to plug a couple talks by Larry McEnerney. [1,2] The tool described in this blog post is all about text-based rules: what the _text_ ought to look like. To improve your own writing (a) learn who your readers are, (b) know what they value, and (c) write so that as readers, they find your work valuable to them. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":28073973,"author":"teddyh","month":"2021-08","story":"On academic writing: a personal note (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Larry McEnerney session on writing rules for grad students","quote":"I have found this video very helpful","text":"I have found this video very helpful: [VIDEO] LEADERSHIP LAB: The Craft of Writing Effectively Do you worry about the effectiveness of your writing style? As emerging scholars, perfecting the craft of writing is an essential component of developing as graduate students, and yet resources for honing these skills are largely under utilized. Larry McEnerney, Director of the University of Chicago's Writing Program, led this session in an effort to communicate helpful rules, skills, and resources that are available to graduate students interested in further developing their writing style."},{"id":23912252,"author":"alextheparrot","month":"2020-07","story":"It's Time to Start Writing (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Writing for thinking versus writing for readers","quote":"don’t expect the writing done for thinking to be useful to readers!","text":"This aligns well with a lecture from the University of Chicago I watched recently[0], which explores how many academics use writing as a tool for thinking (What the article describes) and don’t think about the reader. Writing is an incredibly powerful tool to form thoughts. However, as the lecturer in the video details, don’t expect the writing done for thinking to be useful to readers! When we think, there is usually a certain incrementalism, whereas when we read we’re trying to resolve dissonances in a current mental model. That’s a key distinction, it means you need to make people care or understand why their mental model is broken when writing to informs reader. When we write for our own thinking, we usually understand the general problem already and are just traversing the problem space to better understand different components. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20968102,"author":"lerxst00","month":"2019-09","story":"BLUF: A military standard to make writing more powerful","stance":"rec","why":"Writing advice geared to academics but helpful beyond","quote":"I've found it helpful even outside of that space.","text":"A good video on writing that talks about similar ideas is [VIDEO] This video is geared more towards academic writing, but, I've found it helpful even outside of that space."},{"id":20430029,"author":"7373737373","month":"2019-07","story":"The Good Employee, a story about how you can explain companies with graph theory","stance":"rec","why":"Advice on writing for readers' value","quote":"Recommended","text":"I agree with this. The beginning of the post describes how rereading a previous post has been insightful for the author, then it delves into details, without explaining why, or how this could be useful for the reader . Too much rambling/offtopic content and breaking through the fourth wall. I, as a reader need to know why this could be useful for me first, then I would decide to read it. Recommended: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19006034,"author":"lugg","month":"2019-01","story":"The TypeScript Tax","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture useful for improving writing","quote":"you might find this lecture useful","text":"Not AP, But I'm curious, can you Tldr the tax for typescript? I skimmed half the article but I couldnt actually find what the tax was? In similar vein to the above above poster, you might find this lecture useful: [VIDEO] Your writing has a very nice cadence to it I'm just not sure you grasp the reason I might want to read it!"},{"id":42595402,"author":"kavasmlikon","month":"2025-01","story":"Building a Knowledge System That Enhances Rather Than Replaces Thought","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for writing supporting deep thinking","quote":"","text":"excellent article. IMHO it tackles the gist of what personal knowledge management should be about. true learning/understanding (and intellectual depth for that matter) seems like something that (due to neurocognitive reasons) cannot possibly be achieved only through the process of reading, but is rather a function of the reader's quality of elaboration on what has been read. this inherently requires the reader's wilĺingness to invest work in the form of structured thought and cognitive effort as part of the reading process, which in turn - again due to neurocognitive reasons - for somewhat non-trivial thought and intellectual endeavors requires systematic note-taking and writing ( [VIDEO] ). as a result, pure reading vs the reader's quality of elaboration on what they have read makes the difference between \"feeling smarter\" and actually \"becoming smarter\" as Sönke Ahrens ( [LINK] ) puts it. I fully agree with the \"Zettelgarden Philosophy\" on note-taking as described in this article - it recognises the real purpose and value of personal knowledge management which is based on notes that embody personal comments, thoughts, insights and connections to other concepts/ideas rather than mere facts and summaries pulled out from articles. Though, my \"tooling philosophy\" differs in that I prefer to have my digital zettelkasten stored in simple text-files so that I can leverage the freedom and flexibility of text as a universal interface (grep for search/filter, git for version-control,"},{"id":32498076,"author":"Tomte","month":"2022-08","story":"The Science of Scientific Writing (1990)","stance":"reference","why":"Larry McEnerney grouped with same school of writing","quote":"","text":"George Gopen hails from the same school of writing as Joseph Williams[1] and Larry McInerny[2]. He has also written a book that dives deeper into things[3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] Unfortunately, [3] is a bit tedious, IMO, go for [1], which is the best book about writing, bar none. Second best in principle is Thomas' & Turner's \"Clear and simple as the truth\", but it is hardly applicable to scientific (or technical) writing. If you're thinking about writing essays, get T&T."},{"id":32519282,"author":"ckdarby","month":"2022-08","story":"Ideological Bias in the Psychology of Sex and Gender [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why academics struggle to get published","quote":"The instructor in the video covers why academics struggle getting published","text":"I don't read many academic papers, but are the summaries normally 3 pages alone? It reminded me of this YouTube video: [VIDEO] The instructor in the video covers why academics struggle getting published because they're used to writing for individuals who are paid(E.g TAs & Profs) to read what they wrote."},{"id":25222129,"author":"clubdorothe","month":"2020-11","story":"Writing well","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why readers get frustrated with dense writing","quote":"","text":"By watching the UChicago class[1] on \" Writing Effectively \", this is exactly what happens to some HN readers: 1- You slow down - and read again 2- You don't understand 3- you get angry 4- You're done 5 - (optional) You write about it on HN because 3 and 4 [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25222605,"author":"adamfaliq","month":"2020-11","story":"Writing well","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture based on referenced material","quote":"","text":"[1] is actually the reference material for this [2] lecture, which another commenter has already mentioned. [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["eGffUODWWSE",1],["hJP5GqnTrNo",1],["WNWYk4DdT_E",1],["zVhQOhxb1Mc",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["0Rnq1NpHdmw",1],["KqFNbCcyFkk",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["CZIINXhGDcs",1],["jtMWEiCdsfc",1],["dljNabciEGg",1],["Tl9kD693ie4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["wfYbgdo8e-8",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["LkH2r-sNjQs",1],["uHQ4WCU1WQc",1],["5RKbSi87J5Q",1],["586Zn1ct-QA",1]]},"LIb3L4vKZ7U":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as an entertaining, mind-opening talk on allocator design and composable generic programming.","q":{"x":"It really opened my eyes to how one could very elegantly make a very complex and high-performance heap allocator","i":42690180,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42690180,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2025-01","story":"Comptime as Configuration","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Composing a complex allocator from trivial parts at compile time.","quote":"It really opened my eyes to how one could very elegantly make a very complex and high-performance heap allocator","text":"> The issue with this approach is that the more you can control, the less the compiler can assume. That's absolutely true, but there's a workaround, albeit a complicated one. The compiler internals need the same kind of constraints or traits that abstract code such as language interfaces or template parameters can have. These can then be used to constrain the internals in a way that then would allow assumptions to be safely \"plumbed through\" the various layers. The (big!) challenge here is that these abstractions haven't been well-developed in the industry. Certainly nowhere near as well as the typical runtime \"type theory\" as seen in modern languages. > some very basic ones like allocating heap memory. Well... this is sort-of my point! For example, what's the fundamental difference between allocating memory in some heap[1] structure at runtime and a compiler allocating members in a struct/record/class for optimal packing? IMHO, not much. E.g.: Watch this talk by Andrei Alexandrescu titled \"std::allocator Is to Allocation what std::vector Is to Vexation\": [VIDEO] It really opened my eyes to how one could very elegantly make a very complex and high-performance heap allocator from trivial parts composed at compile-time. There's no reason that a nearly identical abstraction couldn't also be used to efficiently \"bin pack\" variables into a struct. E.g.: accounting for alignment, collecting like-sized items into contiguous sections, extra padding for \"lock\" objects to prevent cache"},{"id":34593450,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What's the best lecture series you've ever seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining, approachable, inspiring keynote.","quote":"they're entertaining, approachable, and inspiring","text":"Not exactly a lecture series, but Andrei Alexandrescu has had some fantastic keynote presentations at CppCon. I don't even program in C++ any more, but I've watched these more than once, they're entertaining, approachable, and inspiring: Speed Is Found In The Minds of People [LINK] std::allocator Is to Allocation what std::vector Is to Vexation [VIDEO] An actual lecture series I've enjoyed watching is \"The WE-Heraeus International Winter School on Gravity and Light\", which covers General Relativity using modern mathematical approaches: [LINK] Again, I haven't done physics in 20 years, but I've found it surprisingly easy to follow because it is so clearly presented."},{"id":25403583,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2020-12","story":"Writing a Memory Allocator (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Modular allocator library built up from pieces.","quote":"the brilliant CppCon 2015 talk by Andrei Alexandrescu","text":"Every time I hear someone talking about writing a memory allocator, it always reminds me of the brilliant CppCon 2015 talk by Andrei Alexandrescu about how he wrote an modular allocator library that can be built up in pieces to make whatever you like. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24769624,"author":"favorited","month":"2020-10","story":"Custom Allocators Demystified","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining case for composable templated allocators over std::allocator.","quote":"Andrei Alexandrescu's 2015 talk about C++'s allocators is great.","text":"Andrei Alexandrescu's 2015 talk about C++'s allocators is great. He's a very entertaining presenter, and he makes case for extremely composable templated allocators (and why std::allocator isn't that). [VIDEO]"},{"id":22999318,"author":"favorited","month":"2020-04","story":"Benchmarking C++ Allocators","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Composable allocator design with a fantastic presentation style.","quote":"it was really interesting. And Andrei's presentation style was fantastic, per usual.","text":"A bit unrelated, but if anyone is interested, I recently watched Andrei Alexandrescu's 2015 talk about a composable allocator design in C++ and it was really interesting. And Andrei's presentation style was fantastic, per usual. Note that this talk is about a std::allocator-like type, potentially on top of one of malloc/jemalloc/etc. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18607277,"author":"p0nce","month":"2018-12","story":"Using D to Create the World’s Fastest File System","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Concrete example of design-by-introspection in allocators.","quote":"(mind bending stuff)","text":"> I would have liked to hear more about what exactly made D so great This WIP article explains very well the USP of D: [LINK] (section 3) This article highlight the value of \"design-by-introspection\" applied to a particular problem space: [LINK] This talk present a concrete example of such: [VIDEO] (mind bending stuff) You might not understand what is new about it, but soon your competitors will understand it."},{"id":14278310,"author":"nly","month":"2017-05","story":"The Horror in the Standard Library","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining, elegant talk on memory allocation in C and C++.","quote":"gave a great, entertaining, and technically elegant talk on memory allocation in C and C++ at Cppcon 2015 that is well worth watching","text":"Actually it is C's malloc and free that is \"broken\". malloc() takes a size parameter, but free() doesn't. This imbalance means it can never be maximally efficient. Whatever GNU stdlibc++ is doing is probably, on balance, a net win for most programs. It's not exactly roses in C++ either of course. You can do better than the standard library facilities. Andrei Alexandrescu gave a great, entertaining, and technically elegant talk on memory allocation in C and C++ at Cppcon 2015 that is well worth watching [VIDEO]"},{"id":38703409,"author":"einpoklum","month":"2023-12","story":"C++ Should Be C++","stance":"rec","why":"Engaging and funny talk on why allocator-type coupling is broken.","quote":"See this CppCon talk by the (engaging and funny) Andrei Alexandrescu","text":"Allocators have an inherently broken design, tying them to types. See this CppCon talk by the (engaging and funny) Andrei Alexandrescu: [VIDEO] std::allocator is to allocation what std::vector is to vexation (or: designing allocators that don't not-work)"},{"id":30345414,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2022-02","story":"How to write untestable code (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows combining small single-purpose pieces via templates instead of branchy code.","quote":"Watch this video of Andrei Alexandrescu's talk in CppCon 2015","text":"I come from the opposite camp. I detest functions that are five pages of boolean logic, which I guarantee you contain at least a dozen bugs, no matter how much you think you've tested it. Code coverage is like those skin creams that say \"Scientifically tested!\" on the label. Yeah, well, tested doesn't mean that it worked. It just means that there was a test. Watch this video of Andrei Alexandrescu's talk in CppCon 2015: [VIDEO] I've done things like this in my career, and it's always worked out brilliantly . Bugs just evaporate when you let the compiler \"combine\" things for you, instead of just plopping everything into a bowl and mashing it together with your hands like some sort of animal. Especially in today's C++ or Rust, you can \"have your cake and eat it too\". It's possible to build up these nice, single-purpose class hierarchies and combine them into complex, powerful constructs via templates / traits. The compiler takes care of the edge cases for you, because it never gets bored or distracted. There's no performance downside because of static dispatch, inlining, and aggressive optimisers."},{"id":20250760,"author":"Quekid5","month":"2019-06","story":"Mimalloc – A compact general-purpose allocator","stance":"rec","why":"Argues for tunable, composable allocator systems.","quote":"Andrei Alexandrescu had a great talk[0] on allocators","text":"Andrei Alexandrescu had a great talk[0] on allocators and this convinced me that if you really care about allocator performance[1] what you need is a tunable and composable system. The whole idea of how and when (runtime/compile-time) to choose the appropriate allocator is also interesting. [0] [VIDEO] [1] I suppose there's a digression to be had here about allocation being trivial and RAII-style deallocation not really being deterministic, but..."},{"id":14611143,"author":"badsectoracula","month":"2017-06","story":"Show HN: Crown – A flexible game engine written from scratch in C++","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk on C++ allocators.","quote":"Andrei Alexandrescu had a great talk about allocators in C++ a couple of years ago","text":"Generally speaking many C++ game engines avoid the STL stuff and reimplement their own more predictable containers, often with custom allocation schemes. The engine at the last game company i worked at, for example, had its own containers and memory allocator and allowed you to define the allocation category and pool per allocator and per object class (so, e.g., dynamic strings would be isolated to their own pool to avoid fragmenting the heap). Related, Andrei Alexandrescu had a great talk about allocators in C++ a couple of years ago: [VIDEO] Also related to the Orthodox C++, the same engine also ousted exceptions and RTTI. I'm not sure about the reason for exceptions, but C++'s RTTI was simply inadequate and instead it was replaced with a custom one made using macros (similarly to wxWidgets and MFC) that allowed automatic object serialization and reflection which was used for all saving and loading, exposing objects to the editor automatically with a common UI and exposing classes and objects to the (custom) scripting language with very little setup. Interestingly most of the stuff the engine had to reinvent seem to be first class citizens in the D language. Also Andrei's allocators also seem to be available (experimentally) there too. Personally i prefer plain old C (C89 even, although with a few commonly available or easily reproducible extras like stdint) because i see C++ as too complex for what it is worth. However D seems to provide more power with less complexity and"},{"id":41176306,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2024-08","story":"Please do not attempt to simplify this code","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"More to the point, the comment in the code mentions the “combinatorial” explosion of conditions that have to be carefully maintained by fallible meat brains. In most modern languages something like this could be implemented using composition with interfaces or traits. Especially in Rust it’s possible to write very robust code that has identical performance to the if-else spaghetti, but is proven correct by the compiler. I’m on mobile, so it’s hard to read through the code, but I noticed one section that tries to find an existing volume to use, and if it can’t, then it will provision one instead. This could be three classes that implement the same interface: class ExistingVolume : IVolumeAllocator class CreateVolume : IVolumeAllocator class SeqVolumeAllocator : IVolumeAllocator The last class takes a list of IVolumeAllocator abstract types as its input during construction and will try them in sequence. It could find and then allocate, or find in many different places before giving up and allocating, or allocating from different pools trying them in order. Far more flexible and robust than carefully commented if-else statements! Similarly, there's a number of \"feature gate\" if-else statements adding to the complexity. Let's say the CreateVolume class has two variants, the original 'v1' and an experimental 'v2' version. Then you could construct a SeqVolumeAllocator thus: allocator = new SeqVolumeAllocator( featureFlag ? new CreateVolumeV2() : new CreateVolumeV1(), new ExistingVo"},{"id":36117845,"author":"einpoklum","month":"2023-05","story":"C++17’s Useful Features for Embedded Systems","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for argument that std::allocator design is broken.","quote":"","text":"> Isn’t polymorphic memory allocator the most significant recent C++ addition for embedded systems I would say no. Run-time polymorphism is overrated IMHO, and more so for embedded systems, again IMHO. C++ in general has been moving towards preferring things happening statically rather than dynamically. > that allows preventing runtime memory allocation after the init phase That's not what allows preventing runtime memory allocation after an \"init phase\". Unless I'm misunderstanding what you mean. ... oh, I think I get it: As a general rule, avoid using standard library data structures with allocators. They may work fine as boilerplate, but are usually not what you want when you have any non-trivial requirements. std::vectors are fine if you don't mind the allocations - but you do, so not even those. You could use custom allocators, but that whole mechanism is totally broken design in the opinion of many; see Andrei Alexandrescu's talk about this subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14153602,"author":"aconz2","month":"2017-04","story":"Modernizing the DOM Tree in Microsoft Edge","stance":"reference","why":"Work on composing allocators in D.","quote":"This work on composing allocators in D","text":"I see something like this and think about how much an ensemble approach to the data structure would help. This keeps popping up in places where you have a very general abstraction (like the DOM) and want to support a broad set of operations and use cases (read-only, insert heavy, traversal heavy, etc.) and so you often choose the structure which supports most of these cases pretty well. But you sacrifice a lot of perf by doing so. What I'm wondering is how well you could do perf-wise by having the DOM be comprised of a heterogeneous set of structures, each of which is chosen based on the history of what operations have been performed on it (ie. we're appending a lot here, let's use a vector of children). This is all similar in spirit and goals of: - JIT compiling, but for the data structures, and not code - This work on composing allocators in D [VIDEO] - ML ensemble methods"},{"id":14170104,"author":"p0nce","month":"2017-04","story":"Metaprogramming is less fun in D","stance":"reference","why":"D goes further in the Stepanov style of metaprogramming.","quote":"I think D goes further in the Stepanov style","text":"In my line of work (real-time), C++ typically reigns supreme but D is just as fast and more productive, so why not use it? It's not really about meta-programming, for me it wins on the basics. And is we talk meta-programming, I think D goes further in the Stepanov style: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44348068,"author":"triknomeister","month":"2025-06","story":"I was surprised by how simple an allocator is","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"As Andrei Alexandrescu famously said, \"Allocator is to allocation what alligators is to allegation\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1]]},"ji5_MqicxSo":{"wy":"Perennially recommended as an inspirational talk for people facing setbacks or choosing a direction, and a source of oft-quoted lines like the brick wall.","q":{"x":"If you haven't watched this, it will change your life.","i":17213004,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17213004,"author":"rebase","month":"2018-06","story":"YouTube’s top creators are burning out","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dying professor shares life lessons for his kids.","quote":"If you haven't watched this, it will change your life.","text":"If you haven't watched this, it will change your life. Even if you have, it's one of the few youtube videos that is worth a re-watch. The book is just as good if not better. [VIDEO] Spoiler: It is a former CS professor at Carnegie Mellon named Randy Pausch. He had terminal pancreatic cancer. This is a dying professor's last lecture. It is a father sharing life lessons for his kids when they are older to watch. Some relevant quotes: - “As you get older, you may find that 'enabling the dreams of others' thing is even more fun.” - “We cannot change the cards we are dealt, just how we play the hand.” - “The key question to keep asking is, Are you spending your time on the right things? Because time is all you have. ” - “Showing gratitude is one of the simplest yet most powerful things humans can do for each other.” - “People are more important than things.”"},{"id":1726795,"author":"sdrinf","month":"2010-09","story":"Ask HN: After almost 30 years the romance is over - What now?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Soul-searching media for deciding direction.","quote":"There are 2 pieces of media I'd strongly recommend for that","text":"Welcome to the law of Diminishing returns. Your choices fundamentally boils down to the following: -Take the blue pill. Stay in wonderland. Pick a technology, any technology. Drive yourself into it deeper. The rabbit hole goes much, much further, than you can imagine. -Take the red pill, and wake up: the rabbit hole goes the other way, too. On first basis, you have the ever-exploding number of scientific disciplines ( [LINK] ). Even further, society isn't merely a collection of engineers, and scientist: other people have all kind of different jobs. Furthermore, they are equally convinced, that their field of choice was the right one; and from their own perspective each one of them was right. Since you're HN, you might have a particular interest towards this field we call \"business\". Now, this is not what people around you -who are called \"managers\" btw- do. A good working definition for engineers is: Business is the ability to actualize a job that needs to be done, on which you're retroactively hired (or fired) by society as a whole. Or, as PG put it: doing stuff what people wants. Regardless of how you choose, a good partition of it is going to be soul-searching. There are 2 pieces of media I'd strongly recommend for that: -Randy Pausch Last Lecture [ [VIDEO] ], and -wishcraft, which is a short book, available freely from [LINK] . On an ending note, I feel for you. Every time I've been down to this alley, I always always wished I'd have done it earlier. If anything, you shou"},{"id":34301885,"author":"RonaldOlzheim","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Tech After Chemotherapy","stance":"rec","why":"Part of an ordered watchlist on timing.","quote":"Please watch this for when","text":"Please watch these videos in the right order: 1 Please watch this video for why: [LINK] 2 Please watch this video for how: [LINK] 3 Please watch this video for what: [LINK] 4 Please watch this for when: [VIDEO] Final note: I believe in the good so much all the time, but it is hard to keep believing in the good all the time. Please watch this when you are having a hard time believing: [LINK] I hope you receive courage, wisdom, temperance and moderation. *Let´s connect on Linkedin. (You can message me anytime) Ron."},{"id":31356924,"author":"PatentlyDC123","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: What are some good inspirational and uplifting books?","stance":"rec","why":"Pausch's outlook and delivery elevate the story.","quote":"Randy Pausch's outlook and delivery really elevated the story for me.","text":"The Last Lecture was great. If you can, watch the lecture too [0]. Even beyond the literal message, Randy Pausch's outlook and delivery really elevated the story for me. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28575514,"author":"___luigi","month":"2021-09","story":"I’m 22 and I’ve failed","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested for a young person feeling like a failure.","quote":"do yourself a favor, and take some time to watch this talk","text":"Oh Boy! Life is a marathon, not a sprint. You are still young (revisit these words when you reach 32, 42, and 52). So, hear me out. You need to work on developing habits, habits of winning, winning every time, develop a process of learning and improving and trust the process. Ignore failures, you might look boring and unsuccessful to some people, but once your habits start to work, it can change your orbit. You’re not failing, boy. You are growing up and this is how life look like. If you read a lot of books/biographies, you will realize how some very successful people have had setbacks. Ignore these negative depressing whispers, and learn, read more. Most of us were in that spot before, do yourself a favor, and take some time to watch this talk \"Last Lecture: Achieving Your Childhood Dreams\", Randy Pausch [VIDEO]"},{"id":28024464,"author":"prakhar897","month":"2021-08","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best non-technical talks of all time?","stance":"rec","why":"Top pick in a list of best non-technical talks.","quote":"1. Randy Pausch Last Lecture: Achieving Your Childhood Dreams","text":"Here's mine: 1. Randy Pausch Last Lecture: Achieving Your Childhood Dreams [VIDEO] 2. Strange answers to the psychopath test | Jon Ronson [LINK] 3. The thrilling potential of SixthSense technology | Pranav Mistry [LINK] 4. Steve Jobs MIT Class: What he learnt after he was fired from Apple? [LINK] 5. Eric Liu: Why ordinary people need to understand power [LINK] 6. Peter Lynch 1994 Lecture [LINK]"},{"id":15669270,"author":"delibes","month":"2017-11","story":"Business questions engineers should ask when interviewing at ML/AI companies","stance":"rec","why":"Reminder for younger readers who may have missed it.","quote":"The Last Lecture is good.","text":"Thanks for reminding me. The Last Lecture is good. Since it's about 9 years ago, some younger HN readers may have missed it: [VIDEO] Cancer sucks."},{"id":7684557,"author":"ehurrell","month":"2014-05","story":"Hamming, \"You and Your Research\" (1995) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant talk on doing important work.","quote":"Maybe also of interest on the topic of doing important work, Randy Pausch's Last Lecture","text":"I was introduced to this recently and it has become one of my favourite talks. Maybe also of interest on the topic of doing important work, Randy Pausch's Last Lecture: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3078623,"author":"elliottcarlson","month":"2011-10","story":"Pancreatic Cancer","stance":"rec","why":"Inspiring last lecture by Pausch.","quote":"I recommend everyone to both watch the Last lecture and read the book.","text":"The Last Lecture is an inspiring book - Randy Pausch was another great man that the technology community had lost and I recommend everyone to both watch the Last lecture and read the book. The video is viewable here: [VIDEO] (as embedded on the OP's submission)"},{"id":2662355,"author":"Xk","month":"2011-06","story":"Hacking the system: how to land meetings with anyone you want","stance":"rec","why":"Pausch's lunch-asking method and the full talk.","quote":"If you haven't seen it, watch the whole talk. It's really good.","text":"This reminds me of Randy Pausch's method of getting someone to sit down for lunch. [1] \"And he asked a question. And I was like, I’m sorry did you say you were Tom Furness? And he said yes. I said, then I would love to answer your question, but first, will you have lunch with me tomorrow? And there’s a lot in that little moment. There’s a lot of humility, but also asking a person where he can’t possibly say no.\" Although, admittedly, that's after you got them to say a few words to you and you're in front of a large group of people. [1] [VIDEO] 19:40 Edit: For those who don't know, here's the setting of this talk: This talk is one in a series given at CMU called the \"Last Lecture\" series with the hypothetical setting of 'what if you had one last talk to give?'. Randy Pausch, a professor at CMU, didn't have to pretend. He was diagnosed with pancreatic cancer, and in August of 2007 (when he gave the talk) was told he had three to six months left to live. If you haven't seen it, watch the whole talk. It's really good."},{"id":1752301,"author":"andre3k1","month":"2010-10","story":"Ask HN: Good books?","stance":"rec","why":"Famous speech on wisdom imparted in a last chance.","quote":"Here's the famous speech","text":"If you're looking for something different then check out The Last Lecture by Randy Pausch. It's is an eye-opening book in a very nontraditional sense. You may remember Pausch as the computer science professor at Carnegie Mellon who was told that he had but a few months left to live (cancer). This book doesn't stray from its topic: \"What wisdom would we impart to the world if we knew it was our last chance?\" If you're looking for motivation and an \"out of the box\" approach to analyzing daily life then this is it. Here's the book: [LINK] Here's the famous speech: [VIDEO]"},{"id":601937,"author":"Tarks","month":"2009-05","story":"Ask HN: Just lost my year long placement because they have no work. Begging for advice ","stance":"rec","why":"Inspirational talk.","quote":"is very inspirational","text":"Thankyou, I'd lost sight of this a little. teaching someone how to deal with a type of situation rather than a specific one is always useful in the long run. If anyone else appreciates this type of thing then Randy Pauche's \"Last Lecture\" is very inspirational [VIDEO]"},{"id":36000657,"author":"melling","month":"2023-05","story":"Andy Rourke: The Smiths bassist dies aged 59","stance":"reference","why":"Reminds of Pausch when hearing of pancreatic cancer.","quote":"I think of Randy Pausch and the Last Lecture.","text":"He died from pancreatic cancer. Anytime, I read someone dying from this particular cancer, I think of Randy Pausch and the Last Lecture. [VIDEO] HN discussion from 2008: [LINK] It’s really a shame no progress has been made in treating this cancer in the last 16 years."},{"id":24223433,"author":"donw","month":"2020-08","story":"LA Mayor Shuts Off Power at Hollywood Hills House That Hosted Large Parties","stance":"reference","why":"Source of advice about getting out of pissing matches.","quote":"","text":"The late Randy Pausch, in his Last Lecture[1] at Carnegie Mellon University, offered up... well, a lot of great advice, but the piece that jumped into my mind was: \"It’s very important to know when you’re in a pissing match. And it’s very important to get out of it as quickly as possible.\" This thing looks awfully like a pissing match, and it's not just with the one homeowner in Hollywood -- it's with every person that feels the same way. I would lay good money the cardinality of that set is not a small number. Doesn't matter whether or not it is a pissing match. It looks like one. Were I running the state, I'd rather spend my time and energy on doing things that would actually Get People To Wear Masks, as opposed to another round of Pissing Match: San Andreas. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16229604,"author":"lloeki","month":"2018-01","story":"The Eve programming language project is winding down","stance":"reference","why":"Source of brick walls quote.","quote":"The brick walls are there to give us a chance to show how badly we want something.","text":"> Today unfortunately we have a culture of “you can’t, it’s hard, it’s not for you, other people do that.” \"The brick walls are there for a reason. The brick walls are not there to keep us out. The brick walls are there to give us a chance to show how badly we want something. Because the brick walls are there to stop the people who don’t want it badly enough. They’re there to stop the other people.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":8816730,"author":"dctoedt","month":"2014-12","story":"We're all communication hoarders","stance":"reference","why":"Pausch's rule to archive all emails.","quote":"said that his rule was to archive all emails, no exceptions.","text":"The late Dr. Randy Pausch, of Last Lecture fame, said that his rule was to archive all emails, no exceptions. [1] IIRC it was because he figured that if everyone saved all their emails, it would would provide future historians with a treasure trove of contemporaneous research material. From a legal perspective, purging emails can lead to accusations of \"spoliation of evidence,\" [2] which in some circumstances can lead to the judge instructing the jury that the jurors can presume that what you destroyed was harmful to your case. You can argue that you didn't purge any relevant emails, but that becomes a he-said/she-said issue. [1] I believe it was in his Last Lecture at [VIDEO] , but it might have been his time-management lecture at [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":7695448,"author":"NanoWar","month":"2014-05","story":"The Meaning of Life","stance":"reference","why":"Source of quote about enabling dreams of others.","quote":"I also try to enable the dreams of others, but this ultimately makes me happy, too.","text":"I also try to enable the dreams of others, but this ultimately makes me happy, too. [ Taken from [VIDEO] ]"},{"id":5579904,"author":"espeed","month":"2013-04","story":"Physicist proposes new way to think about intelligence","stance":"reference","why":"Source for never make a decision until you have to.","quote":"Never make a decision until you have to","text":"\"Trying to capture as many future histories as possible\" (keeping your options open) harmonizes well with Paul Graham's view on procrastination ( [LINK] ) and the tenet of \"put off decisions as long as possible\". UPDATE: Evidently several smart people have had this intuition. I just remembered who said, \"Never make a decision until you have to\" -- it was Randy Pausch in the \"The Last Lecture\" ( [VIDEO] ). And \"delay commitment until the last responsible moment\" is also an agile principle ( [LINK] )."},{"id":4409797,"author":"fl3tch","month":"2012-08","story":"Tim Berners-Lee's Original Announcement (1991)","stance":"reference","why":"Reminder of Pausch.","quote":"Does anybody remember him?","text":"You know what's interesting? The immediate follow up to that first message, by Richard Chimera, mentions Randy Pausch. Does anybody remember him? [VIDEO] Small world."},{"id":138057,"author":"ca","month":"2008-03","story":"Ask YC: what is your favorite startup related quote?","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the brick wall quote.","quote":"The wall is there for you to show how bad you want it.","text":"Randy Pausch (speaking of apparent barriers between you and your goals): \"The wall is there for you to show how bad you want it.\" (From his \"Last Lecture\", [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["YrtANPtnhyg",1],["xYemnKEKx0c",1],["sumZLwFXJqE",1]]},"uUmIdq0D6-A":{"wy":"HN commenters call it their favorite introduction to regenerative agriculture, and several say it changed how they garden or farm.","q":{"x":"Gabe Brown's talk is my favorite introduction to regenerative ag","i":27815555,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28445123,"author":"frereubu","month":"2021-09","story":"Sri Lanka’s overnight flip to organic farming has led to an economic disaster","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Farm without fertilizer via no-till and rotation, focused on profit.","quote":"it was an eye-opener for me","text":"I don't know if you've come across this video on HN before, but I discovered it here and it was an eye-opener for me. It's a guy called Gabe Brown who transformed his farm into one that needed no fertilizer by using no-till and rotation. One of the most compelling things is that he's no tree-hugger (he even has an uncomplimentary comparison with the soil from an organic farm) - his focus is squarely on profit, which goes up when you're not forking out huge amounts of money on chemicals. I thought I'd give it five minutes, then 2.5 hours later I realised I'd watched the whole thing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27815555,"author":"akeck","month":"2021-07","story":"Organic and regenerative agriculture are revitalizing rural Montana economies","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite introduction to regenerative agriculture.","quote":"Gabe Brown's talk is my favorite introduction to regenerative ag","text":"Gabe Brown's talk is my favorite introduction to regenerative ag: [VIDEO] He did a book on it: [LINK] I also like Geoff Lawton's work on food forests: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":26826129,"author":"tastyfreeze","month":"2021-04","story":"The U.S. corn belt has lost a third of its topsoil","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gabe Brown talk that started the commenter's regenerative journey.","quote":"by Gabe Brown set me on the regenerative path.","text":"YouTube soil health videos has all you need. Gabe Brown and Ray Archuleta presentations are easy to consume. Dr. Elaine Ingham focuses more on the soil life in greater detail than most care about but she does provide some important to understand details on rhizosphere interactions. Living Web Farms has quite a lot of lectures about regenerative practices. There are also a myriad of academic papers on soil health as well. This video, [VIDEO] , by Gabe Brown set me on the regenerative path. If you dont want to consume hours of information here is the gist. The soil is alive. Plants use microbes and fungus as an external stomach. Direct sun and disruption/tilling kill the soil life. Without life in the soil you eventually have a desert as the organics are sifted out of the sand by weathering."},{"id":20966640,"author":"tastyfreeze","month":"2019-09","story":"Estimates that mineral levels in vegetables have dropped by up to 90% since 1914","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Long talk on living soil that changed home gardening practices.","quote":"This is a long video but it has completely changed my home gardening practices.","text":"This happened due to extermination of soil life through modern farming practices of heavy tilling, fertilizing and monoculture crops. Dead soil is more susceptible to erosion and requires ever increasing amounts of synthetic fertilizer to grow anything. More soil has been lost from the US than the amount of food that has been produced. Soil fertility can be returned to historical levels by changing farming practices to make living soil. The benefits of treating the soil as a living organism include increased fertility, water infiltration, moisture retention, and nutrient availability as well as decreasing or eliminating synthetic fertilizer requirements. [LINK] This is a long video but it has completely changed my home gardening practices. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44843751,"author":"Sightline","month":"2025-08","story":"Little-known leguminous plant can increase beef production by 60% (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"Gabe Brown on saving money by not buying synthetic fertilizer.","quote":"Go listen to Gabe Brown, he saves thousands and thousands by not not paying for synthetic fertilizers.","text":">If they could increase production by 60% with any additive at all, it would immediately see widespread use. 1. No, that's not true at all 2. It's astounding how everywhere I go online there is someone spouting off nonsense which is then repeated and perpetuated. 3. Go listen to Gabe Brown, he saves thousands and thousands by not not paying for synthetic fertilizers. \"Above every surface acre on earth there's approximately 32,000 tons of atmospheric nitrogen, why would any farmer want to write a check for nitrogen?, I just can't figure that one out\" -- Gabe Brown [VIDEO]"},{"id":37390875,"author":"rapind","month":"2023-09","story":"Farms that create habitat key to food security and biodiversity","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating talks going into detail on soil for farmers.","quote":"I found this fascinating series of talks a few years ago that go into great detail on soil for farmers","text":"As a complete outsider, I found this fascinating series of talks a few years ago that go into great detail on soil for farmers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37590768,"author":"fuzztester","month":"2023-09","story":"‘Less than half’ fresh produce sold globally makes any profit","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"","text":"Watch \"Treating the Farm as an Ecosystem with Gabe Brown Part 1, The 5 Tenets of Soil Health\" on YouTube [VIDEO]"},{"id":34219913,"author":"imtringued","month":"2023-01","story":"Five Things Silicon Valley Gets Wrong About Agriculture","stance":"rec","why":"Offered as relevant video on cover crops.","quote":"Here is a video that might interest you","text":"Would you rather grow cover crops or have soil depletion? Also cover crops don't result in a 50% increase in land usage. Here is a video that might interest you: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32566309,"author":"carapace","month":"2022-08","story":"Study first to link weed killer Roundup to convulsions in animals","stance":"rec","why":"Scientifically grounded techniques that improve topsoil while staying profitable.","quote":"See especially Gabe Brown's talk linked below","text":"More than borderline, there are many many examples these days of people practicing forms of ecologically-informed agriculture that match or exceed the yields of destructive systems and methods. When I first heard about Permaculture in the late 80's it was still pretty obscure, and regenerative agriculture in general was \"hippie-dippy\". Nowadays there are thousand of projects with videos on the youtube, and people are practicing ecologically harmonious farming all over the world. See especially Gabe Brown's talk linked below, he's not replacing current agriculture, just updating it with scientifically-grounded techniques that improve the volume and vitality of his topsoil while being more profitable. Quoting from some notes I've been keeping: There's the \"Grow Biointensive\" method which is designed to provide a complete diet in a small space while also building soil and fertility. They have been dialing it in for forty years and now have a turn-key system that is implemented and functioning all over the world. => [LINK] Grow Biointensive (These folks are also selling their system, but they also have e.g. manuals you can download for free. I find their site curiously hard to use.) ## Permaculture Permaculture could be called \"applied ecology\" (with a kind of hippie spin.) and a similar school (or parallel evolution) called \"Syntropic\" Agriculture. Both of these systems aim to mimic natural ecosystems to create \"food forests\" that produce crops year-round without inputs (no fert"},{"id":27809993,"author":"iorek_dev","month":"2021-07","story":"Organic and regenerative agriculture are revitalizing rural Montana economies","stance":"rec","why":"Realistic view on soil reconstruction aimed at convincing farmers.","quote":"A talk presenting a realistic view on soil reconstruction, what IMO is the best argument to convince farmers","text":"For those who are interested in the topic of soil reconstruction and balance in nature, some more sources: Documentary on the Loess plateau in China: [LINK] Talk on the Caledonian Forest: [LINK] Talk on using the \"herd effect\" in Africa: [LINK] A talk presenting a realistic view on soil reconstruction, what IMO is the best argument to convince farmers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24667317,"author":"carapace","month":"2020-10","story":"“Fungi Can Teach Us a New Way of Looking at the World”","stance":"rec","why":"Soil as an organism; fungal discussion at 80 minutes.","quote":"He talks about fungus starting around 1:20 (80 minutes in)","text":"I had an experience with mycelium that changed my way of looking at the world: [LINK] I am coming around to the POV that soil should be thought of as a kind of organism. Check out e.g.: \"Treating the Farm as an Ecosystem with Gabe Brown Part 1, The 5 Tenets of Soil Health\" [VIDEO] He talks about fungus starting around 1:20 (80 minutes in) [VIDEO] Fungal to bacterial ratios..."},{"id":23799715,"author":"vram22","month":"2020-07","story":"Investors say agroforestry is climate friendly and also profitable","stance":"rec","why":"Gabe Brown on integrated crops and livestock in practice.","quote":"Gabe Brown has been doing regenerative agriculture for over two decades on Brown's Ranch","text":"Gabe Brown has been doing regenerative agriculture for over two decades on Brown's Ranch, over 2000 acres, in Bismarck, North Dakota successfully. Food crops plus cover crops plus livestock, all integrated. Lot of good results compared to neighboring farmers doing the traditional synthetic fertilizer / pesticide / tilling / monoculture approach. There are others like him too. And he's been invited to and giving a lot of talks about it at many confs for years: Watch \"Treating the Farm as an Ecosystem with Gabe Brown Part 1, The 5 Tenets of Soil Health\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":22906621,"author":"VBprogrammer","month":"2020-04","story":"Fruit Trenches: Cultivating Subtropical Plants in Freezing Temperatures","stance":"rec","why":"Gabe Brown's farm-scale low-input farming.","quote":"Gabe Brown has done some interesting stuff in farm scale low input farming.","text":"Not sure if you've come come across it but Gabe Brown has done some interesting stuff in farm scale low input farming. [VIDEO] That said, I think there is a lot of interesting stuff in permaculture design. But in a lot of cases it is a bit of a pyramid scheme / agile consultancy feel going on."},{"id":21111892,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2019-09","story":"Healthy soil is the real key to feeding the world","stance":"rec","why":"Engaging, interesting series on soil.","quote":"I found this video series about soil really interesting","text":"I found this video series about soil really interesting: [VIDEO] It's long, and I was sure I would click away, but the presenter has a great tone and the content is heka cool."},{"id":32734089,"author":"imtringued","month":"2022-09","story":"Mangroves on a Landfill","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in defense of regenerative agriculture.","quote":"","text":"I don't know what scams you are looking at but regenerative agriculture is incredibly complicated and time consuming but not physically labor intensive because it is compatible with mechanization. Conventional argiculture is the simple and lazy version where instead of trying to understand your local ecosystem you just douse everything in fertilizer, pesticide, etc so you don't have to care what is happening in your soil. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27820990,"author":"rattray","month":"2021-07","story":"Organic and regenerative agriculture are revitalizing rural Montana economies","stance":"reference","why":"Brown discusses unsolved weed prevention in no-till organic.","quote":"he says that he hasn't yet found a way to make no-till organic work in terms of weed prevention.","text":"In this talk from 2017, around 1h30m in, he says that he hasn't yet found a way to make no-till organic work in terms of weed prevention. But, he said he was close to figuring it out. [VIDEO] Has any progress been made since? Is this an area where technology could help (eg robotic precision weeding machines)? Disclaimer that I have yet to read your article so it may already be covered."},{"id":27976631,"author":"waterheater","month":"2021-07","story":"A soil-science revolution upends plans to fight climate change","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"One teaspoon of healthy soil contains more bacteria, fungi and other microbes than there are humans on Earth. Those hungry organisms can make soil a difficult place to store carbon over long periods of time.\" The natural respiration of soil microbes is small compared to how much carbon can be naturally sequestered in healthy soil due to sustainable agricultural practices. Healthy soil is well-known to hold substantial amounts of carbon, right along side such organisms. The development of unsustainable agricultural practices (monocultures, single-planting seasons, letting fields lie fallow, tilling, chemical sprays, essentially Monsanto's entire business model) has destroyed soil biodiversity and health. Healthy soil can absorb an inch of rain every few minutes. Fields flood (and crops are subsequently lost) because the ground is hard and crusty, preventing soil absorption. If more cropland soil had the healthy consistency of cottage cheese, flooding wouldn't be an issue. Yes, forced carbon sequestration might not work in the presence of healthy soil. However, fixing the deficient soils created across the world from unsustainable industrial agriculture practices will naturally sequester carbon. I would love to know exactly how much carbon no longer is trapped in our soils that once was due to the last 100+ years of unsustainable industrialized agriculture. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":24837499,"author":"sudoaza","month":"2020-10","story":"No-till no-herbicide farming system in trial since 1981","stance":"reference","why":"Comparison of soils and farming methods.","quote":"Here is a thorough comparison of soils and methods","text":"Experiments on soil compaction show the rolling to be better, the roll needs not to be that heavy, only to kill the vegetable. Also, you don't do a separate pass on the ground, you just roll it at sow time, so no extra gas. Also, the vegetable cover absorbs CO2 and later fix it to the ground. Here is a thorough comparison of soils and methods [VIDEO]"},{"id":22089925,"author":"arrosenberg","month":"2020-01","story":"A biotechnology dream: nitrogen-fixing cereal crops","stance":"reference","why":"Intro to restorative agriculture.","quote":"Restorative Ag 101","text":"> So who are you planning to put to work as agricultural stoop-labor peasants? Are you volunteering? I mean, we used to harvest all our crops that way. The people who did it were generally...not volunteers. I find it strange how we have cell phones and solar power and other these other wonders, and yet arguments always goes straight to slavery anytime the specter of higher labor costs comes up. One would think innovation, especially on this forum. The obvious, immediate way would be to fix the immigration laws and enforcement to allow in as much guest labor as needed. If the economics are an issue, then selective use of farm subsidies are appropriate given the positive value being added to the land in terms of increased productivity and soil quality. Eventually innovation will bring the costs down. > As far as higher yields go, I don't believe you. Another silly comparison to the past - agricultural science has advanced multiple times in the last 80 years. No one would argue that modern, chemistry-based farming isn't productive, simply that it's less productive (and far, FAR more detrimental to the soil) than what modern science around restorative agriculture can produce. Restorative Ag 101: [VIDEO] For what it's worth, I also think Corn yields are probably impacted by how heavily subsidized it is. That's a policy that started with the New Deal in 1933, so it makes sense that the graphic would start to increase there."},{"id":21074611,"author":"arrosenberg","month":"2019-09","story":"Ultraprocessed Foods – A New Theory of Obesity","stance":"reference","why":"Source for how fertilizer and CO2 affect plant nutrients.","quote":"Gabe Brown mentioned it in this lecture on regenerative agriculture.","text":"Gabe Brown mentioned it in this lecture on regenerative agriculture. Sorry, but I don't have a timestamp, it's been a few weeks since I watched it. [VIDEO] I'm not an expert in farming, but I do know a little bit of plant biology. My understanding of what he said was that the combination of atmospheric CO2 and fertilizer increases the growth rate of the plant, but the plant doesn't uptake a proportionately larger amount of other micro nutrients, either because it is biologically rate limited or doesn't have access. Other Googling suggests that it could be a result of selective breeding picking crops that grow faster but are less nutritious, but that would seem to be a correlated problem. Those crops tend to be selected for their ability to grow with synthetic fertilizer and *-cides, with the lack of nutritional value being an unintended consequence."}],"r":[["gkj_91IJVBk",1],["rk6z1vP4Eo8",1],["Kas0tIxDvrg",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["NHWjlCaIrQo",1],["eNliOm9NtCM",1],["VO_1AdYRGW8",1],["Mc_4Z1oiXhY",1],["vxopfjXkArM",1],["_IgqJr8jG8M",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["Z-9rLlFgcm0",1],["-8nqnOcoLqE",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1],["r-A78RgMhZU",1],["4F72VULWFvc",1],["EgiMu8A3pi0",1],["NIu_dJGyIQI",1],["fAxHlLK3Oyk",1],["Em7nqDqQ8oM",1]]},"c12cYAUTXXs":{"wy":"The go-to talk showing how Erlang let a tiny team run a massive messaging system.","q":{"x":"watch this talk and imagine trying to recreate that system with only a handful of server engineers","i":14748689,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":24445167,"author":"kornish","month":"2020-09","story":"Scaling Erlang Developer Experience at WhatsApp [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"In-depth system performance and bottleneck mitigation curriculum","quote":"One of my favorite talks is by Rick Reed about scaling Erlang at WhatsApp.","text":"One of my favorite talks is by Rick Reed about scaling Erlang at WhatsApp. What an absolute savage. He flies through an articulate and in-depth curriculum on system performance and bottleneck mitigation. The talk is called \"That's 'Billion' with a 'B'\" and makes for a great lunchtime watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14748689,"author":"kornish","month":"2017-07","story":"How Discord Scaled Elixir to 5M Concurrent Users","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Specialized tech enabling a handful of engineers to run huge system","quote":"watch this talk and imagine trying to recreate that system with only a handful of server engineers","text":"Not sure why this comment saw a couple downvotes earlier. mbesto is correct: for most startups, most of the time, competitive advantage doesn't come from the underlying tech stack. To make a general statement, most things could be done similarly on any of several platforms. However, when product requirements match exceptionally well with a specialized technology, you can see things that would simply be infeasible or extremely tough using a different stack. WhatsApp + Erlang was one of those cases (watch this talk and imagine trying to recreate that system with only a handful of server engineers using any other tech: [VIDEO] ). Discord + Elixir appears to be another. Curious if anyone has any examples that spring to mind from outside the highly concurrent messaging space."},{"id":8087517,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2014-07","story":"Revisiting 1M Writes per second","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tiny team handling enormous volume and complexity","quote":"how small their team was compared to the volume and complexity of the operation.","text":"My other favorite \"scalability\" study is from WhatsApp: [VIDEO] That's 'Billion' with a 'B': Scaling to the Next Level at WhatsApp (that walk title was create before the acquisition and was mean to imply message count, after the acquisition it got a secondary meaning). The one thing that is fascinating about it, is how small their team was compared to the volume and complexity of the operation."},{"id":32326449,"author":"dpeck","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: What startsups made the best early-tech decisions?","stance":"rec","why":"Glimpse into WhatsApp's own view of its early tech decisions","quote":"is a good glimpse into their own view of it","text":"WhatsApp. Rick Reed’s “That’s Billion with a B” presentation is a good glimpse into their own view of it [VIDEO]"},{"id":15161747,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2017-09","story":"WhatsApp Cofounder on How It Reached 1.3B Users Without Losing Its Focus","stance":"rec","why":"Description of the WhatsApp stack and how Erlang helped","quote":"Here is the description of their stack and how Erlang helped them by Rick Reed at Erlang Factory","text":"Here is the description of their stack and how Erlang helped them by Rick Reed at Erlang Factory: [VIDEO] Here is Jamshid Mahdavi talking about their stack as well but focusing on development, testing and shipping code. Some of the stuff they do will surprise you if you've been at a shop with a large QA team and deployment pipelines with many stages. [LINK]"},{"id":13858908,"author":"otterley","month":"2017-03","story":"A comment left on Slashdot","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk on scaling WhatsApp's Erlang infrastructure","quote":"Here's a great talk from Rick Reed","text":"Here's a great talk from Rick Reed (a former mentor of mine at Yahoo!) about how WhatsApp scaled their Erlang infrastructure: [LINK] Here's another talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11673148,"author":"ddeck","month":"2016-05","story":"Introducing WhatsApp's Desktop App","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting tech stack with huge scale on limited hardware","quote":"Their technology stack (FreeBSD/Erlang) is pretty interesting, more info here: 2014 talks by Rick Reed","text":"It's required because your phone is where your messages are stored. Whatsapp don't retain messages/media after they've been delivered to your phone, which is a compelling privacy feature for many. It's also what allows them to serve such an enormous user base with limited hardware. Their technology stack (FreeBSD/Erlang) is pretty interesting, more info here: 2014 talks by Rick Reed: [VIDEO] [LINK] Slides: [LINK] There's likely no technical reason why you couldn't use a pc instead of a phone for users that want to use the pc as the primary client (with the phone optionally accessing the DB on the pc in the same way that the desktop client does for the phone). Perhaps they've decided that this is a small and declining market. Edit: Slides for second talk [LINK] 440k connections/sec, 1.1 million msgs/sec, 1 billion images/day, and that was in 2014..."},{"id":10226673,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2015-09","story":"Why WhatsApp Only Needs 50 Engineers for Its 900M Users","stance":"rec","why":"Rick Reed explains how the product works","quote":"He explains how the product works","text":"Not \"parent\" but search for Erlang Factory talks they talk about it. Here is one from Rick Reed. He explains how the product works : [VIDEO]"},{"id":8664120,"author":"rbsn","month":"2014-11","story":"Yahoo Mail moving to React","stance":"rec","why":"WhatsApp's goals for scaling to billions of users","quote":"here is a presentation given at Erlang Factory 2014 about their goals with scaling WhatsApp","text":"Just to follow up on topic of WhatsApp and Erlang, here is a presentation given at Erlang Factory 2014 about their goals with scaling WhatsApp to billions of simultaneous users. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8421162,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2014-10","story":"Mnesia and CAP","stance":"rec","why":"How WhatsApp made Mnesia work with a small team","quote":"Here is a video of Rick Reed talking about how they did it","text":"Unrelated, but maybe interesting: WhatsApp was running Mnesia and made it work fantastically. Especially with such a small team of engineers. Here is a video of Rick Reed talking about how they did it (hint it is not a one monolithic Mnesia cluster). [VIDEO]"},{"id":16964716,"author":"kbenson","month":"2018-05","story":"Jan Koum to leave after broad clashes with Facebook","stance":"reference","why":"Server counts and architecture figures from the talk","quote":"he talks about ~550 servers, which includes 250 multimedia servers and 150 chat servers.","text":"There's a talk one of the engineers gives[1] (that someone else posted here so I'm watching it) about their architecture that's published in March of 2014, and in it he talks about ~550 servers, which includes 250 multimedia servers and 150 chat servers. The only places he mentions 16 is when he talks about the \"multimedia database\". I think there were 16 sharded database servers. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14935705,"author":"hellbanner","month":"2017-08","story":"Ask HN: Who is not betting on Elixir and why not?","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence Erlang is proven at massive scale","quote":"WhatsApp Scaling to 1 Billion users in erlang","text":"Yes, I understand, I was just wondering if Erlang would be considered a \"proven language\". From my (limited) Erlang experience, Erlang & Elixir have the same core functionality. Elixir has a \"nicer\" syntax (I prefer Erlang's syntax but most devs I've talked to like Elixir's). So if Elixir isn't proven but the concurrency model & programming paradigms make sense, maybe Erlang is a good choice. * WhatsApp Scaling to 1 Billion users in erlang: [VIDEO] * [LINK]"},{"id":14995477,"author":"hellbanner","month":"2017-08","story":"Ask HN: Who is not betting on Elixir and why not?","stance":"reference","why":"Guess at relevant talk, unwatched","quote":"I haven't watched but this would be a good guess","text":"I haven't watched but this would be a good guess [VIDEO] \"Scaling with A B: Erlang and WhatsApp\""},{"id":9827633,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2015-07","story":"Mnesia and LevelDB: Liberating Mnesia from the limitations of DETS","stance":"reference","why":"How WhatsApp uses Mnesia","quote":"Here is an talk on how WhatsApp uses Mnesia","text":"Klarna (the European payment processor) and WhatsApp architectures are built on Mnesia. Mnesia is Erlang's built-in distributed database. Due to its age it has a few bad corner cases, that could be fixed if it just had a better backend (the front-end API is nice, well designed, supports transactions, is built-in to the lanauge). So this effort brought in the ability to have a better backend, and make Mnesia a better option as a general purpose distributed database. Here is an talk on how WhatsApp uses Mnesia: Erlang Factory 2014 - That's 'Billion' with a 'B': Scaling to the Next Level at WhatsApp [VIDEO] Here is an example of using Mnesia: [LINK]"},{"id":8928868,"author":"ddeck","month":"2015-01","story":"WhatsApp doesn't understand the web","stance":"reference","why":"Details of WhatsApp architecture","quote":"Details on their architecture","text":"I believe the issue is that unlike web chats and apps like Telegram, Whatsapp doesn't store conversations on their servers beyond what is required to deliver messages to the required destination device(s). This presents obvious problems with having not just a web client, but any client other than the main device, since the main device is the only store of the existing conversations. New messages sent also need to be synchronized across devices, which I presume is why it's required to keep your phone connected to the Internet. Otherwise Whatsapp would need keep conversations on their servers until they could be synced, which is very much not their model. Details on their architecture: [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["KtQ9nt2ZeGM",1],["08mwXICY4JM",1],["PDylgzybWAw",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["lKXe3HUG2l4",1],["fhOHn9TClXY",1],["hyry8mgXiTk",1],["rmueBVrLKcY",1],["p5Ac7ap_MAY",1],["ljOoGyCso8s",1],["TneLO5TdW_M",1],["h3gwyHNo7MI",1],["sG9UMMq2dz4",1],["TVwKjGbz60k",1],["BpsMkLaEiOY",1],["PIPjmmmh_os",1],["XJ9ckqCMiKk",1]]},"SGJ5cZnoodY":{"wy":"Strongly recommended in Shenzhen and hardware threads for its view of fast cycles, loose IP and the maker ecosystem.","q":{"x":"There's an excellent documentary on the hardware/hacking ecosystem in Shenzhen on YouTube","i":13534113,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":17539263,"author":"lubujackson","month":"2018-07","story":"Letter from Shenzhen","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows Shenzhen's maker mindset reaching buyers, and how the hoverboard fad arose.","quote":"For a companion piece I highly recommend this hour long vid by Wired about Shenzhen. It's worth your time","text":"For a companion piece I highly recommend this hour long vid by Wired about Shenzhen. It's worth your time: [VIDEO] Things that stood out to me was the maker mindset was so prevalent and competitve that it had filtered down to the buyers. So someone cobbles together some new widget for their phone and starts to sell it. Other people see it selling a nd copy it, but they make modifications. Shoppers see it for the first time and decide they want one with a purple strap, so they shop around until they find it or find someone who will make it that way. This is where the hoverboard fad bubbled up but also how and why it \"flamed\" out. I think it's a fascinating testbed for technology and creativity and economics."},{"id":14979334,"author":"smckt","month":"2017-08","story":"Shenzhen: China’s ‘skyscraper capital’","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A mesmerizing Wired UK feature on Shenzhen.","quote":"I'd highly recommend watching this mesmerizing feature on Wired UK","text":"If interested about Shenzhen, I'd highly recommend watching this mesmerizing feature on Wired UK: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14061364,"author":"tmoreton","month":"2017-04","story":"Shenzhen is a hothouse of innovation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed look at Shenzhen.","quote":"highly recommend watching the documentary.","text":"WIRED UK did a great job going into more detail on Shenzhen, highly recommend watching the documentary. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13534113,"author":"drewmate","month":"2017-01","story":"A 24-year-old made $345K in 2 months by beating Kickstarters to market","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on Shenzhen's hardware and hacking ecosystem and its fast tech cycles.","quote":"There's an excellent documentary on the hardware/hacking ecosystem in Shenzhen on YouTube","text":"There's an excellent documentary on the hardware/hacking ecosystem in Shenzhen on YouTube: [VIDEO] . A lot of people there seem to embrace the ethos of moving fast and ignoring IP laws (both original creator and spoofer) because by the time you get a lawsuit or patent filed, the tech will have moved far beyond what you were trying to protect, and it will no longer be worth your time. Basically, move fast and try to keep up. It's crazy how fast the tech cycles are there."},{"id":12767698,"author":"coord","month":"2016-10","story":"Mirai Botnet Client, Echo Loader and CNC source code","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows the extreme competitive pressure on Shenzhen hardware companies.","quote":"I wholeheartedly recommend WIRED's Future Cities documentary on Shenzhen and the companies that dwell there.","text":"I cannot wait for some type of top-down pressure to force IoT developers to take security seriously. The movement has been pushed into overdrive thanks to insane levels of competition where you either crush your R&D into the smallest breakneck period or you live to see your creation being sold for half of what your budget can allow by other firms lifting your efforts while you're still at the workbench.[1] I've been getting cozy with Shenzhen-based hardware accelerators for the past year as part of a personal side-venture and I have not seen a group so pressured to deliver a product as fast as possible with security being a casual afterthought. To get a decent taste of what it's like, I wholeheartedly recommend WIRED's Future Cities documentary on Shenzhen and the companies that dwell there.[2] Their struggles for ephemeral market-share are endemic of the entire community that's taken over embedded hardware for the past few years. The saddest aspect of it all is that this market competition isn't benefiting the consumer. IoT devices are coming out of the factories poorly engineered, badly maintained for far too short of a time, and as we've learned from this attack, being used as vectors for network intrusion and distributed censorship. Even arduino founder Massimo Banzi's widely-lauded IoT Manifesto[3] fails to approach any comprehensive statement regarding a dev's responsibility to build in some level of security to their devices. It just isn't part of the fast-and-loose cu"},{"id":32804101,"author":"irsagent","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"rec","why":"Item two in a list of loved documentaries, on Shenzhen's copycat manufacturing culture.","quote":"","text":"Here is a list of 20 documentaries I have come to love and return to when I come across them. There is no specific order to them. Enjoy! The * next to the number indicates the last 4 years. If you have seen one listed that you'd like to share what you think about please leave a comment with this structure [# in the list ] Comment... . i.e. [1] Loved learning about Noyce. What a guy. 1. Silicon Valley: Where the Future | PBS: American Experience [1:23:19] [2013] History of the how Silicon Valley came to be. William Shockley. Robert Noyce. Fairchild Semiconductor, The Traitorous Eight. Homepage: [LINK] Kanopy: [LINK] Where to Watch: [LINK] 2. Shenzhen: The Silicon Valley of Hardware | Wired UK: Future Cities [1:07:50] [2016] the evolution of “Shanzhai” – or copycat manufacturing – has transformed traditional models of business, distribution and innovation, and asks what the rest of the world can learn from this so-called “Silicon Valley of hardware\". Youtube: [VIDEO] *2.1. The People's Republic of The Future | Bloomberg: Hello World [30:10] [2019] Shenzhen, tech-fueled entrepreneurs try to navigate an authoritarian regime. Youtube: [LINK] 3. The Pirate Bay Away From Keyboard | TPB AFK [1:22:07] [2013] The history of the Pirate Bay. Torrenting. Legal. Privacy. Homepage: [LINK] Youtube: [LINK] Where to Watch: [LINK] 4. Can’t Get You Out of My Head | BBC [6 Part] [2021] Adam Curtis takes you on \"An Emotional history of how we got to this place.\" Economics. History. Power. Psycholo"},{"id":21987648,"author":"nexus6mike","month":"2020-01","story":"Shenzhen’s economy under strain: blame the trade war?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"there's a great Wired documentary on Shenzhen","text":"Short-term demand seems to be shifting to Vietnam. This seems to have enabled a 'wait and see' approach re: China + tariffs. However, I'd question if Vietnam has enough long-term manufacturing capacity [1] [2] [3] to sustain the short-term advantage it seems to be currently enjoying. Today, China has 16 of the world's 50 busiest ports, including Shenzhen at #3 (all as measured by 2018 TEU volume). [4] However, this is less indicative of capacity than it is of current demand / throughput. Both countries could benefit from building more deep-water ports. China currently has two Panamax ports, with a third on the way. Vietnam has none and is building its first. The U.S., by comparison, has 42 by my count. [5] Separately, there's a great Wired documentary on Shenzhen: [VIDEO] . [1] [LINK] [2] www.scmp.com/economy/china-economy/article/3018403/chinese-manufacturers-returning-home-inefficient-vietnam [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":18717594,"author":"thisisit","month":"2018-12","story":"Jack Ma: US wasted trillions on warfare instead of investing in infrastructure","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the boundary between the city and urban village, and relocation of 150k people.","quote":"the brilliant Wired documentary on Shenzhen","text":"Whenever people talk about failures of government to build infrastructure I can't help but look back at the brilliant Wired documentary on Shenzhen. It depicts a boundary line between the city and urban village. And government had the power to relocate 150k in a snap to get the development going [1] How many countries can actually do that? Sure, couple of hundreds of people can be moved but 100k people, I am sure that cause a riot of sorts and project shut down very quickly. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32358154,"author":"chris_st","month":"2022-08","story":"Amazon to acquire maker of Roomba vacuum for roughly $1.7B","stance":"reference","why":"Examines Shenzhen's copycat manufacturing ecosystem and what the world can learn from it.","quote":"","text":"Bunnie Huang [1] has written/spoke on this topic. I think he mentions it in the Shenzen documentary [2]. Quote from the documentary page: > We examine the unique manufacturing ecosystem that has emerged, gaining access to the world’s leading hardware-prototyping culture whilst challenging misconceptions from the west. The film looks at how the evolution of “Shanzhai” – or copycat manufacturing – has transformed traditional models of business, distribution and innovation, and asks what the rest of the world can learn from this so-called “Silicon Valley of hardware\". 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24458500,"author":"searchableguy","month":"2020-09","story":"Ex-Google boss Eric Schmidt: US 'dropped the ball' on innovation","stance":"reference","why":"Explains cultural and legal reasons behind Shenzhen's hardware industry.","quote":"A documentary by WIRED UK done in 2016 explains many cultural and legal reasons","text":"China’s funding for science and research to reach 2.5 per cent of GDP in 2019: [LINK] A documentary by WIRED UK done in 2016 explains many cultural and legal reasons: [VIDEO] China has hardware industries that work similar to how open source software works. An article on effects of expiring patents on 3D printing (2013): [LINK] 90 Minutes a Day, Until 10 P.M.: China Sets Rules for Young Gamers: [LINK] Many Asian countries have already taken strong steps for digital addiction (which will be the next important problem to tackle after covid, I bet). China’s sustainable urban transport revolution: [LINK] [LINK] > Overall, 79% of people between the ages of 12 and 24 in Brazil, China, India, Indonesia, Kenya, Mexico, Nigeria, and Russia say they feel optimistic about the direction of the world, compared to only about half of those in far more well-off places like Australia, France, Germany, Great Britain, and Sweden. In the United States, the number is 63%. [LINK] > Of the top countries on our survey, China has developers that are the most optimistic, believing that people born today will have a better life than their parents. Developers in Western European countries like France and Germany are among the least optimistic about the future. [LINK] China is also much larger country than US. It has 3x more people than US. Those individuals are going to be at par with people in west at some point. There are so many interesting things to discuss and question."},{"id":22929043,"author":"Jugurtha","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: Any good resources, case studies about bootstrapped Hardware startups?","stance":"reference","why":"Hardware startups in Shenzhen move very fast, with Andrew Huang.","quote":"Apparently, hardware startups there move really, really fast.","text":"Hewlett Packard[0] is interesting. I'm sure one could find companies for which the place was called Silicon Valley. You could also check out Shenzhen and ask Andrew Huang.[1] Apparently, hardware startups there move really, really fast.[2] He also has a book entitled \"The Essential Guide to Shenzhen\", here's a thread about it.[3] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [Vice report with Andrew Huang] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":19981172,"author":"yogthos","month":"2019-05","story":"ARM memo tells staff to stop working with Huawei","stance":"reference","why":"Counterexample showing pioneering happens where IP is not protected.","quote":"This is demonstrably false","text":">There is no incentive for being a pioneer in a culture where IP is not protected. This is demonstrably false [VIDEO]"},{"id":19652426,"author":"freeflight","month":"2019-04","story":"As China Hacked, U.S. Businesses Turned a Blind Eye","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's super weird how everybody considered Trump odd for his hate for China, yet these days so many people just repeat the, usually completely baseless, anti-China FUD. Whether it's Huawei supposedly spying on everybody or the Chinese government putting implants on Super Micro boards, nothing is too absurd to be spread by, out of all parties, Five eyes themselves. Does China hack? Of course, so does the US, it even steals IP from allies. But I seriously doubt the damages for that go into the $57 billion, that's just a piracy-damages like inflated number. In reality, a whole lot of interesting innovation, particularly on the hardware level, has already been happening in China for years already. They gonna out-make the US maker movement, on a massive scale [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18355805,"author":"Kaveren","month":"2018-11","story":"Notorious patent enforcement entity values its entire portfolio at $2, folds","stance":"reference","why":"Interesting on loose IP rights in Shenzhen.","quote":"Watched Shenzhen: The Silicon Valley of Hardware, was interesting in regards to loose IP rights.","text":"The patent system needs to be changed in some way. When people talk about monopolies, they seem to rarely mention that the government enforces awful patents. And you don't even need to make the thing, just patent it! I'm not sure if it's fair to R&D costs to gut patents entirely, but I'd like to see them weakened considerably. Patent trolls should not exist. Glad to see justice in this case. Edit: Watched Shenzhen: The Silicon Valley of Hardware, was interesting in regards to loose IP rights. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18087346,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2018-09","story":"How China Systematically Pries Technology from U.S. Companies","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Keeping customers. See Shenzhen which is a free-for-all but out-innovates about everyone else in product development: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15336709,"author":"a1371","month":"2017-09","story":"China Blocks WhatsApp","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I doubt anybody will ever see this response, but about the Hoverboard, here it is: Hoverboards are originally from China [1] however, most of us have likely learned about them from Kickstarter etc. I think the users of HN would agree that \"invention is never a sudden thing\". The process for hoverboards has been iterative, of course. I have heard claims of having commercial hoverboards as much as five years before they became household names in the US. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14050233,"author":"gcmartinelli","month":"2017-04","story":"Ask HN: The most cyberpunk city in the world?","stance":"reference","why":"Street electronics repair and curbside circuit boards give a cyberpunk feel.","quote":"I got a very strong cyberpunk vibe, especially the last 1/3 of it.","text":"Watching this documentary ( [VIDEO] ) about Shenzhen I got a very strong cyberpunk vibe, especially the last 1/3 of it. People at the streets fixing electronics, cheap/pirated circuit boards being sold at the curb, etc."},{"id":13890840,"author":"aub3bhat","month":"2017-03","story":"Azure Intellectual Property Advantage","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Latest innovation in Cloud Computing! Frivolous Lawsuit Defense As A Service. I think at some point the archaic IP laws in USA would mean that deployment of cutting edge technology ends up happening in China. This is already happening in Shenzen. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12706266,"author":"jsnathan","month":"2016-10","story":"What $50 buys you at the Huaqiangbei electronics market in Shenzhen, China","stance":"reference","why":"Hour-long Wired feature on Shenzhen that also visits the markets.","quote":"Wired did a 1-hour feature recently called 'Shenzhen: The Silicon Valley of Hardware'","text":"Wired did a 1-hour feature recently called 'Shenzhen: The Silicon Valley of Hardware' [1]. They also visit the markets. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12216837,"author":"uola","month":"2016-08","story":"China, Not Silicon Valley, Is Cutting Edge in Mobile Tech","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The thing is that no one is going to explain to you what's going on in China. Understanding the Chinese ecosystem takes considerable effort and the people that do understand would rather talk to others in the know than to a wall. I actually cringe a bit even writing this. If you want to think that there's no difference between PayPal, eBay and modern Chinese versions you are free to do so. But until you've used these things in China (or at least had sufficient imagination), you won't actually know if there is something there. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["lG4VkPoG3ko",1],["WXuK6gekU1Y",1],["taZJblMAuko",1],["RLySXTIBS3c",1]]},"zDZFcDGpL4U":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible, widely shared critique of schooling, and cites it in threads on ADHD, creativity and education reform.","q":{"x":"One of my favorite videos on reforming education","i":5041602,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":12345306,"author":"crack-the-code","month":"2016-08","story":"Overselling A.D.H.D.","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Eye-opening case for rethinking education.","quote":"I strongly recommend this eye-opening video for those of you who haven't already seen it","text":"While I do think that ADHD is a real condition, I don't think that the solution is to medicate children with a very serious life changing drug that alters the core aspects of a child's personality and behavior. The true solution is to revamp our country's education system and provide a framework that can accommodate the wide variety of personalities that come into the classroom. Instead of forcing children to think one way, we should let them think the way they NATURALLY think, and flourish with their native mindset. I strongly recommend this eye-opening video for those of you who haven't already seen it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9574597,"author":"pcurve","month":"2015-05","story":"The Sound of TED: A Case for Distaste","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspiring talk turned into an RSA Animate.","quote":"Some of these talks (not just TED) are incredibly inspiring.","text":"Some of these talks (not just TED) are incredibly inspiring. I particularly love the ones that were turned into RSA Animate. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":5041602,"author":"manglav","month":"2013-01","story":"The Case for a Teacher Bar Exam","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Animated Ken Robinson talk on reforming education.","quote":"One of my favorite videos on reforming education","text":"I have a question for HN. At what point do you become responsible for your own education? Before, you may have not had a public library near you. Nowadays, there is too much information. Curation? Multiple MOOC's have taken the problem and squashed it. Internet Access? I'm not sure, but I remember reading that Comcast gives subsidized rates in poor income areas. Computers / Notebooks? This I'm not sure about at all. What access do people have? Assuming people have access to education, at what point are \"educators\" defunct? I feel as though I would not have gone to high school had these MOOC's been available. However, I did have enough personal curiosity to pursue these things. In college now, I'm not \"spoon fed\" anymore, it is my responsibility to do well, and seek out help if I am struggling. I am just curious where people think that line is drawn, and if it's different for everyone or not. I am for the Bar exam - for goodness's sake, we still have teachers trying to teach Creationism! One of my favorite videos on reforming education, from a very logical perspective from Sir Ken Robinson. Bonus: it's pictorially animated! [VIDEO]"},{"id":23755941,"author":"austinl","month":"2020-07","story":"Academic acceleration in gifted youth: a 35-year longitudinal study","stance":"rec","why":"Ken Robinson on letting kids go deeper at their own pace.","quote":"I'd recommend this talk by Sir Ken Robinson on this topic generally","text":"I wholeheartedly agree, and came out so disillusioned with the public education system that I'm struggling to decide what the right answer will be for my children. I understand that many kids are not this way, but I was the type that read physics textbooks while in elementary school. We had a mentorship program that paired kids with someone from two grades above (e.g. 1st graders with 3rd graders). My reading level was always higher than my mentors' and it made me furious (and probably insufferable). I feel like my entire time in school was spent getting it out of the way so I could study things that genuinely interested me. I would love for my children to not feel that same constraint, but instead feel free to go deeper at their own pace. I'd recommend this talk by Sir Ken Robinson on this topic generally: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23165626,"author":"bsldld","month":"2020-05","story":"A non-moral dilemma about cheating, but it brings up some points (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Diagnoses how rigid school systems disengage kids.","quote":"It diagnose this problem very well.","text":"Isn't cheating a symptom of the failure in how the education system works? This^ very interesting video of Ken Robinson produced by RSA was recently posted by someone on this forum. It diagnose this problem very well. Trying to put kids in a system that is boxed due to constraints of age, time, subjects and grades is very harmful for the real development of individual kids. Kids understand this and get disengaged from the system but still have to force themselves to follow along because they are not provided with an alternative. ^ [VIDEO]"},{"id":23352686,"author":"cmehdy","month":"2020-05","story":"Seven-year-olds as hackers, and why we should imitate them","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrated summary of Robinson's talk including divergent thinking studies.","quote":"The author might like an illustrated summary of a talk given by Sir Ken Robinson","text":"The author might like an illustrated summary of a talk given by Sir Ken Robinson about \"Changing education paradigms\"[0], which includes some of the essence of this article (particularly referring to studies about how many uses one person can think of for a paperclip) [0] RSA Animate: [VIDEO] [1] Full talk (55 mins): [LINK]"},{"id":11800904,"author":"LionessLover","month":"2016-05","story":"On the Wildness of Children","stance":"rec","why":"Short, to-the-point alternative viewpoint.","quote":"Try this for another explanation/point of view (it's kept short and to the point)","text":"An indirect response: Try this for another explanation/point of view (it's kept short and to the point): [VIDEO] (About the speaker: [LINK]"},{"id":10861110,"author":"progressive_dad","month":"2016-01","story":"The concept of “learning styles” is one of the greatest neuroscience myths","stance":"rec","why":"Starting point for helping children learn.","quote":"If you want to help children then you're better off starting here","text":"Most education research is about how to evaluate TEACHERS not children. If you want to help children then you're better off starting here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10226614,"author":"nadams","month":"2015-09","story":"14-Year-Old Boy Arrested for Bringing Homemade Clock to School","stance":"rec","why":"Critique of the education system.","quote":"you should just watch this video","text":"If anyone is actually surprised by the administrations intelligence - I should remind you of 3 events: - A teacher confiscates Linux CDs claiming that the student was essentially distributing illegal copyrighted software - because no software is free [1] - A system administrator was fired for installing/running seti@home on school computers. There is a lot of controversy about this case - but I read one news article (that I can't find right now) where the administration said they would have been ok with cancer research folding@home rather than searching for aliens with seti@home. This combined with the backpedaling of \"oh actually he was a bad employee, stole things, and cost the school millions in extra in electricity costs!\" makes me believe that they just wanted to use it as an excuse to fire him and make the position open for a friend/relative. [2]. - Or an honor roll student suspended for buying candy from another student [3]. His statuses were only restored after it caught media attention. I would go into my rant about the education system but you should just watch this video [4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2479401,"author":"DanielRibeiro","month":"2011-04","story":"MIT Creates The One Video Game You'll Be Thrilled To See Your Kid Get Hooked On","stance":"rec","why":"Why current education systems have the problems they do.","quote":"This RSA video shows some reasons why the current education systems (not only US') has the problems it has","text":"This RSA video shows some reasons why the current education systems (not only US') has the problems it has: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48837616,"author":"mkolodny","month":"2026-07","story":"Show HN: Agent Draw: An agent draws while you talk, built on TLDraw","stance":"reference","why":"Example of RSA-style drawn visual explanation.","quote":"It could be fun to try creating an RSA-esque visual explanation video","text":"Good idea! It could be fun to try creating an RSA-esque visual explanation video instead of using canvas selection ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":45532937,"author":"johnisgood","month":"2025-10","story":"Python 3.14 is here. How fast is it?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Yeah, our education system sucks that much.","text":"I am a SWE without any degrees. :D I dropped out to... study. Yeah, our education system sucks that much. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29185651,"author":"prox","month":"2021-11","story":"How my genius roommate changed my perspective","stance":"reference","why":"Animated take on divergent thinking and changing education.","quote":"RSA Animate on education and changing the paradigm","text":"It’s called divergent thinking. Creativity is a form of intelligence. RSA Animate on education and changing the paradigm: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13131297,"author":"zdean","month":"2016-12","story":"'Clean your desk': My Amazon interview experience","stance":"reference","why":"Ken Robinson on collaboration being called cheating in school.","quote":"Ken Robinson on collaboration in education","text":"Ken Robinson on collaboration in education: \"In the work world, collaboration and team work are essential to success; in school, it's called cheating.\" More here [VIDEO]"},{"id":12387870,"author":"mSparks","month":"2016-08","story":"California bans ITT tech from accepting new students","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This whole premise of this story has me wearing a tin foil hat. [tldr] [LINK] Soooo many questions. so few answers. I'll start with my immediate thoughts. High dropouts are not absolutely a bad thing. In fact its pretty normal in all western schools that have zero focus on \"how to learn\". The \"problem\" is clearly earlier on in the education system, wherby students are reaching college age not knowing \"how to learn\", and suddenly finding its important. Next up - the cost of learning/knowledge material. I know of textbooks from the US being priced in the multi thousand dollar range (or simply not available for student purchase). \"Teachers/Lecturers\" aren't that important (I say that having graduated with a joint 1st from a top UK university having acquired a lot of warnings criticizing me for hardly ever attending lectures, but I wasn't skipping the material, I was skipping lecturers reading from textbooks). All that said, I am left feeling these stories are more about attacking education that provides chances for the lower classes in the US to become middle and upper classes. Nothing about this ITT tech story addresses this one way or the other. Where are the employment stats for those who successfully graduate? Where are the interviews with graduates? I just see a ton of hyperbole he said she said. And whenever I come across that I automatically assume its a political power based decision, rather than a rational \"good for everyone\" one. I know that all sounds a bit confused -"},{"id":11360290,"author":"justifier","month":"2016-03","story":"Why Anti-Authoritarians Are Diagnosed as Mentally Ill (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Abridged version of a talk on divergent thinking decline.","quote":"a delightfully digestible, abridged, version of this talk","text":"maybe what you are referring to: [VIDEO] which is a delightfully digestible, abridged, version of this talk: [LINK] whose transcript you can find here: [LINK] whose figures are drawn from this book: Break Point and Beyond, by George Land relevant bit: So my question to you is what percentage of the people tested of the 1,500 scored at genius level for divergent thinking. Now you need to know one more thing about them - these were kindergarten children. So what do you think? What percentage at genius level? 80? 98%. Now the thing about this was it was a longitudinal study, so they retested the same children five years later aged 8 to 10. What do you think? 50? They retested them again five years later, ages 13 to 15. You can see a trend here can't you?"},{"id":6238292,"author":"officemonkey","month":"2013-08","story":"\"Journey to Justinia\" – how I got my 5-year-old son to sit still for 5 hours","stance":"reference","why":"Ken Robinson on ADHD diagnosis in schools.","quote":"Here's Ken Robinson making speeder's point.","text":"Here's Ken Robinson making speeder's point. [VIDEO] I was gobsmacked when I saw a teacher \"diagnose\" ADHD in one of her students. The fact that this is done regularly is flabbergasting."},{"id":5200145,"author":"mixedbit","month":"2013-02","story":"I'm Slowly Losing My Mind","stance":"reference","why":"Talk claiming ADHD is creative minds bored by dull tasks.","quote":"According to this talk","text":"I'm not sure if this a good recommendation. According to this talk: [VIDEO] ADHD are normal symptoms that occur when a creative mind has to perform dull tasks. ADHD becomes bigger and bigger problem, because today, there are much more stimulants that cause things like traditional school or work to be boring. According to the speaker, curing ADHD with pills solves the problem by reducing creative potential of a mind. A better approach would be to create an environment where people with ADHD can thrive. (Note, that I don't have any real experience with ADHD, this comment is based only on the talk)."},{"id":4874702,"author":"rogerbinns","month":"2012-12","story":"The Insourcing Boom","stance":"reference","why":"Education systems modelled on the industrial age.","quote":"current education systems are extremely broken and closely modelled on the dawn of the industrial age","text":"There are many academic pieces and work on the topic such as [LINK] Obviously some of the difference will be soaked up by new professions that didn't exist before. For example here is a list of jobs that didn't exist 10 years ago [LINK] I am convinced that prosperity is being driven by trade and specialisation as put forward by Matt Ridley [LINK] Specialisation is a form of skill and so the issue is really what happens to unskilled labour. Everyone \"knows\" that education is a fix, but current education systems are extremely broken and closely modelled on the dawn of the industrial age [VIDEO] It will be interesting to see if the cost of living goes down since that will also greatly alleviate things. Increased automation should result in that."},{"id":5200921,"author":"mistercow","month":"2013-02","story":"I'm Slowly Losing My Mind","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"ADHD denial is a very popular position.","text":">According to this talk: [VIDEO] ADHD are normal symptoms that occur when a creative mind has to perform dull tasks. Yes, ADHD denial is a very popular position. It has about as much backing in science as climate denial. The idea that ADHD meds reduce the \"creative potential\" of the mind is pure nonsense."}],"r":[["uF-GSj-Exms",1],["xb_PyKuI7II",1],["YX40hbAHx3s",1],["AxFzf6yIfcc",1],["GNO_CYUjMK8",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["Unzc731iCUY",1],["kYfNvmF0Bqw",1]]},"gkj_91IJVBk":{"wy":"Frequently linked as a sobering, data-grounded counterpoint to techno-optimism about decarbonization and green growth.","q":{"x":"Here's a fascinating talk by Václav Smil at the Driva Climate Investment Meeting in 2019","i":28733072,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":41535494,"author":"carapace","month":"2024-09","story":"Shrinking the economy won't save the planet","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great lecture; Smil skeptical of carbon capture at scale","quote":"It's a great lecture IMO, and he starts bagging on carbon capture around minute 39","text":"> the degrowth position is that people are inevitably going to lead worse lives than at present, but that we can choose between things getting kind of bad, by systematically pursuing degrowth, and things getting apocalyptically bad, which is what will happen if we continue pursuing growth I can't speak for others who advocate for degrowth, but that seems about right to me, with the caveat that I believe ecologically harmonious lifestyles would be more fun and fulfilling than what we have now. The challenge is to return to harmony with Nature while still enjoying the benefits of science and technology. - - - - > fourth, the most urgent environmental problem for human quality of life is global warming, and atmospheric carbon capture is necessary to solve it, a measure which will almost certainly require vast expansion of human energy exploitation and therefore economic growth (because you can have growth without increased resource exploitation, but probably not vastly increased resource exploitation without growth) Václav Smil has me convinced that atmospheric carbon capture on a scale great enough to counter-act our continued fossil fuel use is a pipe dream, pretty much for the reason you say: it takes too much energy to sequester the carbon. Here he is at Driva Climate Investment Meeting in 2019. It's a great lecture IMO, and he starts bagging on carbon capture around minute 39: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30674949,"author":"carapace","month":"2022-03","story":"Launch HN: Living Carbon (YC W20) – Trees that capture and store more carbon","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Makes the case to stop taking carbon from the ground","quote":"Here's a fascinating talk by Václav Smil at the Driva Climate Investment Meeting in 2019, where he makes the point better than I ever could","text":"> the ability to dramatically reduce world hunger is a direct consequence of humans' capacity to improve crop yields such as rice and corn through breeding and engineering. Not through \"breeding and engineering\", through artificial nitrogen fertilizer. We have been turning oil into people . > prevented the starvation of millions of people. That delayed the starvation of millions of people and now we are facing the prospect of the starvation of billions of people. FWIW, I do applauded Maddie and her team, and I wish them luck and hope they succeed. But the only way we are going to avert disaster is to stop taking carbon out of the ground and putting it into the air. That's it. Here's a fascinating talk by Václav Smil at the Driva Climate Investment Meeting in 2019, where he makes the point better than I ever could: [VIDEO] - - - - edit: i'm rate limited at the moment. Replying to HWR_14 below Dwarf wheat was generated through selective breeding, same as the last N-thousand years. And it was only necessary due to the defects of using artificial nitrogen fertilizer: > Taller wheat grasses better compete for sunlight, but tend to collapse under the weight of the extra grain—a trait called lodging—from the rapid growth spurts induced by nitrogen fertilizer Borlaug used in the poor soil. To prevent this, he bred wheat to favor shorter, stronger stalks that could better support larger seed heads. [LINK] Anyway, my point isn't that science doesn't work or isn't good, it's that engine"},{"id":28733072,"author":"carapace","month":"2021-10","story":"‘Green growth’ doesn’t exist – less of everything only way to avert catastrophe","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating talk on growth and the biosphere","quote":"Here's a fascinating talk by Václav Smil at the Driva Climate Investment Meeting in 2019","text":"Here's a fascinating talk by Václav Smil at the Driva Climate Investment Meeting in 2019: [VIDEO] Bill Gates calls him \"one of my favorite thinkers\" and reviews his recent book \"Growth\": > Vaclav Smil ... reaches the ... conclusion: Earth is fragile and “before it is too late, we should embark in earnest on the most fundamental existential [task] of making any future growth compatible with the long-term preservation of the only biosphere we have.” [LINK]"},{"id":20776293,"author":"raaaaraaaa2","month":"2019-08","story":"Singapore Says Musk's Electric Cars Are About 'Lifestyle,' Not Climate","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sobering view on technology not saving us","quote":"check this presentation","text":"On the other hand 1/2 of world looks more or less the same - rural Africa, india, china... If you believe we are highly adaptable and new technologies will 'save us' somehow check this presentation : [VIDEO] ...it's eye opening"},{"id":29656695,"author":"aksss","month":"2021-12","story":"Technology Behind the Lilium Jet","stance":"rec","why":"Smil on slow battery improvement rates","quote":"the whole talk is quite interesting.","text":"I recall Vaclav Smil saying batteries are consistently about 2-3% per year over the long haul. It's not a Moore's law scenario, but a very slow crawl. \"Miracles might happen,\" he says, \"but would I bet on a miracle happening? No.\" This link is to the point in the video where he says this, but the whole talk is quite interesting. [VIDEO] Edit: here's a more recent video of Smil talking about energy density of batteries and the problems there, specifically mentioning the unlikelihood of flying on battery anytime soon. [LINK]"},{"id":27497017,"author":"carapace","month":"2021-06","story":"What can a technologist do about climate change? (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk concluding only absolute energy cuts would work","quote":"The conclusion is sobering","text":"Use less energy. Here's Václav Smil at Driva Climate Investment Meeting 2019 giving a talk called \"Investing in a changing climate – what we can learn from historic energy transitions\". [VIDEO] The conclusion is sobering: \"Only absolute cuts in energy use would work.\" ( [VIDEO] ) We can do this and most of the technology needed is already invented and available, we just have to get our act together as a species and start using our tech efficiently. We don't have to endure a lower quality of life, just eliminating waste would get us most of the way there."},{"id":26983515,"author":"carapace","month":"2021-04","story":"Mining robot stranded on Pacific Ocean floor in deep-sea mining trial","stance":"rec","why":"Historic energy transitions; need to use less energy","quote":"The presentation is IMO very interesting, and the conclusion is sobering","text":"Batteries are not a great idea. E.g. an electric car should use alcohol fuel and a small generator rather than fancy batteries. You can grow sugar crops, convert them to fuel (reclaiming the non-fuel parts), and then use that to power your infrastructure. It's carbon-neutral, environmentally sound (waaaaaaay more than batteries), and you can do it today. No sea mines required. The only downside is that you have to live within your solar power budget, but we have to do that anyway to counter global warming (until cheap fusion becomes a thing.) Here's a video of Václav Smil at Driva Climate Investment Meeting 2019 giving a talk called \"Investing in a changing climate – what we can learn from historic energy transitions\". [VIDEO] The presentation is IMO very interesting, and the conclusion is sobering: \"Only absolute cuts in energy use would work.\" ( [VIDEO] ) We are going to have to use less energy. We don't have to endure lower QoL but we do have to achieve our Quality of Life more efficiently ."},{"id":26481130,"author":"aksss","month":"2021-03","story":"Moore's Law for Everything","stance":"rec","why":"Diagnosis of why Moore's law doesn't apply to everything","quote":"I think Vaclav Smil did an effective diagnosis of this at the Driva Climate Investment Meeting","text":"> “Moore’s Law for everything” should be the rallying cry of a generation whose members can’t afford what they want.\" Ugh. Moore's law doesn't apply to everything, and in fact doesn't apply to most things, and wishing it did won't change that. I think Vaclav Smil did an effective diagnosis of this at the Driva Climate Investment Meeting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26541984,"author":"carapace","month":"2021-03","story":"The operative system for a decarbonised, decentralised, digitised energy system","stance":"rec","why":"Historic energy transitions; only absolute energy cuts would work","quote":"The presentation is IMO very interesting, and the conclusion is sobering","text":"Small-scale alcohol fuel production integrated into regenerative agriculture. (This is different from large-scale industrial ethanol production.) You grow e.g. sugar beets, convert them to alcohol and some byproducts (that go back into the farm as feed and fertilizer), use the alcohol in generators and vehicles. You're operating within your solar budget, the atoms that make up the alcohol came from water and air so all your nutrients and trace minerals stay on the farm and the fuel is carbon-neutral (all the carbon released when you burn the fuel originally came from the air in the form of carbon dioxide, not from underground fossilized carbon), and it can be done with simple off-the-shelf tech that has been around for literally thousands of years. You don't have to scrap your ICE cars and trucks, you can modify them to work with alcohol as fuel (some cars it's a software mod!) and the exhaust is not poisonous. - - - - Here's a video of Václav Smil at Driva Climate Investment Meeting 2019 giving a talk called \"Investing in a changing climate – what we can learn from historic energy transitions\". [VIDEO] The presentation is IMO very interesting, and the conclusion is sobering: \"Only absolute cuts in energy use would work.\" ( [VIDEO] ) We use too much power. And when I say \"we\" I mean mostly the USA but the rest of the world is ramping up rapidly. Unless there's a miracle (like clean fusion generators that kids can make in the garage out of chicken wire, tires, and ducktape) th"},{"id":33563370,"author":"carapace","month":"2022-11","story":"New report paints dire picture of America's future as climate crisis accelerates","stance":"reference","why":"Smil on 1960s-level energy use not hurting quality of life","quote":"Vaclav Smil (among others) has pointed out that this really just means going back to a 1960's level of energy use for the USA","text":"Vaclav Smil (among others) has pointed out that this really just means going back to a 1960's level of energy use for the USA, and that's really not much of a hardship or downgrading of quality of life. Václav Smil at Driva Climate Investment Meeting 2019 [VIDEO] We have the technology to keep up or improve QoL while we reduce emissions. Just curbing our waste energy would get us halfway there, something like that. (E.g. insulating houses, plugging drafts, better grid management, etc.)"},{"id":31668056,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2022-06","story":"Germany to Introduce Bill to Accelerate Wind Energy Expansion","stance":"reference","why":"Smil calling wind turbines embodiment of fossil fuels","quote":"A reminder that Vaclav Smil has called wind turbines 'the perfect embodiment of fossil fuels'","text":"\"Germany’s economy and climate ministry will present a package of measures to speed up the expansion of wind energy, documents reviewed by Reuters showed on Wednesday, as the country turns to renewables to cut its use of Russian fossil fuels.\" A reminder that Vaclav Smil has called wind turbines 'the perfect embodiment of fossil fuels' : [VIDEO] (Václav Smil at Driva Climate Investment Meeting 2019)"},{"id":31865348,"author":"carapace","month":"2022-06","story":"A Random Distribution of Wealth (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Smil on returning to 1960s energy consumption","quote":"Vaclav Smil says something like \"Would it be so bad, going back to a 1960's level of energy consumption?","text":"Vaclav Smil says something like \"Would it be so bad, going back to a 1960's level of energy consumption?\" in the context of energy usages (and therefore roughly CO2 emissions) of Western and particularly American lifestyles. (I'm paraphrasing: Václav Smil at Driva Climate Investment Meeting 2019 [VIDEO] ) > hedonic adaptation pretty much guarantees that no matter what our position we will always want more There's an Indian holy man, now deceased, who said we need to learn to practice \"ceiling on desires\". It may be that only the expression of our higher values can clear this logjam? > so how this could happen without damage to the environment One thought that occurs to me is if we could create a kind of split-level economy. There would be a real-world ecologically-informed economy that met basic needs as efficiently as possible. On top of that substrate we would have a computer-mediated virtual economy where there were no (or very few) effective limits on hedonistic adaptation (or status-symbol seeking, or whatever). It seems like we're seeing something like that emerge \"naturally\" anyway, eh? NFT & \"crypto\", in-game purchases, whatever FB's metaverse becomes, etc. Eventually, people might submit voluntarily to enter \"the Matrix\": your physical body kept perfectly safe, and the only limits are your imagination? Not my cup of tea, but not insane on the face of it, eh?"},{"id":31268812,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2022-05","story":"Dude, where's my fuel?","stance":"reference","why":"Smil on wind turbines as embodiment of fossil fuels","quote":"how Vaclav Smil calls wind turbines 'the perfect embodiment of fossil fuels'","text":"I think the other tradeoff over the long run is long-run lifetime costs of replacing \"solar panels, and the wiring, fuses, breaker box, charge controller\" under the assumption that these aspects do have lifetime costs and are at best rebuildable and at worst produce waste (e.g. think about how Vaclav Smil calls wind turbines 'the perfect embodiment of fossil fuels' - [VIDEO] ). It's important to ask these questions of what the tradeoffs really are when thinking about Energy Returned on Energy Invested, whether e-fuels would actually decrease new car production (i.e. older cars get used longer if e-fuels really make sense), the political economy ramifications if these things really worked, etc. In prior waves of interest of things like algae-based fuels (see [LINK] ), my rough understanding is that they could be done but the numbers just didn't work out. Finally, it's really worth pointing out the concern of any of these things falling under solutionism when also thinking about the state of our planet from a more integrated framework such as the planetary boundaries: [LINK]"},{"id":26421195,"author":"aksss","month":"2021-03","story":"Nuclear power looks to regain its footing 10 years after Fukushima","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Hydrocarbons have nothing to worry about, even with nuclear. We are running into fossil fuels (our global usage is still climbing at a rapid pace), we have not flatlined, much less started to bend the curve downwards. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23568245,"author":"aksss","month":"2020-06","story":"536 was ‘the worst year to be alive’ (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There is zero chance of 100% replacement of fossil fuels with renewables. Zero. Without a Thanos solution. [VIDEO] EDIT: I mean, you guys are downvoting these comments, and I'm sorry to tell you things you don't want to hear, but would prefer that you respond with contrary information rather than downvoting. Happy to alter my views and engage in information sharing."}],"r":[["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["rk6z1vP4Eo8",1],["Kas0tIxDvrg",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["NHWjlCaIrQo",1],["eNliOm9NtCM",1],["VO_1AdYRGW8",1],["Mc_4Z1oiXhY",1],["vxopfjXkArM",1],["_IgqJr8jG8M",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["Z-9rLlFgcm0",1],["-8nqnOcoLqE",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1],["d1EB1zsxW0k",1],["Sp9H0MO-qS8",1]]},"rOwMW6agpTI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the definitive technical deep dive into Stuxnet's zero-days from someone who analyzed it first-hand.","q":{"x":"For an amazing technical deep dive into each 0-day Stuxnet vulnerability watch this talk with Bruce Dang from Microsoft[1].","i":27443182,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27443182,"author":"nodesocket","month":"2021-06","story":"Microsoft Patches Six Zero-Day Security Holes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Technical deep dive into each Stuxnet zero-day.","quote":"For an amazing technical deep dive into each 0-day Stuxnet vulnerability watch this talk with Bruce Dang from Microsoft[1].","text":"Are these on the same level as Stuxnet? For an amazing technical deep dive into each 0-day Stuxnet vulnerability watch this talk with Bruce Dang from Microsoft[1]. I really enjoy his natural speaking style (it's like talking about Stuxnet over beers with him). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5290889,"author":"tbrock","month":"2013-02","story":"Stuxnet Missing Link Found, Resolves Some Mysteries Around the Cyberweapon","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Microsoft team analysis of how Stuxnet worked.","quote":"I was on the edge of my seat!","text":"If anyone hasn't seen this video from the Microsoft team analyzing how this went down, I highly suggest watching. I was on the edge of my seat! [VIDEO]"},{"id":2591163,"author":"JonnieCache","month":"2011-05","story":"Did the US write Stuxnet? Deputy Defense Secretary won't deny it","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Opcode-level detail on Stuxnet exploits, with humor.","quote":"It's well worth watching anyway, lots of good info on the various exploits used by stuxnet, down to the opcode level.","text":"Also, check out this excellent 27C3 presentation from Bruce Dang, a microsoft malware analyst, where he cracks numerous gags about mossad. [VIDEO] It's well worth watching anyway, lots of good info on the various exploits used by stuxnet, down to the opcode level. He's a pretty funny guy as well."},{"id":2238740,"author":"spudlyo","month":"2011-02","story":"Black ops: how HBGary wrote backdoors for the government","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains Stuxnet's zero-day vulnerabilities.","quote":"I watched an excellent video explaining the 0-day vulnerabilities that stuxnet used","text":"LNK files can point to DLLs that get loaded by Explorer when it draws the icon for the link target. I only know this because I watched an excellent video explaining the 0-day vulnerabilities that stuxnet used by Microsoft leet hax0r Bruce Dang. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38961506,"author":"akira2501","month":"2024-01","story":"Engineer Used Water Pump to Get $1B Stuxnet Malware into Iranian Nuclear Plant","stance":"rec","why":"Thorough analysis showing Stuxnet relied on Microsoft zero-days.","quote":"even back then, the analysis was pretty thorough","text":"Does anyone else remember the 27C3 presentation on this? My take away was that Stuxnet was _largely_ enabled by 0day vulnerabilities in Microsoft's products. The especially damning one was the print spooler script running vulnerability. Anyways, from this perspective, even back then, the analysis was pretty thorough: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11112338,"author":"samstave","month":"2016-02","story":"Unilateral Israeli changes to Stuxnet caused its exposure, angering US","stance":"rec","why":"Tech talks on Stuxnet and Duqu design.","quote":"If you haven't watched the tech talks on stuxnet and duqu, please do - super interesting.","text":"Stuxnet was such an amazing event. The speculation at first, the quasi confirmation a little later based on certain items in the code. The dissection of it and the tech talks by Microsoft and others on its design and build... And then that it had a successor - and now it comes out that Israel and US squabbling over what team did what which allowed it to be exposed. It is such an amazing cornerstone in the cyberpunk reality of where the world is heading. If you haven't watched the tech talks on stuxnet and duqu, please do - super interesting. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":2677618,"author":"phlux","month":"2011-06","story":"Stuxnet Virus Weapon Now Being Sold On The Black Market","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed, long analysis of Stuxnet.","quote":"detailed analysis of stuxnet","text":"Watch these in this order: [LINK] - brief overview great animation [LINK] - ted talk on stux [VIDEO] detailed analysis of stuxnet the last one is long and Bruce appears to be an arrogant prick, but it is clear - cyber war has been here for a while."},{"id":2691438,"author":"phlux","month":"2011-06","story":"LulzSec releases trove of AZ law enforcement documents","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed analysis of Stuxnet.","quote":"detailed analysis of stuxnet","text":"PSY-OP MOTHERFUCKER, CAN YOU HACK IT?! This is NOT script kiddies going at it, this is bio-stux.... See the following: Watch these in this order: [LINK] - brief overview great animation [LINK] - ted talk on stux [VIDEO] . detailed analysis of stuxnet the last one is long and Bruce appears to be an arrogant prick, but it is clear - cyber war has been here for a while. --- As I have mentioned, cyber war is here - and the legislation is lacking in its awareness - but becuase we have incompetant douches in charge of tech legislation, we will have physical war used as a metaphor to cyber warfare and thus have draconian influences from physical imprisonment being applied to digital situations. Utterly broken."},{"id":2213224,"author":"dmix","month":"2011-02","story":"Stuxnet is now on GitHub","stance":"rec","why":"Microsoft employee breaks down each Stuxnet exploit.","quote":"a Microsoft employee broke down each of the exploits that Stuxnet used at a conference recently","text":"For those curious, a Microsoft employee broke down each of the exploits that Stuxnet used at a conference recently: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29205474,"author":"samstave","month":"2021-11","story":"How many people have Q Clearance?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I recall the two biggest stories about foreign spies in these labs: 1. Chinese blamed for stealing Los Alamos secret by stashing hard drives behind a copy machine and grabbing them later. 2. Chinese engineer at JPL worked there for decades and was thought to be a nice family in Pasadina - but lived in a really super spartan house and the wife was the engineer's handler and their kids were the mules to haul data back to ccp 3. China's trickle hack on Lockheed by phishing employees who had attended defense conferences 4. China hacking Lockheed providers in Taiwan with an air gap and sneaker-net to gain access to Lockheed laptops via USB exploits (this was one of the factors, IIRC why epoxy in USB ports was common) -- ((But if you ever had any SGI O2 machines -- there was an additional port (serial I think - I don't think they were USB) but this 'option' was several thousand more $ -- but it was on every machine and all you had to do was punch out the plastic cover from the case) 5. Israel + USA hacking Iran via STUXNET in the same method with air-gaps etc. 6. The guy who worked at oak ridge, took on a Russian Mail Order Bride and got her a job in oak ridge (accounting I think, I can't recall) -- and it turned out she was a Russian handler, but they denied it -- but everyone knew that every single person from the USG who went to Russia was assigned a female handler, and these handlers were highly trained, and they had several levels of handlers above them to ensure non-defection"}],"r":[["gd5uJ7Nlvvo",1],["JWD1Fpdd4Pc",1],["0g9BcZvQbXU",1],["gQnmP7UD_zk",1],["XI5frPV58tY",1],["lHcTKWiZ8sI",1],["8b7erU_DOfE",1],["VI_riscmviI",1],["mPqWstVnRjQ",1],["3CWCqJl0BEs",1],["C6nAxiym9oc",1],["YThelfB2fvg",1],["2Op3QLzMgSY",1],["NQFqDKRAROI",1],["O4Ko7NW2yQo",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1],["CS01Hmjv1pQ",1],["jSo5v5t4OQM",1],["NHopJHSlVo4",1],["4XpnKHJAok8",1]]},"rI8tNMsozo0":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an alternative or companion to 'Simple Made Easy', and quotes its ideas about simplicity, complexity and 'hairballs' in discussions of frameworks, JSON and data design.","q":{"x":"maybe watch this version instead, since it's almost the same talk but for a Rails Conference, with a few references","i":8857448,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":35757642,"author":"stcg","month":"2023-04","story":"The Egison Programming Language","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Timeless talk on simplicity in software","quote":"any talk by Rich Hickey is worth watching","text":"Related: the great and timeless talk called \"Simplicity Matters\" by Rich Hickey - [VIDEO] (By the way, any talk by Rich Hickey is worth watching)"},{"id":22068475,"author":"tosh","month":"2020-01","story":"The Roots of Lisp (2001)","stance":"rec","why":"Good entry point into Hickey's ideas and Clojure","quote":"Rich Hickey’s talks were a great entry point for me","text":"another angle: while Clojure is a Lisp with its own distinct flavor it gives you … * a large community of practitioners and professionals (great for asking questions, finding collaborators, …) * easy access to libraries in the js/jvm/.net ecosystems * a style that relies more on data and transformations of data (illuminating simplicity) in any case: it is worth digging deeper, one thing that kept me away was not knowing where to start (analysis paralysis). in hindsight picking any Lisp would have been great (instead of postponing). Find a thread and start pulling :) Rich Hickey’s talks were a great entry point for me [LINK] e.g. [VIDEO] (the “Simplicity Matters” keynote at Rails Conf 2012) edit: Land of Lisp is a great book as well"},{"id":20773921,"author":"cutler","month":"2019-08","story":"Why Clojure?","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey challenges Ruby developers on complexity at Rails Conf","quote":"Rich Hickey's Sermons On The Mount changed the game of programming once and for all.","text":"Clojure is by far the best programming language I've ever used. Rich Hickey's Sermons On The Mount changed the game of programming once and for all. With Clojure you could finally have your Lisp cake and eat it. Witness the sheer chutzpah of the guy when he basically told Ruby devs they were doing it wrong at Rails Conf in 2012 ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":16840291,"author":"geksilla","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey's talk listed among favorite tech talks","quote":"","text":"Rich Hickey - \"simple made easy\" [VIDEO] Rob Pike - \"concurrency is not parallelism\" [LINK] Uncle Bob Martin - \"future of programming\" [LINK] Martin Kleppmann - \"transactions: myths, surprises and opportunities\" [LINK] Simon Brown - \"software architecture vs code\" [LINK]"},{"id":15866907,"author":"hbt","month":"2017-12","story":"Simplistic programming is underrated","stance":"rec","why":"Explores what simplicity means in programming","quote":"If you have any interest in the topic, I recommend Rich Hickey talk","text":"The blog post barely gets into the idea of what simplicity means in programming. If you have any interest in the topic, I recommend Rich Hickey talk \"Simplicity matters\" [VIDEO] My personal favorite is: Living with complexity 2010 by Don Norman (Author of Design of everyday things) [LINK]"},{"id":15084205,"author":"cstrahan","month":"2017-08","story":"D as a Better C","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk distinguishing simple from easy","quote":"Rich Hickey (creator of Clojure) gave a great talk on easy vs simple","text":"I'll let the parent answer you (though I have similar sentiments as someone with language implementation experience, and FWIW, I have professional experience with Go, Ruby, JavaScript, Clojure, C#, and I've used Haskell, F#, and much more in my free time). I will, however, point out that you're likely using a different definition of simple (\"easily understood or done; presenting no difficulty. \") than is often used in technical circles (\"composed of a single element; not compound\"). Simplicity and ease are distinct, and one does not necessarily imply the other; it's not hard to find convoluted designs that feel easy due to familiarity, while a much smaller, consistent design will feel difficult because the concepts (while fewer in number) are foreign. Rich Hickey (creator of Clojure) gave a great talk on easy vs simple: [VIDEO] Here's something familiar to most professional web developers: JavaScript. But it's also complicated: [LINK] Here's the spec: [LINK] And here's something unfamiliar and often considered difficult: lambda calculus. And yet it's very simple. Here's the entire definition of lambda expressions (lifted from Wikipedia): --- Lambda expressions are composed of: * variables v1, v2, ..., vn, ... * the abstraction symbols lambda 'λ' and dot '.' * parentheses ( ) The set of lambda expressions, Λ, can be defined inductively: 1. If x is a variable, then x ∈ Λ 2. If x is a variable and M ∈ Λ, then (λx.M) ∈ Λ 3. If M, N ∈ Λ, then (M N) ∈ Λ Instances of rule 2 are know"},{"id":13790940,"author":"NegatioN","month":"2017-03","story":"How we secretly introduced Haskell and got away with it","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey talk on simplicity in software","quote":"I liked Rich Hickeys talk","text":"Prodcedural code can also be well compartmentalized and tested. I was also until recently quite convinced OOP was the only way to go, but I'm seeing signs everywhere that a lot of the design-problems I've met over the past few years are at least magnified by OOP. The abstraction promised by OOP is a good thing, however very few people are able to consistently make good, reusable and maintained abstractions. To the point where it becomes a weakness rather than a strength. I don't want a billion wrapper-objects that obfuscate my code, and makes the surface area of it bigger than it has to be. Often I struggle understanding code more because of how it was separated, than because of the complexity of what it actually does. I liked Rich Hickeys talk \"Simple made Easy\" [1] and \"Brian Will: Why OOP is Bad\" [2] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":8857448,"author":"sivers","month":"2015-01","story":"Is it really 'Complex'? Or did we just make it 'Complicated'? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Rails-conference version of Hickey's simple versus easy talk","quote":"maybe watch this version instead, since it's almost the same talk but for a Rails Conference, with a few references","text":"Very interesting related talk about complecting things - \"Simple Made Easy\" - by Rich Hickey, inventor of Clojure: [LINK] If you're a Ruby person, maybe watch this version instead, since it's almost the same talk but for a Rails Conference, with a few references: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41047922,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2024-07","story":"Types as Interfaces","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey's argument for flexible maps over fixed structs","quote":"Basically when you use a hash / JavaScript-style object rather than a fixed struct definition for your data","text":">> This is a lot like schemaless databases. The flexibility of not having to fully specify the full schema does not mean that you don't benefit from specifying parts of it? Indeed, if you are indexing things, you have to specify those parts. But it is hard to argue with a straight face that schemaless tools don't have some strong benefits. This is very similar to what Rich Hickey argued in \"Simplicity Matters\": [VIDEO] Basically when you use a hash / JavaScript-style object rather than a fixed struct definition for your data (which amounts to a schema- less database in aggregate) Easy addition of new properties Co-existence of different versions of objects (newer objects will typically have more properties) Introspection of properties and validation rules / reflection"},{"id":23930862,"author":"slifin","month":"2020-07","story":"Nubank acquires Cognitect","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why some products are hard in cookie-cutter frameworks","quote":"This talk kind of eludes to why that is","text":"If what you're comfortable with is Spring or Drupal or Rails and those work for your use case then more power to you At the same time it's true there are products like roam research that are fundamentally harder to achieve in a cookie cutter environment This talk kind of eludes to why that is [VIDEO] but it's worth learning there's many mature pieces of tech in this community that are astounding If you need boring.lib then reach into Java/JavaScript with the easiest interop I've ever used"},{"id":21448691,"author":"taffer","month":"2019-11","story":"PostgREST","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I want my code simple[2], stupid and obvious","text":"> much as you call property setters or getters in OOP languages Good design is obvious and orthogonal[1]. If you write setters in an OOP language in such a way that they do surprising things, i.e. not just setting a value, then I would call that bad design. > which is good for loose coupling, modularity, etc. What do you gain by using triggers in this case? All you get is mental overhead, because whenever you use DML you have to keep in mind that there might be a trigger hiding somewhere that does strange things. If you call a procedure instead, you make it clear that you want to do more than just a simple update or insert. > [...] it is possible for the method call to make use of concurrency and parallelism constructs [...] to do a unknown number of things in an unknown order Why would I want this? I want my code simple[2], stupid and obvious, and not convoluted, clever and surprising[3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":20007866,"author":"wa1987","month":"2019-05","story":"Murray Gell-Mann Has Died","stance":"reference","why":"Famous talk possibly inspired by complexity science ideas","quote":"Definitely seems like a source of inspiration for Rich Hickey's famous talk","text":"Never seen the term 'plectics' before. Definitely seems like a source of inspiration for Rich Hickey's famous talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17834052,"author":"atakiel","month":"2018-08","story":"The rise and rise of JSON (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey's argument for why simple data structures beat complex ones","quote":"json is just more simple than xml, as it clearly states which parts are maps and which are sequences","text":"Rich Hickey has a good point in his Rails Conf 2012 \"Simplicity Matters\" talk[0] - json is just more simple than xml, as it clearly states which parts are maps and which are sequences. Xml has an inherent, implicit order in everything, due to its history as a document markup language. If you think xml through the lens of jsx pragma style transformation, xml element and it's attributes are more or less a (typed) map. Similarly element's children is an (ordered) sequence. Where as in json you can have maps inside maps, in xml you always have to wrap maps inside an ordered sequence, as maps can only be passed to element as its children. You can totally use xml without paying attention to the order, but the order is still there, complicating things. [0] Rails Conf 2012 Keynote: Simplicity Matters by Rich Hickey - [VIDEO]"},{"id":17493711,"author":"icc97","month":"2018-07","story":"Simple, correct, fast: in that order","stance":"reference","why":"Distinguishes simple versus easy in software","quote":"Simple-Complex vs Easy-Hard","text":"> Folks are using \"simple\" and \"easy\" interchangeably here. That's probably inappropriate. Agreed, see Rich Hickey's \"Simplicity Matters\" presentation on the difference [0]. Simple-Complex vs Easy-Hard [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15297826,"author":"erokar","month":"2017-09","story":"How I stopped loving Angular","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey's simplicity framing applied to Angular","quote":"Angular is sometimes easy, seldom simple and mostly complected [1].","text":"A thorough review by an experienced Angular developer. After having used Angular 2/4 in a greenfield project for the last 6 months, I agree with most of what he says. TypeScript is great, but Angular is sometimes easy, seldom simple and mostly complected [1]. 1. Simplicity Matters by Rich Hickey: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14664664,"author":"codecurve","month":"2017-06","story":"Fantastic DSLs and where to find them","stance":"reference","why":"Simple made easy ethos behind Clojure","quote":"I think most Clojure programmers will argue that taking the time to understand that philosophy _is_ worth the investment.","text":"Sure, no arguments there. It does save time in JavaScript and a large part of that is because the language has been designed around mutability. Part of that trade-off is that JavaScript can't make the same guarantees about what happens when you pass an object into a function. It's harder to be confident that a given program is correct. Immutability is just a part of the \"simple made easy\"[1] ethos of Clojure and I think most Clojure programmers will argue that taking the time to understand that philosophy _is_ worth the investment. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12140871,"author":"ClashTheBunny","month":"2016-07","story":"Zenzizenzizenzic","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey explaining difference between simple and easy","quote":"Rich Hickey explaining the difference at rails conf","text":"This is the difference between easy and simple. Easy to use is often hard to learn. This is simple. Many of the best tools are this way. Violins are another great example. I like clojure for a similar reason: Rich Hickey explaining the difference at rails conf: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11675144,"author":"gavinpc","month":"2016-05","story":"After reading “Rails is yesterday’s software”, I need to reply","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey calls large gem collections hairballs","quote":"I've started calling all packages hairballs since watching that.","text":"The original post (not this OP) was clearly a response to Rich Hickey's talk \"Simplicity Matters,\" which was given at a Ruby conference.[0] Particularly the part about \"1,000 gems,\" which Hickey calls \"hairballs\" in the talk. I've started calling all packages hairballs since watching that. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10637665,"author":"pcote","month":"2015-11","story":"The Triumph of Stupidity (1933)","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey's ideas on complexity generalized to societies","quote":"I think his ideas could be generalized to societies as well.","text":"Complication kills, not complexity. Complex systems are made up of simple independent pieces. Those pieces work together naturally often leading to new behaviors. Because they are independent, they can also adapt easily to changes in the environment. Complication is different. Complicated systems have dependencies that are more tightly bound together. Because of this, they can't adapt as easily to environmental changes. Consequentially, this creates conflict between system and environment that can often be destructive or violent. Or at least that's my social hypothesis. I based my idea on the Rich Hickey \"Simple vs. Easy\" talk on software complexity. I think his ideas could be generalized to societies as well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9483065,"author":"wging","month":"2015-05","story":"Simplify: move code into database functions","stance":"reference","why":"A different Hickey talk than Simple Made Easy","quote":"He watched a different Hickey talk, 'Simplicity Matters'","text":"He didn't watch 'Simple Made Easy'. He watched a different Hickey talk, 'Simplicity Matters': [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["QM1iUe6IofM",2],["lnnsDi7Sxq0",1],["Rqfkuc5mawg",1],["ecIWPzGEbFc",1],["5ZjhNTM8XU8",1],["udlMSe5-zP8",1],["SrKj4hYic5A",1],["cPNkH-7PRTk",1],["lKXe3HUG2l4",1],["9Q6sLbz37gk",1],["7jiPeIFXb6U",1]]},"Mc_4Z1oiXhY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a sobering, still-relevant look at the geopolitical consequences of warming, including why official reports lag the data.","q":{"x":"This is a great dissertation on how the GeoPolitics will play this little game out","i":18171472,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18171472,"author":"artur_makly","month":"2018-10","story":"Major Climate Report Describes a Strong Risk of Crisis as Early as 2040","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dissertation on how climate geopolitics will play out.","quote":"This is a great dissertation on how the GeoPolitics will play this little game out","text":"Oh but this gets better! This is a great dissertation on how the GeoPolitics will play this little game out: [VIDEO] - by Gwynne Dyer"},{"id":31530020,"author":"carapace","month":"2022-05","story":"Poll: Is Large-Scale Climate Geoengineering Inevitable?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on climate geopolitics, including unilateral geoengineering by affected nations","quote":"there's a fascinating talk by one Dr. Gwynne Dyer","text":"I've mentioned it on HN before, so apologies if you've already seen it, but there's a fascinating talk by one Dr. Gwynne Dyer: \"Geopolitics in a Hotter World\" (Sept. 2010) video: [VIDEO] Transcription: [LINK] > I talked–actually, the head of the Bangladesh Institute of Strategic Studies about this (you didn’t even know that existed, did you?). Well, there is one, it’s quite serious–run by a General, bright guy. I said, “have you heard about geoengineering?” and he smiled–seraphically–and he said, “Mmm. Yes. Your question?” > And I asked the question, “Do you think that this is something the Bangladesh government might want to do a little bit, before, let’s say, the US government or the Chinese government?” > He said, “yes it has crossed our minds.”–and then he stopped talking."},{"id":28870093,"author":"carapace","month":"2021-10","story":"Global Climate Report – September 2021","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on climate geopolitics, including unilateral geoengineering by affected nations","quote":"There's a fascinating talk from about eleven years ago","text":"There's a fascinating talk from about eleven years ago: Dr. Gwynne Dyer – Geopolitics in a Hotter World – UBC Talk Transcribed (Sept. 2010) [LINK] video: [VIDEO] Among other things he talks about is the possibility of unilateral geoengineering by some of the nations that will be feeling the effects sooner and more, uh, compellingly than others: > I talked–actually, the head of the Bangladesh Institute of Strategic Studies about this (you didn’t even know that existed, did you?). Well, there is one, it’s quite serious–run by a General, bright guy. I said, “have you heard about geoengineering?” and he smiled–seraphically–and he said, “Mmm. Yes. Your question?” > And I asked the question, “Do you think that this is something the Bangladesh government might want to do a little bit, before, let’s say, the US government or the Chinese government?” > He said, “yes it has crossed our minds.”–and then he stopped talking."},{"id":26686291,"author":"Qwertious","month":"2021-04","story":"Lithium battery costs have fallen by 98% in three decades","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why IPCC reports lag behind current data.","quote":"I highly recommend watching the whole thing","text":"There's a video on YouTube, Gwynne Dyer -- Geopolitics in a Hotter World (2010) ( [VIDEO] ), which very briefly touches on it IIRC. I'm talking about the bit that starts at 8m30s on the video, but I highly recommend watching the whole thing (or at leas the 1-hour talk part - the Q&A is practically inaudible for me). Basically, the IPCC doesn't publish studies - they study compilations of studies, every 4-5 years. And when they start compiling, they have a cut-off date at the very start and only use studies that have been both completed and have passed peer review from before that date. Meanwhile, the studies themselves have the same problem - they obviously can't start the study with incomplete data and update the data as they go. The studies need to start with data that finished before they started the study. And on top of that, they're necessarily a reserved body (Icd say \"conservative\" but that's ambiguous in politics) - IPCC is run by a bunch of governments trying to figure out the bare minimum of emissions reduction they need to commit to, to not be negligent - they don't want to hear bad news unless it's certain. The end result is that the data in the report is 10+ years out of date."},{"id":21027651,"author":"carapace","month":"2019-09","story":"Researchers are racing to find out why methane levels are increasing so fast","stance":"rec","why":"Long, good talk: things worse than publicly said.","quote":"Long, good, hard to summarize but I'll try: Things are worse than publicly talked about","text":"Geopolitics in a Hotter World – UBC Talk Transcribed (Sept. 2010) [LINK] Video [VIDEO] Long, good, hard to summarize but I'll try: Things are worse than publicly talked about; there are a few options (geo-engineering). \"People always raid before they starve.\" - - - - We have the solutions already. Cut-n-paste from a comment yesterday, apologies if you've seen it already, I think it's worth it: For practical advice on what to do I recommend Toby Hemenway's videos in re: Permaculture [LINK] Especially \"How Permaculture Can Save Humanity and the Planet – But Not Civilization\" and the sequel \"Redesigning Civilization with Permaculture\". Permaculture is a school of applied ecology (the word itself is a portmanteau of PERMAnent agriCULTURE) that has adherents and practitioners world-wide. It's not the only form of regenerative agriculture either. See also [LINK] Oceanic 3D farms! And now they are building reefs?"},{"id":21028870,"author":"dougmwne","month":"2019-09","story":"Researchers are racing to find out why methane levels are increasing so fast","stance":"rec","why":"Ten-year-old video on geopolitics of climate change.","quote":"Someone else just posted this 10 year old video on the geopolitics of climate change.","text":"You lead with the idea that people in power have made a secret decision to do away with democracy and deploy totalitarianism. This is by definition a conspiracy. The rest of what you said, plus what you just said about departments of people already looking at this I find quite realistic. Someone else just posted this 10 year old video on the geopolitics of climate change. I wanted to make sure you saw it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20747271,"author":"carapace","month":"2019-08","story":"Scientists Have Been Underestimating the Pace of Climate Change","stance":"rec","why":"Climate impacts on food, borders and geoengineering.","quote":"Dr. Gwynne Dyer gave a talk, \"Geopolitics in a Hotter World","text":"Dr. Gwynne Dyer gave a talk, \"Geopolitics in a Hotter World\" – Sept. 2010 > So when the Hadley Centre put these numbers out they were incorporating (which the IPCC does not) the early impacts of positive feedbacks, as well as just how much carbon dioxide will we put into the air by 2060. First time that had been done, but it gives you a really frightening number…and then they did the map. Which is again, something that’s not normally done, and the consequence is we don’t have a lot of time. We’re in deep trouble. And the military know this; the military know this everywhere. > the rule of thumb is that you lose 10% of global food production for every 1 degree Celsius higher average global temperature. > So food becomes the critical issue and the countries most impacted are tropical and subtropical and this is where the geopolitics comes from. > “people always raid before they starve.” > There’s already a well established pattern: when you’re in deep trouble and you can’t feed your family, head north. > But the dirty secret is that you can only shut a border if you’re willing to kill people. > No government that cannot feed its population survives. > We’re going to go through the point of no return (and probably a good deal further) in terms of the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere. Almost certain. > And there is maybe a way to cheat: and that is geoengineering. > “we’ve crunched the numbers and we figure we could get one degree Celsius of cooling average worldwide ju"},{"id":17125565,"author":"artur_makly","month":"2018-05","story":"We’ve Passed the Carbon Tipping Point","stance":"rec","why":"Closing arguments on low-tech ways to reverse warming.","quote":"watch Gwynne Dyer‘s closing arguments","text":"surprisingly there are low-tech ways to reverse the heating. watch Gwynne Dyer‘s closing arguments: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36689474,"author":"carapace","month":"2023-07","story":"Dr. Gwynne Dyer – Geopolitics in a Hotter World – UBC Talk Transcribed (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Video of Dyer's UBC talk, matching the transcript being shared","quote":"You can download a .wmv file from the above link or watch it here","text":"This is a transcript of this talk: [LINK] > Webcast sponsored by the Irving K. Barber Learning Centre, Dyer has worked as a freelance journalist, columnist, broadcaster and lecturer on international affairs for more than 20 years, but he was originally trained as an historian. He received degrees from Canadian, American and British universities, finishing with a Ph.D. in Military and Middle Eastern History from the University of London. You can download a .wmv file from the above link or watch it here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25258879,"author":"carapace","month":"2020-11","story":"Avoiding GMOs isn’t just anti-science. It’s immoral. (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk suggesting nations may pursue geoengineering unilaterally.","quote":"there's a very interesting talk about that[4]","text":"> As global warming progresses, a lot of the seeds that we are used to being able to grow aren't going to grow anymore, and the land that we used to grow them on will be turning into a desert. We can find or develop new drought- and heat-tolerant varieties, like the tepary bean (Phaseolus acutifolius)[1]. More importantly, we can alter the climate directly though our patterns of agriculture and horticulture.[2][3] > Within 50 years there's going to be mass migrations and tremendous demand for the land that is still viable for growing crops. FWIW, there's a very interesting talk about that[4] and one of the take-aways is that we may well see e.g. Bangladesh unilaterally pursuing geoengineering solutions, not waiting for international consensus. > GMO definitely isn't the only solution, and it may not even be the best one but it is one field that we need to deeply investigate and try out if we want our species to survive with a similar standard of living to what we enjoy today. I'm not actually against genetic engineering, I just think we should wait a few hundred years, until we have a better idea of how life works -OR- some extra-terrestrial biospheres in which to experiment without risking Earth at all. In the meantime, I believe we already have all the biological diversity we need to tackle the coming challenges already, we just have to recognize and use it. In any event, the OP is BS propaganda, not science, eh? [1] \"Phaseolus acutifolius, or the Tepary bean, is a legume n"},{"id":20554926,"author":"kossTKR","month":"2019-07","story":"The human body is already close to thermal limits in many regions","stance":"reference","why":"Foreshadowed refugee crisis and border walls from climate extrapolation.","quote":"foreshadowing the refugee crisis, Syrian War and the border wall all from climate extrapolation","text":"Yes almost all policy making / war efforts are created from realpolitical or resource oriented goals. It's the old orders of power and the conglomeration of corporate entities that actually create the shooting script for various events including the creation of a border wall. The identity politics or the thin veneer of hollywoodesque narratives above most especially foreign policy is just exactly that, fantasy, while it's the military-industrial complex that in reality drafts the plans together with PR companies. Look for example at military historian Gwynne Dyers's talk here from 2010 both foreshadowing the refugee crisis, Syrian War and the border wall all from climate extrapolation without the identity-smokescreen. [VIDEO] The whole border issue is extremely muddy though as it's not classically right-wing to protect the working classes from wage dumping by not letting millions of people in but has become a left-wing issue because class consciousness and economic policy has mostly been replaced with superficial identity based issues because they are allowed by the status quo. This is because they don't interfere with the increase in inequality and fall in real-wages happening in plane sight while pop-leftwingers are busy screaming about identity based issues (not that those aren't important). From a resource perspective one could argue that the ever more transnationally oriented elite couldn't give two f's about protecting the sovereignty of yesterdays empires and nation st"}],"r":[["VVp8UGjECt4",1],["r9rGX91rq5I",1],["gkj_91IJVBk",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["rk6z1vP4Eo8",1],["Kas0tIxDvrg",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["NHWjlCaIrQo",1],["eNliOm9NtCM",1],["VO_1AdYRGW8",1],["vxopfjXkArM",1],["_IgqJr8jG8M",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["Z-9rLlFgcm0",1],["-8nqnOcoLqE",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1],["wGt4XwBbCvA",1],["9eyFDBPk4Yw",1]]},"rRbY3TMUcgQ":{"wy":"HN loves it as a funny, shorter sequel and even uses it to introduce Erlang in university classes.","q":{"x":"I use Erlang the Movie and its Sequel whenever I teach my classes at Oxford Uni.","i":18656186,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18656186,"author":"francescoc","month":"2018-12","story":"Twenty Years of Open Source Erlang: A Retrospective from the trenches","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Used in university classes to teach Erlang","quote":"I use Erlang the Movie and its Sequel whenever I teach my classes at Oxford Uni.","text":"I use Erlang the Movie and its Sequel [VIDEO] whenever I teach my classes at Oxford Uni. #studentlife :-)"},{"id":12629923,"author":"davidw","month":"2016-10","story":"The State of JavaScript – Survey results","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious Erlang parody worth sharing","quote":"Erlang the Movie II\", which I'd also highly recommend","text":"I don't care about downvotes, I thought that video was hilarious and enjoy sharing it. He's the same guy behind \"MongoDB is web scale\" and \"Erlang the Movie II\", which I'd also highly recommend: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5661893,"author":"lelf","month":"2013-05","story":"Erlang's Open Telecom Platform (OTP) Framework [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic Erlang parody sequel; just watch","quote":"Don't think wtf. Just watch.","text":"And if you haven't seen it, here is some classic. “Erlang: the Movie”: — [LINK] And sequel “Erlang: the Movie II”: — [VIDEO] Don't think wtf. Just watch."},{"id":19710361,"author":"ubercow","month":"2019-04","story":"Erlang: The Movie [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Wonderful remix of the Erlang movie","quote":"I came here just to post another wonderful remix","text":"It's funny you mention that, I came here just to post another wonderful remix, \"Erlang The Movie II: The Sequel\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":16841644,"author":"paulsutter","month":"2018-04","story":"What Erlang Taught Me About Distributed Systems","stance":"rec","why":"Shorter, funnier sequel to the original","quote":"The sequel is shorter and more fun","text":"The sequel is shorter and more fun, “all erlang needs is an image upgrade.. we need something fresh, something edgy” [VIDEO]"},{"id":6935946,"author":"qohen","month":"2013-12","story":"Erlang: The Movie","stance":"rec","why":"Sequel by gar1t, creator of MongoDB is Web Scale","quote":"check out \"Erlang The Movie II: The Sequel\", by Garrett Smith (aka gar1t)","text":"And, after you've watched the original, check out \"Erlang The Movie II: The Sequel\", by Garrett Smith (aka gar1t): [VIDEO] (You are likely familiar with one of gar1t's earlier videos, the classic \"Mongo DB is Web Scale\": [LINK] And he's got more on his YouTube channel: [LINK] )"},{"id":10365356,"author":"kelseydh","month":"2015-10","story":"Twitter Expected to Begin Layoffs and Stop Headquarters Expansion","stance":"reference","why":"Erlang movie linked in a scaling discussion","quote":"","text":"Not at all. Have you ever thought about caching Twitter? It's an archetypical example of a site that's extremely difficult to cache well, which is the source of so many of its scaling problems. Every person's new feed is uniquely generated based on their followers. That means you can only at best cache at the individual tweet level -- you still need to uniquely generate a news feed for each user. This means that for every user you need to individually grab information simultaneously from the same high velocity tweets, while still being able to present information to each user uniquely based on conditions regarding how twitter should display tweets for that specific user individually. That's incredibly demanding data wise because it makes horizontal distribution of load much more difficult --- a huge number of people are all making demands on the same set of data resources, and you can't easily cache that data because of how real time and quickly updating it is. In comparison WhatsApp on Erlang is basically just an example of extremely dramatic real time horizontal scaling. Hosting hundreds of millions of simultaneous chats is relatively trivial in Erlang, given each one is operating under its own separately scalable chat process. Since very few chats share data with one another, horizontal scaling is easy because you don't have the issue of the same resource getting called simultaneously by millions of unique users at the same time, who all need that resource represented back"},{"id":45887269,"author":"bmacho","month":"2025-11","story":"How I fell in love with Erlang","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"trying to sell Erlang (called Outlaw Techno Psyhchobitch in the video) with rock and hot chick","text":"There is also Erlang the Movie 2 trying to sell Erlang (called Outlaw Techno Psyhchobitch in the video) with rock and hot chick: [VIDEO] And they were right, rebranding was all Erlang needed 13 years ago"},{"id":41828868,"author":"hayley-patton","month":"2024-10","story":"Common Lisp implementation of the Forth 2012 Standard","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Three of the trendiest names we could think of. Open. Telecom. Platform.","text":"\"We changed the name to OTP.\" \"Three of the trendiest names we could think of. Open. Telecom. Platform.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":19710114,"author":"spiralganglion","month":"2019-04","story":"Erlang: The Movie [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This video is ripe for remixing.","text":"This video is ripe for remixing. Here's the (short) silly cut I made of it last summer: [LINK] Here's another (longer) remix: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10424022,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2015-10","story":"Getting started with OTP: creating process families","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Also known as Outlaw Techno Psychobitch in Erlang community","text":"Also known as Outlaw Techno Psychobitch in Erlang community, per [0], as popularized by the creator of the famous \"MongoDB is Web Scale\". Context [1]. [0] - [VIDEO] [1] - [LINK]"},{"id":10137257,"author":"yellowapple","month":"2015-08","story":"Phoenix 1.0","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"A lot of Elixir folks try to claim that the syntax doesn't matter. They're lying to themselves: of course the syntax matters. Not only is it much easier and more consistent for the human brain to parse (even more so than Ruby, in my opinion; unlike Ruby, everything that should take a block - like 'def' and 'defmodule' - do (pun intended) take a block), but it also goes above and beyond what Erlang offers with things like its pipe operator ('|>'), macro support, defaulting to binary strings (if you use double-quotes; single quotes are Erlang-style lists of characters, which - while they have their uses (aside from the obvious interoperability with Erlang code) - aren't really as efficient), and (in my observation) greater consistentcy between what you can do in the REPL ('iex') and what you can do in actual source files. They're both great languages, but I think Elixir is a bit more refined, learning from Erlang's advantages and disadvantages and improving on them. It's basically what you get if you watch \"Erlang The Movie II: The Sequel\" [0] and actually try to write a language for Ruby hipsters with excellent taste in noserings while humming along to Bananarama :) [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9678259,"author":"supdog","month":"2015-06","story":"Ask HN: What are the coolest new web technologies/frameworks to learn right now?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this is a constructive parody of Erlang OTP web tech with some NSFW language","text":"You may be looking for Outlaw Techno Psychobitch: [VIDEO] Edit: this is a constructive parody of Erlang OTP web tech with some NSFW language."},{"id":9574819,"author":"rpcope1","month":"2015-05","story":"Ada 2012: A New Language for Safe and Secure Software (2012)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it can be like Outlaw Techno Psychobitch aka Erlang","text":"Sounds like you needs someone like Rex to give Ada an image upgrade: [VIDEO] ; it can be like Outlaw Techno Psychobitch aka Erlang. Seriously though, I agree with you on the first part, we throw the title engineer around a lot more than we should on this side of the pond."},{"id":7278291,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2014-02","story":"Ask HN: Why isn't Erlang more popular?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It obviously stands for Outlaw Techno Psychobitch ;).","text":"> (which doesn't bother to define what O, T, and P stand for.) It obviously stands for Outlaw Techno Psychobitch ;). [VIDEO] (Erlang The Movie II)"}],"r":[["nLApFANtkHs",1],["xrIjfIjssLE",1],["Z2DU0qLfPIY",1],["vYA0f6R5KAI",1],["EfGD2qveGdQ",1],["vq2nnJ4g6N0",1],["bzkRVzciAZg",1]]},"X-iSQQgOd1A":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a beautiful example of what simple rules and compute shaders can produce, and many commenters were inspired by it.","q":{"x":"reminded how beautiful coding can be","i":41397942,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41397942,"author":"Risord","month":"2024-08","story":"The secret inside One Million Checkboxes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reminded commenter how beautiful coding can be.","quote":"reminded how beautiful coding can be","text":"At the time I was lost joy of coding too but I was able to found it again. One key point was to ignore learning new tech if it was not absolutely necessary and focus just creating new things. I think it all started from Sebastian Lague's video which reminded how beautiful coding can be. [ [VIDEO] ]"},{"id":36078330,"author":"Udo","month":"2023-05","story":"Ze Frank True Facts: The Smartest Slime","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explores path-finding via trails and gradient signaling.","quote":"Sebastian Lague made an excellent video","text":"Sebastian Lague made an excellent video \"Ant and Slime Simulations\" which explores how path-finding algorithms can use trails and gradient-based signaling to solve spatial problems: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26823938,"author":"ehsankia","month":"2021-04","story":"Time flies in Google Earth’s biggest update in years","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing ant and slime simulation, especially part two.","quote":"this recent video Coding Adventures video was pretty mind blowing","text":"Speaking of ant and slime simulation, this recent video Coding Adventures video was pretty mind blowing: [VIDEO] (Especially part 2 starting around 10m in)."},{"id":36005569,"author":"Nezteb","month":"2023-05","story":"Orb Farm","stance":"rec","why":"Ant behavior replicated in code, with source available.","quote":"You might like these projects","text":"I love ants and replicating their behavior in code! You might like these projects: \"Coding Adventure: Ant and Slime Simulations\" by Sebastian Lague: Video: [VIDEO] Code [LINK] \"C++ Ants Simulation 1, First approach\" by Pezzza's Work: Video: [LINK] Code: [LINK]"},{"id":27403973,"author":"Berg0X00","month":"2021-06","story":"Compute Shader 101 [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Sparked interest in compute shaders and shows what is possible.","quote":"can recommend it to those that want to see a bit of what is possible with compute shaders","text":"Thanks for sharing this! I have not watched the full video yet but definitely will. Recently got interested in compute shaders thanks to this video: [VIDEO] and can recommend it to those that want to see a bit of what is possible with compute shaders."},{"id":26634245,"author":"theossuary","month":"2021-03","story":"Show HN: Play with Shaders Inspired by Slime Mold","stance":"rec","why":"Replicates slime molds in Unity with high-res results.","quote":"replicates slime molds in Unity","text":"This reminds me of a coding adventure YT video that replicates slime molds in Unity, look at the end to see some high res videos of it - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["iphcyNWFD10",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["ObhK8lUfIlc",1],["JBgG_VSP7f8",1],["pkCLMl0e_0k",1],["tcTZvNLL0-w",1],["DkMwYxfSYNQ",1],["BwGsr3SzCZc",1],["sI1C9DyIi_8",1],["DxL2HoqLbyA",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1]]},"NH1Tta7purM":{"wy":"HN repeatedly points people to it as the best overview of low-latency trading engineering.","q":{"x":"Here are some talks that will give you a small overview into that world","i":23191261,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":44844054,"author":"benreesman","month":"2025-08","story":"HRT's Python fork: Leveraging PEP 690 for faster imports","stance":"rec","why":"The original talk on C++ trading systems","quote":"The OG talk on this is Carl Cook's","text":"Doesn't the fact that a modern FPGA-centric (probably ASICs in the mix too at this point) hybrid NIC/order-parser/state-machine thing is rumored to be able to hit glass-to-glass of ~20-40ns mean that the speed game is hotter than ever? Do you mean that because it involves a lot of hardware design now? The days of being able to offer around the inside in C++ on a regulated securities exchange are over, but there's still C++ driving the thing, that 20ns \"tick to trade\" or however it's being measured in some instance is still pretty basic response stuff, light speed is still a thing. There's a C++ program upstairs running the show, and it's trying to do it's job in under a mike for sure. The OG talk on this is Carl Cook's: [VIDEO] But there are more recent talks (Optiver is especially transparent about it but other people talk about it too): [LINK] , that's David Gross at CppCon last year, it can't have changed that much since last year."},{"id":37452536,"author":"vgatherps","month":"2023-09","story":"Unpacking Elixir: Real-Time and Latency","stance":"rec","why":"What it takes to achieve very reliable ultra-low latencies","quote":"Is a free great watch about the things you have to do to very reliably have such low latencies","text":"This is almost certainly incorrect, unless it’s using a very stretched definition of “hft” and using microseconds to mean 99th% <1ms instead of say <5us (reasonably competitive software range depending on your trade). They might be forwarding non latency sensitive client/internal orders through a system written in erlang, or using erlang for an orchestration layer, but they’re not competing with other hft traders in one. Source: I work in the field and have built <5us systems. [VIDEO] Is a free great watch about the things you have to do to very reliably have such low latencies"},{"id":32315441,"author":"benreesman","month":"2022-08","story":"Understanding Jane Street","stance":"rec","why":"Carl Cook's talk on low-latency trading","quote":"I'd also recommend any of Carl Cook's talks","text":"I find this stuff fascinating, and this article is way above average for online posts about proprietary/algorithmic/quantitative/low-latency trading (very leaky Venn diagram there). I have a few nitpicks but overall it's informative and it's an interesting format: viewing an industry through the lens of a particular firm, especially one as fascinating as Jane. Anything that develops literacy in modern finance amongst the lay public is a good thing in my book. If this stuff floats your boat I'd also recommend any of Carl Cook's talks, e.g. [VIDEO] . Optiver is AFAIK in a somewhat different business than Jane, but they're also players (or were last I had any inside baseball). Too many people got their worldview on this industry from \"Flash Boys\", and I say this as a Lewis fan, is criminally stupid at best and in bad faith at worst (if you want a well-researched, accessible alternative: [LINK] is about a zillion times better). It's a pretty short list of places I'd ever go through some grueling and semi-arbitrary gauntlet to work for, but Jane is on it for sure. I hope the author(s) do Medallion next."},{"id":25101656,"author":"jeffreygoesto","month":"2020-11","story":"Ask HN: Am I too late for the “Data Science” wave?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on bare-metal performance concerns","quote":"You might i.e. check out talks like [0].","text":"First of all, it leaves the team behind if you use features that are too advanced. Makes collaboration hard if you have wildly diverging skills (in both ways). Also, it did in our case take you away so far from the bare metal, that the code was elegant, but slow. It did not play well with our static allocators and allocated/deallocated way too much, especially temporaries. You might i.e. check out talks like [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25217499,"author":"vgatherps","month":"2020-11","story":"Java is better than C++ for high speed trading systems","stance":"rec","why":"Reasons for and limits of using C++ for low latency","quote":"Here’s a good talk on the reasons for and limits of using C++ for low latency systems","text":"I’ve seen this sentiment before and worked on both a “low latency Java” team and low latency C++ teams. I have some sympathy for the idea that the JVM is better since it means you won’t spend all your time chasing crash reports. The thing is, like another comment hinted at, is that this issue is generally more reflective of the environment you build in than the technology choice. Here’s a good talk on the reasons for and limits of using C++ for low latency systems: [VIDEO] If I had to rank in terms of reliability of trading infrastructure I’ve used/worked on, * Low Latency C / C++ infra at fully automated trading firm. Very much run by the programmers and quants and also fairly small. By far the fastest (near limits of what you could do) and also most reliable. * Low Latency Java execution infrastructure. Pretty reliable, not that fast, had some issues with GC battling and manual memory management to avoid GC, etc. There was a pretty clear latency floor (still quite low) even when “doing everything right” that serious native infrastructure beat. * C++ market making infra at a firm run by manual traders. It was by far the slowest and least reliable. Echos the experience of “spent hours debugging weird crashes”. The culture was very “A trader asked for this and needs it done yesterday. Also this refactoring business doesn’t sound like adding new features, drop it”. What I saw is that if you hire people who have a good idea of what they’re doing and keep a culture of technical e"},{"id":23191261,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: What programming skills are required by HFT or Trading firms","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of the low-latency trading world","quote":"Here are some talks that will give you a small overview into that world","text":"Basically Java, .NET and C++, with heavy focus on C++. Being able to write allocation free algorithms, even on GC languages, lock free data structures and good knowledge of all multi-core programming paradigms and distributed computing. Here are some talks that will give you a small overview into that world, CppCon 2017: Carl Cook “When a Microsecond Is an Eternity: High Performance Trading Systems in C++” [VIDEO] Core C++ 2019 :: Nimrod Sapir :: High Frequency Trading and Ultra Low Latency development techniques [LINK] Open source Java HFT code from Chronicle Software, [LINK] \"Writing and Testing High-Frequency Trading Engines\", from Cliff Click [LINK] However in these domains every ms counts, even how long cables are, so also expect Verilog, VHDL, and plenty of custom made algorithms running directly on hardware. \"A Low-Latency Library in FPGA Hardware for High-Frequency Trading\" [LINK] You can get an overview of the typical expectations here, [LINK]"},{"id":15732582,"author":"log_base_login","month":"2017-11","story":"Coders who trade: Wall Street designs its staff for the future","stance":"rec","why":"Optimizing HFT code with theoretical points useful beyond C++","quote":"he provides some interesting theoretical points along the way that might be advantageous to you","text":"Thanks for the praise, and for the insight per your experience. Sounds like a great gig! I looked you up, and see that you are likely working exclusively in Haskell, but thought you might derive some value from Carl Cook's presentation on optimizing HFT code at the most recent cpp convention. I recognize the languages are distinctly different, but he provides some interesting theoretical points along the way that might be advantageous to you. Here's a link: [VIDEO] Also, I can sympathize with your plight to run scalable sims. I've done some work in bioinformatics, and am building a small cluster at home so that I can learn to write code that'll scale to more massively parallel systems that are de rigueur in that domain. Cheers!"},{"id":31128286,"author":"kolbe","month":"2022-04","story":"Magic-trace – High-resolution traces of what a process is doing","stance":"reference","why":"Optiver's low-latency techniques, including avoiding main memory","quote":"There was a great presentation from 2017 about some of Optiver's low latency techniques","text":"Do you know this for a fact? I've done some work in the industry where I needed to make fast software, but never the like sub-microsecond tick-to-trade type fast, so I really don't know. There was a great presentation from 2017 about some of Optiver's low latency techniques[1]. I had assumed they released it because the had obviated all of them by switching to FPGAs, but I don't know. Either way, he suggested that if you ever needed to ping main memory for anything, you already lost. So, I wouldn't have thought DDIO plays into their thinking much. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22945487,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-04","story":"A possible new back end for Rust","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Enough theory, \"Scientific Computing: C++ Versus Fortran\" (1997) [LINK] \"Micro-Optimisation in C++: HFT and Beyond\" [LINK] \"The Speed Game: Automated Trading Systems in C++\" [LINK] \"When a Microsecond Is an Eternity: High Performance Trading Systems in C++\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":17283816,"author":"zeusk","month":"2018-06","story":"Intel 28-core fantasy vs. AMD 32-core reality","stance":"reference","why":"Optiver engineer describing their low-latency process","quote":"Here is a guy from optiver talking about their process at CppCon","text":"> I can imagine few cases where first-to-right-the-bell performance on a single core determines if you get a specific quote in HFT but that's that. That is actually the case from what I've heard, A lot of them buy consumer chips then disable all but one core and overclock it to the max. Here is a guy from optiver talking about their process at CppCon: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HdHlfiOAJyE",1],["efPrtcLdcdM",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["YQ2QtKcK2dA",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["-I_jE0l7sYQ",1],["xnqTKD8uD64",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["JfmTagWcqoE",1],["D7Sd8A6_fYU",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["XkDEzfpdcSg",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["NqKyHEJe9_w",1],["gV5obrYaogU",1],["uknEhXyZgsg",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1]]},"Q7jcBGLIpus":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a calm, evidence-based debunking whenever the Navy UFO videos come up.","q":{"x":"Mick West has some great videos on it","i":32790237,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":48067769,"author":"mrandish","month":"2026-05","story":"US Government releases first batch of UAP documents and videos","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thorough, evidence-based analysis of the Gimbal video.","quote":"extremely well-researched videos analyzing UFO claims","text":"For anyone else who has a UFO-crazy uncle, I've found Mick West's YouTube channel to be invaluable [LINK] . Mick is a retired video game programmer (Spider Man, Guitar Hero, Tony Hawk), who does extremely well-researched videos analyzing UFO claims. He's not flashy or trying to be entertaining, just thorough, evidence-based and scientifically rigorous. He'll even do controlled experiments, recreations and 3D models to validate what's going on. And he's unfailingly respectful no matter how unhinged the claim. His work explaining the \"Gimbal Video\" is a good example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32790237,"author":"rhinoceraptor","month":"2022-09","story":"Navy Says All UFO Videos Classified, Releasing Them Will Harm National Security","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains UFO videos with geometry, imaging, and gimbals.","quote":"Mick West has some great videos on it","text":"All of the hyped UFO videos released over the past few years are easily explainable given a basic understanding of geometry, imaging and gimbals. Mick West has some great videos on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27497306,"author":"treesprite82","month":"2021-06","story":"Sam Harris on UFO Disclosure","stance":"rec","why":"Mick West analyzes the three Pentagon videos.","quote":"I'd recommend checking out Mick West's videos on them","text":"> I found 3 pentagon confirmed videos today of Unidentified flying shapes going super fast. Whatever they are, this is pretty surreal If you're talking about the 3 videos I assume you are, I'd recommend checking out Mick West's videos on them: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23672118,"author":"acqq","month":"2020-06","story":"Astronomers detect regular rhythm of radio waves, with origins unknown","stance":"rec","why":"Mick West explains the UFO videos in a playlist.","quote":"Mick West's full playlist with explanations","text":"Not weird at all: [VIDEO] Mick West's full playlist with explanations: [LINK] An article mentioning Mick West: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":23008009,"author":"DSingularity","month":"2020-04","story":"Pentagon officially releases UFO videos [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the Navy UFO videos with domain knowledge.","quote":"I was also freaked out by these videos until I saw this","text":"Apparently, no real gymnastics needed — just some domain knowledge. I was also freaked out by these videos until I saw this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26556336,"author":"modzu","month":"2021-03","story":"New details on military UFO sightings","stance":"reference","why":"Suggests the objects assumption may be wrong.","quote":"what do you make of this?","text":"just because it hasn't been explained easily does not imply there's a problem with our beliefs about technology. for example, that they are objects at all is already an assumption that could be wrong. what do you make of this? [VIDEO]"},{"id":24122448,"author":"acqq","month":"2020-08","story":"I Hope the Search for Extraterrestrial Life Finds Nothing (2008) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Carefully debunked old material.","quote":"already carefully debunked by Mick West","text":"> these have low credibility Lower then low, to the point that whenever somebody mentions them, I believe there was just a \"wish to be true\" and then stop. When checked, it was just a \"rerelase\" of the old already published material (one can also think about to whose benefit that distraction was) which was already carefully debunked by Mick West: [VIDEO] Mick West's full playlist with explanations: [LINK] An article mentioning Mick West: [LINK] Also, one day after NYT published their article \"No Longer in Shadows, Pentagon’s U.F.O. Unit Will Make Some Findings Public\" they published a correction too: \"Correction: July 24, 2020 An earlier version of this article inaccurately rendered remarks attributed to Harry Reid , the retired Senate majority leader from Nevada. Mr. Reid said he believed that crashes of objects of unknown origin may have occurred and that retrieved materials should be studied; he did not say that crashes had occurred and that retrieved materials had been studied secretly for decades. An earlier version also misstated the frequency with which the director of national intelligence is supposed to report on unidentified aerial phenomena. It is 180 days after enactment of the intelligence authorization act, not every six months.\" Emphasis mine, now imagine how many articles in how many media were written before the above correction, and it will explain what most of the consumers got as the message (namely, the first, utterly fake version with fake \"crashes\")."},{"id":23059487,"author":"mouzogu","month":"2020-05","story":"The US Navy Admits UFO Videos Are Real","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the phenomenon, though commenter questions why pilots would not know.","quote":"One thing that is not really explained is why experienced pilots would not be aware","text":"I think you mean this one: [VIDEO] One thing that is not really explained is why experienced pilots would not be aware of the phenomena explained in this video. Seems to me they would experience this all the time. fwiw I don't think it's actually an extraterrestrial object."}],"r":[["y-uuk4Pr2i8",1],["meiU6TxysCg",1],["-ga41edXw3A",1],["GcJxRqTs5nk",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["PLyEO0jNt6M",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["hz_mMLhUinw",1],["qsEjV8DdSbs",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1]]},"doaQC-S8de8":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a deeply researched example of film analysis and one of the most educational videos on YouTube.","q":{"x":"check out the wonderful commentary by 'Every Frame a Painting'","i":23435884,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23435884,"author":"Daub","month":"2020-06","story":"Akira Kurosawa’s hand-painted storyboards","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Wonderful commentary on Kurosawa's composition of movement.","quote":"check out the wonderful commentary by 'Every Frame a Painting'","text":"If you havne't already done so, check out the wonderful commentary by 'Every Frame a Painting': [VIDEO] (Composing Movement) [LINK] (The Geometry of a Scene) I don't normally recommend YouTube channels for film commentary, but this channel is very good indeed."},{"id":20948104,"author":"_hardwaregeek","month":"2019-09","story":"Postmortem: Every Frame a Painting (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deeply researched film analysis with well-chosen filmmaker interviews.","quote":"Or Sidney Lumet's interview in the Kurosawa video^[2].","text":"What I love about Every Frame a Painting is the depth of its research. It's quite clear that the creators had an extensive knowledge of film. The videos always include wonderfully selected interviews with the filmmakers that emphasize the point far more than just a person talking. The Deakins interview in Shot | Reverse Shot taken from Cinematographer Style is a nice example^[1]. Or Sidney Lumet's interview in the Kurosawa video^[2]. Taking from film criticism solidifies the video's arguments, creates an amazing rhythm to the videos (Tony and Taylor are clearly great editors) and also prevents the \"annoying person endlessly lecturing to me about film\" feeling that I get with some other channels. I've thought about doing videos of my own on various topics (the usage of eyes and glances in Il Posto, Kieslowski's poetry, the reflexivity of In the Mood for Love, etc.) but the YouTube film criticism industry is pretty saturated. Perhaps a different format? [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37219116,"author":"keiferski","month":"2023-08","story":"Can’t Hear the Dialogue in Your Streaming Show? You’re Not Alone","stance":"rec","why":"Compares Kurosawa's movement with dialogue-driven Avengers films.","quote":"It reminds me of this excellent Every Frame a Painting video that compares","text":"Part of this, I think, is also the shift to dialogue-based scripts. It reminds me of this excellent Every Frame a Painting video that compares Kurosawa’s effective use of movement with the barely-moving, dialogue-driven Avengers films. A huge percentage of films/shows today are what Hitchcock called, “photographs of people talking” and aren’t actually cinematic. If they were more cinematic, hearing every line of dialogue wouldn’t be as important to understanding the story. Start at about 4:25: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30188508,"author":"SAI_Peregrinus","month":"2022-02","story":"Over-reliance on CGI in movies","stance":"rec","why":"Contrasts Kurosawa's movement with the static Avengers staging.","quote":"Every Frame a Painting has a good video on Kurosawa's use of movement in his films","text":"I think we're sort of aggressively agreeing with each other. It's not the CGI (or lack thereof) that makes the modern superhero films bad (except as a sort of demoscene-with-infinite-budget visual spectacle where they're great). It's the writing. To a lesser extent the cinematography is poor, which makes it hard to really feel what's going on. There's lots of unnecessary camera movement, frenetic jump cuts during fights, and all the colors are dull and muted. Every Frame a Painting has a good video on Kurosawa's use of movement in his films, and actually uses The Avengers as a contrasting example and how the cinematography & direction makes the scene in question feel meaningless.[1] So I agree that it's not the CGI that's the problem. It's the writing, the direction, the cinematography, the editing, the music[2] (though they improved since the linked video came out, directly in response to it), etc. The films are bad in many ways, but the CGI isn't really one of them. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":19692206,"author":"yarapavan","month":"2019-04","story":"Ask HN: What are the most educational,interesting or insightful vids on YouTube?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among the most educational YouTube videos.","quote":"Akira Kurosawa - Composing Movement,","text":"Selected Twitter responses: - Why Planes Don't Fly Faster, [LINK] - Akira Kurosawa - Composing Movement, [VIDEO] - Richard Hamming: \"Learning to Learn\", [LINK] - Rise and fall of Third Reich, [LINK] - The men who built America, [LINK] -History of the World, [LINK]"},{"id":44049249,"author":"Daub","month":"2025-05","story":"Veo 3 and Imagen 4, and a new tool for filmmaking called Flow","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses motion and formal attributes in Kurosawa's films.","quote":"Every Frame a Painting, where he talks about motion in Kurosawa","text":"As an artist and designer (with admittedly limited AI experience), where I feel AI to be lacking is in its poverty of support for formal descriptors. Content descriptors such as 'dog wearing a hat' are a mostly solved problem. Support for simple formal descriptors such as basic color terms and background/foreground are ok, but things like 'global contrast' (as opposed to foreground background contrast), 'negative shape', 'overlap', 'saturation contrast' etc etc... all these leave the AI models I have played with scratching their heads. I like how Veo supports camera moves, though I wonder if it clearly recognizes the difference between 'in-camera motion' and 'camera motion' and also things like 'global motion' (e.g. the motion of rain, snow etc). Obligatory link to Every Frame a Painting, where he talks about motion in Kurosawa: [VIDEO] The abiding issue is that artists (animators, filmmakers etc) have not done an effective job at formalising these attributes or even naming them consistently. Every Frame a Painting does a good job but even he has a tendency to hand wave these attributes."},{"id":18407897,"author":"rrauenza","month":"2018-11","story":"The Art of Film Grain [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Kurosawa uses weather and environment to make scenes feel alive.","quote":"I just watched the Kurosawa one the other night","text":"I just watched the Kurosawa one the other night: [VIDEO] When I just read Spielberg's quote, >The grain is always moving, it’s swimming, which means that even in a still life, let’s say a flower on a table, that flower is alive even if it’s not moving. it made me think immediately of what was said about Kurosawa, except that Kurosawa used weather/environment instead."},{"id":15634671,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2017-11","story":"“One Damn Slide After Another”: PowerPoint at Every Occasion for Speech (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Kurosawa's sense of movement as a model for composition.","quote":"Akira Kurasowa's sense of movement[1][2]","text":"I think the main issue with Prezi-style presentations is that it demands too much skill to use properly. A better interface with sensible defaults and much less emphasis on wow-factor would help a lot here. Normal powerpoint \"only\" requires a basic awareness of composition and narrative coherence, which already is too much to ask in practice. To make good Prezi presentation you need to have the skills of a cinematographer/editor. Specifically, the skill of continuity editing[0] so your audience can follow what is going on. What we want is the calm, easy to read compositions of, say, Yasujirō Ozu or Wes Anderson, and Akira Kurasowa's sense of movement[1][2]. Instead everyone uses it in a way that it feels more like the worst aspects of Bayhem[3]. I still believe Prezi-like presentation tools have the potential to be good, because they could be used to map relationships in your narrative to spatial relationships. But that would require a) that you are aware of those relationships yourself and b) that you posses the skills to translate that into spatial compositions that are coherent. This is obviously an unrealistic demand, so instead the tools should assist you with that. It needs is an interface that helps users structure their story, with sensible default conversions. Maybe something like Treesheets[4], where you can create a tree-based hierarchy in your topics, and then a conversion of that to a spatial hierarchy that is easy to follow, and not a distraction from the actual"}],"r":[["LA_DrBwkiJA",1],["5_KcQt0z-eE",1],["kYfNvmF0Bqw",1],["S89BHnaxULo",1],["UWEjxkkB8Xs",1],["n1QEj09Pe6k",1]]},"oTugjssqOT0":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a practical, memorable guide to prioritization, and some rewatch it every year.","q":{"x":"So much so that I rewatch it about once a year","i":25274310,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25274310,"author":"will_pseudonym","month":"2020-12","story":"Writing well","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Commenter rewatches it yearly for lasting value","quote":"So much so that I rewatch it about once a year","text":"Absolutely. In that same vein, I've gotten a lot out of so many similar videos which are ostensibly about one thing, but to effectively use what they're teaching, you have to start learning/mastering something else entirely. \"Indirect learning\" is one way to put it, and a term I picked up from Randy Pausch's Last Lecture[0]. I've gained so much from watching that video, as well as his Time Management video[1]. So much so that I rewatch it about once a year, because every time you rewatch something, you're a different person in certain ways, and you often appreciate things much more the more you change and grow as a person. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4270820,"author":"allardschip","month":"2012-07","story":"How to hack the beliefs that are holding you back","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Pausch's lecture on tackling the hardest task first","quote":"Worth watching every minute","text":"Reminds me of Annette Benning in American Beauty: \"I will sell this house today. I will sell this house today.\" Not starting on activities because you don't like them or you don't feel not comfortable will drag you down. You'll be thinking about it all day. I tell myself in the morning to the most unpleasant thing on my to do list first. Eat the hairy frog first! I learned from Randi Pausch. Worth watching every minute: [VIDEO] -edit spelling"},{"id":25787615,"author":"will_pseudonym","month":"2021-01","story":"Time Management by Randy Pausch [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Pausch's explanation of prioritizing important, non-urgent tasks","quote":"I haven't seen it put as well as Randy Pausch did in his talk.","text":"\"This is the most important slide in the entire talk. So, if you want to leave after this slide, I will not be offended, because it is all downhill from here.\" [VIDEO] (Timestamped link of the \"Time Management\" talk by Randy Pausch, which is what the OP PDF is from) That part in the talk is about the \"Eisenhower Matrix\", popularized by Steven Covey in \"The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People\", on the importance of prioritizing important, non-urgent tasks over urgent, unimportant tasks. You can read about it here as well, but I haven't seen it put as well as Randy Pausch did in his talk. [LINK] PS If you haven't seen his \"Last Lecture\", it's also fantastic. I watch it about once a year to re-center myself, and remember to focus on the truly important things in life. [LINK]"},{"id":583569,"author":"screwperman","month":"2009-04","story":"Ask HN: I'm in 10th grade and I hate school. Any suggestions?","stance":"rec","why":"Pausch's lecture helps with procrastination and stress","quote":"I'd recommend you watch the late Randy Pausch's other lecture","text":"Just to put my advice in perspective: I'm an Indian who will possibly be attending (a pretty good) public university this year. I'm the typical HN/reddit geek: started programming when I was 9, know around 9 programming languages, quite some bit of theory, etc. etc. In school in India, I was bored all the time, got mediocre grades, and had donkeys for teachers. Here's what I feel you should do: 1. Challenge yourself So, you've got to choose from these kinds of activities: A: Activities that you enjoy doing B: Activities that *might* impress (say) the college admissions staff I mostly did stuff exclusive to type A; bad idea . Of course, never do anything that comes only within B. Find what comes under the union of the two. You're in America: you don't hunt for opportunities, you are flooded with them. Someone on this thread recommended \"taking every AP exam available\". Sure, do that: it's really important. But that's not ambitious at all : AP exams cover only about 10% of what Indian entrance examinations do, and you get to use \"calculators\" and \"appendices\" (more like textbooks). Do something real , like aspire to be an IMO/IOI/IPhO/IChO/ISEF medalist (if you don't know what those are, shame on you). 2. Manage your time I was absolutely dismal at this. But I've started doing it for a month and it really does wonders: I have stopped procrastinating and started to feel less stressed out. I'd recommend you watch the late Randy Pausch's other lecture ( [VIDEO] ) or read Getting T"},{"id":420637,"author":"bluefish","month":"2009-01","story":"Do you set goals?","stance":"rec","why":"Randy Pausch's time management lecture with many good points","quote":"I've found this lecture by the late professor Randy Pausch regarding time management to be very interesting.","text":"Not goal related directly; I've found this lecture by the late professor Randy Pausch regarding time management to be very interesting. I'm not sure I agree with his hands-free phone + bike use tip, but I think his talk has a lot of good points. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29664348,"author":"rasengan0","month":"2021-12","story":"My productivity app for the past 12 years has been a single .txt file (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Inspired commenter's plain-text logging format","quote":"inspired by Randy Pausch","text":"Yay! Good to read validation after trying so many: wikis, org, TheBrain, Freemind, etc. It's like a return to the old .LOG in notepad trick. After a score, I'm came to the same conclusion as the article; plain text just works. I log in vim using ISO 8601 date stamped records in the log format of (priority - datetime stamp - keywords/tag - content) inspired by Randy Pausch [VIDEO] others noticed too: [LINK] Sorting by date, priority or keyword keeps me on track and helps for quarterly summaries. A simple bash loop runs annually to give me a fresh 365 dashboard of days but inserts are a datetime stamp command - keywords autocompleted - then actual typing the meat of the task/content Because all text is in a single file, vim autocomplete saves typing as I use CamelCase for keywords; vim dict helps too. Caveat: For enjoyment, I still use fountain pens and paper for a running top 3 priority and scribbling offline. Backup interop is via TiddlyWiki for reference archive and sync with a plain text Zettelkasten (heavy on vim gf, Rg, FZF and jq for TiddlyWiki json export) Plain text is built to last."},{"id":8250058,"author":"willhinsa","month":"2014-08","story":"A Financial Model Comparing Car Ownership with UberX in Los Angeles","stance":"reference","why":"Value of time spent traveling","quote":"You may not be losing wages, but you are losing time.","text":"You may not be losing wages, but you are losing time. I think that the 50% assumption in the model is wrong, too, but in the opposite direction. There are activities that many people can do while riding in a vehicle with no loss of quality, like reading. Randy Pausch - Time Management [VIDEO]"},{"id":1308214,"author":"imp","month":"2010-04","story":"Ask HN: Self improvement programs","stance":"reference","why":"One item in a list of self-improvement resources","quote":"Randy Pausch, Time Management","text":"I would be wary of self improvement programs that charge money. I'm not sure what exactly you've looked into, but there's a lot of information out there for free or cheap. I've spent a decent amount of time reading self-improvement books/blogs, and for me the stuff that made the most impact were things that were tailored to the problems I was having at the time. Here's an overview of what I've read over the years that have helped me to some extent: I Can Do Anything if I Only Knew What it Was: [LINK] Paul Graham's Essays: [LINK] How to Win Friends and Influence People: [LINK] Four Hour Work Week: [LINK] Randy Pausch, Time Management: [VIDEO] GTD: [LINK] Steve Pavlina: [LINK] 7 Habits of Highly Effective People (reading this now): [LINK] So I would say \"it depends\" on what you want out of life and where you are now. Self improvement is a life-long task and you will need advice that changes as you progress."}],"r":[["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["hfQdf93e63I",1]]},"vzfy4EKwG_Y":{"wy":"Recommended as the clearest explanation of why immutable data need not be slow, and as the introduction to Roc.","q":{"x":"As a starting point for those nitty grittys, this talk is probably as good as any","i":31920208,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":42947503,"author":"greener_grass","month":"2025-02","story":"Roc rewrites the compiler in Zig","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces Roc, a fast functional language without GC.","quote":"I first learned about Roc from this talk","text":"We already have many great GC'd functional languages at that performance level. I first learned about Roc from this talk: [VIDEO] That's a niche that is not currently filled (well, maybe MLTon) and that has me very excited! I'm sure I'm not alone here."},{"id":41722112,"author":"greener_grass","month":"2024-10","story":"Serialization Is the Secret","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces Roc and how functional constructs become in-place mutation.","quote":"Roc Lang might interest you","text":"1. Structural sharing - most data doesn't change so you create a copy that reuses much of the original structure 2. Garbage collection and lexical scopes - you clean up memory quickly and in batch 3. Compiler optimizations - you turn functional constructs into imperative ones that reuse and mutate memory at compile-time, where it is provably safe to do so Roc Lang might interest you: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38349589,"author":"yashrk","month":"2023-11","story":"Roc – A fast, friendly, functional language","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how Roc turns pure functional code into fast imperative machine code.","quote":"The unique selling point of Roc is clever optimization to convert purely functional source code to deep-imperative fast machine code","text":"If you are interested, «why yet another programming language?». The unique selling point of Roc is clever optimization to convert purely functional source code to deep-imperative fast machine code, while keeping all the correctness of functional algorithms. See this video of Richard Feldman for details — «Outperforming Imperative with Pure Functional Languages»: [VIDEO] Among those clever optimizations: - static reference counting (no GC, like in Rust, but with no borrowing and borrow problems); - stack allocation of the data with no external links; - hidden («opportunistic») mutability even in the cases, where Haskell or Lisp will copy the value."},{"id":31920208,"author":"Akronymus","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Functional vs. Imperative Implementation of Algorithms","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how functional compilers achieve efficiency without naive copying.","quote":"As a starting point for those nitty grittys, this talk is probably as good as any","text":"> Functional programming experts say that their code is efficient because the compiler translates it to an efficient representation on the CPU. They have TCO and other things. But they don't mutate data and hence every function call creates a new data in memory taking up space. How do they overcome that? By letting the compiler make assumptions. For example, consecutive map calls can be converted into a single one. If the data isn't shared with something else, the compiler can mutate it. MUCH easier to paralellize maps. As a starting point for those nitty grittys, this talk is probably as good as any [VIDEO]"},{"id":30372113,"author":"aquadrop","month":"2022-02","story":"Haskell2020 Is Dead, but All Hope Is Not Lost (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk introducing Roc, an Elm-inspired language.","quote":"I just saw nice talk by Richard Feldman [1] about it","text":"Maybe [LINK] ? It's not production ready, I just saw nice talk by Richard Feldman [1] about it and it's inspired by Elm. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29388772,"author":"memco","month":"2021-11","story":"4x Smaller, 50x Faster","stance":"rec","why":"Creator explains opportunistic in-place mutation in functional language Roc.","quote":"Roc-lang, which is a functional, systems language in development uses something called opportunistic in-place mutation to do just that.","text":"> How would one optimize for immutability in this case, other than turning it back into mutability behind the scenes? Roc-lang, which is a functional, systems language in development uses something called opportunistic in-place mutation to do just that. Here's a video where the creator talks about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29389928,"author":"niels_bom","month":"2021-11","story":"4x Smaller, 50x Faster","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why immutable data can be fast.","quote":"why immutable does not necessarily mean slow and given enough attention can mean higher performance in certain cases","text":"This talk by Richard Feldman about Roc (a new language) goed into why immutable does not necessarily mean slow and given enough attention can mean higher performance in certain cases: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30222132,"author":"yewenjie","month":"2022-02","story":"High-order Virtual Machine (HVM): Massively parallel, optimal functional runtime","stance":"reference","why":"Talk about the Roc language, listed among exciting new languages.","quote":"Up and coming Languages I am excited about","text":"So many new programming languages coming up with clever features that makes me wish I could mix and match their core features while using libraries from more popular languages. Up and coming Languages I am excited about - 1. Roc - [VIDEO] 2. Zig - [LINK] 3. Scopes - [LINK] 4. Gleam - [LINK] 5. Odin - [LINK] 6. Koka - [LINK] 7. Unison - [LINK]"}],"r":[["fWDxdoRTZPc",1]]},"yQ16_YxLbB8":{"wy":"HN readers recommend it as an unfiltered view of Jobs' strategic thinking and decisiveness, including the famous answer on why OpenDoc was killed.","q":{"x":"For those that haven't seen it the whole thing is worth it","i":39061622,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39061622,"author":"fidotron","month":"2024-01","story":"Mourning Google","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Unfiltered Q&A showing Jobs' strategic understanding in 1997","quote":"For those that haven't seen it the whole thing is worth it","text":"For those that haven't seen it the whole thing is worth it: [VIDEO] This Q&A is the evidence for why I think many tech people underestimated him, and as you observe such unfiltered Q&As do not occur with other tech CEOs, though he was not CEO at the time. He's not 100% right about how things would go, but he was a lot more right than anyone else proved to be. Some of his answers to the really tough questions are not stellar, but Apple was in a truly terrible state at the time, and there were not nice answers to give. The fact that he stood there ready to take the hits spoke volumes though."},{"id":23027169,"author":"sjwright","month":"2020-04","story":"Tesla Q1 2020 Update","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jobs on decisiveness and fixing mistakes during Apple's turnaround","quote":"That YouTube video is fantastic IMHO.","text":"I personally feel there's a big overlap between Jobs and Musk—but none more so than the fact that they had the key combination of sufficient intelligence, clear vision, high self-confidence and (most importantly) strong influence over corporate direction. One of my favourite Steve Jobs quotes is just after he returned to Apple and began dismantling the massive pile of legacy cruft. When asked about a particular (terrible) technology that Apple scrapped, Steve went on a particularly insightful rant that holds numerous insights into what he believes to be key to a successful company. And history proved those insights to be exactly right. The quote— \"Some mistakes will be made along the way. That's good. Because at least some DECISIONS are being made along the way, and we'll find the mistakes and we'll fix them.\" [VIDEO] In other words, as long as you're right often enough, being decisive is more important than being right every single time. Musk strikes me as sharing that mentality. He's right often enough that it's more efficient to \"fix\" the mistakes than to suffocate big decisions with big committees. And it's not like any big committee is sure to be right more often than Jobs/Elon anyway. (That YouTube video is fantastic IMHO. Watched with the benefit of hindsight it shows that Apple's success wasn't an accident. Steve really did understand the formula that ultimately led to it.)"},{"id":21677175,"author":"mistersquid","month":"2019-12","story":"Inside Macintosh (1985) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jobs' thoughtful Q&A on strategy, NIH syndrome and networking after returning to Apple","quote":"what he says in response to the questions following the OpenDoc question is deeply thought-provoking","text":"Thanks for linking to the 1997 WDC (only 1 \"w\"!) Q&A with Jobs. For those hoping to cut to the chase, the timecode for Jobs' prompt and the hapless attendee who asks \"What about OpenDoc\" is 4:26 [0] The four and a half minutes prior are interesting (thoughtful introduction, Jobs' framing of the session and his brief thoughts about Apple's current position), but what he says in response to the questions following the OpenDoc question is deeply thought-provoking. Many of the ideas Jobs shares show the general shape of recent and current Apple business decisions macOS and iCloud, for example. Especially interesting are his discussions of NIH syndrome, networking, and more. Thanks, twoodfin, for pointing to this session which punctuates Jobs' return to Apple the year previous. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17105512,"author":"pfranz","month":"2018-05","story":"Jim Black's Steve Jobs Story","stance":"rec","why":"1997 developer Q&A showing Jobs discussing technology strategy","quote":"a one hour q&a with developers he did in 1997 soon after returning to Apple","text":"I've heard both of those stories from multiple places. I don't know exactly how you'd define \"technical,\" he clearly wasn't a strong engineer, but I have heard multiple examples of him managing technical things. I remember around the same time he was known for memorizing chips and vendors very very well that he could source them very cheaply. I think a good firsthand example is a one hour q&a with developers he did in 1997 soon after returning to Apple: [VIDEO] He doesn't necessarily show off deep technical knowledge, but he is able to discuss strategy about technology directly with developers. Managing technology is his competitive advantage."},{"id":43654180,"author":"danso","month":"2025-04","story":"How a $2k 'Made in the USA' Phone Is Manufactured","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs fielding technical questions from developers","quote":"Jobs could do a reasonable job of taking questions from a technical audience","text":"> * Steve Jobs was one of these people. A clever marketing guy who relied on others for technical heavy lifting. I suggest going back and re-watching some of his presentations, like the unveiling of the iPhone. Every word he said was meticulously planned and very rehearsed.* Before Apple entered its iPod era, Jobs could do a reasonable job of taking questions from a technical audience [VIDEO]"},{"id":22780522,"author":"npunt","month":"2020-04","story":"Why Amazon got out of the Apple App Store tax and other developers won’t","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs explaining why OpenDoc was killed and his view of Newton","quote":"Here's SJ's rather famous response at WWDC 97 to why OpenDoc was killed","text":"If we're going to mine ancient history, lets put up a disclaimer that any company that exists for a 40+ years and is trying to invent new things is going to change their mind about stuff. If they didn't, they probably wouldn't be very good at inventing things. There's context to consider with Apple in the 90s, which was weeks from bankruptcy. Here's SJ's rather famous response at WWDC 97 to why OpenDoc was killed, and a little later on he describes his opinion of Newton [1]. Hard to disagree with the sentiments. Killing OpenDoc and Newton was a way to keep the company alive and prepare for the next big thing. 4 years later, out pops the iPod. 10 years later, the iPhone. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21675635,"author":"twoodfin","month":"2019-12","story":"Inside Macintosh (1985) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs explains killing OpenDoc at WWDC 97","quote":"Somewhat famously, the first question Steve Jobs took at his developer Q&A at WWDC ‘97 was about OpenDoc","text":"Somewhat famously, the first question Steve Jobs took at his developer Q&A at WWDC ‘97 was about OpenDoc & the bullet he put in its head. [VIDEO] Honestly, the “component software model” had at least hundreds of millions of dollars in development thrown at it by all the major players and went essentially nowhere at the application level. It had some nice architectural properties for developers, and COM at least has had a long and prosperous history as a building block. But it’s hard to imagine any possible world where business or consumers spent a significant fraction of their software dollars on components rather than full applications. So Steve was probably right."}],"r":[["6AeWu1fZ7bY",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1],["P3AyI_u66Bw",1],["d_zt3kGW1NM",1],["d3kfEeMZ65c",1]]},"Jib2AmRb_rk":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an enthusiastic, funny talk straight from the creator, and cites it for facts like the name's pronunciation and Airbus support.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend to watch it, if only to get a sense of the smart and funny energy that Dr Richard Hipp puts off!","i":28076075,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28076075,"author":"srcreigh","month":"2021-08","story":"In Praise of PostgreSQL","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny, energetic Hipp talk; SQLite competes with fopen.","quote":"I highly recommend to watch it, if only to get a sense of the smart and funny energy that Dr Richard Hipp puts off!","text":"This point is echoed by the creator of SQLite, Dr Richard Hipp, in his excellent talk \"SQLite: The Database at the Edge of the Network\". [1] \"SQLite doesn't compete with client-server databases, it competes with fopen\" (I highly recommend to watch it, if only to get a sense of the smart and funny energy that Dr Richard Hipp puts off!) [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15649228,"author":"8ig8","month":"2017-11","story":"Work on SQLite4 has concluded","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Enthusiastic, passionate talk by SQLite's creator.","quote":"I really enjoyed it. Probably because he seems so enthusiastic and passionate.","text":"I stumbled on this video presentation by Dr Hipp recently (may have been from another HN comment). I really enjoyed it. Probably because he seems so enthusiastic and passionate. SQLite: The Database at the Edge of the Network with Dr. Richard Hipp [VIDEO]"},{"id":16588513,"author":"kzrdude","month":"2018-03","story":"Why Is SQLite Coded in C? (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk touching on industry projects like airlines funding SQLite.","quote":"This talk is really interesting too","text":"This talk is really interesting too [VIDEO] big industry projects (like the airline mentioned) probably contribute"},{"id":13569391,"author":"__derek__","month":"2017-02","story":"Ask HN: Is sqlite good for long-term persistence?","stance":"rec","why":"Hipp discusses SQLite's long-term support plans, tied to aircraft makers.","quote":"Hmmmm, there's a better talk by D. Richard Hipp around","text":"> Hmmmm, there's a better talk by D. Richard Hipp around, but I'm not finding it atm. In the better talk he explains they (SQLite) are planning to be around for the 30 year timeframe you mention. Something to do with an airplane manufacturer looking for long term guarantees, but I don't remember the details. Here's the video[1] linked to from a tweet[2] linked to above.[3] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":42551614,"author":"chasil","month":"2024-12","story":"Fun facts about SQLite","stance":"reference","why":"Possible discussion of Microsoft-requested changes to SQLite.","quote":"I am fairly certain that Dr. Hipp has discussed changes in SQLite that were desired by Microsoft","text":"I am fairly certain that Dr. Hipp has discussed changes in SQLite that were desired by Microsoft for integration into Windows, which came to pass via a foundation membership. I believe that this was mentioned in the video below (I am not able to verify for now): [VIDEO] It would be interesting to see what is required for an organization to negotiate non-trivial changes in SQLite."},{"id":41506785,"author":"zamadatix","month":"2024-09","story":"SQLite is not a toy database","stance":"reference","why":"Creator's original thinking on what the name means.","quote":"that's how the creator originally thought about it","text":"It \"is\" (that's how the creator originally thought about it) but it also isn't really prescribed as the only interpretation [VIDEO] Though I'll also note the \"lite\" crowd often interpret the \"L\" as double duty/shared (like the \"m\" in \"dramedy\" isn't necessarily interpreted as solely part of \"drama\" or \"comedy\")."},{"id":32581855,"author":"srcreigh","month":"2022-08","story":"How SQLite scales read concurrency","stance":"reference","why":"Creator says the name like S.Q.L.-ite.","quote":"The creator says it like Escue Ell-ite emphasis on the Es- and -ite.","text":"The name SQLite isn't Sequel-lite. It's S.Q.L.-ite akin to graphite or Levite. It means a thing of SQLy stuff. The creator says it like Escue Ell-ite emphasis on the Es- and -ite. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31216344,"author":"srcreigh","month":"2022-04","story":"How to Corrupt an SQLite Database File","stance":"reference","why":"Hipp explains how SQLite is pronounced.","quote":"the name SQLite is not SQL-lite but SQL-ite (think graphite).","text":"Either way, the name SQLite is not SQL-lite but SQL-ite (think graphite). SQLite is an element of SQLey stuff. Source: [VIDEO] I'd link the exact timestamp, but then you might watch less than the entire talk, which would be unfortunate."},{"id":24535407,"author":"snthpy","month":"2020-09","story":"DuckDB – An embeddable SQL database like SQLite, but supports Postgres features","stance":"reference","why":"Hipp comments on SQLite's stable file format being a limiting factor.","quote":"an unchanging file format, or at least a backwards compatible one, is a key feature of sqlite","text":"DuckDB looks very interesting and I'm quite excited to examine it more closely in the next few days! I just wanted to add to the discussion that an unchanging file format, or at least a backwards compatible one, is a key feature of sqlite. See for example Richard Hipp's comments here [1] (I think he also mentioned earlier in the talk that the file format has become a limiting factor now in terms of some of the refactoring that they can do). The file format therefore seems likely to be a major factor in the long term success of this project and I am glad to see that you are taking your time before settling on any architecture here. Given that you are targeting the data science and analytics space, what are your plans for integration with arrow and the feather file format? From a purely user/developer perspective, arrow's aim of shared memory data structures across different analytics tools, seems like a great goal. I know Wes McKinney and Ursa Labs have also spent quite some time at the file storage part of this, see for example the Feather V2 announcement [2]. What are your thoughts on the tradeoffs they considered and how do you see the requirements of DuckDB in relation to theirs? From the Carnegie Mellon DuckDB talk [3], I saw that you already have a zero-copy reader to the pandas memory data structures, so the vision I have is that DuckDB could be the universal SQL interface to arrow datasets which can then also be shared with more complex ML models. Is that something tha"},{"id":23511151,"author":"nathan_f77","month":"2020-06","story":"SQLite as an Application File Format (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've been reading through many of the other pages on the SQLite website. I didn't realize that SQLite is developed by a company (Hwaci) and funded by selling support, licenses, and testing services. I had always assumed it was an open source project backed by a non-profit, similar to the Apache Software Foundation. This is another very interesting example of an open-source business. I would be interested to learn more about how the Hwaci company operates (revenue, number of employees, etc.). I find this very interesting: > We are a 100% engineering company. There is no sales staff. Our goal is to provide outstanding service and honest advice without spin or sales-talk. They list some \"$8K-50K/year\" and \"$85k\" price tags directly on their \"Pro Support\" webpage. These would usually be behind a \"Schedule a Call\" or \"Get Quote\" button. I've been thinking about doing something similar with my own on-premise licenses and support contracts. I'm not very good at sales and I don't really want to hire a sales team, so I'd be interested to know how this worked out for them. I also liked this sentence, which is very similar to Basecamp's philosophy (and both companies were started around the same time - 1999 vs 2000): > Hwaci intends to continue operating in its current form, and at roughly its current size until at least the year 2050. It's interesting to think that SQLite could have raised money and grown into a billion-dollar public company with thousands of employees. I'm going to li"},{"id":18039213,"author":"faitswulff","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: What open source project, in your opinion, has the highest code quality?","stance":"reference","why":"Hipp explains how Android adoption drove DO-178B-level testing.","quote":"Dr. Hipp said he started really following it when Android came out and included SQLite","text":"They actually have to test to that degree to follow aviation standards (DO-178b [0]) because they're used in aviation equipment. Dr. Hipp said he started really following it when Android came out and included SQLite and suddenly there were 200M mobile SQLite users finding edge cases: [VIDEO] Lightly edited transcript here: > It made a huge difference. That that was when Android was just kicking off. In fact Android might not have been publicly announced, but we had been called in to help with getting Android going with SQLite. [Actually], they had been publicly announced and there were a bunch of Android phones out and we were getting flooded with problems coming in from Android. > I mean it worked great in the lab it worked great in all the testing and then [...] you give it to 200 million people and let them start clicking on their phone all day and suddenly bugs come up. And this is a big problem for us. > So I started doing following this DO-178b process and it took a good solid year to get us there. Good solid year of 12 hour days, six days a week, I mean we really really pushed but we got it there. And you know, once we got SQLite to the point where it was at that DO-178b level, standard, we still get bugs but you know they're very manageable. They're infrequent and they don't affect nearly as many people. > So it's been a huge huge thing. If you're writing an application deal ones, you know a website, a DO-178b/a is way overkill, okay? It's just because it's very expen"},{"id":15612004,"author":"grzm","month":"2017-11","story":"A Minimalist Guide to SQLite","stance":"reference","why":"Hipp mentions Airbus wanting SQLite support for the airframe's lifetime.","quote":"Here's Dr. Richard Hipp mentioning that Airbus requested sqlite support for the lifetime of the airframe","text":"Here's Dr. Richard Hipp mentioning that Airbus requested sqlite support for the lifetime of the airframe: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37165964,"author":"mpereira","month":"2023-08","story":"SQLite: the secret behind Apple's success? (humor)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I saw the original in Dr. Richard Hipp's \"SQLite: The Database at the Edge of the Network\" and thought it was pretty funny.","text":"Sorry. It's just a joke. I saw the original in Dr. Richard Hipp's \"SQLite: The Database at the Edge of the Network\" and thought it was pretty funny. [VIDEO] I added a \"(humor)\" tag at the end."}],"r":[["f6kdp27TYZs",1],["T-TwcmT6Rcw",1],["RjfbvDXpFls",1],["2AXv49dDQJw",1],["OSGv2VnC0go",1],["B1J2RMorJXM",1]]},"2NBG-sKFaB0":{"wy":"HN points to it as the standard example of procedural content generation and extreme size optimization in games.","q":{"x":"I suggest that you look at the result in awe","i":25081412,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25081412,"author":"fxtentacle","month":"2020-11","story":"Metaprogramming for Madmen (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A whole FPS in 96k.","quote":"I suggest that you look at the result in awe","text":"Before you read the story, I suggest that you look at the result in awe: [VIDEO] That whole game is 96k. Meaning it's roughly 0.07% of the size of an empty Electron app."},{"id":11852828,"author":"sclangdon","month":"2016-06","story":"Source code for 4kb demoscene production “Elevated” released","stance":"rec","why":"Farbrausch's 96kb FPS worth checking out.","quote":"are particularly worth checking out for the uninitiated.","text":"Although they generally didn't compete in the 4kb genre, Farbrausch open-sourced many of their demos and demo-making tools a few years ago [1]. fr-041: Debris [2], and kkrieger [3] (the 96kb FPS) are particularly worth checking out for the uninitiated. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7740083,"author":"makmanalp","month":"2014-05","story":"Metaprogramming for madmen","stance":"rec","why":"Windows-only 96kb 3D FPS from Farbrausch.","quote":"kkrieger (windows only 96kb 3D FPS, video here","text":"This is an amazing insight into how demos are written. Farbrausch stuff has always been inspirational to me. It's very interesting is the sort of extreme version of the idea that data model and code are inherently tied: Especially in functional programming circles it's a maxim that you design your data models first, specifically for your task, and then the rest of the code is pretty much pattern-matching around that. This takes that coupling and makes it even tighter, to serve a higher purpose. I almost feel like given enough time, this could be a JIT: an executable that adaptively strips code and self-minifies over time as you run it. :) Also check out: - kkrieger (windows only 96kb 3D FPS, video here: [VIDEO] ) - the previous post ( [LINK] )"},{"id":7489276,"author":"makmanalp","month":"2014-03","story":"Show HN: My Isometric Voxel Engine 6 Months Later","stance":"rec","why":"96kb first person shooter with amazing graphics.","quote":"the 96kb first person shooter that has (relatively) amazing graphics","text":"Wow, great work, this reminds me of a lot of demoscene stuff where, when faced with space limitations, programmers aggressively used procedural generation combined with prerendering and caching. The most iconic example I can think of is .kkrieger, the 96kb first person shooter that has (relatively) amazing graphics: [LINK] Video here: [VIDEO] There might be valuable lessons to be had in that domain that might save you some time."},{"id":47803864,"author":"Jach","month":"2026-04","story":"A Tiny Yellow Handheld Changed How Duke University Teaches Game Design","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a size-constraint game, impressive but not advancing design.","quote":"is my favorite of this genre of constraints, but it's mainly impressive for being possible at all","text":"That's the intention, sure, and as long as prospective Masters students know that's what they're getting into and paying for, and are looking forward to it, then it's fine or whatever. But it still strikes me as a silly constraint, just as it would be to require an in-house engine that sucks, or requiring students to develop for some old Nintendo hardware, or requiring students to fit everything in under 96k.[0] Anyone can add arbitrary constraints to anything, and lots of interesting questions will arise from figuring out how to deal with (or work around) such constraints. But is the constraint to develop for this specific device (and all the sub constraints that implies) actually a good one vs. any other set of constraints, especially for the purposes of game design? I doubt it. Especially how some of the constraints like only using black-and-white graphics are easily enforced without also requiring such a specific niche device. [0] .kkrieger ( [VIDEO] ) is my favorite of this genre of constraints, but it's mainly impressive for being possible at all (and you can read up on some of the developer notes for how much effort was put into satisfying this constraint). It didn't actually advance the design of FPSes or anything, and FPS design ideas could be better learned by making and iterating on an FPS without the tiny size constraint. If students want to impose extra constraints on themselves, like developing for the Playdate and making use of its crank for game control, go fo"},{"id":29847885,"author":"Guest19023892","month":"2022-01","story":"Nvidia tool generates full 3D models from a single still image","stance":"reference","why":"Notable 96KB game using procedural generation.","quote":".kkrieger got a lot of attention for using this method and only being 96KB.","text":"Are you referring to procedurally generated content? [LINK] .kkrieger got a lot of attention for using this method and only being 96KB. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24764798,"author":"onion2k","month":"2020-10","story":"Top games from the JS13K 2020 competition","stance":"reference","why":"Gameplay of a seriously impressive 96KB 3D FPS.","quote":"made a 3D FPS game in 96KB years ago that was seriously impressive.","text":"A group of devs from the demo scene legends Farbrausch made a 3D FPS game in 96KB years ago that was seriously impressive. 3D games with procedural textures and shaders can compress very well indeed. [LINK] (gameplay video [VIDEO] )"},{"id":8008896,"author":"Jach","month":"2014-07","story":"Procedural Content Generation in Games","stance":"reference","why":"Favorite example of an FPS in a 96 KB EXE.","quote":"My favorite example is the FPS .kkrieger","text":"My favorite example is the FPS .kkrieger ( [VIDEO] ) which is entirely contained in a 96 KB EXE. Recently I learned the source for that (and other projects) was released a couple years ago, even if in a pretty messy state: [LINK] Skimming around I found this snippet at the end of chapter six. Are the authors even aware of .kkrieger? As we can see, all existing work on generating graphical game has targeted games in the style of classic arcade games and home console games from the early 80’s, or simple arcade games. There is still considerable work to be done here, and nobody has yet constructed a system that could generate novel graphical games of high quality, comparable to the novel high-quality board games produced by Cameron Browne’s Ludi system. However, there is also considerable opportunities in devel- oping game description languages that can effectively and economically describe other types of games, and game generators that take into account the specific game design affordances and challenges that come with such games. For example, what would it take to generate playable, interesting and original FPS games?"},{"id":5460375,"author":"estebank","month":"2013-03","story":"Simple Minecraft Clone in 580 lines of Python","stance":"reference","why":"Introduced commenter to procedural assets in games.","quote":"introducing me to the potential of procedural assets in games.","text":"I still remember how I was blown away when seeing .kkrieger[1][2] for the first time, introducing me to the potential of procedural assets in games. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["biYciU1uiUw",1],["ySuUZEjARPY",1],["wqu_IpkOYBg",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1]]},"CCOdQsZa15o":{"wy":"Recommended as the standard short overview of missing middle housing and its link to zoning reform.","q":{"x":"you need to watch the NotJustBikes overview of the Missing Middle problem","i":29419190,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27727210,"author":"benrbray","month":"2021-07","story":"The Growth Ponzi Scheme","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series on car-dependent American cities, shared with friends","quote":"also has an excellent series of videos based on work from Strong Towns.","text":"The Not Just Bikes [1,2,3] YouTube channel also has an excellent series of videos based on work from Strong Towns. I link to these videos whenever a friend asks why I chose to leave the United States. :) [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29419190,"author":"quadrangle","month":"2021-12","story":"Facing housing crisis, L.A. voters back duplexes in single-family neighborhoods","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of the missing middle housing problem","quote":"you need to watch the NotJustBikes overview of the Missing Middle problem","text":"Seems to me you need to watch the NotJustBikes overview of the Missing Middle problem [VIDEO] Super dense cities are efficient in some ways, but they have tons of other problems and they externalize tons of costs. What we're missing in the U.S. is human-scale density, especially in the form of cohousing arrangements. Tall high-rises are not actually efficient. You know how much energy it takes to have elevators going up and down 40 floors along with all the other stuff? Land is not the single factor to calculate when we want efficiency."},{"id":29224421,"author":"meerab","month":"2021-11","story":"California plans to turn the screws on NIMBY cities","stance":"rec","why":"Another perspective on NIMBY and housing","quote":"(Love this channel!)","text":"This unfortunately true in California. NIMBY moment is strong. Another perspective.. [VIDEO] (Love this channel!)"},{"id":26663490,"author":"rossng","month":"2021-04","story":"A billion for every chip-maker who 'makes in India,' sources say","stance":"rec","why":"Explains gentle density and missing middle housing","quote":"I think these videos convey the idea well","text":"Yes, and it's often referred to as 'gentle density'. The idea is to build relatively dense housing, but at a human scale - mostly 3-5 storeys. Combined with narrow streets that support walking and cycling, this creates enough density to support a 'real' city centre with shops, restaurants, offices and so on. I think these videos convey the idea well: * [LINK] * [VIDEO] Of course, this is not to say that you _can't_ build other kinds of housing. But many cities are badly balanced."},{"id":34221226,"author":"greggman3","month":"2023-01","story":"Airbnb removed my negative review","stance":"reference","why":"Zoning as net negative for society","quote":"zoning has arguably been a net negative on most societies in many ways","text":"AirBnB sux but zoning has arguably been a net negative on most societies in many ways [LINK] [VIDEO] for me what I noticed lately is that in California it's impossible to have a quiet modern apartment because nearly 100% of modern apartment buildings are only allowed to be built on busy noisy high traffic streets. If you want a quiet street you're forced to buy/rent a house"},{"id":32741045,"author":"gernb","month":"2022-09","story":"San Francisco braces for commercial real estate crash","stance":"reference","why":"Codes favor car-dependent living","quote":"Many of those codes have been designed to protect incombents from competition","text":"Many of those codes have been designed to protect incombents from competition and various other forms of encouraging one form of living (car dependent) over others. Plenty of people in Japan, Korea, Hong Kong, etc live happy lives in much much smaller living areas. This makes them more affordable. But building codes in many places in the USA disallow making places that small. Other examples of questionable codes are codes that require parking spaces, codes that require front yards, codes that require backyards, etc.... [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":32302236,"author":"xracy","month":"2022-08","story":"Illegally placed concrete blocks have taken over public parking in Seattle","stance":"reference","why":"Missing middle housing pays for itself","quote":"There's a lot of the missing middle in Seattle for housing","text":"I will grant that Seattle is currently investing in public transit. And that in the next 10-15 years, they have the opportunity to improve that situation greatly (providing people good/consistent transit to downtown). There's a lot of the missing middle in Seattle for housing: [VIDEO] Which has been demonstrated to pay for itself (and support other businesses). Zoning for this would go a long way towards helping to alleviate the increasing cost of housing. (Also actively/readily) makes more money than it costs to maintain. I think there's also something to be said for getting rid of cars in Downtown Seattle (still need to provide good accessible options). I originally clicked on this article in hopes that was what people were doing. Tangent: Shuffling homeless people around with cement \"eco\" blocks (legitimately interested in the etymology of that name) turned out to be the sad waste of resources we were going for here... Mental Health feels a lot more difficult than this. Probably needs more of a national fix cause healthcare is too expensive and inaccessible in the US."},{"id":31257376,"author":"otikik","month":"2022-05","story":"Nature has enormous emotional and cognitive benefits on people","stance":"reference","why":"Compares US urban core and suburbs pattern with Europe","quote":"Not Just Bikes has a video about that","text":"I can’t claim to have visited all major cities in the US, but they do seem to match the “urban core and suburbs” pattern, compared with my experiences visiting European cities. Not Just Bikes has a video about that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30933263,"author":"sascha_sl","month":"2022-04","story":"IPCC’s starkest message yet: extreme steps needed to avert climate disaster","stance":"reference","why":"Shows car-dependent suburbs as the problem","quote":"","text":"This is a false choice. Limiting limitless consumption is not automatically reduction in the standard of living for most people. There is a huge effect when cutting emissions of the top 10%, even a tiny bit. [1] The graph is likely hiding that if the top 10% was segmented more, it would be very hop heavy - even limiting it to the median of the top 10% should do a lot. The US is also uniquely positioned here to cut their emissions in half and still have more emissions per capacita than Europe[1]. And no, the problem is not that the US is large and cars are required; cars are fine for rural people, but they are not fine for the spawling car-dependent suburban areas US cities keep creating. [2] [1]: [LINK] [2]: (video) [VIDEO]"},{"id":29081808,"author":"ClumsyPilot","month":"2021-11","story":"Zillow seeks to sell 7k homes for $2.8B after flipping halt","stance":"reference","why":"Related to zoning reform and housing supply","quote":"We need to get institutional 'investment' out if house flipping and fix zoning.","text":"They are gonna need cars and furniture, yet those are not an investment. Is USA running out of land, or Timber, or unemployed people that could be employed to build a house? We need to get institutional 'investment' out if house flipping and fix zoning. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29081851,"author":"ClumsyPilot","month":"2021-11","story":"Zillow seeks to sell 7k homes for $2.8B after flipping halt","stance":"reference","why":"Shows duplex and triplex alternatives illegal in most places","quote":"there are duplex and triplex homes, illegal in most of us, and other variations","text":"\"But there's also the side of people wanting single family homes instead of apartment' Most people who can't afford a home would not reject a decent apartment, they just want somewhere to live. Also those are not the only two choices, there are duplex and triplex homes, illegal in most of us, and other variations [VIDEO] \"Nothing wrong with people in a community wanting to set standards for their area.\" So in this \"democracy\" people who don't own a house have no voice and no right? Why does this remind me of some other mechanism for opressing undesirables?"},{"id":29340918,"author":"dkdbejwi383","month":"2021-11","story":"Greening of the Earth Mitigates Surface Warming (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Medium-density cities versus sprawl or towers","quote":"The YouTube channel \"Not just bikes\" talks about this.","text":"> Personally I think that Americans just don't realize that it's not cities that suck, it's American cities that suck. Plenty of pleasant cities to live in in the world. The YouTube channel \"Not just bikes\" talks about this. In the USA, it is commonly seen that there are only two possible configurations for human settlements: sprawling suburbs, or Manhattan-style high-density towers. The medium-density style of city common across a lot of Europe and Asia is not very well known in the USA and Canada outside of a select few older cities which developed to a large enough size before the automobile took over. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27727391,"author":"zucker42","month":"2021-07","story":"The Growth Ponzi Scheme","stance":"reference","why":"Explains missing middle housing","quote":"there might be strong demand for missing middle[1] housing if only it were legal and frictionless to build it.","text":"It's not just density, though that's part of it. A big part is walkability (and otherwise making the city not just car accessible). That can be done without increasing density, by for example, adding wide sidewalks and bike lanes, eliminating maze-like cul-de-sac-heavy street layouts, and decreasing speeds in commercial areas (i.e. enforcing a stronger divide between low speed streets meant for people and high speed roads meant for cars). Another part is mixed-use zoning, where small businesses are intermixed with homes instead of people relying on driving to megastores in shopping centers far from where they live. By decreasing the necessity of the car you decrease the cost of road maintenance and by making places more walkable you make businesses more accessible and therefore more successful. I would suspect that many neighborhoods could be kept at the same density and be significantly rethought in line with the Strong Towns ethos to be more comfortable places to live. Density increases might be necessary in high COL areas because those places are in high demand, but there's plenty of bad city design in places outside uber-expensive metropolitan areas. And there might be strong demand for missing middle[1] housing if only it were legal and frictionless to build it. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bnKIVX968PQ",2],["MWsGBRdK2N0",1],["uxykI30fS54",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["XfQUOHlAocY",1],["RQY6WGOoYis",1]]},"n02tImce3AE":{"wy":"Cited as a fun, experimental debunking of tonewood claims that shows electronics dominate electric guitar sound.","q":{"x":"I recently came across an excellent video where a guitarist tries to determine exactly which parts of an electric guitar affect its tone.","i":31001309,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31001309,"author":"fenomas","month":"2022-04","story":"The Legend of the Music Tree","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Methodically swaps electric guitar parts to find what affects tone.","quote":"I recently came across an excellent video where a guitarist tries to determine exactly which parts of an electric guitar affect its tone.","text":"An adjacent topic, since TFA is about acoustic guitars and this is about electric, but I recently came across an excellent video where a guitarist tries to determine exactly which parts of an electric guitar affect its tone. He did it by methodically swapping parts around on a cheap kit guitar and comparing the sound to a quality model, and he winds up finding the body shape/material appear to have no effect at all. He even gets closely matching sounds out of a \"guitar\" with no body at all, by keeping certain other elements (strings, pickups, electronics) the same as the real model. Really surprising results! Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47565224,"author":"gadtfly","month":"2026-03","story":"Comparison shows audiophiles waste a lot of money","stance":"rec","why":"Systematic experiments on sacred and sensitive targets.","quote":"check out this guy who ran a much more systematic set of experiments","text":"If this is appealing to you, check out this guy who ran a much more systematic set of experiments on even more sacred and sensitive targets: Tested: Where Does The Tone Come From In An Electric Guitar? [VIDEO] Tested: Where Does The Tone Come From In A Guitar Amplifier? [LINK]"},{"id":36658718,"author":"dmbche","month":"2023-07","story":"Amateurs obsess over tools, pros over mastery","stance":"rec","why":"Shows experimentally the guitar itself has little to do with tone.","quote":"this is pretty fascinating - he shows experimentaly that the guitar itself has little to do with tone.","text":"[VIDEO] If you're interested, this is pretty fascinating - he shows experimentaly that the guitar itself has little to do with tone. Other than that better made instruments could (no promises!) hold their tuning a little better, but you can cheaply buy locks or spend some money having that re-done. More than this and I have a hard explaining what a \"pro\" guitar could do to help an amateur. You get the same tones out of it, pedals and amps have much more impact on sound anyway. And seriously, unless you have musical training, you'll never hear the difference between epiphone and gibson, or squier and fender."},{"id":31512907,"author":"CharlesW","month":"2022-05","story":"Simple And Terrible Machines","stance":"rec","why":"Fun and technically interesting look at guitar simplicity.","quote":"is a fun and technically-interesting video","text":"If you're interested in how simple they can get, \"Tested: Where Does The Tone Come From In An Electric Guitar?\" is a fun and technically-interesting video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40070822,"author":"mrob","month":"2024-04","story":"Everything we can't describe in music","stance":"reference","why":"Debunks tonewood claims for electric guitars.","quote":"for electric guitars this is thoroughly debunked","text":">Different wood types will produce different tonal characteristics. True for acoustic guitars, but for electric guitars this is thoroughly debunked: [VIDEO] Previous HN discussion: [LINK]"},{"id":33721565,"author":"dktoao","month":"2022-11","story":"Japanese have been producing wood for 700 years without cutting down trees","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that electric tone comes from electronics.","quote":"Electric instruments in general derive their sound mostly through the electronics.","text":"Electric instruments in general derive their sound mostly through the electronics. There are quite a few high end electric guitar companies (like PRS) that would like you to believe otherwise. If you are in the market for an electric instrument please don't have it made from rare, questionably sourced hardwoods! [VIDEO]"},{"id":30723625,"author":"cma","month":"2022-03","story":"Down the mechanical keyboard rabbit hole","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment shows neck wood has almost no impact on tone.","quote":"Neck wood seems to have almost no impact","text":"Neck wood seems to have almost no impact: [VIDEO] Most tone there was found to be in pickup height adjustment."}],"r":[["wcBEOcPtlYk",1],["LPqtfC70QTU",1],["g3Ud6mHdhlQ",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["wjZofJX0v4M",1],["F-yDKeya4SU",1],["SCFXV6o7cro",1]]},"ne99laPUxN4":{"wy":"Cited as the more accessible companion to the paper, and as the most interesting treatment of AD via compiling to categories.","q":{"x":"There's a talk that Conal gives that makes the paper a bit more accessible","i":32920650,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31017426,"author":"infogulch","month":"2022-04","story":"Differentiable Programming – A Simple Introduction","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compiling to categories makes forward and reverse mode AD composable","quote":"The most interesting thing I've seen on AD is","text":"The most interesting thing I've seen on AD is \"The simple essence of automatic differentiation\" (2018) [1]. See past discussion [2], and talk [3]. I think the main idea is that by compiling to categories and pairing up a function with its derivative, the pair becomes trivially composable in forward mode, and the whole structure is easily converted to reverse mode afterwards. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: Talk at Microsoft Research: [VIDEO] Other presentations listed here: [LINK]"},{"id":32920650,"author":"sriku","month":"2022-09","story":"Automatic Differentiation in 38 lines of Haskell","stance":"rec","why":"Talk that makes the AD paper somewhat more accessible","quote":"There's a talk that Conal gives that makes the paper a bit more accessible","text":"I'm yet to give reverse mode a second try, but my attempts at forward mode (linked in parent post) were on my own without the paper's help. There's a talk that Conal gives that makes the paper a bit more accessible - [VIDEO] - but only a bit more. I know it'll take effort to wrap my head around the implementation details ... but the paper certainly helped clarify things I was stumbling around about."},{"id":21525236,"author":"noblethrasher","month":"2019-11","story":"Differentiable Programming Manifesto","stance":"rec","why":"Elliott's nice presentation on automatic differentiation","quote":"He also gave nice presentation on AD","text":"He also gave nice presentation on AD: [VIDEO] (surprised that you didn't already mention it, considering the venue ;-))"},{"id":18492499,"author":"infogulch","month":"2018-11","story":"Automatic Differentiation in Machine Learning: A Survey [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Video presentation of the Simple Essence of AD paper","quote":"I found it pretty awesome, just in case you hadn't seen it.","text":"Another paper related to AD was posted last month: The Simple Essence of Automatic Differentiation [0]. Video presentation [1]. I found it pretty awesome, just in case you hadn't seen it. [0]: https//news.ycombinator.com/item?id=18306860 [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18306915,"author":"bmc7505","month":"2018-10","story":"The Simple Essence of Automatic Differentiation [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Longer talk by the author on the award-winning AD paper","quote":"Conal Elliott recently gave a longer talk about it at Microsoft Research","text":"This paper won a best paper award at ICFP 2018. Conal Elliott recently gave a longer talk about it at Microsoft Research: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37263260,"author":"eli_gottlieb","month":"2023-08","story":"Understanding Automatic Differentiation in 30 lines of Python","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"AD is just a Cartesian lens of Jacobians and total derivatives in the category of smooth functions, what's the problem? [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5MxH1sfJLBQ",1],["2A2NY29iQdI",1],["j5v8D-alAKE",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["p8u_k2LIZyo",1],["Ecy1FBdCRbQ",1],["htYh-Tq7ZBI",1],["Y9FZ4igNxNA",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["bx3KuE7UjGA",1],["3kEfedtQVOY",1],["XRYN2xt11Ek",1]]},"-f-CFQxaUY4":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a researcher-led answer on omega-3 dosing, with the takeaway that most people take too little.","q":{"x":"it's what the dose range is: we're all underdosing.","i":32366890,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33853735,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2022-12","story":"Omega-3 supplements don't provide as much benefits as it claimed on the internet","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with a researcher who studied fatty acids for over 40 years.","quote":"I would suggest watching this video with Dr. Rhonda Patrick and Dr. Bill Harris","text":"If interesting in Omega-3 benefits, I would suggest watching this video with Dr. Rhonda Patrick and Dr. Bill Harris who has researched fatty acids for over 40 years. [1] [1] - [VIDEO] [video]"},{"id":32366890,"author":"DiabloD3","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Longevity for your parents, what to do?","stance":"rec","why":"Key takeaway is the effective dose range: most people underdose omega-3.","quote":"it's what the dose range is: we're all underdosing.","text":"The NMN research didn't pan out in the end, just throwing that out here. There is something involved in NMN, but taking it (or any of the precursors or metabolites) alone doesn't actually seem to reliably cause what they were looking for. Most likely, it's something like the curcumin vs ground turmeric issue (curcumin doesn't work alone, it combines with another chemical found in turmeric to produce the active chemical, and none of the curcumin supplements work), or with glucoraphanin and myrosinase to produce sulforaphane (popularized by Dr Rhonda Patrick; sulforaphane is short lived, and you need to package them correctly to not self-react in a supplement while it's sitting there on a shelf, so the delivery mechanism is the hard part), or with the flavor of garlic, allicin, another short lived chemical produced by alliin and alliinase (which is why crushing garlic is important instead of slicing, and ground garlic and factory produced minced garlic will never have that magical flavor). Also, as for Omega 3, watch [VIDEO] Dr Rhonda Patrick interviews Dr Bill Harris; the main take away from this isn't that it works (we all know that already), it's what the dose range is: we're all underdosing."},{"id":32223397,"author":"lambdaba","month":"2022-07","story":"Ancient Siberian dogs relied on humans for seafood diets","stance":"rec","why":"High-dose EPA reduces inflammation; omega-3 levels linked to mortality risk.","quote":"I learned a lot here","text":"I learned a lot here, but I'm not able to restitute it accurately now: [Dr. Bill Harris on the Omega-3 Index: Increasing Omega-3 to Promote Longevity & Transform Health ]( [VIDEO] ). From this and other sources, I can summarize an answer as this: omega-3s in large enough doses, doses rarely used in studies, and especially EPA, is extremely good at reducing inflammation (see \"resolvins\" and EPA), is a good natural blood thinner and improves blood lipids. The doctor interviewed above thinks that the reduction of inflammation is the main benefit. This finding stuck with me: smokers with a good level of omega-3s had the same risk of death in a 10 year period than non-smokers with low omega-3s. Re: ratios and food sources; of course there are. Seafood often has almost exclusively omega-3s as far as I'm aware, though I'm sure this doesn't apply to farm-raised salmon, for instance. Re: conversion rate from ALA, I couldn't find good references initially, but I've found someone citing a \"0.5-20%\" range for conversion of ALA to EPA/DHA (and I think the conversion to DHA is even lower, or there was some additional caveat). So that's the point, there is a wide variance."},{"id":30587185,"author":"loudtieblahblah","month":"2022-03","story":"Vitamin D supplements lower risk of autoimmune disease, researchers say","stance":"rec","why":"Talk with an omega-3 researcher of 300+ papers on fatty acids.","quote":"Here's a fantastic talk between Dr Rhonda Patrick and Dr. Bill Harris.","text":"Before people get all excited... Prescription strength EPA can cause heart arrhythmia once you start crawling above 4g a day. No idea if it can happen at doses lower. [LINK] I still think 1G-2G of fish oil, with more of a lean to EPA, is probably beneficial over the long run. I, personally, take about 1-1.5G a day of a third-party tested brand but only do so 4 days a week. I give myself a break from supplements over Fri-Sun. Here's a fantastic talk between Dr Rhonda Patrick and Dr. Bill Harris. From the YT Description, he is the author of more than 300 scientific papers on fatty acids and health. He is a professor in the Department of Medicine in the Samford School of Medicine at the University of South Dakota, the co-inventor of the Omega-3 Index, founder of OmegaQuant Analytics, and president and founder of the Fatty Acid Research Institute. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30458825,"author":"loudtieblahblah","month":"2022-02","story":"Soybean oil affects hypothalamus, causes genetic changes in mice: study (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"They're both polyunsaturated fats. While an over-simplification Omega3s (which ALA is) tend to be anti-inflammatory and have tons of positive effects of cardiovascular health (up to a point - more on this later) while Omega6s, which are necessary for our survival, do tend to have an inflammatory effect. The goal is to be high-ish in Omega3s without getting mercury poisoning and low in Omega6s. The modern diet - so if you go out to eat at all, if you cook with corn/canola/soybean/cottonseed oils or shortenings like Criso, if you use 90% of most modern pre-made marinades, salad dressings, cereals, chips, crackers, cookies, and even most bread (not including some brands of butter bread), they all load up on \"seed oils\" (Meaning LA). When people say a good diet is rich in whole foods and low in processed foods - this is what is at the heart of it. Processed foods tend to be high in omega6s, high in sugar, and high in sodium,. The latter 2 are largely for taste, or in salt's case taste and preservation. Omega6s are used b/c the oils are super cheap. These are oils that couldn't exist without modern industry, in the bulk in which we produce them. (unlike say..beef tallow, coconut oil, butter, olive oil, ghee, duck fat, etc.) And initially they were sold by non-food industries as a way to maximize profits on byproducts from other industrial processes. They have incredibly high smoke points and they don't go rancid or spoil, so if you want to make say... Cheeze-Its or Pringles, it's "}],"r":[["ZUG9qYTJMsI",1],["5SaFTm2bcac",1],["zz4YVJ4aRfg",1],["06e-PwhmSq8",1],["sWN13pKVp9s",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["9nXop2lLDa4",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["aDejwCGdUV8",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["xc8epam4NyY",1],["G0Cp7DrvNLQ",1],["MVYrJJNdrEg",1]]},"5Tek9h3a5wQ":{"wy":"Held up as a classic debate with a clear winner and Baldwin's powerful oratory, still relevant today.","q":{"x":"he mesmerises the audience by telling them what it's like to grow up black in America not even knowing you're black","i":25987825,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25987825,"author":"wombatmobile","month":"2021-02","story":"James Baldwin, Here and Elsewhere","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Baldwin's Cambridge appearance on growing up black in America","quote":"he mesmerises the audience by telling them what it's like to grow up black in America not even knowing you're black","text":"Baldwin could see things that ordinary commentators couldn't because he saw the world through other people's eyes, and felt the machinery of their thoughts. In this 1965 appearance at Cambridge University, he mesmerises the audience by telling them what it's like to grow up black in America not even knowing you're black, until one day, as a young child, you catch yourself in the mirror and realise you aren't the person your culture is built for. You actually don't exist, because there is no space in your culture's stories for you. What brings the predominantly white male audience at Cambridge Union to their feet for a standing ovation, beyond Baldwin's faultless elocution, is his mastery of white American experience. He speaks about racism from inside out, considering that what has happened to white Americans who have succumbed to the disease of racism is worse than what has happened to black Americans who suffer it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23593381,"author":"BoiledCabbage","month":"2020-06","story":"The history that James Baldwin wanted America to see","stance":"strong_rec","why":"1965 Baldwin and Buckley debate with clarity still relevant today","quote":"I'd also recommend watching Baldwin's debate with William F Buckley.","text":"I'd also recommend watching Baldwin's debate with William F Buckley. It's fascinating to see his clarity of thought, and how much it still ties to arguments of today. Different words, different terms, and more euphemisms now but same arguments of thought. The topic of the debate is \"Has the American dream been achieved at the expense of the American negro?\" [VIDEO] Or even just watch the first 10 mins in from the youtube link point. The first 5+ mins slowing build to his point. Just watching their debate styles is fascinating."},{"id":26981113,"author":"pasabagi","month":"2021-04","story":"New evidence that British workplaces are losing viewpoint diversity","stance":"rec","why":"Classic debate where Buckley comes across weakly against Baldwin","quote":"I think it might be worth it to watch a classic of the 'free debate' genre","text":"As a sort of counterpoint, I think it might be worth it to watch a classic of the 'free debate' genre, Buckley vs Baldwin[1], that happened in the sixties in Cambridge. It pitted a black civil rights activist against the young Buckley, later a stalwart of American conservatism. I don't know if it's my bias speaking, but I felt that in the debate, Buckley comes across as a bully, with very little argumentative meat, who doesn't really respond to Baldwin's points. For what its worth, I didn't really like Baldwin's delivery either, but at least I felt there were substantive points there that were worth addressing. In a way, this is probably the best one can hope for when you stage this kind of event - Buckley is, at least, well educated, but I can't help but think the debate might have been far more interesting if it was Baldwin vs Malcolm X - because ultimately the problem with the Buckleys of the world is that they defend institutions that were not built on ideals, but rather economics, and as such the defenses themselves are generally sophistic and without anything really interesting in them. If I was a student, I would probably agitate to exclude a Buckley, and instead reach for a Malcolm, and I expect somebody would accuse me of being anti-free-speech, since the debate that would result would no longer span a societal divide, and both parties in the debate would be on 'one side' of the broader social issue. However, from an intellectual standpoint, it just sometimes is the "},{"id":37808073,"author":"tsimionescu","month":"2023-10","story":"Vocal Fry Register","stance":"reference","why":"Buckley using vocal fry to sound refined","quote":"using it to sound prim and proper","text":"The point is that vocal fry, even when it is an affectation and not just your normal speech, used to be seen as a marker of eloquence and high class. British actors, male ones, used vocal fry extensively. Sean Connery's James Bond uses it in the famous \"Bond. James Bond\" introduction [0], for just one example. Or listen to William F Buckley, an American high class conservative (buffoon that presented as an intellectual) using it to sound prim and proper [1]. It is an objective fact that hating vocal fry is just a fad, and that loving it has been a fad for at least as long. You are free to dislike it, but you can't pretend it should be hated on objective grounds, and it definitely doesn't convey boredom or low effort. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] - in a debate where Buckley's arguing the \"dignified\" position that black people shouldn't receive more rights"},{"id":36433468,"author":"troyvit","month":"2023-06","story":"Turning Joe Rogan down was the right thing to do","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a structured debate with a clear winner","quote":"The James Baldwin / William Buckley debate is a good example[1].","text":"Debates can also be structured enough that if the debaters follow the rules it becomes possible to see clear winners, or at least surface important ideas for people to chew on. The James Baldwin / William Buckley debate is a good example[1]. Somehow though I don't think you'd get that kind of structure in a Joe Rogan podcast. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["wGt4XwBbCvA",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1]]},"Ac7G7xOG2Ag":{"wy":"Used as the canonical reference for the jargon-parody trope, and as a joke whenever technobabble appears.","q":{"x":"Doug Zongker's presentation similarly instructs people how to make better presentations to peers by demonstrating what not to do","i":45814034,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45814034,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-11","story":"Learning to read Arthur Whitney's C to become smart (2024)","stance":"rec","why":"Teaches communicating with interdisciplinary audiences","quote":"Bud Haggert still teaches smart people something very important about communication with interdisciplinary crowds","text":"\"Still it's amusing how Whitney's style seems to personally offend people\" So it was for the lols after all... Notably, a Lisp interpreter written in Lisp is a common assignment for second year CS students. In a production setting, there is little reason to misappropriate resources for such toys. Bud Haggert still teaches smart people something very important about communication with interdisciplinary crowds: [VIDEO] Doug Zongker's presentation similarly instructs people how to make better presentations to peers by demonstrating what not to do: [LINK] Best of luck =3"},{"id":38423644,"author":"causality0","month":"2023-11","story":"Rockwell Retro Encabulator [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Argues the original is better than the Rockwell version","quote":"the older Turbo Emcabulator video is far superior","text":"I don't know why people are always posting the Rockwell version when the older Turbo Emcabulator video is far superior. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27739580,"author":"NelsonMinar","month":"2021-07","story":"New mystery AWS product ‘Infinidash’ goes viral despite being entirely fictional","stance":"rec","why":"Classic engineering parody training videos","quote":"Bud Haggart's industrial training videos for the Turboencabulator from the 70s/80s are things of beauty.","text":"There's a proud tradition of this kind of parody: the Turboencabluator is one of the most famous in engineering, a joke that goes back to 1944. [LINK] Bud Haggart's industrial training videos for the Turboencabulator from the 70s/80s are things of beauty. See [VIDEO]"},{"id":19981562,"author":"Anarch157a","month":"2019-05","story":"Five Hundred and Seven Mechanical Movements (1908)","stance":"rec","why":"Classic parody video the commenter thinks the reader will like","quote":"I think you're gonna like this one [1].","text":"I think you're gonna like this one [1]. The B-25 Mitchell instructional that was linked here some few weeks ago is also amazing [2]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":43668903,"author":"paranoidrobot","month":"2025-04","story":"Tunarr: Create and configure live TV channels from media on your servers","stance":"reference","why":"Rabbit hole of encabulator videos","quote":"You could also go down the Turbo Encabulator rabbit hole","text":"There are youtube channels with old TV Ads. Some of those will be non-existant. You could also go down the Turbo Encabulator rabbit hole: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":29756135,"author":"myrandomcomment","month":"2022-01","story":"Turboencabulator","stance":"reference","why":"The original turboencabulator video","quote":"","text":"Here is the fun take - [LINK] And the original - [VIDEO] Also curious as this was on Twitter the other day...how social mixed if this is the OPs reason for sharing."},{"id":28751170,"author":"robocat","month":"2021-10","story":"McKinsey never told the FDA it was working for both the FDA and opioid makers","stance":"reference","why":"The original of the long-running trope","quote":"the original is here","text":"That is the long running “Turbo Encabulator” trope, the original is here [VIDEO] but there are some wonderful rerenditions and tributes if you search for those keywords on YouTube."},{"id":48579110,"author":"bizzletk","month":"2026-06","story":"Leaked financial docs show OpenAI is losing billions of dollars a year","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Wouldn't the real story be to get government contracts? Those are more immune to public fickleness and market competition and usually have truly ludicrous margins. If they're the only ones who ̶a̶r̶e̶ ̶w̶i̶l̶l̶i̶n̶g̶ ̶t̶o̶ ̶b̶e̶ ̶t̶h̶e̶ ̶e̶n̶g̶i̶n̶e̶ ̶f̶o̶r̶ ̶a̶u̶t̶o̶n̶o̶m̶o̶u̶s̶ ̶k̶i̶l̶l̶b̶o̶t̶s̶ can draw a reciprocation dingle-arm to reduce soinosoidal repleneration, then \"I'm sure the government will buy it\" [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40903408,"author":"contingencies","month":"2024-07","story":"Detect Migrating Birds with a Plastic Dish and a Cheap Microphone","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"PoE works better (cheaper, higher bandwidth, differential signalling for higher EMI resistance, readily shared, etc.). Plastic covers are ~free (splash out and deploy duct tape if necessary). MEMS mic -> I2C -> cheap MCU -> ethernet. Theory: Capture digitally, and send the data - forget XLR and other expensive analog 'professional' fancabulation[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40699790,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2024-06","story":"Experts vs. Imitators","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The article is conflating buzzword laden hype-cycles with qualitative artifacts. It takes experience to recognize most software is still garbage, but more importantly determine which parts of the garbage heap is useful. The primary problem is the very definition of any unique terminology or product is distorted by the industry itself to fit a marketing niche. After a few years people sound like they had a stroke, and bought a Turbo Encabulator: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40479523,"author":"leecoursey","month":"2024-05","story":"Startup is about to install bladeless rooftop wind turbines on box buildings","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Oh, dear. I read as far as \"Its generator system is a rotor-stator system with a highly efficient 5 kW permanent magnet generator\", then immediately thought of the Turbo Encabulator: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36984602,"author":"lucidguppy","month":"2023-08","story":"‘How Do I Do That?’ The New Hires of 2023 Are Unprepared for Work","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I agree - college is a filter - not a tech tree. In engineering school you solve problems similar to industry - but its more the difficulty than the similarity. New grads can handle the difficulty your job provides. If you train them - they should get the hang of it. Industry leads academia in undergrad - not the other way around. Industry knew that new grads needed 6-12 months of niche education (this is the general operation of Turbo Encabulators [VIDEO] ). It was in their budget. Now.... not so much."},{"id":35761362,"author":"kstenerud","month":"2023-04","story":"Just Simply – Stop saying how simple things are in our docs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of the turbo encabulator presentation [1]. Quote: \"The latter consisted simply of six hydrocoptic marzlevanes, so fitted to the ambifacient lunar waneshaft that side fumbling was effectively prevented.\" [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31427413,"author":"ethbr0","month":"2022-05","story":"Parmigiano Reggiano makers embedding tiny trackers in rind to fight cheese fraud","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> That works even if you remove the \"Blockchain\". That's a key criteria for being Web 3.0. Now basically, the only new principal involved is that instead of value being generated by the relative motion of conductors and fluxes, it is produced by the modial interaction of magneto-reluctance and capacitive-interactance... [VIDEO]"},{"id":29206851,"author":"JadeNB","month":"2021-11","story":"Nuclear radiation used to transmit digital data wirelessly","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> This sounds like a line from the turbo encabulator. In case you're one of the lucky 10,000: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19736190,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2019-04","story":"Changes to Models S and X allow them to travel longer without larger batteries","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've been having issues with my transmission's cardinal grammeters going out of sync. I've heard if I get a turbo encabulator [0], it would supply an inverse reactive current into the unilateral phase destractors, which would fix the sync issue, but the price tag is more than I can swallow. I might just have to deal with the side fumbling of the ambifacient lunar waneshaft, even if it means having to regularly replace the spurving bearings. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16813074,"author":"pg_bot","month":"2018-04","story":"Dapps","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Fred may as well be describing the turboencabulator[0]. I get that VCs should swing for the fences, but this seems like they are totally ignoring first principles when it comes to cryptocurrency/blockchain apps. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11024182,"author":"meatsock","month":"2016-02","story":"The Turbo-Encabulator in Industry (1944)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"encabulation is a great topic of study with many interesting video demos turbo encabulator v1 [VIDEO] rockwell turbo encabulator [LINK] rockwell retro encabulator [LINK] chrysler turbo encabulator [LINK] chrysler turbo encabulator diagnosis and service [LINK] fort worth vintage flying museum specific service notes [LINK]"}],"r":[["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["LnzuMJLZRdU",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["DovunOxlY1k",1],["qEJ4hkpQW8E",1],["EBCYvoC4muc",1],["A2zKARkpDW4",1]]},"EUXnJraKM3k":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up whenever Microsoft product naming, branding or marketing is confusing, pointing to it as a long-running self-aware critique.","q":{"x":"brutal but hilarious commentary on Microsoft's inherent dysfunction when it comes to product naming and packaging","i":46864072,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46864072,"author":"jnaina","month":"2026-02","story":"Claude Code is suddenly everywhere inside Microsoft","stance":"rec","why":"Brutal, hilarious take on Microsoft's product naming and packaging dysfunction.","quote":"brutal but hilarious commentary on Microsoft's inherent dysfunction when it comes to product naming and packaging","text":"It's over 19 years old, but this video is a brutal but hilarious commentary on Microsoft's inherent dysfunction when it comes to product naming and packaging. Still on point decades later. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32794044,"author":"gumby","month":"2022-09","story":"iPhone 14 Pro comes with dual-frequency GPS","stance":"rec","why":"Short funny internal Microsoft video on feature-list marketing.","quote":"This great (short and funny) internal video from Microsoft was supposed to be a wake up call","text":"Many tech products are still sold on feature lists. Few people care any more. This great (short and funny) internal video from Microsoft was supposed to be a wake up call, but nothing really changed: [VIDEO] The fact that Apple doesn't really play that game seems to drive some people wild."},{"id":27258440,"author":"alisonkisk","month":"2021-05","story":"If Microsoft Designed macOS (Concept)","stance":"rec","why":"Original 2006 Microsoft iPod packaging video, better than the concept video.","quote":"The original \"Microsoft iPod\" video from 2006 is much better","text":"It's just a few lines of text complain about Windows, and some windows UI screenshots copied onto a max desktop. Not worth 2 minutes of video. The original \"Microsoft iPod\" video from 2006 is much better [VIDEO]"},{"id":5086165,"author":"untog","month":"2013-01","story":"Microsoft hires designer behind \"The Next Microsoft\", Andrew Kim","stance":"rec","why":"Old MS-produced parody of its own marketing.","quote":"is still worth a laugh","text":"This is supposedly an MS-produced parody of it's marketing efforts. It's very old now (I think it uses a 1st gen iPod?) but is still worth a laugh: [VIDEO] Thankfully MS have learnt a lot of lessons since."},{"id":49179510,"author":"thomassmith65","month":"2026-08","story":"\"Gravity is worth asking about.\"","stance":"reference","why":"Viral 20-year-old Microsoft packaging parody.","quote":"This reminded me of a 20 year old 'viral' video","text":"This reminded me of a 20 year old 'viral' video \"Microsoft Designs the iPod Package\" : [VIDEO]"},{"id":48863362,"author":"jerf","month":"2026-07","story":"Anyone else get a vague GitHub shakedown notice?","stance":"reference","why":"Classic Microsoft-made packaging parody.","quote":"that video came from Microsoft itself","text":"Oof, you're reminding me of Microsoft Redesigns the iPod Packaging video: [VIDEO] For those lucky 10,000 just learning about that today, for extra frisson , enjoy the fact that that video came from Microsoft itself : [LINK]"},{"id":47652818,"author":"thomasjudge","month":"2026-04","story":"How many products does Microsoft have named 'Copilot'? I mapped every one","stance":"reference","why":"Parody critique of Microsoft product branding.","quote":"this is a critique - from microsofties I think - of MSFT product branding","text":"Related: (parody): if you haven't seen it, this is a critique - from microsofties I think - of MSFT product branding [VIDEO]"},{"id":43581204,"author":"Svip","month":"2025-04","story":"Microsoft will kill Remote Desktop soon, insists you'll love replacement","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Microsoft knew about its naming and marketing problems long ago.","quote":"Even 20 years ago, Microsoft knew they had problem with its naming and marketing[0]","text":"Even 20 years ago, Microsoft knew they had problem with its naming and marketing[0], but I guess it didn't matter. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41175224,"author":"ryandrake","month":"2024-08","story":"No price Microsoft could pay Apple to use Bing: Google antitrust ruling excerpts","stance":"reference","why":"Parody of Microsoft's botched product marketing.","quote":"It was funny because all of Microsoft's brand looked exactly like that back in those days","text":"Reminds me of the \"Microsoft Re-Designs the iPod Packaging\" video[1] from nearly 2 decades ago (wow, I feel old). It was funny because all of Microsoft's brand looked exactly like that back in those days, and you could really picture them screwing the product marketing up that badly. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40420824,"author":"tornadron","month":"2024-05","story":"Introducing Copilot+ PCs","stance":"reference","why":"Old Microsoft iPod video that is still relevant.","quote":"had to dig it up and still kinda relevant","text":"reading all of these comments, reminded me of the old \"Microsoft iPod\" video, had to dig it up and still kinda relevant---it's 18 years old (!!!) [VIDEO]"},{"id":36468166,"author":"flir","month":"2023-06","story":"Has Xbox lost the console wars?","stance":"reference","why":"Decades-old internal Microsoft video on its marketing dysfunction.","quote":"that video was made within Microsoft","text":"You remind me of this [VIDEO] which is a couple of decades old now. Funny thing is, that video was made within Microsoft. It's fascinating that everyone can be aware of the problem, but they still can't steer the behemoth."},{"id":35664478,"author":"Jack000","month":"2023-04","story":"How to Become a “Designer Who Codes”","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how hard restrained, minimalist design is to execute.","quote":"This video is a bit old now but it's as true now as it's ever been","text":"Actually I don't think most programmers are capable of designing something like spacex.com - every image on the site fits the minimalist theme (and there are hundreds). The photos are always dark in the appropriate areas so the text is readable, and they're dynamically cropped/resized with good positioning for all breakpoints. - the front page appears to be simple, but if you click through the site there are actually a ton of dynamic illustrations and charts like this: [LINK] The key thing is that the aesthetic is executed consistently and with restraint. This video is a bit old now but it's as true now as it's ever been: [VIDEO] Minimalism is the most difficult aesthetic to pull off, because it's easy for the end result to look unfinished, cheap or lazy. I think the problem is not that coders can't be designers, but that coders generally don't really care about design, and I say that as a coder. If you gave the spacex project to the average coder, you'll probably end up with something like sap.com or fortinet.com Web design has converged a lot in the last 20 years because people don't want to learn a new UI for every website they visit. This makes good web design harder , because now if you want to create something memorable and distinctive, you'll need to do it in spite of the navigation and functional parts looking identical to every other site."},{"id":33310192,"author":"alexwasserman","month":"2022-10","story":"Apple's sleeping advertising business","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates cluttered PC branding versus Apple's clean experience.","quote":"","text":"That Apple could do it doesn’t mean they will, and the article doesn’t present much actual evidence it will. In Apple long-running saga with Ads it’s always seemed like Apple hates ads because it’s other companies content (and so priorities, aesthetic, and feel) jammed inside an Apple product. And Apple hates anything that ruins the Apple Experience :tm: Paying to be the first App Store entry is great, because it’s Apple showing off the normal Apple content (an app card) within a search list of app cards. But in an app that cuts to some cheap, ugly, non-Apple aesthetic ad - that’s pretty unappealing and ruins the Apple Experience. It’s tougher to craft that Apple type experience while also selling out. I think they’re also aware of the implicit value to their business of being the non-Ad driven eco-system. It’s all part of being premium. Selling to the users who also pay for Netflix premium, Hulu ad-free, etc. It’s built into their business model. In some ways it’s been like that for years - PC laptops come coated in ads from the Intel Inside stickers and pre-installed crapwear, to the design and logos on the product boxes themselves. I’m reminded of this: [VIDEO] I think Apple sorta, maybe wants Ads because it’s so lucrative, but also recognizes those challenges are real, and tough, and destroy their brand quickly."},{"id":7188661,"author":"mturmon","month":"2014-02","story":"Microsoft, Past and Future","stance":"reference","why":"Packaging parody made by an MS team out of frustration.","quote":"which was made by a MS team as an illustration of their frustration","text":"One example of this effect is the packaging parody [VIDEO] which was made by a MS team as an illustration of their frustration."},{"id":4315864,"author":"snowwrestler","month":"2012-07","story":"On Apple's new ads","stance":"reference","why":"Classic parody critiquing Microsoft's product packaging design.","quote":"This line was part of a classic critique of Microsoft's packaging design.","text":"> They could show the product in use. This line was part of a classic critique of Microsoft's packaging design. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42754695,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2025-01","story":"Microsoft just renamed Office to Microsoft 365 Copilot on Windows for everyone","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"was reminded of this classic","text":"As far as I can recall, this has been their branding strategy: 1996: Rename everything to \"MSN\" including a news TV channel 2000: Rename everything \".NET\" even if it's not a programming language 2005: Rename everything that remotely touches the internet to \"Live\" 2013: Rename everything to \"365\" 2025: Rename everything to \"Copilot\" even things that are not just an LLM Also sprinkle \"Windows\" liberally over everything in the late 90's-00's Did I miss anything? Edit: was reminded of this classic [VIDEO]"},{"id":40090194,"author":"adolph","month":"2024-04","story":"Meta.ai Oh My","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> so threw some shit on top and called it a cherry Do you mean metaphorical feces or do you mean \"shit\" in the general sense of a collection of unspecified materials? I think the canonical example of deciding chocolate wasn't good enough and throwing shit on top was what happened to the LP400 [0] or \"Microsoft Re-Designs the iPod Packaging\" [1]. 0. [LINK] 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13782463,"author":"i336_","month":"2017-03","story":"AMD to consider coreboot/Libreboot support for Ryzen","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Trustonic TEE OS I have visions of the Windows 95 3D screensaver, with the walls, floor and ceiling displaying the many, many NDAs you need to sign to get access to this. (I must admit that what initially came to mind was ~2:25 in [VIDEO] (2:55).) In all seriousness, this may be the tricky bit. AMD may need to release the PSP spec and get Libreboot to take it from there. Considering the huge amount of support for this though, I can totally see a crowdfunded firmware implementation for the PSP working out."},{"id":4249930,"author":"InclinedPlane","month":"2012-07","story":"Microsoft and NBC officially call it quits","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Microsoft is exceptionally bad at this, even if other companies are pretty bad about it too.","text":"Yes. However, all I have to say to this is \"The New iPad\". Edit: Wait, I had something else to say: [VIDEO] (Microsoft is exceptionally bad at this, even if other companies are pretty bad about it too.)"},{"id":2893421,"author":"justinsb","month":"2011-08","story":"Linux snickers at Microsoft's victory declaration","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"once every faction at Microsoft has had their say, you end up with this","text":"I've seen some of the previews. I don't put too much stock in them - Longhorn looked pretty awesome as well, until it shipped as Vista. My concern is that once every faction at Microsoft has had their say, you end up with this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["a1zDuOPkMSw",1]]},"fNpsgTIpODA":{"wy":"Commenters point to it as a clear take on why functional languages stall outside niches, and its lessons are seen as applying to any technology trying to go mainstream.","q":{"x":"although aimed at haskell programmers, is really about any technology looking to grow into the mainstream","i":28521098,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":31774803,"author":"JoelMcCracken","month":"2022-06","story":"Why Rust is a great choice for startups","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible talk on why Haskell lacks a Rails-like killer framework.","quote":"here is a talk that goes into this problem.","text":"> compelling framework Sadly no. There are contenders for this that have arisen over the years, but we don’t have a RoR for Haskell yet. If you’re curious, here is a talk that goes into this problem. It’s very accessible IMO. [VIDEO] To be honest, the compelling reason to learn Haskell for me was so I could engage with various academic literature. I just kept running into things I didn’t understand which were tangential to the topic of the paper, and it seemed like the most straightforward way to solve this was to start with learning Haskell. Once I learned it, I realized I was wrong in my biases, and Haskell is a fantastic tool for normal programming, with some important caveats."},{"id":28521098,"author":"platz","month":"2021-09","story":"Functional programming is not popular because it is weird (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on growing a technology into the mainstream.","quote":"although aimed at haskell programmers, is really about any technology looking to grow into the mainstream","text":"this is also a good talk in a similar vein, although aimed at haskell programmers, is really about any technology looking to grow into the mainstream Gabriel Gonzalez – How to market Haskell to a mainstream programmer - [VIDEO]"},{"id":24404593,"author":"platz","month":"2020-09","story":"What killed Haskell, could kill Rust, too","stance":"rec","why":"Applies Crossing the Chasm to why Haskell hasn't hit the mainstream.","quote":"this talk adopts that book to programming laguage (or tools more generally)","text":"The reason why Haskell still doesn't really break through in the corporate world is not a failure of arrogance, but a marketing failure in the strictest sense of the word 'marketing'—which means prioritizing the right features to target the right market. A large proportion of the talk linked below is based on the book “Crossing the Chasm\". The book was originally written for startups trying to break into the mainstream, and this talk adopts that book to programming laguage (or tools more generally), particularly regarding Haskell. Gabriel Gonzalez – How to market Haskell to a mainstream programmer: [VIDEO] Gabriel also suggests that in order to \"cross the chasm\" you have to offer a best-in-class experience in some area, and suggests that Haskellers adapt the language to facilitating building interpreters, which is it particularly well suited for. So, base on the above, I don't think the OP's emphasis on the emotional analysis of Haskell programmers is anything more than the OP's just-so story, although the notion of parochialism may have some crossover with the concept of marketing."},{"id":24334137,"author":"platz","month":"2020-08","story":"Motivation – Keli Language","stance":"rec","why":"Applies Crossing the Chasm to marketing Haskell to mainstream programmers.","quote":"The reason why FP still doesn't really break through in the corporate world is a marketing failure","text":"The reason why FP still doesn't really break through in the corporate world is a marketing failure, in the strictest sense of the word marketing, which means prioritizing the right features to target the right market. A large proportion of this talk is based on the book “Crossing the Chasm\". The book was originally written for startups, and this talk adopts the book to an open source audience, especially Haskell developers. Gabriel Gonzalez – How to market Haskell to a mainstream programmer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36136529,"author":"ReleaseCandidat","month":"2023-05","story":"Why your F# evangelism isn't working","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There is now (well, 2020) a Haskell version of this idea","text":"There is now (well, 2020) a Haskell version of this idea (Moore's \"Crossing the Chasm\") too, as a conference talk: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["QZy4Yml3LTY",1],["_8bhtEgB8Mo",1],["ftcIcn8AmSY",1],["2DIl3Hfh9tY",1],["Agu6jipKfYw",1],["MEUWxNifJJ8",1]]},"jmTwlEh8L7g":{"wy":"HN regulars treat Domas as a favorite hacker and bring this talk up in any discussion of CPU backdoors, hidden instruction sets or processor security.","q":{"x":"Christopher Domas is one of my absolute favorite all-time hackers. He does such a fantastic job of explaining his work.","i":49286925,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49286925,"author":"MattSteelblade","month":"2026-08","story":"Spaghettifying DRAM","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Among favorite talks by Domas, who explains his work fantastically","quote":"Christopher Domas is one of my absolute favorite all-time hackers.","text":"I cannot wait for the accompanying Black Hat talk. Christopher Domas is one of my absolute favorite all-time hackers. He does such a fantastic job of explaining his work. Some of my favorite talks of his: - Psychological Warfare in Reverse Engineering [LINK] - The MoVfuscator [LINK] - Hardware Backdoors in redacted x86 [VIDEO]"},{"id":28182090,"author":"daniel-thompson","month":"2021-08","story":"Compiling C Programs Into “Mov” Instructions (And Only “Mov” Instructions)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Spectacular talks by Christopher Domas","quote":"And yes, they are spectacular.","text":"These links don't work for me, but the videos are on YT: - [VIDEO] - [LINK] And yes, they are spectacular."},{"id":49286937,"author":"aecsocket","month":"2026-08","story":"Spaghettifying DRAM","stance":"rec","why":"Amazing x86 discoveries by Domas","quote":"being amazed at what he's been able to discover","text":"Holy shit, Christopher Domas is back. I remember watching his Defcon talks on x86 shenanigans[^1][^2] and being amazed at what he's been able to discover. Then he got whisked away by Intel and now drops this. I'm excited. [^1]: [LINK] [^2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29490992,"author":"not1ofU","month":"2021-12","story":"Intel updates mysterious ‘software-defined silicon’ code in the Linux kernel","stance":"rec","why":"One of many amazing talks by Domas before Intel hired him","quote":"This is one of many amazing videos this guy did before they hired him.","text":"This guy now works for intel: DEF CON 26 - Christopher Domas - GOD MODE UNLOCKED Hardware Backdoors in redacted x86 [VIDEO] This is one of many amazing videos this guy did before they hired him."},{"id":32398680,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Do you worry about CPU backdoors like IME?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on CPU security rings and undocumented instructions","quote":"There are more recent talks that get deeper into security rings in the CPU.","text":"What are the implications of such hardware on user security? Threat persistence, i.e. persistent rootkits, persistent remote monitoring if one has privs to tickle the CPU instructions that give access. It is just a matter of time before there are public tools for script kiddies to manage the OS within the CPU. Here [1] is an example of someone slowly making progress decoding all the undocumented instructions and here is a talk with a brief overview of the tools. [2] This is not specific to Intel's ME but it is the way people will eventually tame/exploit that beast in my opinion. There are more recent talks that get deeper into security rings in the CPU. This video [3] more related to your question but not specific to ME however can be used to access ME and much more. If this appears to difficult and time consuming just know that some folks out there have the documentation for the undocumented instructions. You may find some tools on github and other public repos for disabling ME. Use with care. Test on an identical model of system that is at the same firmware/BIOS and same model of CPU as those tools can brick your CPU. As @rocket_surgeron stated, one can buy CPU's with ME disabled but that will not disable the \"God Mode\" referenced in [3]. [1] - [LINK] [2] - [LINK] [3] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":30839159,"author":"runnerup","month":"2022-03","story":"A PCIe network interface card that adds full router capabilities to your servers","stance":"reference","why":"Prior-year talk backing up the skeptic-addressed claims","quote":"here's his talk from the next year","text":"If the downvotes are due to poor quality discussion, that's fair enough. If they're due to skepticism, here's his talk from the next year: [0]: [VIDEO] \"DEF CON 26 - Christopher Domas - GOD MODE UNLOCKED Hardware Backdoors in redacted x86\""},{"id":28402268,"author":"throwaway47292","month":"2021-09","story":"Web-based editor","stance":"reference","why":"Pointing at Domas's x86 god mode talk","quote":"","text":"haha maybe Christopher Domas knows :D [VIDEO] [x86 god mode] but there is a difference between knowing vaguely how internal combustion engine works versus treating your car as a magic box from the future. (which of course now most cars are, you can not even disable the tire pressure alert after you pump your tires without going to the service shop) I fear that nobody will even teach the fetch-execute cycle to our kids."},{"id":27347244,"author":"corebuffer","month":"2021-05","story":"Alternate Instruction Set","stance":"reference","why":"Defcon talk on the alternate instruction set in VIA CPUs","quote":"Related: a defcon talk about it","text":"Related: a defcon talk about it (DEF CON 26 - Christopher Domas - GOD MODE UNLOCKED Hardware Backdoors in redacted x86) [VIDEO] Website collecting info on thin clients, which many are VIA, if anyone gets interested in exploring it from a recycled unit: [LINK] EDIT: also [LINK] - Christopher Domas: Hardware Backdoors in X86 CPUs"},{"id":26052106,"author":"ddingus","month":"2021-02","story":"Dunfield 6809 Portable","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in a long retro computing reply","quote":"","text":"You are taking these ideas into crazy places! Fun to think about. Given this context, I have a few thoughts: Contemporary processors may be interesting when simplified some. Your projects make me think about the Zachtronics games. [LINK] That is an assembly language game with surprising depth. People, who play it seriously will learn quite a lot. Using older hardware in tandem with current is a lot of fun. The terminal, for example, still does exactly what it was designed to do and is still useful today. A C64 has a user port for this kind of thing. Apple 2 computers saw all manner of expansion cards capable of data capture, control, computation... making ones own card with a PIA was a standard type project, just as connecting a circuit to the user port was. BASIC was often enough for many basic controls, say regulating temperature, or turning things off and on based on time, or other data states, assembly language brought more of the speed possible, and with more speed, we get more posibilities. Take one bit wired to a speaker. That is what the Apple and IBM PC shipped with to make sound. The PC had a spiffy timer to bang out notes, and the Apple had nothing but a toggle. Speaker on, or off. Here is where the fun part was back in the day: At first, it seems useless. Access an address and the speaker pops out. Do it again, and the speaker pops in. One can type this right into the computer with a BASIC POKE statement, or the monitor by address and watch the speaker change stat"},{"id":20234384,"author":"kbenson","month":"2019-06","story":"Everybody Be Cool, This Is a Robbery","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on vulnerability through additional onboard processors","quote":"two Defcon talks I watched in the last day or two","text":"While somewhat interesting, I'm not sure there's much to discuss unless the presentation or details are already available. Interestingly, this reminds me of two Defcon talks I watched in the last day or two, which also cover vulnerability through additional processors onboard.[1][2] 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"}],"r":[["XH0F9r0siTI",2],["HlUe0TUHOIc",1],["R7EEoWg6Ekk",1]]},"k6U-i4gXkLM":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a simple, direct entry point for beginners who want to learn computer science, not just programming.","q":{"x":"Professor keeps everything simple and direct because this class is designed for beginners with no or little programming experience.","i":2457355,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":4766690,"author":"mokash","month":"2012-11","story":"Ask HN: How would a high schooler find CS mentor?","stance":"rec","why":"One resource among many for a high schooler to learn CS","quote":"Check this video out.","text":"Make the entire Internet your mentor. It's filled with information and answers to all sorts of questions. Look at some programming tutorials such as codecademy. Maybe find some books on computer architecture, learn about mathematics that might apply to computer scientists. Here are some links to get you started. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Also, learn to use YouTube to search for answers. Check this video out. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2718793,"author":"jgrahamc","month":"2011-07","story":"Khan Academy Computer Science","stance":"rec","why":"MIT intro course teaching computer science, not just programming","quote":"I've really enjoyed watching the MIT 6.00 course","text":"I've really enjoyed watching the MIT 6.00 course (Introduction to Computer Science and Programming): [VIDEO] The Khan videos seem to be about programming instead of computer science."},{"id":2457355,"author":"rblion","month":"2011-04","story":"How can I learn to program? ","stance":"rec","why":"Simple, direct MIT intro course for beginners","quote":"Professor keeps everything simple and direct because this class is designed for beginners with no or little programming experience.","text":"Here is an MIT Course: Introduction To CS and Programming [VIDEO] Professor keeps everything simple and direct because this class is designed for beginners with no or little programming experience."},{"id":1520426,"author":"bdlang","month":"2010-07","story":"A Gentle Introduction to Programming Using Python","stance":"rec","why":"First lecture of a good CS course that uses Python","quote":"I should mention this is a really good (IMHO) CS course that happens to use Python.","text":"Here's the MIT OCW index for EE and CS courses: [LINK] Here's the MIT OCW index for 6.00 w/ Grimson (2008): [LINK] Here's the first video lecture on YouTube: [VIDEO] I should mention this is a really good (IMHO) CS course that happens to use Python."}]},"x5Efg42-Qn0":{"wy":"HN commenters credit it as a life-changing talk, with some reporting reduced prescriptions after following the method.","q":{"x":"I'm super thankful to have found out about this life-changing video by Todd Becker on how to reverse myopia","i":38792338,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38792338,"author":"blacksoil","month":"2023-12","story":"High myopia is now the leading cause of blindness in Japan, China, and Taiwan","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reverse myopia via reduced prescription and eye-focus exercises","quote":"I'm super thankful to have found out about this life-changing video by Todd Becker on how to reverse myopia","text":"The TLDR is that a lot of people have very high myopia that causes severe eyeball elongation, which then causes retinal damage/detachment, which sadly could progress into blindness.. I myself was -5.75 and -6.25 with some non-negligible cylinders. I experienced discomfort when looking at my phone phone and reading book with my glasses on. I General check up said that my eyeball pressure and all were normal, but I know the discomfort is nothing good and can be quite serious. I read up online and found out that myopia could cause eyeball elongation, which could cause discomfort and ultimately could cause retinal damage/detachment. The discomfort I was experiencing was probably due to my elongated eyeball. I prayed and began looking for way to work around my myopic eyes without having to get yet another thicker glasses... And I'm super thankful to have found out about this life-changing video by Todd Becker on how to reverse myopia: [VIDEO] . The TLDR is that eyeball elongation (and hence myopic eyes) can be reversed in a way very similar to how strength training is conducted (hormesis). You simply need to keep wearing reduced prescription and do eye-focus exercises regularly. It sounds too good to be true, doesn't it? I was scared too at first, and when I went to a glasses store to get a lower prescription glasses, the store attendant warned me too. But I went ahead anyway. My latest eyes exam before the attempt (back in August 2021) was -6.75 and -6.5 with -0.25 and -0.5 cyls."},{"id":32647342,"author":"blacksoil","month":"2022-08","story":"Show HN: I built a tool to help you read Hacker News on Kindle","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Method that reduced prescription by 1.5","quote":"Todd Becker's method to reduce myopia? I've been doing it in the past 1 year and am happy to report that I've had 1.5 reduction in my glasses prescription","text":"[VIDEO] Are you aware of Todd Becker's method to reduce myopia? I've been doing it in the past 1 year and am happy to report that I've had 1.5 reduction in my glasses prescription (from 6.75). There's a PDF from a different person with a concrete guide on how much to reduce. I can share it if you're interested, feel free to email me at denny at nusantara-cloud.com :)"},{"id":45401909,"author":"rramadass","month":"2025-09","story":"The doctor who says we can improve our vision – at any age","stance":"rec","why":"Explains using hormesis to cure myopia","quote":"This explains how to use Hormesis ( ) to cure myopia.","text":"Important article particularly for us folks who sit in front of Computers for most of our waking hours. Key points: he says that working on our vision has the power to transform how we think, feel and function. In fact, our vision is so fundamental to our health and wellbeing, Appelbaum describes it as “the new microbiome”. “But vision is so much more complex. It’s how our brain processes, organises and stores the information coming through the eyes.” He adds: “Vision is a direct reflection of brain function.” We have to focus on the Whole Organism rather than just the Vision part i.e. stop overloading the Visual System, sync our Circadian Rhythms with the day/night diurnal cycle and understand/use Visual Technology (eg. Nits) properly. Some relevant articles/videos; 1) Myopia: A Modern Yet Reversible Disease by Todd Becker - [VIDEO] This explains how to use Hormesis ( [LINK] ) to cure myopia. 2) How Hormesis Works: The Biology of Beneficial Adaptation to Stress by Todd Becker - [LINK] 3) How optimizing Circadian Rhythms can increase Healthy Years by Sachin Panda - [LINK] Gives a practical 6-step process to sync our circadian rhythms; the visual system is key here. 4) The Circadian Code by Sachin Panda - [LINK] and [LINK] 5) Brightness levels & Nits explained - [LINK]"},{"id":32647380,"author":"blacksoil","month":"2022-08","story":"Show HN: I built a tool to help you read Hacker News on Kindle","stance":"rec","why":"Non-surgical method to cure myopia","quote":"A year ago I came across Todd Becker's method to cure myopia here from HN as well.","text":"AFAIK, the way LASIK works is by cutting down part of the lens, which essentially is to reshape the eye to compensate for the elongated eye ball. Given it's an invasive surgery, personally I'm not too comfortable. A friend of mine also shared that his girlfriend can't no longer drive at night after having a LASIK surgery due to the glare and halo effect. A year ago I came across Todd Becker's method to cure myopia here from HN as well. In case interested to check it out: [VIDEO] There's also a PDF from a different guy with some practical guide. Feel free to email me at denny at nusantara-cloud.com if you want me to share it."},{"id":28001380,"author":"kkoncevicius","month":"2021-07","story":"Myopia: A Modern yet Reversible Disease","stance":"reference","why":"Points to a sceptical audience comment in the talk","quote":"There is a sceptical comment from the audience at 36:50 [1].","text":"There is a sceptical comment from the audience at 36:50 [1]. Maybe someone here has the knowledge to comment about that? Is it true what the person says - that the actual problems with the eyes won't be fixed, you would just get better quality vision through some other way doing this? [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["n8Dd5aVXLCc",1],["144uOfr4SYA",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["ffkIQrq-m34",1],["GcJxRqTs5nk",1],["pQ0CvjAJXz4",1],["yErKTVdETpw",1],["f7CW7S0zxv4",1],["A-fxij3zM7g",1]]},"5Vt8zqhHe_c":{"wy":"HN regards it as an inspiring, funny case for modest, bootstrapped success over venture-funded risk.","q":{"x":"Which I thought was amazing and inspiring.","i":24562684,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24562684,"author":"jimbokun","month":"2020-09","story":"Small Tech","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing and inspiring talk.","quote":"Which I thought was amazing and inspiring.","text":"That’s a shame, because the article led me to listen to this talk: [VIDEO] Which I thought was amazing and inspiring."},{"id":25250319,"author":"totemandtoken","month":"2020-11","story":"How to Run a Ponzi Scheme for Tech People","stance":"rec","why":"Related talk from the Pinboard founder.","quote":"Reminds me a lot of this talk from the Pinboard guy","text":"Reminds me a lot of this talk from the Pinboard guy: [VIDEO] Or this talk from the Bandcamp guy about financial sustainability: [LINK]"},{"id":10267556,"author":"kbenson","month":"2015-09","story":"What Happens Next Will Amaze You","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk by Maciej Ceglowski on succeeding modestly.","quote":"Barely succeed! It's easier! [2] (which I saw within the last couple of days) are quite good","text":"I agree. In general, I found this presentation to ramble far more and make weird connections than some of his other ones, which I remember liking. Presentations and talks such as Web Design: The first 100 years [1] (even if it was a while back and I don't remember it all that clearly), and Barely succeed! It's easier! [2] (which I saw within the last couple of days) are quite good (if memory serves). 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11443434,"author":"idlewords","month":"2016-04","story":"Apply HN: Pinboard – Make Y Combinator Great Again","stance":"reference","why":"The author's own talk making the case for modest success.","quote":"I gave a talk called \"Barely Succeed - it's easier!\" where I tried to make the case.","text":"I gave a talk called \"Barely Succeed - it's easier!\" where I tried to make the case. There's a video version here [ [VIDEO] ], unfortunately I haven't transcribed it yet."},{"id":12004803,"author":"xyience","month":"2016-06","story":"The Moral Economy of Tech","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I realized he is a brilliant comedian, but also pretty far left","text":"There's a point in this talk [VIDEO] where I realized he is a brilliant comedian, but also pretty far left, and a lot of his funny criticisms just boil down to criticizing for not being as far left as he is or wants you to be. So I don't think his mind is being poisoned -- it's already poisoned, from the point of view of someone not as far left. ;) I think a lot of tech people lean libertarian, which is a strange double-think ground of left and right views, because the role of a technologist has inherently anti-left and anti-right components. Technology is upstream of culture, upstream of society, to work on it implies defying authority whether in the form of a single sovereign or public opinion. While much of the world is still the same, much has been remade according to man's desire, through technology, and there is still much more to be remade. And often not a collective mankind, but an individual, often male, or a small group of individuals, often male. That kind of influence is inherently anti-democratic, anti-egalitarian, but anti-authoritarian as it is highly individualistic. Yet its benefits can be made private (until someone else figures out how to do the same, anyway) or open to all according to the whim of the creator, because it's fundamentally just knowledge that once derived can be shared endlessly. The comments about techies not going out of their domain to try and solve things is just nonsense, as cross domain work happens all the time. Applied technology is o"}],"r":[["nXaxk27zwlk",1],["coFIEH3vXPw",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["9QMGAtxUlAc",1],["79fvDDPaIoY",1],["YKuRkGkf5HU",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["uKfKtXYLG78",1]]},"5gnpCqsXE8g":{"wy":"HN commenters link it to argue that cultural subversion and normalization are playing out today, though mostly as a reference in unrelated threads.","q":{"x":"One of the most important videos on the entire Internet","i":32263238,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32263238,"author":"MikeAshley178","month":"2022-07","story":"West London faces new home ban as electric grid at capacity due to data centres","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Considered extremely important by the commenter","quote":"One of the most important videos on the entire Internet","text":"Shaving a few minutes off your shower, walking to the shop instead of driving, turning your thermostat down by a few degrees will make no f@@kin difference what so ever to the horror that is coming. The whole point of these exercises is not to save energy but to engage the population to believe that they are part of the problem and therefore part of the solution. They are not. One of the most important videos on the entire Internet, if you cannot understand why then you are probably already lost. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40339051,"author":"coretx","month":"2024-05","story":"Scott Galloway: How the US is destroying young people's future [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Cold war video on subversion the commenter sees repeating","quote":"The following ancient cold war era video will probably make you understand what i mean and more","text":"The following ancient cold war era video will probably make you understand what i mean and more: [VIDEO] History is repeating itself."},{"id":13758504,"author":"returnbuyer","month":"2017-02","story":"Ask HN: How would you turn Twitter around?","stance":"rec","why":"Bezmenov's account of cultural subversion","quote":"Listen to some Yuri Besmenov [1] if you don't know what I'm talking about.","text":"They're not weakening twitter by weakening western society. If anything, weakening the society with divisive gender war and identify politics empowers twitter. The western economy suffers and more people become reclusive and stay inside all day on the internet. It's basic culture warfare and it's been happening for decades. Listen to some Yuri Besmenov [1] if you don't know what I'm talking about. Basically my point is they don't care, because any division in their enemies is a good thing for them. While they retain immense strength and nationalism. I'll give you an example of poisoning the well. So many dual-citizen self admitted Zionists in America and Europe are pro-refugee. They adamantly fight for more refugees in these host countries. However their home country, Israel. Is entirely anti-refugee to a shocking degree. It's all part of the same pie. Marxism, communism, feminism, egalitarianism. Destruction of western civilization. [1] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":32553906,"author":"moneytide1","month":"2022-08","story":"To Become Wise, Do Less","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> What could be more \"leisurely\" than religion, that which is, after all, concerned with the ultimate end and the highest good? \"... blindly raging industriousness brings riches and honour but at the same time deprives the organs of refinement that make it possible to enjoy the riches and honour; also, that this chief antidote to boredom and to the passions at the same time dulls the senses [0] and makes the spirit resistant to new attractions. (The most industrious age - our own - doesn't know how to make anything of all its industriousness and money except still more money and still more industriousness, for more genius is required to spend than to acquire! Well, we'll still have our 'grandchildren'!) If education is successful, each virtue of the individual is a public utility and a private disadvantage with respect to the highest private end - probably involving some deterioration of the spirit and the senses or even a premature demise: consider from this standpoint the virtues of obedience, chastity, piety, and justice.\" Friedrich Nietzsche, The Gay Science, 21. To the teachers of selflessness The title of the article describes the merit of deliberate inaction to gain the virtue of wisdom. Wikipedia (universal standardized knowledge database edited by verified \"wise\" people?) defines it as the \"ability to contemplate and act using knowledge, experience, understanding, common sense and insight.\" I agree with the author, Jideofor Onwugbenu, that there is utility in the pen"},{"id":32553913,"author":"moneytide1","month":"2022-08","story":"To Become Wise, Do Less","stance":"reference","why":"Bezmenov's description of the normalization stage","quote":"","text":"We can assume that as the dust settles in the 2090s, when the self-sterilized crusaders die off without surviving children, we can write it off as the necessary loss of the gullible feeble minds that the state failed to educate. To mitigate this loss means keeping them in a productive career but nothing politically influential if we are to contain the spread. Keep a watchful eye out for the \"Chuck&Larry\" tax dollar distribution fraud which was intended to reward the child-rearing capacity of the newly minted loyal family. \"... Normalization. At that stage, the self-appointed rulers of the society don't need revolution anymore... Stabilizing the country by force. So all the sleepers, activists, social workers, liberals, homosexuals, professors, and Marxist/Leninists are being eliminated. Physically sometimes. They've done their job already, the are not needed anymore. The new rulers need stability to exploit the nation, to take advantage of the victory.\" [15] Prevention is possible via fellowship, but their \"freedoms\" are the same as yours, which allows you to more easily identify these casualties of the lawless information distribution avalanche. We are \"redirecting the blow\" in our own way by allowing the termination of susceptible lineages in humane fashion to create \"lebensraum\" for the citizens who understand that strong family means the human duty to yield/educate/endow your replacements to carry on the mission because \"non est ad astra mollis e terris via\". \". . . so th"},{"id":13604212,"author":"ideamonk","month":"2017-02","story":"HyperNormalisation (2016) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Bezmenov discusses normalisation in context of subversion","quote":"KGB propagandist Yuri Bezmenov talks about normalisation in context of subversion","text":"From history - KGB propagandist Yuri Bezmenov talks about normalisation in context of subversion - [VIDEO] (1983, LA)"}]},"VFFlO7yBIBM":{"wy":"People recommend it as a rare, unpolished look at Gates's mid-90s thinking, including predictions that aged well and badly.","q":{"x":"I strongly recommend watching the impromptu interview Bill Gates gave back in the 90s while visiting the Wired office","i":24104041,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24104041,"author":"pengaru","month":"2020-08","story":"Interview with Bill Gates on Covid, social media","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gates' 1990s thinking, including predictions that aged badly","quote":"I strongly recommend watching the impromptu interview Bill Gates gave back in the 90s while visiting the Wired office","text":"I strongly recommend watching the impromptu interview Bill Gates gave back in the 90s while visiting the Wired office, for gaining some valuable perspective on his thinking, and how comically wrong he's been before. My internet is too flaky here at the moment to verify, but I believe this is the URL: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43137109,"author":"antaviana","month":"2025-02","story":"20 years working on the same software product","stance":"rec","why":"Gates describing software products as subscriptions in 1996","quote":"a long but interesting interview","text":"I believe it is in this interview from 1996 where I saw Bill Gates saying the same thing about Microsoft products, he saw them as subscriptions. It’s a long but interesting interview. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24870901,"author":"sramam","month":"2020-10","story":"How Satya Nadella turned Microsoft around","stance":"rec","why":"Shows structured thinking at Microsoft and Gates in 1996","quote":"provides an insight into the structured thinking at Microsoft/Gates even back in 1996","text":"This. This [1] wired interview provides an insight into the structured thinking at Microsoft/Gates even back in 1996. I'm sure this kind of thinking permeates any successful company, to have a video of this depth, and now available for public viewing, is really unique. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JFaOYYSxSEA",1],["c2lgEyNaop4",1],["X29lF43mUlo",1],["Ja5Q75hf6QI",1],["7xTGNNLPyMI",1],["jK0BkTPUGQY",1],["tzr7hRXcwkw",1],["RKT-sKtR970",1]]},"iG9CE55wbtY":{"wy":"HN refers to it constantly in schooling debates, as a favorite talk or as the basis for arguments about creativity, though some commenters dispute its conclusions.","q":{"x":"I love this Ted talk","i":33320635,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33320635,"author":"robomartin","month":"2022-10","story":"Colleges that ditched admission tests find it harder to fairly choose students","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues for letting kids excel at what drives them.","quote":"I love this Ted talk","text":"Lots of interesting elements in your comment. > Even with more resources, as long as we're going to insist on mainstreaming students with behavioral problems or who just don't care about the educational process, outcomes for the other students in the rooms are going to be affected. The insistence on equality at all levels and all things is a problem. I know there are people reading this comment thinking \"this guy is a bigot\". If you are that person, please stop, calm down and think. Everyone understands that there are people who will never been competitive swimmers, basketball players or good dancers. While I am not terrible at sports, I could not become one of those three, no matter what I do. One of my kids will never be an artists. Just not interested, at all. Another cannot think about anything else. Why is it that we want to shove everyone into the same mold when it comes to intellectual capacity, IQ, etc.? Should I force one of my kids into art class and the other into engineering? No. That would be insane. We don't all have to be engineers. It would be a really sad planet if that were the case. It is not bigoted to suggest we need to allow kids to excel at what really drives them. Expose them to a range of topics, of course. Don't torture them by forcing them into a mold. Should a kid who is an artist not graduate high school if they don't pass algebra, physics and calculus? Why? I love this Ted talk: [VIDEO] The whole thing is interesting, however the story that start"},{"id":41932946,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2024-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite YouTube video, ever?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed as a favorite YouTube video.","quote":"Sir Ken Robinson: Do Schools Kill Creativity","text":"Russ Olsen, author of Getting Closure, Eloquent Ruby and Design Patterns with Ruby, telling the story of the moon landing as a conference talk. Inspired me beyond measure and brought tears to my eyes. [LINK] —- George Lakoff’s lecture on Moral Politics. Instantaneous understanding of political language and identity politics. Incredibly important, especially today in time of extreme polarization. [LINK] —- Sir Ken Robinson: Do Schools Kill Creativity [VIDEO]"},{"id":31545285,"author":"johnisgood","month":"2022-05","story":"My students cheated... a lot","stance":"rec","why":"Robinson argues the education paradigm should change and how.","quote":"Sir Ken Robinson has a lot to say about the education paradigm and why it should be changed","text":"Quite sad that nothing came out of [LINK] . Sir Ken Robinson has a lot to say about the education paradigm and why it should be changed, and in what ways. He appeared on TED[1][2], too, since then, and probably elsewhere as well. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44157511,"author":"repeekad","month":"2025-06","story":"If you are useful, it doesn't mean you are valued","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in relation to ADHD not equaling stupidity.","quote":"I think of Sir Ken Robinson whenever I hear anyone say ADHD is synonymous with “stupid","text":"Or you’re clever and lazy but haven’t found the right general to promote you to high leadership yet. I think of Sir Ken Robinson whenever I hear anyone say ADHD is synonymous with “stupid” [VIDEO]"},{"id":35968133,"author":"robomartin","month":"2023-05","story":"U.S. Marshals Spied on Abortion Protesters Using Dataminr","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as background to a point on schooling's shortcomings.","quote":"","text":"> High schools can't really do that, unless they are structured more like universities. That's not true. There's a very large gap between graduating 18 year old's who are only good for box-stacking, making coffee or going to college (with a lot of help in all cases) and actually graduating people who can deliver value to an employer and is culturally ready for society. Take critical thinking as an easy example. Or logic, philosophy, art, music, trades and other important areas that would benefit society if exposed during the K-12 period. I say this both as a parent and an employer. The average high school graduate is useless to me, in almost any capacity. The average college graduate isn't very far from that. This isn't new, it has been discussed here on HN many times in the past. Small to medium companies don't have the time or resources to engage in education after education. This is where this concept of wasting a year of a student's time with coursework nobody cares about is very relevant. As for my own kids, I made sure to heavily supplement their US high school education with learning that made them valuable upon graduation. A simple result from this was landing knowledge-based jobs making more than $35/hr right out of high school, when their peers where struggling with minimum wage work almost throughout their university experience. As a result of that, my oldest had a solid financial footing when he was done with university. All of this was due to education prior to c"},{"id":22246497,"author":"grabbalacious","month":"2020-02","story":"What's Gone Wrong in Schooling?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the view that school hurts creativity and curiosity-led learning.","quote":"Most thoughtful people seem to agree that school is bad for creativity","text":"Most thoughtful people seem to agree that school is bad for creativity and that real learning is led by curiosity. [VIDEO] But we still have little idea how curiosity works exactly or how an institution could be turned from an exam factory into something a bit closer to a child's bedroom and computer at home, i.e. to a place of play. For most of life we need permission to play. Parents allocate 'screen time'. At university one is effectively granted permission to do 'curiosity-led' research by an external grant agency. So what does institutionalised play look like? Perhaps something like a university. Yet how much could university life be said to be 'following the fun'? [LINK]"},{"id":18016519,"author":"kozhevnikov","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: How can I get my children excited about programming?","stance":"reference","why":"Cites the Gillian Lynne story from the most popular TED talk.","quote":"Ken Robinson telling the story of Gillian Lynne","text":"Reminded me of Ken Robinson telling the story of Gillian Lynne [1] in the most popular Ted Talk \"Do schools kill creativity?\" [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16840041,"author":"a_d","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"reference","why":"One entry in a list of favorite talks.","quote":"Ken Robinson - Do schools kill creativity?","text":"Bret Victor - Inventing on principle [LINK] Alan Kay - Power of Simplicity [LINK] Steve Jobs - Marketing (unveiling Think Different) [LINK] Robert Cialdini - On Influence [LINK] Peter Thiel - On Zero to One (Notes on Starts Ups or How to Build the Future) [LINK] Naval Ravikant on Reading, Happiness, Systems for Decision Making, Habits, Honesty and More (Farnam Street podcast) [LINK] Josh Wolfe - On \"This is who you are up against\" podcast [LINK] Richard Feynman - Fun to Imagine [LINK] Paul Graham - Startup school (2008) [LINK] James Burke - Connections (BBC documentary) <No link> Jim Al-Khalili tells the story of the Atom (BBC) [LINK] Elon Musk's 2003 Stanford University Entrepreneurial Thought Leaders Lecture [LINK] Ken Robinson - Do schools kill creativity? [VIDEO] Paul Stamets on mycology, bioremediation and fungi- Joe Rogan Experience [LINK]"},{"id":15177194,"author":"roceasta","month":"2017-09","story":"Five-Year-Olds Can Learn Calculus (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Linked to support the claim that coercive schooling undermines creativity.","quote":"Assuming their creativity is relatively intact...","text":"Have you noticed that most adults don't know what nine times seven is? If you ask them on the street they'll mostly either get it wrong or tell you quite rudely to go away. This is despite (and because of) a decade of grind at school. Or have you noticed that people are unable to hold a conversation in a foreign language despite years of 'study' in a classroom? Whereas an enthusiastic individual can pick up basic conversation for themselves in a matter of months. This is because coercion in education is: (1) painful, (2) prophetic, (3) it doesn't work! What happens in real life is that if somebody needs to know something because they are keen to do something else then they will learn it efficiently, 'just in time' and precisely to the extent needed. Assuming their creativity is relatively intact... [VIDEO]"},{"id":39666740,"author":"perilunar","month":"2024-03","story":"Profession by Isaac Asimov (1957)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I'm not convinced either","text":"I'm not convinced either: Do schools kill creativity? | Sir Ken Robinson | TED [VIDEO] (edit: apparently it's the most watched TED Talk of all time)"},{"id":30242116,"author":"bambax","month":"2022-02","story":"What Was the Ted Talk?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"But really? Isn't the logical conclusion the opposite?","text":"> Among the first talks posted was Sir Ken Robinson’s “Do Schools Kill Creativity?,” which has since become the most viewed TED talk of all time Here's the video: [VIDEO] At the 7'15 point, the speaker makes a big joke about Shakespeare \"being seven, being in someone's English class\" and the audience rolls over with laughter -- the joke being, of course, that it's ludicrous to think anyone ever tried to teach English to the greatest English writer of all time. The speaker continues with the same idea and same joke about Shakespeare's father. But really? Isn't the logical conclusion the opposite? That Shakespeare's English teacher was the best English teacher there ever was, because (he) helped him grow into the author we know now? That his father, or his mother, or someone, probably helped him? Instead we have this idea that Shakespeare, like Jesus, was born perfect and that what he had to do was overcome the stupidity and shortsightedness (and meanness) of everyone around him, until he could explode and really be himself. This idea is a tale for little kids. But it's not just absurd, it's destructive."}],"r":[["H9B5mYfBPlY",1],["SWEts0rlezA",1],["8bZh5LMaSmE",1],["PY9DcIMGxMs",1],["o2_SZ4tfLns",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["-FENubmZGj8",1],["mf5otGNbkuc",1],["RuOvn4UqznU",1],["Unzc731iCUY",1],["nv7-YM4wno8",1],["f_0QlhYlS8g",1]]},"tcTZvNLL0-w":{"wy":"Recommended in Mars-mission threads as an accessible account of Zubrin's argument that Mars is feasible within a decade.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this documentary","i":10398374,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10398374,"author":"p4bl0","month":"2015-10","story":"To save on weight, a detour to the moon is the best route to Mars","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary arguing Mars is feasible in ten years with current tech.","quote":"I highly recommend this documentary","text":"If you have one hour if front of you, I highly recommend this documentary: [VIDEO] In it they explain how it would be feasible to be on Mars in ten years with current technology and not so much additional fundings."},{"id":8959427,"author":"Udo","month":"2015-01","story":"The Economic Viability of Mars Colonization (1995) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on Zubrin's Mars work over the years.","quote":"Also worth mentioning is the Mars Underground documentary, detailing Zubrin's work and mission over the years","text":"Also worth mentioning is the Mars Underground documentary, detailing Zubrin's work and mission over the years: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8689149,"author":"skriticos2","month":"2014-12","story":"NASA: We're sending humans to Mars","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on Mars Direct and NASA's bloated Mars program.","quote":"There is a free movie on YouTube telling the tale","text":"Sometime in the past fifty years NASA became paralyzed not by budget cuts, but by political in-fighting, fractured organizational structure and lots and lots of red tape. And then there is all that pork that goes around with the entrenched contractors. The tale of the last Mars program that NASA put together is a great example of this. They got handed a technically sound plan to get there in reasonable time with reasonable resources. Then every org-unit in NASA wanted to add their own part to stay relevant and it got so bloated both technically and financially that it had to be put to rest. So unless they convince me that they managed to get their internal structural problems under control, I don't think they get anytime to Mars within this century. And certainly not before the private sector does. [LINK] p.s.: There is a free movie on YouTube telling the tale: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10363181,"author":"devy","month":"2015-10","story":"Journey to Mars: Pioneering Next Steps in Space Exploration [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as prior Mars plan by Zubrin.","quote":"","text":"Not impressed. Considering Dr. Robert Zubrin's Mars Direct plan and later revised Mars Semi-Direct plan announced in the early 90s even with the space technologies back then. [1][2][3][4] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["X-iSQQgOd1A",1]]},"zZ_nI9E9g0I":{"wy":"Recommended to Haskell learners as a rare example of building a real program step by step beyond toy examples.","q":{"x":"the Jekor Redo [1] videos are fantastic, and demonstrate the slow building up of more complicated things than \"reverse this string.","i":7199464,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7199464,"author":"traviscj","month":"2014-02","story":"A Lazy Evaluation – Why I Find Learning Haskell Hard","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Builds up complex Haskell programs slowly beyond toy examples.","quote":"the Jekor Redo [1] videos are fantastic, and demonstrate the slow building up of more complicated things than \"reverse this string.","text":"I haven't ever done anything too significant in Haskell, but the Jekor Redo [1] videos are fantastic, and demonstrate the slow building up of more complicated things than \"reverse this string.\" I haven't watched his xmonad [2] or pandoc [3] videos yet, but I would bet that they are highly worthwhile as well. - 1: [VIDEO] - 2: [LINK] - 3: [LINK]"},{"id":29720034,"author":"mavelikara","month":"2021-12","story":"Redo: A recursive, general-purpose build system","stance":"rec","why":"Live coding of djb's redo in Haskell, good for learning Haskell.","quote":"I recommended it for anyone wanting to learn Haskell.","text":"Chris Forno (jekor) made an implementation of djb's redo in Haskell. The videos of him live coding it are on YouTube, and I recommended it for anyone wanting to learn Haskell. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8156829,"author":"minikomi","month":"2014-08","story":"Getting It Done with Haskell","stance":"rec","why":"Screencast implementing redo in Haskell, helpful for making real things.","quote":"There's some great screncasts though which have been helpful.","text":"There's definitely a stage when you want to graduate from bottom-up tinkering & learning the underlying concepts to making things -- this is where I feel there's a big gap in the haskell materials right now. There's some great screncasts though which have been helpful. [LINK] - Implement a sed clone in Haskell (a bit broken towards the end but a great look at a small incremental project) [VIDEO] - implementing redo in Haskell"}]},"5cWck_xcH64":{"wy":"HN points to it when discussing exposed IoT and industrial devices, as an eye-opening look at how much is reachable online.","q":{"x":"Warning - watching this may readjust your faith in humanity.","i":14246906,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":20195802,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2019-06","story":"The NYC subway system runs on OS/2","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on retrofitted old systems ending up on the internet.","quote":"A great presentation on this (and many other things) here","text":"The answer is: quite probably. A lot of old systems get retrofitted with additional features/terminals/addons on top to make them \"more modern\" and as a result end up being connected to the internet. A great presentation on this (and many other things) here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14246906,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2017-05","story":"Vigilantes are working hard to infect IoT devices with the Hajime botnet","stance":"rec","why":"Eye-opening on exposed telnet devices like stoplights.","quote":"Warning - watching this may readjust your faith in humanity.","text":"Defcon 20 - Dan Tentler - \"Drinking from the caffeine firehose we know as shodan\" [VIDEO] Warning - watching this may readjust your faith in humanity. Telnet is just the tip of the iceberg. edit: (15:42) \"Econolite [stoplights] listening on telnet. There are intersections [with] stoplights; you can telnet into them and put them in 'test mode'[sic], and the warning says, 'Don't do this, you could kill people!'\""},{"id":7670503,"author":"jrvarela56","month":"2014-04","story":"Hacker taps into baby monitor, shouts at sleeping infant","stance":"rec","why":"Shows magnitude of exposed vulnerable devices needing no credentials.","quote":"Check out this great talk given at Defcon","text":"It's both amazing and scary that this kind of vulnerability is fairly common. Check out this great talk given at Defcon ( [VIDEO] ) to get an idea of the magnitude. These are systems that do not require any kind of tampering or credentials. Most times credentials are given to you in a prompt!"},{"id":21822811,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-12","story":"Reject Google Nest or Amazon Ring Corp Surveillance and a Dystopic Future","stance":"reference","why":"People browse exposed cameras online for fun.","quote":"Some people simply think browsing cameras on the internet is fun.","text":"> Doorbell cameras aren't compelling targets for hackers They will be when people discover creative new ways to abuse them, but it doesn't even needs to be a \"compelling target\". Some people simply think browsing cameras on the internet is fun. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10959256,"author":"saboot","month":"2016-01","story":"Internet of Things security is so bad, there’s a search engine for sleeping kids","stance":"reference","why":"Shows non-internet devices like stoplights and power plants exposed.","quote":"Some devices which were never meant to be connected to the internet are out there.","text":"It's much worse than just passive webcams. Some devices which were never meant to be connected to the internet are out there. Stoplights? HVAC Systems? Carwashes? Ice Rinks? POWER PLANTS? Yes! [VIDEO] EDIT: I looked at his more recent talk from last November ... the situation has not improved \"115 batshit stupid things you can put on the internet in as fast as I can go by Dan Tentler\" [LINK] Featuring Spanish Chicken Controls"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"QTJRwKOFddc":{"wy":"Commenters link it as a demo of what a live, inspectable, Smalltalk-style environment in the browser can be, in threads about Alan Kay, Smalltalk and web limitations.","q":{"x":"Dan Ingalls himself presenting his continued work in the browser at JSConf US 2012","i":21046473,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":47098587,"author":"morphle","month":"2026-02","story":"Show HN: A native macOS client for Hacker News, built with SwiftUI","stance":"rec","why":"Ingalls demo of fixing the browser's limitations with the Lively Kernel","quote":"Two demos says it all","text":"I will not invest any time in improving badly designed software. You can't fix a broken wheel. Your HN newsreader app tries to improve the broken wheel. The least you could have done is make the comment edit field WYSIWYG, make it modeless, see what the text will look like while you type, not after you click update or when you click edit in the tread reader. Your code is just a very limited webbrowser. The webbrowsers, html are a very broken wheel. Alan Kay, the designer of personal computing, explains why [LINK] This lecture Alan aimed at this audience, the computer science (programming) students at University of Illinois, where they programmed this broken wheel 20 years after Alan had showed them how do do it better. Paul Graham should not have based HN (Hacker News) on the web and html but on WYSIWYG, then you would not have had to fix it with your app. The Lively Kernel would be another way to fix html but retain the web. Two demos says it all: [LINK] [VIDEO] Dan Ingalls implemented most of Alan Kay's invention of the personal computer, in these demo's he shows how to fix the webbrowsers broken wheel a bit. Their Squeak, Etoys and Croquet fixed it completely."},{"id":47101854,"author":"morphle","month":"2026-02","story":"CERN rebuilt the original browser from 1989","stance":"rec","why":"Ingalls demo of fixing the browser's limitations with the Lively Kernel","quote":"Two demos says it all","text":"You can't fix a broken wheel. This web link post, the original NEXT webbrowser, tries to celebrate and revive the reinvention of the broken wheel. The World Wide Web, browser and html standards are a very broken wheel. Alan Kay, the inventor of personal computing, explains why: [LINK] Some of the comments of youtube are fun too. This lecture Alan aimed at this particular audience, the computer science (programming) students at University of Illinois, where they programmed the second browser, the second broken wheel 20 years after Alan and Dan had showed them how do do it better. Dan Ingalls implemented most of Alan Kay's invention of the personal computer, in the following demo's he shows how to fix the webbrowser's broken wheel a bit. The Lively Kernel would be another way to fix html but retain the web. Two demos says it all: [LINK] [VIDEO] Their Squeak, Etoys and Croquet fixed it completely: Early Croquet demo: [LINK] Croquet in webbrowser: [LINK] Demo of webbrowser replacement: [LINK] Squeak and all its predecessors: [LINK] Etoys: [LINK]"},{"id":21046473,"author":"apaprocki","month":"2019-09","story":"Smalltalk-72 Instruction Manual (1976) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Dan Ingalls presenting his browser-based live environment at JSConf 2012","quote":"Dan Ingalls himself presenting his continued work in the browser at JSConf US 2012","text":"Dan Ingalls himself presenting his continued work in the browser at JSConf US 2012 (whole conf was packed in, standing-room only): [VIDEO] Surprised that video has so few views!"},{"id":11965253,"author":"mmiller","month":"2016-06","story":"Alan Kay has agreed to do an AMA today","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of the Lively Kernel live web environment.","quote":"If you want a demo, you can take a look here","text":"It's difficult at this point to come up with an example, since we don't have it yet (that I know of), but to give you an idea, take a look at Lively Kernel [LINK] If you want a demo, you can take a look here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35744933,"author":"JHonaker","month":"2023-04","story":"Plan 9 from User Space","stance":"reference","why":"Shows live inspectable Morphic-style UI in the browser","quote":"You don't have to imagine it. You can see it here [1] or just download a Smalltalk system yourself.","text":"I have been using Emacs as my daily driver for about a decade. It's the closest thing we have to a \"popular\" Smalltalk environment. The desire to bring everything into Emacs is the same desire I felt after experiencing what computing could be in Smalltalk. One of the really striking things that you're confronted with in a modern Smalltalk is the Morphic UI system. Imagine that, with one click, you could drop into a new window with a live inspector and a (very freeform, repl-ish that's not a repl) REPL loaded into the internal context of any button, window, pane, scrollbar, you name it that you can see right now. (Even my own vocabulary is stifled with the traditional UI/UX we have now!) Now imagine you can do that on any application on your computer. Now imagine you can drag those subcomponents around, rearrange them, drag them onto new windows, modify their code, spawn new ones, and so on all while the thing keeps running and functioning . If you break something, it's okay. The debugger pops up and allows you to do the same thing that you did when you inspected the widget for any point in the frame in the stack trace . Yes, you can debug, live, multiple levels of your stack trace at the same time, that have live, editable versions of the code as well. You don't have to imagine it. You can see it here [1] or just download a Smalltalk system yourself. If the Smalltalk community focused on building some tooling to allow you do develop other languages inside a Smalltalk image ba"},{"id":20395865,"author":"gambler","month":"2019-07","story":"The challenges of teaching software engineering","stance":"reference","why":"Ingalls demo of live Smalltalk-style programmable environments in the browser","quote":"","text":"Sounds more like \"the challenge of using a mish-mash of non-integrated tools with no interface\". Developers could definitively solve this by moving away from 70+ year old abstractions (files, terminal) towards things more in line with Smalltalk ideas. But I guess it's better to make thousands of beginners suffer each year, instead of admitting that the current way of doing things is conceptually flawed and outdated. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] (And no, there is nothing special about copy-pasting commands and Unix pipelines that cannot be easily recreated in UI if the UI is designed to be programmable. )"}],"r":[["awADEuv5vWY",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["1e8VZlPBx_0",1],["id1WShzzMCQ",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["P1ww1IXRfTA",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1],["N9c7_8Gp7gI",1],["04Wk9Oi_Fsk",1]]},"SrKj4hYic5A":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a quick, wonderful look at infinite towers of interpreters and what they make possible.","q":{"x":"Here's a wonderful demonstration of the power of nestability","i":18745511,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":36813678,"author":"Zambyte","month":"2023-07","story":"I have written a JVM in Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on an infinite tower of interpreters","quote":"This talk isn't about VMs, but it is about an infinite tower of interpreters.","text":"This talk isn't about VMs, but it is about an infinite tower of interpreters. You may find it interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18745511,"author":"tosh","month":"2018-12","story":"Rich Hickey on the Power of Lisp REPLs (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstration of the power of nestable interpreters","quote":"Here's a wonderful demonstration of the power of nestability","text":"> […] it is important to think broadly about the power of multiple sessions, even from the same user. At first we are talking about 2 sessions, for human and IDE. But it is quite common in rich Common Lisp IDEs like Lispworks for people to have multiple listener (REPL) sessions open at the same time (against the same runtime). You can launch long running processes that produce streaming output, switch to another listener and continue interaction and development, have separate listeners for separate contexts/state/command-history etc. There's a lot to Lisp development that's not RPC. And of course a socket REPL server could support multiple simultaneous users, where 'users' might be programs. > Have you ever worked with an environment like Lispworks or Allegro? If not, I would definitely recommend trying it. […] > [LINK] > [LINK] > Note multiple listeners, data inspectors and more. Maybe not the latest GUIs, but the capabilities should be a source of inspiration for any Clojure IDE. […] > Here's a wonderful demonstration of the power of nestability: > \"Programming Should Eat Itself\" by Nada Amin [VIDEO]"},{"id":15633570,"author":"minsight","month":"2017-11","story":"Collapsing towers of interpreters [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Quick overview of the tower of interpreters idea and its possibilities","quote":"This is a very intereresting video featuring one of the authors (Nada Amin).","text":"This is a very intereresting video featuring one of the authors (Nada Amin). Strangely enough, I watched it a few hours ago. It's a quick overview of the \"tower of interpreters\" idea and some possibilities that it brings. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39837825,"author":"freethejazz","month":"2024-03","story":"Explorative Programming","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the commenter's exposure to Black, a Scheme extension","quote":"","text":"Another take on non-linear explorational programming is Black, which is an extension of scheme. Different motivations, but you might find something interesting there. How I was exposed to it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10786135,"author":"juliangamble","month":"2015-12","story":"An Incremental Approach to Compiler Construction (2006) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote by Nada Amin, cited to identify her","quote":"","text":"Nada Amin, who gave the keynote at Strange Loop in 2014 \"Programming Should Eat Itself\" [VIDEO] has worked through the implementation [LINK] and provided the corresponding workshop documentation in her github account. [LINK] She's also been involved in contributing to the next generation Scala compiler (Dotty)."}],"r":[["rI8tNMsozo0",1],["udlMSe5-zP8",1],["cPNkH-7PRTk",1],["lKXe3HUG2l4",1],["9Q6sLbz37gk",1],["7jiPeIFXb6U",1]]},"gisdyTBMNyQ":{"wy":"HN likes the clear animation of how slip rings and piping let a house rotate.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott did a video last year that has a great 3D render of the system","i":39469541,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39469468,"author":"dabluecaboose","month":"2024-02","story":"Rotating house goes up for sale in New Zealand","stance":"rec","why":"Animation of slip ring for electricity, water and sewage","quote":"a very good animation of how its electricity/water/sewage slip ring works","text":"Unsure about this particular house in NZ, but there's one in California that Tom Scott did a video on with a very good animation of how its electricity/water/sewage slip ring works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39469541,"author":"alargemoose","month":"2024-02","story":"Rotating house goes up for sale in New Zealand","stance":"rec","why":"Great 3D render of the rotating house system","quote":"Tom Scott did a video last year that has a great 3D render of the system","text":"It’s not the same rotating house, but Tom Scott did a video last year that has a great 3D render of the system that designer used to facilitate their build! [VIDEO]"},{"id":37863610,"author":"isaidthis","month":"2023-10","story":"The Midwit Smart Home","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott visit to a custom rotating house","quote":"The video is worth watching","text":"The owner of a custom rotating house in California cited a similar reason for moving. The video is worth watching but here is the relevant section: \"I've had 18 heart procedures. My wife is in perfect health. The odds of me outliving her are zero...We're moving back to Coronado. This is a complex house, even though I think it's simple! For her to try and teach somebody else how to run everything and do everything would be difficult if something happened to me. So we're just kind of jumping ahead.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":36996472,"author":"jpalomaki","month":"2023-08","story":"I thought this rotating house was impossible [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how electricity and piping work in a rotating house","quote":"The part where it shows how the electricity and piping works was pretty interesting.","text":"The part where it shows how the electricity and piping works was pretty interesting. Skip to ~3:00 to see this [1] Patent shown in the video [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}]},"lvTqbM5Dq4Q":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a clear, approachable explanation of Shor's algorithm and what quantum computers actually do.","q":{"x":"Minute Physics has a really good explanation of Shor's algorithm, and by proxy, Quantum computing.","i":21340515,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":35496552,"author":"diginova","month":"2023-04","story":"Quantum Computing 101","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of Shor's algorithm","quote":"Also Minute Physics video for the Shor's Algorithm is good explaination","text":"This video clears a most important misconception everyone has regarding QC which is \"How a QC does operation's parallelly and what does that even mean when you can only get 1 output irl?\" I hope I myself have understood it correctly if that's right Also Minute Physics video for the Shor's Algorithm is good explaination [VIDEO]"},{"id":27456859,"author":"xmprt","month":"2021-06","story":"What makes quantum computing so hard to explain?","stance":"rec","why":"Walks through quantum factoring that classical computers cannot match","quote":"There's also this video which walks through how quantum computers solve a very specific factoring problem","text":"This helped me a lot and I think it made the rounds on hackernews a while back as well. [LINK] There's also this video which walks through how quantum computers solve a very specific factoring problem (Shor's Algorithm) in a way that classical computers cannot. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21340515,"author":"glandium","month":"2019-10","story":"Quantum supremacy: the gloves are off","stance":"rec","why":"Really good explanation of Shor's algorithm and quantum computing","quote":"Minute Physics has a really good explanation of Shor's algorithm, and by proxy, Quantum computing.","text":"Minute Physics has a really good explanation of Shor's algorithm, and by proxy, Quantum computing. [VIDEO] And the followup: [LINK]"}],"r":[["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["kUfF2MTnqAw",1],["L-x8DYTOv7w",1],["4hZxo96x48A",1],["qTBAW-Eh0tM",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["ett_8wLJ87U",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1]]},"qbKGw8MQ0i8":{"wy":"The go-to reference for why file operations are slow on Windows and how antivirus filters, not NTFS itself, are the cause.","q":{"x":"This is a great talk on the topic","i":41085991,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42820396,"author":"miki123211","month":"2025-01","story":"Wild – A fast linker for Linux","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on optimizing for Windows file close costs.","quote":"There's a good talk on this at","text":"And as a result of this, if you want to optimize your program to be fast on Windows, you will make very different optimization decisions than on other platforms. For example, Windows runs virus scans on close(), which makes it very slow. This means that sometimes it makes sense to have one or more background threads exclusively dedicated to closing files. There's a good talk on this at [VIDEO]"},{"id":40940573,"author":"ablob","month":"2024-07","story":"Show HN: Dut – a fast Linux disk usage calculator","stance":"rec","why":"Explains NTFS's preferred access patterns and Linux speed-ups.","quote":"I want to take this opportunity to recommend the talk","text":"I want to take this opportunity to recommend the talk \"NTFS isn't that bad\" ( [VIDEO] ). NTFS prefers a different access pattern than most usual file systems. I remember that a part of the talk was about speed-ups on Linux as well. So even if it doesn't sway your opinion it should enhance your perspective on how file systems work."},{"id":41085991,"author":"unchar1","month":"2024-07","story":"SQLite: 35% Faster Than the Filesystem","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on why NTFS seems slow.","quote":"This is a great talk on the topic","text":"That may be due to a combination of Malware detection + most unix programs not really written to take advantage of the features NTFS has to offer This is a great talk on the topic [VIDEO]"},{"id":41086699,"author":"amiga386","month":"2024-07","story":"SQLite: 35% Faster Than the Filesystem","stance":"rec","why":"Porting rustup to Windows, cutting install from 3m30s to 14s.","quote":"Here's a talk on porting rustup to Windows","text":"NTFS can be especially awful if you're used to Linux behaviour, port code over to Windows and expect the same performance. Here's a talk on porting rustup to Windows: [VIDEO] To begin with, it takes rustup 3m30s to install on Windows. After rejigging the code again and again away from its naive implementation which works fine on Linux, to perform sympathetically towards NTFS, it takes the same rustup 14s to install. That's quite a performance gain! But it needed a lot of changes to rustup, and to Windows itself."},{"id":32833146,"author":"jmmv","month":"2022-09","story":"When to use Bazel?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on NTFS speed and how the Rust installer was optimized.","quote":"Also take a look at this talk on NTFS, file system speed, and how the presenter optimized the Rust installer","text":"Add your npm directories to the virus file exclusion lists of Windows Defender. Note that some of the directories may not be obvious (those under AppData for example). The speed improvements are massive for tools like these that touch lots of small files. Also take a look at this talk on NTFS, file system speed, and how the presenter optimized the Rust installer: [VIDEO] . There is an awful lot that can be done to tools that deal with lots of files to perform well under Windows too."},{"id":43772333,"author":"amiga386","month":"2025-04","story":"MinC Is Not Cygwin","stance":"reference","why":"How rustup devs restructured code to speed up Windows.","quote":"See this talk on how the developers of rustup massively improved their performance on Windows by restructuring their code","text":"The thinness is actually part of the problem. POSIX and Windows APIs don't work like each other. For example, if you were a Unix archiver extracting a file, you'd call stat() open() write() close() chmod() utimes(). On Linux/BSD that's 1 file open/close, but on Windows, that's 4 file opens/closes because the Windows APIs need an open filehandle, while the POSIX APIs take a filepath. Linux/BSD have LRU caches of filepath->inode because they're designed around these APIs, Windows doesn't. Cygwin has to open/close a Windows filehandle each time because it can't make the calling code pass a filehandle instead. So it may be more comfortable and familiar, but also Windows APIs are unsympathetic to code designed for Unix originally. See this talk on how the developers of rustup massively improved their performance on Windows by restructuring their code: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41500943,"author":"nullindividual","month":"2024-09","story":"Windows NT vs. Unix: A design comparison","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Windows file slowness is due to filters like Defender.","quote":"Robert Collins explains that performance is just as good as Linux","text":"But it simply isn't slower than Linux. Robert Collins explains that performance is just as good as Linux and the performance loss on Windows is due to file system filters (Defender)[0]. This is what DevDrive intends (and does) fix. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38770359,"author":"bayindirh","month":"2023-12","story":"Where Are the Sanity Checks?","stance":"reference","why":"Source on rustup's stat overhead problem on NTFS.","quote":"","text":"However, the code traverses that amount of data. It doesn't matter if you pass over the same inode n times. From OS point of view, there's no difference. Of course you can write a more complicated calculation algorithm, but it'll require tons of syscalls to determine the type of the file handle you're interacting with and will be probably very slow. This is what rustup did inadvertently back in the day, and it caused NTFS to melt down to a halt because of stat() overhead [0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37187109,"author":"CoolCold","month":"2023-08","story":"Microsoft's backwards compatibility is insane","stance":"reference","why":"Source for claim that open/close overhead comes from antivirus inspection.","quote":"","text":"to my understanding it's not really IO (reading/writing bytes), but overhead on OpenFile/CloseFile being under AV/Defender inspection. I'm taking that understanding basing on [VIDEO] with \" \"NTFS really isn't that bad\" - Robert Collins (LCA 2020) \" > Why was rustup slow (3m30s to install (not including download time)) in early 2019 on Windows, and why isn't it slow (14s to install) now? >Early in 2019 I was developing some things in Rustlang on Windows, got frustrated at the performance of rustup (the installer for rust) and looked for bug. I found one which blamed a combination of NTFS, small files and anti-virus software for the problem. This wasn't an entirely satisfactory answer to my mind. Fast forward a bit, and with a combination of changes to rustup and to Windows itself and rustup is now pleasantly usable.... which also improved performance for rustup on NFS mounted home directories."},{"id":37245343,"author":"CoolCold","month":"2023-08","story":"Bun v0.8.0","stance":"reference","why":"Explains antivirus hooks on file close.","quote":"You may want to take a look on","text":"Here likely meant that system level hooks of antiviral software are invoked on CloseFile call when you do writes, not on OpenFile. You may want to take a look on [VIDEO]"},{"id":32165142,"author":"d2wa","month":"2022-07","story":"TeamViewer installs suspicious font only useful for web fingerprinting","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why closing files is slow on Windows.","quote":"The problem is that closing files takes a lot of time on Windows.","text":"> Even if windows indexing aint malicious, it certainly behaves as if it is. The problem is that closing files takes a lot of time on Windows. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25158407,"author":"for_xyz","month":"2020-11","story":"Windows Subsystem for Linux: The lost potential","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on file close costs from antivirus on Windows.","quote":"","text":"> the bottleneck is that NTFS is a crappy filesystem. Care to explain why do you think NTFS is crap? From my experience it's usually bad assumptions about files under windows and every application or library tries to stick to posix interface when dealing with files (open, read/write, close) which tends to block for longer periods on windows than on linux counterparts which results in significant perfomance loss . Linux first software will always outperform windows implementation and Windows first software will outperform Linux implementations unless you provide separate code paths to properly handle underlying OS architecture and assumptions. On Windows closing the file handle is extremely costly operation due AV checks and file content indexing [1] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["IV3dnLzthDA",1],["Qct6LKbneKQ",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1]]},"-EVqrDlAqYo":{"wy":"HN people send others here to hear Hawkins explain his theory in his own words, including reference frames and the old versus new brain.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend the Lex Fridman interview to listen to Jeff explain live","i":26241750,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26241750,"author":"gamegoblin","month":"2021-02","story":"The Thousand Brains Theory of Intelligence (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hawkins explains his Thousand Brains theory live","quote":"Highly recommend the Lex Fridman interview to listen to Jeff explain live","text":"Highly recommend the Lex Fridman interview to listen to Jeff explain live: [VIDEO] While Numenta doesn't have the amazing results of DeepMind and friends, I think they are doing really great stuff in the space of biologically plausible intelligence."},{"id":26344348,"author":"Geee","month":"2021-03","story":"Myths about how the brain works","stance":"rec","why":"Explains old versus new brain parts and neocortex","quote":"Jeff Hawkings explains the difference between the 'old parts' and 'new parts' pretty well in this interview","text":"I wouldn't say that the Triune brain theory is a 'myth'. It's just not accurate, but it's not a complete lie either. There certainly are different parts of the brain that have evolved at different times, and have different functions. The older parts have evolved to specific functions, while neocortex, which is found in mammals, has a uniform structure and is capable of learning and adapting to different tasks. 75% of human brain is neocortex. Jeff Hawkings explains the difference between the 'old parts' and 'new parts' pretty well in this interview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23480467,"author":"jcims","month":"2020-06","story":"YOLOv5: State-of-the-art object detection at 140 FPS","stance":"reference","why":"Hawkins outlines hierarchical reference frames for understanding scenes","quote":"The idea of hierarchical reference frames (outlined a bit by Jeff Hawkins here","text":"Has anyone (beyond maybe self-driving software) tried using object tagging as a way to start introducing physics into a scene? E.g. human and bicycle have same motion vector, increases likelihood that human is riding bicycle. Bicycle and human have size and weight ranges that could be used to plot trajectory. Bicycles riding in a straight line and trees both provide some cues as to the gravity vector in the scene. Etc. etc. Seems like the camera motion is probably already solved with optical flow/photogrammetry stuff, but you might be able to use that to help scale the scene and start filtering your tagging based on geometric likelihood. The idea of hierarchical reference frames (outlined a bit by Jeff Hawkins here [VIDEO] ) seems pretty compelling to me for contextualizing scenes to gain comprehension. Particularly if you build a graph from those reference frames and situate models tuned to the type of object at the root of each each frame (vertex). You could use that to help each model learn, too. So if a bike model projects a 'riding' edge towards the 'person' model, there wouldn't likely be much learning. e.g. [Person]-(rides)->[Bike] would have likely been encountered already. However if the [Bike] projects the (rides) edge towards the [Capuchin] sitting in the seat, the [Capuchin] model might learn that capuchins can (ride) and furthermore they can (ride) a [Bike]."},{"id":20328398,"author":"tim333","month":"2019-07","story":"Jeff Hawkins: Thousand Brains Theory of Intelligence [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There's a youtube generated one if you go to","text":"There's a youtube generated one if you go to [VIDEO] and click the three dots under the vid. I don't know if there's a way to get those things into an easy to read format? Clicking select all and pasting to the text editor sort of works but could be better."}],"r":[["E7MQb9Y4FAE",1],["bQECSInWVPY",1],["xLYYh4aPXAM",1],["VMj-3S1tku0",1],["VUhlNx_-wYk",1]]},"0rb9CfOvojk":{"wy":"Recommended in threads on German rail as the data-backed explanation of how reported punctuality understates real delays.","q":{"x":"there's the great talk from David Kriesel on the 36C3 [1] about how Deutsche Bahn is... \"doing statistics\" to hide their delays. It's well worth the hour!","i":37224916,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37224916,"author":"martin_a","month":"2023-08","story":"I Walked Across Luxembourg – A Hike from the West to the East of the Grand Duchy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great talk on how Deutsche Bahn statistics hide delays.","quote":"there's the great talk from David Kriesel on the 36C3 [1] about how Deutsche Bahn is... \"doing statistics\" to hide their delays. It's well worth the hour!","text":"I think they want to get rid of the trains going to Interlaken directly. At least that's what I read in the SZ [0]. If you haven't seen it, there's the great talk from David Kriesel on the 36C3 [1] about how Deutsche Bahn is... \"doing statistics\" to hide their delays. It's well worth the hour! [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33132317,"author":"luckylion","month":"2022-10","story":"German rail operator says train disruption caused by sabotage","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining CCC talk on Deutsche Bahn reliability and accuracy (in German).","quote":"There was an entertaining talk at a CCC event a few years back","text":"That's why you should hire the PR companies they're using, not the train companies themselves. Partially joking, I think the image stems from the past, but the system degrades when it's not maintained appropriately. There was an entertaining talk at a CCC event a few years back which looked at DB reliability and accuracy (in German): [VIDEO]"},{"id":47246351,"author":"mimischi","month":"2026-03","story":"Bet on German Train Delays","stance":"reference","why":"Infamous 36C3 talk analysing German train delays, in German.","quote":"David Kriesel gave the infamous talk called “BahnMining” at 36C3 highlighting this.","text":"For context, David Kriesel gave the infamous talk called “BahnMining” at 36C3 highlighting this. IIUC it’s only available in German: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46254797,"author":"spflueger","month":"2025-12","story":"Germany's train service is one of Europe's worst. How did it get so bad?","stance":"reference","why":"Data-backed talk showing German rail getting worse.","quote":"Here is the link to his talk directly","text":"Quite some years ago there was some data backed evidence of this I would say. [LINK] Here is the link to his talk directly [VIDEO] Its true that the network is quite dense and used by also cargo trains, but there is no denying that things got worse and worse. I constantly experience delay to do some stuff not working. I forgot even the minute threshold when a train is still punctual according to DB. I believe it's 10min by now, which can be deadly if you need to switch trains :)"},{"id":37883048,"author":"josefx","month":"2023-10","story":"Germany's terrible trains are no joke for a nation built on efficiency","stance":"reference","why":"Independent data mining of train delays showing official stats omit cancelled stops.","quote":"A few years ago David Kriesel did some independent data mining on the reported delays.","text":"> Here are the official statistics: [LINK] ... A few years ago David Kriesel did some independent data mining on the reported delays. One point that came up is that official statistics do not even cover train stops that where cancelled and I think it was also mentioned that cancelling stops was one of the ways used to make up for lost time if needed to avoid knock-on effects. German presentation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37225092,"author":"martin_a","month":"2023-08","story":"I Walked Across Luxembourg – A Hike from the West to the East of the Grand Duchy","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on German rail delays; commenter defers to it for more detail.","quote":"I think David Kriesel has it in his talk","text":"This kind of hits the core. As the DB (Deutsche Bahn) is a private company now (although they are 100% owned by the state), they have to make a profit. How do you make a profit? Either raise prices or lower costs. Doing both is the best and that's what they do. Raising prices is easy, it just gets more and more expensive, even the \"cheap\" Deutschlandticket is too expensive, experts say it should probably be around 29 Euro/month to make a real change. How do you save costs? You do only the bare minimum of maintenance, don't invest in building more tracks and instead reduce tracks and track switches. That's what they've done the last 20 years or so. [0] This leads to ICEs being stuck behind slower trains as there are simply no more tracks to switch to and pass cargo trains etc. So, if some train gets stuck somewhere or has a defect, every other train is stuck behind that for some time. Now, as the state subsidizes some parts of Deutsche Bahn, building something new is also cheaper than maintaining what is there. So they let some things rot down so far that they have to rebuild it completely. As the subsidization is then higher, that's better for the turnaround numbers. There have been thoughts about splitting off building and maintenance of the tracks completely of the rest but that didn't work so far. In that scenario the state would take care of the infrastructure and DB would just \"rent\" it to drive on it. To add something else to it: I think David Kriesel has it in his talk"},{"id":45474248,"author":"zwnow","month":"2025-10","story":"Digital ID – The New Chains of Capitalist Surveillance","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Public transportation is a complete joke","text":"8 years, wow. Try 30 and actually growing up in this country. School is letting people down, rent prices are exploding. Public transportation is a complete joke ( [VIDEO] ) Sorry, but with how rich Germany is I can and should expect more from this country."}]},"P76Vbsk_3J0":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear, well-paced introduction to Clojure and Lisp ideas like code as data and macros, straight from the language's creator.","q":{"x":"It's just a great talk.","i":6103627,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6103627,"author":"Jach","month":"2013-07","story":"How do I “think in AngularJS” if I have a jQuery background?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Slick Clojure intro covering code as data, evaluation model, Java interop.","quote":"It's just a great talk.","text":"Indeed. Rich Hickey's 2-part presentation on \"Clojure for Java Programmers\" was a really slick presentation of a Lisp. (Focusing on the 'code is data' aspect from the beginning (at least once he starts talking about the language specifics themselves, he does spend a while motivating dynamic languages) rather than talking a lot about just higher order functions and macros and so on. He goes over the evaluation model and its difference from a Java/C++ model, he shows how to interop with Java... It's just a great talk.) Part 1: [VIDEO] Part 2: [LINK] Slides: [LINK]"},{"id":29776009,"author":"greggyb","month":"2022-01","story":"Understanding the Power of Lisp (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of Clojure data types, evaluation model and macros.","quote":"Watch through ~1h:30m to get a feel for what macros are used for in Clojure's core.","text":"> So that's how I view Lisp macros - creating code on the fly and executing it. This is an incorrect assumption. In compiled Lisps[0], macros are a compile-time construct, which manipulate the data structures that represent your code[1]. All manipulation occurs at compile-time. In interpreted Lisps, macro evaluation is temporally intertwined with program execution, but the two phases are logically distinct. [0]: Compilation time in a Lisp is not necessarily similar to compilation in other languages. Working at a REPL does not imply that interpretation is happening. Clojure is an example of a Lisp that is exclusively compiled. Even when sending individual expressions to the REPL, they are compiled before being evaluated. [1]: From a reply elsewhere in this thread: [VIDEO] See this video for an overview of the core of Clojure. The link starts with an overview of Clojure's data types and data structures (skipping the intro for the reasons behind Clojure). This leads directly into the evaluation model, which is based on the data structures of Clojure. Watch through ~1h:30m to get a feel for what macros are used for in Clojure's core. This gives a very good overview of the evaluation model for macros; this is generally applicable to Lisp macros outside of Clojure as well, though there are nuances to different dialects. I recommend that you start at the linked time, but if you want to jump straight to the evaluation model, you can jump to ~47m."},{"id":29775923,"author":"greggyb","month":"2022-01","story":"Understanding the Power of Lisp (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"From a Clojure perspective, here are a few things to think about and explore to help understand where the power and value of macros lie. 1. Clojure does not have all of its various conditionals as language built-ins. There is a single conditional construct built in to the language, if. The rest are all defined in terms of if. Function arguments are eagerly evaluated in Clojure. How do you write a short-circuiting function in Clojure? (when predicate consequent) as a function must evaluate both the predicate and the consequent. If consequent is slow to calculate then your whole when expression is as slow as consequent, even if predicate is false and we do not need consequent. when is a Clojure macro based on the if special form. 2. core.async is a mere library.[0] This implements CSP (same idea as Go's channels) and async programming (the sort of stuff that needs compiler support in other languages such as C# or Go) as a library. If Rich Hickey didn't write core.async, you could build this control flow yourself for async programming. In fact, you can create arbitrary control flow mechanisms using macros. Common Lisp's entire object system can be implemented in macros. 3. The only special forms in Clojure are def, if, fn, let, loop, recur, do, new, ., throw, try, set!, quote, and var. The rest of what you would consider the core of Clojure consists of functions and macros. Much of the control flow functionality is implemented in macros.[1] The core of macros is that they allow "},{"id":25298127,"author":"Jach","month":"2020-12","story":"State of Common Lisp Survey 2020","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I'm explaining why Lisp is hard to learn. It is not hard to learn in general, there are too many counterexamples of people of all sorts and skills learning it without these issues you are having, learning it as their first, learning it later in their careers... (CL is big , I'll give you that, but each piece can be chewed one at a time and does not seem to be individually that difficult.) So I can hypothesize why it's hard to learn for you . My first guess is still that you aren't meeting Lisp in its own terms and are trying to make Lisp fit your mental model of some other language and its ecosystem. This isn't going to work, and the failure mode (if I'm right) has nothing to do with Lisp but with biases in general heuristics of learning. You don't need to \"unlearn\" the way you're used to doing things, but you do need to separate yourself from it for a bit until you actually grasp the Lisp (and its ecosystem's) way(s) and can then see the connections, tenuous and non-existent as some may be. I'll risk raising some of those connections below, but really, things need to be understood on their own terms first or you wind up with misconceptions. (I've seen it multiple times with Java/Python programmers getting confused at the other's \"import\" statement.) > strange terminology It's not Lisp's fault that it's old. Being old means it will have terminology that may be alien to what we have today with nothing today being a perfect fit, or alternatively sometimes it expresses mostly "},{"id":24894991,"author":"pgt","month":"2020-10","story":"An Intuition for Lisp Syntax","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Lisp has only one rule called the Operational Form: (operator arg1 arg2 arg3 ...) The Operational Form is just a list denoted by parentheses. The operation or 'function' comes first, followed by its arguments or operands, e.g. (+ 1 2 3) => 6 The + symbol resolves to the plus function and is passed in the arguments 1, 2 and 3. Nested forms are evaluated inside-out: (+ 5 (- 10 6)) => 9 Traditionally, Lisp only has one data literal: `(linked lists)`, but Clojure adds `[square brackets for vectors]` and `{:curly brackets}` for hash maps. The colon denotes a keyword, which is a symbol which only ever resolves to itself and is commonly used for labeling things, e.g. keys in a map. Notice how the operational form is just a list with some symbols and data literals. In Lisp, the syntax for writing data structures and the syntax for writing code is the same syntax . And since a function operates on data and produces new data, what if a function could operate on code and produce new code , since code is just data? We call such a function a macro (and I don't mean Excel macros). Macros run at \"compile-time\" (technically 'read time') and the code output is executed at \"run-time\" (or during 'evaluation'). The benefit of macros is that if your language is missing a feature, you can add it. You can see this in practice by looking at the source code for the `and` and `or` logic functions in Clojure, which are typically built-ins, but in Clojure they are just macros bootstrapped on top of the "},{"id":9814395,"author":"istvan__","month":"2015-07","story":"John Carmack working on Scheme as a VR scripting language","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Same here, I never understood Java interfaces, abstract classes, and a ton of other \"features\" but picking up Clojure was a breeze. I don't understand why complicating thing that supposed to be simple helps you by any mean. On the top of that, I have seen several cases when Java programmers tipped over in their own code because of the complexity that they thought they understand, except there was a particular case when it was doing something else then expected. Reasoning about Clojure (LISP) code is always easy because if you follow best practices you have small functions with very little context to understand. On the top of these, I see the ratio of LOC 50:1 (worst case even higher) for Java : Clojure code that does the same thing. Usually people get triggered and say why does it matter, but in reality less code is better for everybody. Easier to understand, less chance of errors, etc. Correctness was long time lost for majority of Java developers, just put out a survey and you can see it for yourself. It is also pretty common practice not to handle exceptions well and just let a DNS not found error explode as an IOexception and good luck tracking down what caused it (literary happened to me). I understand that the average Java dev does not see any value in LISP (Clojure) but it silly to expect that the average of any group is going to lead the scientific advancement of any field including computer science. One tendency that you can see if you are walking around with open ey"}],"r":[["dCuZkaaou0Q",1],["stxVBJem3Rs",1],["FjHJ7FmV0M4",1],["495nCzxM9PI",1],["KZokQov_aH0",1],["GDzBE6vz5r0",1],["F8sZRBdmqc0",1],["OSGv2VnC0go",1]]},"nSKp2StlS6s":{"wy":"Shared as developer humor, with commenters insisting any Dylan Beattie mention must come with this song.","q":{"x":"You Give REST a Bad Name\" is also excellent.","i":34302905,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":25859832,"author":"thunderbong","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: Anyone know any funny programming jokes?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"I laughed way too hard on a number of them","text":"Man, those Dylan Beattie's videos are out of this world!! I laughed way too hard on a number of them - You give REST a bad name - [VIDEO] We're Gonna Build a Framework - [LINK] Enterprise Waterfall - [LINK]"},{"id":34302905,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2023-01","story":"Tail Call Optimization: The Musical","stance":"rec","why":"Dylan Beattie's parody song about REST, performed at conferences.","quote":"You Give REST a Bad Name\" is also excellent.","text":"I want to highlight Dylan Beattie while we're posting our favorites. [LINK] His song DMCA is my little favourite, but \"You Give REST a Bad Name\" is also excellent. And he does perform at conferences! (Mostly .NET ones) [VIDEO]"},{"id":42777869,"author":"swyx","month":"2025-01","story":"Guitar chord karaoke with Vamp, Chordino, and FFmpeg (2022)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"any dylan beattie post must be accompanied with a recommendation for his hit single","text":"any dylan beattie post must be accompanied with a recommendation for his hit single, You Give REST a Bad Name [VIDEO]"},{"id":41159471,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-08","story":"Why does everyone hate Haskell, jazz, and pure math?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sometimes I wonder if my late-in-life recent appreciation for maths* be just a symptom of having failed to die {in a bizarre gardening accident, driving off Skyline Blvd} while my body could still handle following the childish dream of hex, bugs, and rock&roll. * [LINK] \"Haskell? I was into those guys before they were cool. Miranda, man, that even had diagonal comprehensions... Have you heard of Hope? Kiff this, mec...\" Lagniappe: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24455917,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2020-09","story":"Tell HN: Happy Programmers' Day to all devs in HN","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"we can blast Dylan Beattie and the Linebreakers","text":"Good point. In the spirit of [LINK] I propose we add \"european programmer's day\", celebrated on 5.12 (Dag Sinterklaasje)? Let's all meet here again. I'll bring a bottle or two of Pastis 512 (unfortunately adjusted with magic marker instead of a hex editor) and we can blast Dylan Beattie and the Linebreakers: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["ZZ3F3zWiEmc",1],["wOlcB-JxkFw",1],["ytpRnRke6I0",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["9zinZmE3Ogk",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["Pae2AMnmUVA",1],["rwhUpu9MfZ0",1],["7NfX0vlCa4k",1],["PQccNdwm8Tw",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["90NsjKvz9Ns",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["7Pq-S557XQU",1],["FjA0EQPeUGM",1],["MBRqu0YOH14",1],["QM1iUe6IofM",1],["iGFqfTCL2fs",1],["Wm2h0cbvsw8",1]]},"ne6tB2KiZuk":{"wy":"HN shares it as a delightful demo of the pentatonic scale's near-universal appeal and of a crowd syncing without instruction.","q":{"x":"Have you seen the video where Bobby McFerrin leads a whole crowd to sync perfectly with each other","i":36077781,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36077781,"author":"mandmandam","month":"2023-05","story":"Neanderthal flute: The oldest musical instrument in the world","stance":"strong_rec","why":"McFerrin leads a crowd into sync via the pentatonic scale","quote":"Have you seen the video where Bobby McFerrin leads a whole crowd to sync perfectly with each other","text":"Let's assume the thing is a flute. It's been reconstructed and plays beautifully. The odds of a hyena making such a thing with it's teeth are in silly territory, and we have other similar flutes that are nearly as old (relatively speaking). Given that, the fact that they made it using a scale so resonant with humans that it is still in very popular use sixty thousand years later - that's incredible. I felt the article drastically undersold the awesomeness of that, showing far too much restraint. Have you seen the video where Bobby McFerrin leads a whole crowd to sync perfectly with each other, without rehearsal, thanks to the pentatonic scale [0]? That's spiritual stuff ; it's 'sophisticated artistic expression'. That's the same scale that was (almost certainly) intentionally used to make this flute. 0 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":23391021,"author":"rrmm","month":"2020-06","story":"What Did Bach Sound Like to Bach?","stance":"rec","why":"Fun demo of the pentatonic scale's universal appeal","quote":"Bobby Mcferrin has a fun demo about this","text":"Some stuff based on pentatonic scales (blues) would probably make sense. From what I recall it's almost universal in different cultures. Bobby Mcferrin has a fun demo about this [VIDEO] But yeah I bet some jazz would be baffling. I wonder how fast it'd take people to get into it. You could probably find some indigneous populations to work with."},{"id":12528628,"author":"dietrichepp","month":"2016-09","story":"Music theory for nerds","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Also, this notation has a slight problem. That problem is that sheet music is terrible. > This has got to be some of the worst jargon and notation for anything, ever. I hear it bandied around in these articles that music notation is awful. Can someone explain to me why? I'm not much of a musician, but I can work my way through sheet music if necessary, or write it when I want to. I don't remember what it was like to learn to read and write music so I'm not going to understand why it's so bad just by thinking about it. First of all, the lines and spaces make a ton of sense to me. When you're playing, reading, or writing a piece, you spend most of your time in a key with a diatonic scale. Maybe you're thinking about the chords: I, vi, ii, V7. The lines and spaces are great for giving you that kind of information. I guess this \"completely obscures the relationship between the pitches\". I guess that's the case if you forget what key you're playing in. The alternative would be to make the staff fully chromatic, but that sounds like it has a bunch of disadvantages for most music. The chromatic staff ( [LINK] ) looks terrible to me, since it spreads out the notes farther. I've already memorized all of the diatonic scales, and now the diatonic scales are jumping around, and the staff covers a shorter interval so you get more of those 8va or 15ma. Hummingbird ( [LINK] ) seems like classical notation, just with more redundancy and the symbols changed around a little bit. Maybe it's b"}],"r":[["w_znRopGtbE",1],["tybOi4hjZFQ",1],["zU8alMWUmDI",1]]},"wO2lWmgEK1Y":{"wy":"Pointed to as an in-depth, journalistic account of Brexit's economic aftermath.","q":{"x":"watch this excellent documentary from the Financial Times (FT)","i":33260323,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33260323,"author":"open-source-ux","month":"2022-10","story":"Brexit update (August 2022) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"In-depth documentary on Brexit's effects.","quote":"watch this excellent documentary from the Financial Times (FT)","text":"For a more in-depth look at Brexit, watch this excellent documentary from the Financial Times (FT): The Brexit effect: how leaving the EU hit the UK [VIDEO]"},{"id":33456098,"author":"shagie","month":"2022-11","story":"Bank of England expects UK to fall into longest ever recession","stance":"rec","why":"One of several interesting Brexit videos.","quote":"some of the ones that I've watched that are interesting.","text":"With the mess of England's prime minster, a number of Brexit videos showed up in my feed on Youtube... some of the ones that I've watched that are interesting. A German documentary (it has English subtitles for the German narration)): British Fishermen and Brexit | ARTE.tv Documentary [LINK] A financial times article on Brexit - The Brexit effect: how leaving the EU hit the UK | FT Film [VIDEO] A TLDR from earlier this week: No-one Likes Brexit Anymore: What Happens Next? [LINK] (the main part on this relevant is the polling and the corresponding events) --- There's a fair bit of Brexit-regret going on now and some people apparently still think its a good idea. At the time of the referendum, it appears that quite a few people who did a day's work in Britain thought that it was a good idea, but have since come to realize that it wasn't."},{"id":33306246,"author":"madog","month":"2022-10","story":"Global media ask what's happened to Britain","stance":"rec","why":"Covers the aftermath of Brexit.","quote":"there was a good Financial Times film [1] recently that covered the after effects of Brexit.","text":"Just to add to this there was a good Financial Times film [1] recently that covered the after effects of Brexit. Covid and Ukraine have allowed politicians to divert attention away from the economic disaster that Brexit has been, and have been handy scapegoats, but the fact Brexit has damaged the U.K. economically is undeniable. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41863322,"author":"mrtksn","month":"2024-10","story":"All software in EU under product liability from 2026","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as reporting on Brexit's trade impact.","quote":"","text":"Sure, here's the list of the things you have to do to trade with EU that you previously didn't had to: [LINK] [LINK] Commercial Invoice: A detailed invoice for each transaction is needed for customs clearance, containing information like the value of goods, description, and terms of sale. Packing List: Provides details of the goods being shipped, quantities, weights, and packaging information. It's used by customs to verify contents. Commodity Codes (HS Codes): Classify the goods you are trading. These codes determine the duties and VAT that may apply. Certificates of Origin:If your goods are eligible for preferential treatment (e.g., lower tariffs), you might need proof of origin to claim this benefit under the EU-UK Trade and Cooperation Agreement (TCA). VAT and Duties: If you're selling to EU consumers, you may need to register for VAT in the EU or use the One-Stop Shop (OSS) scheme for simplified reporting. Proof of Transport:ocuments such as bills of lading or airway bills to demonstrate that goods have left the UK or entered the EU. Product-Specific Documentation: ome goods (e.g., food, chemicals, or electronics) may require additional certifications, safety data sheets, or compliance with product standards (e.g., CE or UKCA marking). Trade Agreement Compliance: Ensure that your business complies with the terms set out in the UK-EU Trade and Cooperation Agreement, especially regarding rules of origin and applicable tariffs. Here is some reporting on the impact of the bu"},{"id":38821229,"author":"zoenolan","month":"2023-12","story":"Brexit has failed for UK, say clear majority of Britons – poll","stance":"reference","why":"Similar FT video on Brexit's economic effects.","quote":"The FT did a similar video a year ago [7]","text":"What pmuk said in a sibling comment, plus - Destabilising Northern Ireland [1] - Problems harvesting crops [2] - Breaking international law [3] - Irish supply chains have been reconfigured to bypass the UK [4] Economics Explained recently did a couple of videos on the UK economy and the current problems. Not limited to Brexit but also not made better [5][6]. The FT did a similar video a year ago [7] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33455964,"author":"jsemrau","month":"2022-11","story":"Bank of England expects UK to fall into longest ever recession","stance":"reference","why":"Companies moved operations to Europe due to import taxes.","quote":"I'd agree after watching this YT video by the Financial Times.","text":"I'd agree after watching this YT video by the Financial Times. Suddenly having to pay import taxes led many companies to move operations to mainland Europe. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4PaWFYm0kEw",1],["CbIZU8cQWXc",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["LxgMdjyw8uw",1],["rvskMHn0sqQ",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["hyCIpKAIFyo",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["fD-R35DSSZY",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["bLk1cjZ4ll0",1]]},"0olpSN6_TCc":{"wy":"Often named as a great first video from Practical Engineering and as the explanation of reinforced earth.","q":{"x":"Practical Engineering has a great video on this simple but pervasive technology.","i":17888433,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":25652591,"author":"davidwf","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: What YouTube channels do you subscribe to?","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested as a great first video from the Practical Engineering channel.","quote":"Practical Engineering: -- -- Great first video","text":"I keep a Google Document of these to share just in case people ask about my YouTube interests. I Actually Watched All of Them I have actually watched ALL of the videos on these channels and I don’t even feel bad about it :-) I actually like new videos from some of the ones below (like NativLang, for example) but they maybe don’t hold up in the early videos. - Space Time: PBS Physics, sooo good -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - Lindsay Ellis: Really, really intelligent video essays about film and culture -- [LINK] -- Great First Video: [LINK] - Periodic Videos: Chemistry, including a video for every element -- [LINK] -- Great first video (obviously): [LINK] Other Math and Science - 3Blue1Brown: Amazing math visualizations -- [LINK] -- Great first video, how e^pi*i works: [LINK] - NumberPhile: Fun math videos -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - PBS Eons: Biology! -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - PBS Infinite Series: Math math math, really really hard math -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - SciShow: Science! More poppy but fun -- [LINK] -- Silly first show: [LINK] -- Actual physics: [LINK] - VSauce: Science-y? -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - Practical Engineering: -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [VIDEO] Other Humanities - Alliterative: Connections via language -- [LINK] -- Great first video about gin and gimlets: [LINK] - Bon Appetit: Such fun food stuff! -- Gourmet Makes: [LINK] -- It’s Alive: [LINK] - Masaman: Genetics and human migration (an"},{"id":17888433,"author":"cwkoss","month":"2018-08","story":"Cargo ships that sink when their cargo suddenly liquefies","stance":"rec","why":"Great explanation of mechanically stabilized earth, a simple pervasive technology.","quote":"Practical Engineering has a great video on this simple but pervasive technology.","text":"I wonder if sheets of a cloth-like material could be used in loading similar to Mechanically Stabilized Earth Practical Engineering has a great video on this simple but pervasive technology. [VIDEO]"},{"id":49234259,"author":"milesvp","month":"2026-08","story":"The Grid That Doubles the Strength of the Ground","stance":"reference","why":"Shows reinforcement increasing shear strength of sand.","quote":"Ha! just looked up the video","text":"I was suprised to see the use of wiremesh in sand castle building some years ago. Same concept though. You add something that helps with shear strength and you can build much taller sand buildings. It’s really counter intuitive to see too, since the sand could clearly sieve through the mesh. Ha! just looked up the video, seems it’s another practical engineering video I was thinking of [VIDEO]"},{"id":35754459,"author":"hourago","month":"2023-04","story":"The Ancient Architecture That Defies Earthquakes","stance":"reference","why":"Related demonstration of sand reinforced to hold a car.","quote":"It reminds me of the video","text":"It reminds me of the video \"Sand Castle Holds Up A Car! - Mechanically Stabilized Earth\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":14174169,"author":"afarrell","month":"2017-04","story":"Ask HN: How do you unwind at the end of a day?","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as a civil engineering channel among favorites.","quote":"Practical Engineering: Mostly Civil Engineering","text":"Ooooh, these are nice. Here are mine: Extra History: stories of historical incidents — [LINK] Real Engineering: mostly materials science — [LINK] - Wendover Productions — mostly transportation - [LINK] Tom Scott: a bit of everything — [LINK] - Jay foreman: Urban planning and maps — [LINK] The Engineer Guy: Manufacturing — [LINK] Practical Engineering: Mostly Civil Engineering — [VIDEO] 3Blue1Brown: Math, visualized to me more intuitive — [LINK]"},{"id":12969603,"author":"steamer25","month":"2016-11","story":"The Most Effective Weapon on the Modern Battlefield Is Concrete","stance":"reference","why":"Explains reinforced earth, where the commenter learned about it.","quote":"Reminds me of reinforced earth which I learned about from this video","text":"Reminds me of reinforced earth which I learned about from this video: [VIDEO] (which is also from the pretty great Practical Engineering channel)."}],"r":[["62tIvfP9A2w",1],["K8gV05nS7mc",1],["v0YEaeIClKY",1],["WB2-2j9e4co",1],["4Z75A_JMBx4",1],["Z0oFJ2jbkDI",1]]},"37NaHRE0Sqw":{"wy":"Pointed to as evidence that Java can do fast, cheap message-passing concurrency, as an alternative to Erlang or Scala actors.","q":{"x":"Kilim's author did a nice Google Talk","i":898541,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":1150640,"author":"lucifer","month":"2010-02","story":"Why Scala Actors are Slow","stance":"rec","why":"Kilim as a promising JVM message passing alternative competitive with Erlang.","quote":"Kilim seems quite promising and seems to hold its own quite well in benchmarks against Erlang.","text":"\"Asynchronious message passing is old and great idea. Instead of synchronizing objects and dealing with deadlocks we try to isolate objects and let them exchange messages. Erlang proved it to be extremly powerful tool applicable to wide range of highly scalable mission critical applications. In JVM landscape big interest to this approach started after introducing Scala actors. \" That statement is so very poorly informed! JMS (\"surprise\"?) has been a sign of \"interest\" in \"Asynchronious message passing\" in the \"JVM landscape\". I am not up on my Scala, but the author fails to convey (based on what I do know ;) the competence level that would lend weight to his attempt to address (much less support) the topic. For those interested in other JVM alternatives, Kilim seems quite promising and seems to hold its own quite well in benchmarks against Erlang. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [scroll to end to see the impressive results for Kilim.] [LINK] [edit: added pdf link]"},{"id":898541,"author":"wynand","month":"2009-10","story":"Concurrency Patterns: Java, Scala, and Clojure","stance":"rec","why":"Kilim author's Google talk on lightweight actors in Java.","quote":"Kilim's author did a nice Google Talk","text":"Actors are decoupled from the means by which they are executed. Scala has a nice system where you can run thousands of event-based actors in a thread pool. It grows the pool as needed (as threads block). See [LINK] While it may use threads, Scala's actor system effectively allows you to create thousands of actors. Kilim is actually a pure (depending on whether you see a byte-code processing system as pure) Java system that does the same thing (as far as I can tell): [LINK] Kilim's author did a nice Google Talk: [VIDEO] EDIT: Fixed some grammar and added video link."},{"id":3876999,"author":"eternalban","month":"2012-04","story":"Why Erlang? ","stance":"reference","why":"CSP style concurrency on the JVM via Kilim.","quote":"[CSP style concurrency on JVM]","text":"Erlang is a CSP system, first and foremost, and not actors. [CSP style concurrency on JVM]: [VIDEO] (Kilim, it should be noted, was benchmarked as scaling better than Erlang/OTP.) [LINK] (paper in top comment is worth the read.) Disclaimer: I think Erlang/OTP rocks. But JVM has its moments, as well, and in fairness has earned the \"most advanced VM\" title."},{"id":647020,"author":"tezza","month":"2009-06","story":"Plurk Comet: Handling of 100.000+ open connections","stance":"reference","why":"Kilim talk showing Java message passing can be very fast.","quote":"In defence of Java, it is very quick when done right. See Kilim[1] for examples.","text":"SPEED :: In defence of Java, it is very quick when done right. See Kilim[1] for examples. SUPPORT :: On top of the speed Java has a supported hardware stack (Solaris). So if doing 100K connections is important to you, you can find engineers who can diagnose difficult problems on your whole stack. -------------- [1] [VIDEO] at 23:23 . Java is quick without Kilim too, this is just an example with stats."}]},"6qD9XElTpCE":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an oral history of the algorithm straight from its inventor.","q":{"x":"Shor (yes the one who invented Shor's Algorithm) gave a create 30 min presentation here","i":27850847,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":32294691,"author":"FartyMcFarter","month":"2022-07","story":"Shor, I’ll do it (2007)","stance":"rec","why":"Peter Shor discusses invention of the algorithm.","quote":"There's a video featuring Peter Shor about the invention of this algorithm","text":"I don't fully understand all the details, but even I can tell that this algorithm is beautiful. Mixing together the concepts of modular sequences, periods, and Fourier transforms, plus doing this fast with computers that barely exist in order to find factors or numbers is just an amazing construction. There's a video featuring Peter Shor about the invention of this algorithm: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27850847,"author":"freemint","month":"2021-07","story":"Google tries out error correction on its quantum processor","stance":"rec","why":"Shor gives oral history of his algorithm and error correcting codes.","quote":"Shor (yes the one who invented Shor's Algorithm) gave a create 30 min presentation here","text":"If you are interested in the oral histoy of Shors algorithm and error correcting quantum codes. Shor (yes the one who invented Shor's Algorithm) gave a create 30 min presentation here [VIDEO] ."},{"id":40503340,"author":"shagie","month":"2024-05","story":"What You Shouldn't Know About Quantum Computers","stance":"reference","why":"Shor tells the story of his algorithm.","quote":"Hear the story of Shor's Algorithm, straight from the source, Peter Shor.","text":"[VIDEO] > Hear the story of Shor's Algorithm, straight from the source, Peter Shor. Shor's part of the story starts in '81. Though, I generally agree with the it is hard to make predictions that far out. We've got the math to do some higher things, but we still don't have flying cars and fusion power."}],"r":[["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["MkJkyMuBm3g",1],["C2RO34b_oPM",1]]},"FlQYU3m1e80":{"wy":"HN points to it as a convincing energy-budget analysis in debates over space datacenters, while debating its assumptions.","q":{"x":"the Scott Manley video alas has sort of convinced me this is pretty feasible, that the energy budget is quite doable.","i":48529335,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48529335,"author":"jauntywundrkind","month":"2026-06","story":"Cooling in Space","stance":"rec","why":"Convincing energy budget analysis, though somewhat generous in estimates","quote":"the Scott Manley video alas has sort of convinced me this is pretty feasible, that the energy budget is quite doable.","text":"I went in wanting this to look real bad. But the Scott Manley video alas has sort of convinced me this is pretty feasible, that the energy budget is quite doable. [VIDEO] I do think this is a pretty generous estimate he ends up at. I don't think emissivity is perfectly well dealt with. I don't know if his earth reflection emissivity includes the high energy thermal transfer from particles that do exist on the near vacuum (exosphere be hit, ya'll) or if that matters. But his figures seem ballpark correct & my concerns are at best marginal."},{"id":48122408,"author":"db48x","month":"2026-05","story":"Starship V3","stance":"rec","why":"Runs the numbers showing satellites can radiate heat from their own surface","quote":"So please follow along as Scott Manley runs the numbers","text":"It’s very easy to overestimate the difficulty of cooling things in space, unless you actually run the numbers. So please follow along as Scott Manley runs the numbers: < [VIDEO] >. Basically a Starlink v3 satellite has an estimated power budget of 20kW. Add in the heat absorbed from the environment (both directly from sunlight and reflected off of the Earth) and you’ll find that it must reject about 22kW of heat. That’s a fair amount, but at 65°C it can radiate it all away just using it’s own surface area! No radiator required at all! Of course the power density of computer racks has been going up over the years. If you want to reach 100kW per satellite then they will need a modest radiator, but nothing extravagant. It would still be smaller than the solar panels, and far smaller than the ones on the ISS. And don’t forget that because radiated heat goes up as the fourth power of temperature, raising the temperature of the system by even a small amount raises the radiation emitted by a lot. If you design the system to run hotter you can minimize the size of the radiator. Most chips these days are designed to max out at 100°C to 110°C without lasting damage, although running them at that temperature 24/7 may reduce their lifespan. There will be some sweet spot in the middle. And it turns out that a Starlink v3 already has a volume somewhat larger than a 48U rack. You talk about launching 250k satellites in order to have 250k GPUs in orbit, but that’s ridiculous. A real compute "},{"id":48556794,"author":"jauntywundrkind","month":"2026-06","story":"Cooling in Space","stance":"reference","why":"Starts from 80C and discusses a higher-temperature tweet and its density","quote":"","text":"He starts with something hot today but viable, 80C. And he goes on to cite and discuss a M*sk tweet about hotter, 97C, and what that density that enables. [VIDEO] and [VIDEO]"},{"id":48563200,"author":"keeganpoppen","month":"2026-06","story":"SpaceX to buy Cursor AI coding agent operator Anysphere for $60B","stance":"reference","why":"Shows space datacenter cooling in a Starlink-like form factor","quote":"it actually is not that impossible, and in fact can be done in a form factor not that different from Starlink satellites","text":"it actually is not that impossible, and in fact can be done in a form factor not that different from Starlink satellites: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48117750,"author":"YZF","month":"2026-05","story":"Starship V3","stance":"reference","why":"Scott Manley concludes cooling a datacenter in space is not impossible","quote":"tl;dr -> not impossible.","text":"[VIDEO] \"Is It Really Impossible To Cool A Datacenter In Space?\" - Scott Manley tl;dr -> not impossible."},{"id":48218381,"author":"roer","month":"2026-05","story":"SpaceX S-1","stance":"reference","why":"Scott Manley's math on space cooling, with corrections in top comment","quote":"The math still checks out though.","text":"I don't see how that would lessen their concerns? Exactly because space is a vacuum, cooling is harder since you can't do convection. The math still checks out though. Scott Manley did a video on it, and the top comment has some corrections: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47614660,"author":"shagie","month":"2026-04","story":"SpaceX Files to Go Public","stance":"critical","why":"Scott Manley's analysis of cooling datacenters in space, questioned on one-rack-per-satellite assumption","quote":"The bit that I wasn't entirely sold on with his analysis was that he was working on 1 satellite per rack","text":"The bit that I wasn't entirely sold on with his analysis was that he was working on 1 satellite per rack [VIDEO] (YouTube auto transcribe) > ... but how do you scale this up right 20 kow used to be enough to power a full rack of computer gear but we're seeing predictions now of 100 kilowatts per rack and that's just one rack in a data center racks right the 48U 19in rack which is you know big and it's just dense with computers how does that fit into a flat satellite well it turns out that like 19in rack is about 50 cm wide it's about 1 m deep per unit and if you've got a 24 1/2 square meter satellite and you take all your one U units and stack them ... One rack per satellite doesn't seem like it is that compelling of a story. Put a 100 kW rack in my basement in the winter and hook up the power and I'll be happy to deal with the waste heat for several months (and then ship it to somewhere in the southern hemisphere)."}],"r":[["YE8MVNMzpbo",1],["aeWyp2vXxqA",1],["wymmCdLdPvM",1],["c33AZBnRHks",1],["6JwEYamjXpA",1],["c5QF2HjHLSE",1],["tAACYA1Mwv4",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["oaDTiWaYfcM",1]]},"HA7GwKXfJB0":{"wy":"HN links it as an accessible visual explanation of the problem of mapping brain connectivity.","q":{"x":"found this TED talk that visualizes the problem pretty well","i":10120312,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10120312,"author":"mentos","month":"2015-08","story":"Silicon Brain: 100,000 ARM cores [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes the problem of mapping brain connections","quote":"found this TED talk that visualizes the problem pretty well","text":"What technology do we have right now to map the connections of the brain? Is there anything that currently exists with enough granularity to take a snapshot of our neurons and their connections? edit: thanks for the replies, did some research found this TED talk that visualizes the problem pretty well [VIDEO]"},{"id":6397860,"author":"SapphireSun","month":"2013-09","story":"Daniel Dennett: The Normal Well-Tempered Mind","stance":"rec","why":"Explains mapping the brain's connectome","quote":"Here are two fun explanatory videos from TED/TEDx","text":"I really like this conception of brains working as internally competing and cooperating ferral units. I'll have to give it some more thought in the morning. I do have an entreaty for you though: In this era of high throughput computing, elegant biotechnology, and the coming of the BRAIN initiative from the national level, I believe the best way to attack the problems of mind/brain right now is hard work and the scientific method. We need more data and we need to analyze it. We're hiring developers for eyewire.org - a game to map the brain by crowd sourcing/gamifying the analysis of electron micrographs of stained retinal slices from mice. We're developing the tools to map the connectome. For real. The PI for my lab, Dr. Sebastian Seung, is mentioned in this article, and I love working with him. There are other absolutely incredible people working here too that will blow your mind. Send an email to support at eyewire dot org if you want in or email me personally (see link in my profile) if you want more details. (I won't be able to get back to you instantly - I'm totally swamped, but if you're hired, based on my experiences so far, it'll be worth the wait). Here are two fun explanatory videos from TED/TEDx: [VIDEO] - Sebastian Seung talking connectomes [LINK] - Amy Robinson talking EyeWire Thanks for reading. I hope this post isn't too far out of our community norms - I don't see too many (large) job postings in the comments, but I felt like this article is the perfect place t"},{"id":7569853,"author":"christiangenco","month":"2014-04","story":"Cryonics or why freezing your body could be a good idea","stance":"reference","why":"Connectome scanning mentioned as an alternative to freezing","quote":"","text":"There's a great Penn & Teller \"Bullshit\" segment about Cryonics (mildly NSFW; it's Penn & Teller): [LINK] Is it possible? Certainly - the Wood Frog evolved to freeze itself every winter[1] and successfully unthaw itself. It floods its blood with sugars so it doesn't form ice crystals, solving the problem of tissue destruction, then unthaws in the Spring from the outside in and restarts its biological systems. How does modern cryonics solve this problem? Antifreeze. The fluid that poisons you if it enters your system[2]. For you to be revived, then, we'd have to figure out: * how to unthaw your body in a way that won't destroy essential parts of it * how to cure you of Ethylene glycol poisoning * how to cure you of the thing you died from * how to bring you back to life Perhaps a clever solution will be thought up that will just do a quick scan of your connectome[3] and upload it to a simulation. But why would anyone go through the trouble? Presumably this would be many years in the future. Would anyone even care enough in an overpopulated future-Earth to start bringing back their ancestors? When's the last time you called your grandparents? The bottom line is that if you're truly desperate for an \"afterlife\" (or, perhaps more accurately, extra-life), cryonics is probably your best bet presently. That bet, however, is rationally just an incredible waste of money that would more effectively prolong your effect on the world if instead it was used to support the people and ideals"}],"r":[["QPKKQnijnsM",1],["D1R-jKKp3NA",1],["rQKis2Cfpeo",1],["ZggCipbiHwE",1]]},"O1lZ9n2bxWA":{"wy":"HN points to it as the informative explanation of US turn signal quirks whenever signal design comes up.","q":{"x":"did a very informative (though a bit ranting) video on them in the US. Worth a watch and really interesting.","i":28661929,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28661929,"author":"gizdan","month":"2021-09","story":"Congratulations, Mini, you made the stupidest turn signals ever","stance":"rec","why":"Informative, slightly ranting video on US turn signals","quote":"did a very informative (though a bit ranting) video on them in the US. Worth a watch and really interesting.","text":"Speaking of terrible turn signals, Technology Connections on YouTube did a very informative (though a bit ranting) video on them in the US. Worth a watch and really interesting. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25567817,"author":"ljf","month":"2020-12","story":"Why Is There a Bucatini Shortage in America?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains US brake-light turn signals and their issues","quote":"good video on the set up (and issues)","text":"For those who haven't been to the US, I was blown away that some cars use brake lights as the turn indicator - good video on the set up (and issues): [VIDEO]"},{"id":39599306,"author":"branko_d","month":"2024-03","story":"European crash tester says carmakers must bring back physical controls","stance":"reference","why":"Technology Connections covered red US turn signals","quote":"Technology Connections channel had a video about that a while back","text":"> Then again, it also boggles my mind how car makers in the US continue to use flashing red lights as the turn signal instead of yellow lights. Technology Connections channel had a video about that a while back: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28663081,"author":"masklinn","month":"2021-09","story":"Congratulations, Mini, you made the stupidest turn signals ever","stance":"reference","why":"US allows red turn signals and flashing brake lights","quote":"The US allows not only red turn signals, but implementing turn signals by flashing brake lights.","text":"> I seem to recall reading amber turn signals are required the EU but not US. Yes ( [VIDEO] ). The US allows not only red turn signals, but implementing turn signals by flashing brake lights."}],"r":[["u4ZoJKF_VuA",1]]},"OMi6xgdSbMA":{"wy":"Recommended for its insight into game design and cited as a vivid example of how tricky localization can be.","q":{"x":"combining one of my fave video series with one of my fave gamedevs.","i":32372340,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32372340,"author":"collegeburner","month":"2022-08","story":"Cramming 'Papers, Please' onto Phones","stance":"rec","why":"War Stories episode with Lucas Pope on Obra Dinn.","quote":"combining one of my fave video series with one of my fave gamedevs.","text":"Arstechnica did a war stories with him on Return of the Obra Dinn, combining one of my fave video series with one of my fave gamedevs. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22628693,"author":"aequitas","month":"2020-03","story":"Why Adventure Games Suck (1989)","stance":"rec","why":"Insights into Obra Dinn game design.","quote":"Also worth watching are these insights into the game design","text":"Pope nailed in with Obra Dinn in how he implemented the detective gameplay and story telling. The game had me with several wtf story moments. This is one of those games that I'm reluctant to start playing as I don't want it to end to soon but can't stop playing once I've started it. Also worth watching are these insights into the game design (and a chance to enjoy the soundtrack again) (spoiler warning): [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":37088339,"author":"dale_glass","month":"2023-08","story":"Internationalization (i18n) best practices for front-end developers","stance":"reference","why":"Example of tricky localization of items in Obra Dinn.","quote":"as the maker of Obra Dinn discovered","text":"A particularly tricky case of this is with usernames and user defined content. Eg, a notification like \"Alice is online\" in some languages requires knowing Alice's gender. Which may be something that's not even stored anywhere in the system. There's probably some language out there that requires some other piece of personal info for a correct translation. To make things tricky, try having a multitude of items that you refer to: \"You're holding a dagger\". Now you need to have a serious discussion with your translators, because this is going to get all kinds of tricky, as the maker of Obra Dinn discovered: [VIDEO] And to make things extra-tricky, allow users to create content. \"Alice gave you a banana\", where \"banana\" is a custom object Alice made herself. Most translation efforts seem to give up at this point and resort to something stilted like \"Alice: online\""}],"r":[["NcOb3Dilzjc",1],["Gksxu-yeWcU",1]]},"Yo8kfV9kONw":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as evidence that the BBB is a pay-to-play shakedown whenever BBB ratings or accreditation come up.","q":{"x":"Watch the whole video if you haven't seen it before.","i":7039819,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7039819,"author":"aestra","month":"2014-01","story":"Court to Yelp: Reveal names of negative reviewers","stance":"rec","why":"20/20 investigation of BBB.","quote":"Watch the whole video if you haven't seen it before.","text":"Hey, that's the BBB we are talking about here. [VIDEO] Watch the whole video if you haven't seen it before."},{"id":2838124,"author":"rkalla","month":"2011-08","story":"My experience with a 'negative review' scammer.","stance":"rec","why":"20/20 cover story on BBB practices.","quote":"For anyone else interested in this topic (or doubtful about the claim) 20/20 did an cover story on this","text":"For anyone else interested in this topic (or doubtful about the claim) 20/20 did an cover story on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7135313,"author":"aestra","month":"2014-01","story":"Square thinks I don’t exist","stance":"reference","why":"20/20 on BBB shakedown.","quote":"The BBB is a scam.","text":"They just have to pay the BBB tax to remove it. [VIDEO] That's the shakedown the GP was referring to. The BBB is a scam."},{"id":3325625,"author":"tonio09","month":"2011-12","story":"College: The biggest scam we'll ever buy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think college is like better business bureau. A scam. You can learn everything you want yourself for free, instead of going to a college and pay an enormous amount of money for it. Especially now with the Internet, most materials you find online are multiple times better quality than what you get at college. Most graduates need to receive considerable additional training when they join a company. Bottom line is: You don't go to college to learn; you go to college to get a paper certificate that you can show to others. It's the problem of signalling and unfortunately, there's no easy solution for it. Colleges profit from the information imbalance and lack of trust in the job market. Some people recommend that instead of your certificates, your past projects should make up your resume. But then you get a catch 22 because others won't risk starting a project with you, unless they can see that you're capable from your resume. The only way to break out of this is the people you're going to work with must already know your abilities beforehand. Hence, you can start a start-up with your close friends or on your own, or become a vice president by knowing the right people at a company. For everything else, you need a resume for which Colleges are the gate keepers. And they won't give away that power for free. So enjoy your debt. Signalling [LINK] Better Business Bureau Scam [VIDEO] p.s. I can't say that the free market can sort college tuition fees out or colleges should be regulate"},{"id":3067989,"author":"rkalla","month":"2011-10","story":"FeeFighters Loses BBB Accreditation Over Investigative Blog Post","stance":"reference","why":"20/20 found BBB ratings tied to paid signups.","quote":"20/20 did an investigation[1] of the BBB and found exactly the same thing.","text":"For anyone on the fence about the claims, 20/20 did an investigation[1] of the BBB and found exactly the same thing. They worked with companies with complaints against them and low ratings that were called by BBB representatives asking them to re-up their registrations. Without much coaxing the BBB agents clarified that the ratings could be \"reinstated\" or \"take care of\" if the signup process was completed. Once the businesses did that, in every case, the scores were re-adjusted to A or A+ for those companies. Conversely, companies that didn't re-up would have all their past complaints re-instated on their review page and scores drop to C/D/F levels. Not unlike the Yelp stuff we saw going on last week or the week before here on HN. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2960096,"author":"rkalla","month":"2011-09","story":"Lone Yelp review dogs business owner","stance":"reference","why":"20/20 look at how BBB operates.","quote":"here is a 20/20 investigation look at it","text":"For those that haven't seen it, this is exactly how the Better Business Bureau (BBB) operates as well, here is a 20/20 investigation look at it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1984297,"author":"cdr","month":"2010-12","story":"Is the Better Business Bureau a protection racket?","stance":"reference","why":"20/20 investigation of BBB finances and executive pay.","quote":"The executives of the BBB make hundreds of thousands a year, according to 20/20","text":"The BBB is a hugely profitable enterprise with a huge payroll. I'm not sure they're even classified as a non-profit. The executives of the BBB make hundreds of thousands a year, according to 20/20: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["tk862BbjWx4",1]]},"ZXtimhT-ff4":{"wy":"HN users use it as a concentration, sleep and tinnitus aid, preferring steady noise over music.","q":{"x":"I've experimented with different kinds of noise, but this type seems to work the best","i":11933482,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11933482,"author":"keketi","month":"2016-06","story":"Tinnitus in relation to neck/head muscle tension","stance":"rec","why":"Pink noise to give hearing something to work with against tinnitus.","quote":"I've experimented with different kinds of noise, but this type seems to work the best","text":"I was in a similar situation a while ago. You should check out this app: [LINK] I went to a tinnitus clinic and they did a profile of the tinnitus I hear. Then they configured this app to amplify sounds of those frequencies to mask my tinnitus. When I'm hearing a low buzz the app can almost completely remove it, but it doesn't work quite as well with a high frequencies. You'll need rubber ear buds that form a tight fit with your ear canal. Since the app only amplifies sounds it doesn't really do anything in a totally quiet environment. You might want to play pink noise to give your hearing something to work with. I've experimented with different kinds of noise, but this type seems to work the best: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5873986,"author":"Diederich","month":"2013-06","story":"Music to Let you Concentrate","stance":"rec","why":"Ten hours of pink noise as concentration aid instead of music.","quote":"These days, I tend to listen to this","text":"These days, I tend to listen to this: [VIDEO] I have a music composition and conducting background, so any kind of music ends up distracting me at some level."},{"id":32016387,"author":"fictorial","month":"2022-07","story":"Music for Programming","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here are some artists, tools, etc that I have experienced","text":"Here are some artists, tools, etc that I have experienced \"in the zone\" states which I absolutely cannot achieve on my own without some kind of stimulation. Deepchord (dub techno) – [LINK] Binaural Beats – [LINK] Pink Noise – [VIDEO] Sci-Fi background – [LINK]"},{"id":18560635,"author":"fernandokokocha","month":"2018-11","story":"Ask HN: What's your routine/ways to keep being productive day by day?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"listening to noise instead of music while working","text":"I've read a few books on the topic recently, here's what works for me (some followed by resources): - going sleep early-ish (before 11 PM) - at least 7 hours of sleep - swimming before work - cutting down on meat - less worry (stoicism) - \"Happy\", Derren Brown - writing down a master to-do list of literally everything (it litters your head, really) - \"Getting Things Done\", David Allen - saying \"no\" to things you're not certain about - staying out of internet (internet-less Pomodoros) - \"Deep Work\", Cal Newport - listening to noise instead of music while working - [VIDEO] But honestly, first 3 bullets are the most important ones; if done, I'm super concentrated the whole day (strangely enough, these 3 are basic stuff, you probably already know that from your mama)."},{"id":9244695,"author":"machinshin_","month":"2015-03","story":"Ask HN: Do you meditate?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"any of the above while having having pink noise playing","text":"Yes. I do a lot of different ways of it though, whatever strikes my mood. Much of it is the many variations in chinese martial arts: (either no music, or mild music, like rain patter, nothing w/ lyrics) I find meditation is a lot about your breath, so you can do all of these either with normal breathing or these variations: 1) n/n*2/x breathing. (i'm only at n=3 atm) 2) full lung & empty lung variations (this link does a decent job explaining : [LINK] ) The different types of mediation i do are: 1) moving: Tai Chi 2) standing: holding ball (from tai chi) 3) sitting : Grand Circle of Heaven (found this link which explains it well : [LINK] ) (imagine the energy flow in a circle though your body as you breath) 4)(This one i think i made up on my own) : any of the above while having having pink noise playing: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HI09kat_GeI",1],["kTKn1aSOyOs",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["frAEmhqdLFs",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["l7rce6IQDWs",1]]},"_YysNM2JoFo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a concise, quick explanation of the OEIS pattern when threads touch the integer sequence encyclopedia.","q":{"x":"Very good video on Sloane's Gap, for the impatient","i":6575520,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":13294968,"author":"Houshalter","month":"2017-01","story":"2017 is not just another prime number","stance":"rec","why":"Plot revealing underrepresented numbers in the OEIS","quote":"And if you plot each number by the number of times it occurs, it makes a really interesting pattern","text":"Well the first number that doesn't appear in OEIS is 18159. That make it interesting to me , but apparently this number isn't interesting to mathematicians. Of course since OEIS is finite, there will always be numbers which don't make it. What's really interesting is that some numbers are disproportionately underrepresented. They appear much less often than other numbers of the same size. And if you plot each number by the number of times it occurs, it makes a really interesting pattern: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6575520,"author":"jlgreco","month":"2013-10","story":"Sloane's Gap.","stance":"rec","why":"Concise explanation of Sloane's Gap","quote":"Very good video on Sloane's Gap, for the impatient","text":"Background on the On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences: [LINK] Very good video on Sloane's Gap, for the impatient: [VIDEO] The article is particularly interesting to me for it's examination of what complexity theory tells us to expect for the distribution of 'interesting' integer sequences. That cultural influences can (probably) have such a dramatic and measurable effect on something like mathematics is fascinating as well."},{"id":21368638,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-10","story":"The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the gap between clusters of integer importance","quote":"Neil Sloane appeared[4] several times on Numberphile.","text":"When you plot the \"importance\" N(n) of the positive integers, where N(n) = The number of occurrences of n in the OEIS an interesting anomaly appears[1]. The \"importance\" of the integers fall into two clusters, separated by Sloane's Gap [2][3]. Also, Neil Sloane appeared[4] several times on Numberphile. In the last few videos of that[4] playlist (\"Amazing Graphs\"), Sloane shares some of the particularly interesting sequences from the OEIS. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"}],"r":[["UyFF4cpMVag",1],["zQGOcOUBi6s",1],["HKQDSgBHPfY",1],["sULa9Lc4pck",1],["EgMqhEMhVV8",1],["nsjDnYxJ0bo",1]]},"yhGjCzxJV3E":{"wy":"HN points to it as the definitive example of making 3D rendering look like 2D animation through cel shading, outlines, and framerate tricks.","q":{"x":"showing the lengths they went to achieve a high-res hand-drawn look while developing Guilty Gear Xrd.","i":25357769,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":45024430,"author":"nathan_compton","month":"2025-08","story":"Will Smith's concert crowds are real, but AI is blurring the lines","stance":"rec","why":"How 3D simulates 2D animation and stylistic framerate choices.","quote":"if you watch this GDC talk about the way that Arc System Works uses 3d to simulate 2d animation","text":"This is an artistic choice with a variety of film precedents. Its not exactly the same thing, but if you watch this GDC talk about the way that Arc System Works uses 3d to simulate 2d animation, it gets some of the ideas across: [VIDEO] Artists might want to produce a lower framerate just to make something look filmic (eg, 25 frames per second) or hand animated, but it can also be a deliberate stylistic choice for other reasons. Eg, the recentish Mad Max films used subtle undercranking to make action scenes feel more intense, and part of that effect is a more noticeable frames and I think there is a bit of that in the Spiderverse films too."},{"id":25357769,"author":"flobosg","month":"2020-12","story":"Designing 2D graphics in the Japanese industry","stance":"rec","why":"Lengths taken to achieve a hand-drawn look in 3D.","quote":"showing the lengths they went to achieve a high-res hand-drawn look while developing Guilty Gear Xrd.","text":"Nice resource. While not strictly 2D, it brings to mind this GDC talk [1,2] from Arc System Works showing the lengths they went to achieve a high-res hand-drawn look while developing Guilty Gear Xrd. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":33535953,"author":"grumbel","month":"2022-11","story":"AI and the Future of Pixel Art","stance":"reference","why":"Example of 3D with trickery to look 2D.","quote":"","text":"Only if you keep it really low resolution and low framerate. If you want something higher resolution, multiple camera angles or fluidly animated doing it in 2D quickly becomes impractical. Guilty Gears for example started out with 2D sprites and then went 3D with Guilty Gears Xrd[1][2], but with a lot of trickery to keep it looking 2D'ish, though that always was more anime-style than pixel art. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19896810,"author":"corysama","month":"2019-05","story":"3D Game Shaders for Beginners","stance":"reference","why":"End result of iterating on cel shading and outlines.","quote":"Many iterations and improvements from there gets you to this","text":"A very simple implementation of cell shading is to run the result of traditional lighting through a lookup table in the form of a 1D texture. Having runs and steps of solid color posterizes the smooth lighting input so that it resembles painted cell artwork. A very simple implementation of outlining is to draw the object a second time solid black, with the vertex positions extruded out in the direction of the vertex normals, and with backface culling flipped so that you see only internal faces. Those black, internal faces peeking out around the edge of the regular mesh form the outlines. That's a good starting point. Many iterations and improvements from there gets you to this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RjD1aLm4Thg",1]]},"-s5szfPYKY4":{"wy":"HN cites it in threads on sugar, nutrition science and industry influence over health research.","q":{"x":"This little panel discussion on the sugar industry’s efforts is a great watch.","i":30464150,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30464150,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2022-02","story":"Soybean oil affects hypothalamus, causes genetic changes in mice: study (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Panel on the sugar industry's efforts to mis-educate the public.","quote":"This little panel discussion on the sugar industry’s efforts is a great watch.","text":"Actually there has been a well documented effort by the sugar industry to mis-educate our way in to this crisis so it seems perfectly reasonable that better correct education about health could help a lot. It’s not just about not putting too much in your mouth, it is about making informed decisions about what to eat. It’s about deciding which foods to eat and understanding what the trade offs are between various choices. That’s non trivial and requires education to get right. For example for breakfast should you have a bagel and cream cheese, a bowl of cereal, eggs and toast, a grapefruit, or nothing at all? These are things we can educate on. I spent a month recently absorbing a lot of the videos created by NutritionFacts.org. The real answers are guided by scientific research and knowing them can make a huge difference. This little panel discussion on the sugar industry’s efforts is a great watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29849976,"author":"forkerenok","month":"2022-01","story":"Sugar not so nice for your child’s brain development","stance":"reference","why":"Endocrinologist explains fructose and fiber in more depth.","quote":"here's a more expanded explanation from the same endocrinologist in another video","text":"From that same video linked by the parent post ( [LINK] ). I watched it a while ago, so in my own words: The key is fiber. When you eat fruit, you consume fructose with fiber. Fiber hinders absorption of fructose. Many processed foods have a lot of fructose and no fiber, because fiber doesn't help the shelf life. EDIT: here's a more expanded explanation from the same endocrinologist in another video [VIDEO]"},{"id":29012797,"author":"RedBeetDeadpool","month":"2021-10","story":"Covid vaccine profits mint 9 new pharma billionaires","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on food industry influence over health science.","quote":"","text":"Can't disagree with that, better than any drug. They should be the first prescribed thing even though they aren't. But if that happens the definition of eating healthy will be compromised by the capitalistic superorganisms, so lets at least keep that in mind [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uxykI30fS54",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["EpRrD58Ah3Q",1],["KkpqA8yG9T4",1],["zb7Bs98KmnY",1],["YSfCdBBqNXY",1],["CM0aohBfUTc",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["hx7BXih7zx8",1],["tTBWfkE7BXU",1]]},"0E77j1imdhQ":{"wy":"Used as the real example of human-powered flapping flight, in contrast to hoaxes and other human-powered aircraft discussions.","q":{"x":"real science/engineering awesomeness like this","i":3745233,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":3745233,"author":"knowtheory","month":"2012-03","story":"‘Bird Man’ Hoaxster Comes Clean on Dutch Television","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Real science and engineering awesomeness versus a hoax.","quote":"real science/engineering awesomeness like this","text":"Why not deride the film maker? The problem i have with him is the same problem that i have with all liars. Namely, that the world is such an interesting and fascinating place, that wasting time on hoaxes and stupid \"ha ha! i tricked you!\" sorts of media is largely pointless. This dude can claim that his effort was really an \"experiment\" about online media and how to tell a story, but frankly, I think that's bullshit. This guy isn't doing research about what it takes a viral video to study viral videos so that society might better understand how it is that information is passed between members of a network (and there are folks who study this sort of thing). This dude is just trying to make a project of his go viral. I really see very little distinction between this project and any other sort of fame-whoring, save for the amount of effort put in. So, please tell me if you can, why this hoax is an improvement, or even a useful fiction in the face of real science/engineering awesomeness like this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46454320,"author":"antipurist","month":"2026-01","story":"Reality crushed Ÿnsect startup that had raised over $600M for insect farming","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a human-powered ornithopter.","quote":"Human-powered ornithopter","text":"Human-carrying birds have been built. Piloted ornithopter: [LINK] Human-powered ornithopter: [VIDEO] Also, Quetzalcoatlus northropi could've been powerful enough to carry a human."},{"id":3993095,"author":"yurisagalov","month":"2012-05","story":"Atlas: A Human-Powered Helicopter","stance":"reference","why":"Same team set a Guinness record with a human-powered ornithopter.","quote":"the same team that recently set the Guinness World Record by building a Human Powered Ornithopter","text":"This project is from the same team that recently set the Guinness World Record by building a Human Powered Ornithopter ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":3995543,"author":"pygy_","month":"2012-05","story":"Atlas: A Human-Powered Helicopter","stance":"reference","why":"Shows sustained altitude and speed for nearly 20 seconds.","quote":"It sustained altitude and speed for a while (short of 20 seconds).","text":"> Where is the difference to a glider? It sustained altitude and speed for a while (short of 20 seconds). See this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3732669,"author":"katovatzschyn","month":"2012-03","story":"Man Supposedly Flies With Custom-Built Bird Wings","stance":"reference","why":"Real human-powered flapping flight by engineers over years.","quote":"Here is an example of what this man is claiming to have done, actually being done, by a team of engineers working for years","text":"The disregard for the physics of the situation is actually quite insulting. The \"demonstration\" is so far from realistic its not even worth a debunking. Here is an example of what this man is claiming to have done, actually being done, by a team of engineers working for years: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3732856,"author":"6ren","month":"2012-03","story":"Man Supposedly Flies With Custom-Built Bird Wings","stance":"reference","why":"Comparison: a human-powered articulated wing.","quote":"As comparison, a human-powered articulated wing","text":"Conceptually plausible: the cyclic motion of articulated wings (flapping) seems understood in models e.g. [LINK] Jarno's idea of a powered exoskeleton wing to provide x20 power is right in principle, but needs light batteries: Prof Otten [LINK] As comparison, a human-powered articulated wing: [VIDEO] A non-articulated wing [LINK] (18km) And the famous Gossamer Albatross flew from England to France [LINK] Also [LINK] To the downvoters: haters gonna hate, hackers gonna hack"}]},"E3URhJx0NSw":{"wy":"During early Covid, HN recommended it as an in-depth, informed explanation of transmission and outbreak dynamics.","q":{"x":"I urge everyone of you to watch this interview and to be more informed.","i":22550511,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22550511,"author":"spectramax","month":"2020-03","story":"Don't Touch Ya Face","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Epidemiologist explains airborne spread, masks and hygiene in detail","quote":"I urge everyone of you to watch this interview and to be more informed.","text":"Dr. Michael Osterholm was on Joe Rogan's podcast explaining in detail how this virus spreads [1] - Washing hands and not touching face is inconclusive but it makes sense to keep up the hygiene. That's not the primary means of how this virus spreads. - Virus primarily spreads via airborne means, simply by breathing (not even coughing or sneezing) is enough. It stays suspended in air (you can imagine the negligible weight of a 0.1um particle compared to the fluid mechanical forces from circulating air). - N95 masks (contrarily to the popular myth) are the most effective means of stopping the spread. Shortage of masks for hospital workers is an orthogonal issue. I urge everyone of you to watch this interview and to be more informed. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22556585,"author":"cletus","month":"2020-03","story":"Why some governments appear not to be acting on the Covid-19 threat","stance":"rec","why":"Long discussion of outbreak duration, risk factors, school closures and vaccine timelines","quote":"So I encourage people to go watch the Joe Rogan podcast on this","text":"So I encourage people to go watch the Joe Rogan podcast on this [1]. A few snippets: - He describes this as more like a \"winter\" than a \"blizzard\" in that this is going to go on for months; - No one really knows what the mortality rate is but it's become clear there are certain risk factors eg the fatality rate among people over 70 in China is about 3x for men than women but men are much more likely to smoke than women in China in this age group. - Keeping schools closed is a mixed bag. For one, kids below 9 or so don't seem to get sick or die. In China only 2% of cases are under age 19. There are other diseases like this (eg Hepatitis-A). If you keep kids home, some people will lose their jobs or simply be unable to work as they take care of those children. Some of those people will be health care professionals. The lack of those will likely kill people; - It's unclear yet what other risk factors (other than age and smoking) there are. Obesity in the developed world is a big cause for concern as this unfolds. A stat quoted is that 45% of people in the US aged 45 and older are obese or severely obese; - It's largely a question of when not if you'll get this; - It's a myth that we'll have a vaccine before ~18 months. You can't rush this. It's like trying to rush a pregnancy. - Italy is a window to how most places will be in ~3 weeks; - We, as a society, have a short attention span. We could've developed a vaccine for coronaviruses after previous outbreaks (SARS/MERS) but there"},{"id":22590705,"author":"ApolloRising","month":"2020-03","story":"Ask HN: Where to get legitimate information about the coronavirus?","stance":"rec","why":"Informative interview with a leading infectious disease epidemiologist","quote":"This was very helpful to me","text":"This was very helpful to me: [VIDEO] Michael Osterholm is an internationally recognized expert in infectious disease epidemiology. He is Regents Professor, McKnight Presidential Endowed Chair in Public Health, the director of the Center for Infectious Disease Research and Policy (CIDRAP), Distinguished Teaching Professor in the Division of Environmental Health Sciences, School of Public Health, a professor in the Technological Leadership Institute, College of Science and Engineering, and an adjunct professor in the Medical School, all at the University of Minnesota. Look for his book \"Deadliest Enemy: Our War Against Deadly Germs\" for more info. [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":32649958,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2022-08","story":"Observations from our Joe Rogan Experience experience","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an example of a good early Rogan interview","quote":"his interview with Michael Osterholm [1] was actually really good","text":"> Joe also likes to deflect ... I think there's some truth to what he says here. For some topics he actually just does let people talk. And that's fine. But there are some topics where Joe isn't just \"asking questions\" but is actively pushing a particular viewpoint. The first and most obvious one is vaccines. Covid has broken this man's brain. If you go back to March 2020, his interview with Michael Osterholm [1] was actually really good. It was one of the first I saw that recognized how serious this actually was. But for whatever reasons, Joe has decided vaccines are bad and he's had a parade of grifters and charlatans on to back up that view (eg Robert Malone). There is absolutely no evidence in the world that will change his mind. The second is all his manosphere [2] content. He really rode the wave of popularity of Jordan Peterson in particular and really gave him a platform with no pushback whatsoever. The recent rise of Andrew Tate is just the latest manifestation of this (fun fact: Tate dated JP's daughter a couple of years ago). The third is transgender people. Trans people broke a lot of people in a way that's reminiscent of 1990s era homophobia. Bill Maher is another example of this. So a big part of Joe's popularity isn't just a \"dumb guy asking questions\", it's that he pushes very normative and popular opinions. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":23995639,"author":"aaron695","month":"2020-07","story":"Hygiene Theater Is a Waste of Time","stance":"reference","why":"Osterholm on handwashing being of limited use against a respiratory virus","quote":"Michael Osterholm talks a bit on this in March and how hand washing is somewhat pointless","text":"Michael Osterholm talks a bit on this in March and how hand washing is somewhat pointless - [VIDEO] You can talk about hand washing stopping other diseases etc but by ignoring how C19 is about breathing why would you expect people to suddenly believe masks matter. Even pushing outdoor dining and activities, if we are taught it's contact, once again people won't respond."},{"id":22550414,"author":"simplyinfinity","month":"2020-03","story":"Denmarks shuts down all schools, daycare facilities, universities, highschools","stance":"reference","why":"CDC-linked expert says young people mostly don't get severely sick","quote":"kids up to 19 mostly don't get sick, or if they do, it's not as bad as in adults over 40","text":"According to cdc expert, kids up to 19 mostly don't get sick, or if they do, it's not as bad as in adults over 40 [VIDEO]"},{"id":22550873,"author":"Consultant32452","month":"2020-03","story":"Denmarks shuts down all schools, daycare facilities, universities, highschools","stance":"reference","why":"Explains social distancing as slowing spread to protect healthcare capacity","quote":"Basically all of this social distancing is about slowing the spread so as to not overwhelm the healthcare system, not stop it.","text":"Yes. The Joe Rogan experience episode with Michael Osterholm talked about this. Basically all of this social distancing is about slowing the spread so as to not overwhelm the healthcare system, not stop it. They can calculate how much you impact the speed of spread if you limit gatherings to 1k, 100, 50, 5, etc. From there it's a somewhat subjective risk assessment of what you want to recommend, bearing in mind that destroying the economy results in deaths from downstream effects. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22553586,"author":"sb057","month":"2020-03","story":"Don't Touch Ya Face","stance":"reference","why":"Epidemiologist on airborne transmissibility and limited value of face-touching advice","quote":"the reason this strain is wrecking so much havoc is because of how transmissible it is","text":"Michael Osterholm of the University of Minnesota said as much on JRE yesterday.[1] Obviously touching your face won't decrease your chances of catching coronavirus (or any other disease), but the reason this strain is wrecking so much havoc is because of how transmissible it is, especially through simple breathing. The implication being, if you get it on your hands, you've almost certainly already breathed it in. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22573315,"author":"dba7dba","month":"2020-03","story":"South Korea's drive-through testing for coronavirus","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter has watched the episode and vouches the claim isn't a lie","quote":"This is not a flat out lie.","text":"I would love to see you link/source. I have watched [VIDEO] with Michael Osterholm as guest. This is not a flat out lie."},{"id":22580407,"author":"nate_meurer","month":"2020-03","story":"Open letter from Italy to the international scientific community","stance":"reference","why":"Osterholm describes wet markets as breeding grounds for new diseases","quote":"the perfect experiment that no university would let you do","text":"No, not even close. Those wet markets are a fucking nightmare. There are truly optimized for pulling new diseases into the human population. Thousands of animals, hundreds of different species, many known to be reservoirs of potential human pathogens, crammed together in close proximity with each other and with humans who eat them. In his interview with Joe Rogan, Michael Osterholm called it \"the perfect experiment that no university would let you do\": [VIDEO] Their discussion of wet markets starts at around 1:01:00."},{"id":22601564,"author":"jMyles","month":"2020-03","story":"Shelter in place for Bay Area counties","stance":"reference","why":"Osterholm's views on shelter-in-place and school closures","quote":"his recent (very informative) interview with Joe Rogan","text":"> these steps are informed, authoritarian, and right-headed I have to say, I think this is a very insensitive thing to say. Being in a position of privilege to likely avoid the consequences of authoritarian tendencies is wonderful, but most people don't enjoy such a position. Authoritarian tactics are per se wrong. > And with all due respect, what relevance does Vitamin D have to do with the price of tea in China? I hope you're not suggesting this thing will magically go away if we just get our vitamin supplementation right. I didn't suggest that, no. But it is one of the interventions that makes sense. And generally, obviously, fresh air and exercise are important for overall health and wellness. Tangentially related: warmth and humidity is likely, according to some researchers, to drastically reduce replication and transmission. [0] [1] So getting out in the sun (with reasonable social distancing) can't hurt, and it might help. (Even more tangentially: some researchers believe that very high heats, such as with sauna therapy, can even further reduce replication, based on this 2004 paper [2]) 0: [LINK] 1: [LINK] 2: [LINK] > Do you have a citation on CIDRAP opposing school closures? I couldn't easily find it. Michael Osterholm himself has spoken extensively on his apprehension about shelter-in-place as a policy to combat coronaviruses, including COVID-19, including during his recent (very informative) interview with Joe Rogan, during which he specifically discussed school clo"},{"id":22643497,"author":"jacobedawson","month":"2020-03","story":"State projections for Covid-19","stance":"reference","why":"Succinct explanation of droplet and aerosol spread","quote":"There is a very succinct explanation by Michael Osterholm","text":"There is a very succinct explanation by Michael Osterholm [1][2] where he says that the virus is spread both by droplets & aerosols, and that sharing the same air with another person is the primary vector (that isn't intended to sound as alarmist as it probably does). From what I understand the best prevention is to reduce face-to-face contact with other people where possible, but an N95 mask would definitely help prevent contraction in public spaces. Of course, these measures seem extreme since ~80% of people will barely notice they are infected / have mild symptoms, but it's all about peak-load reduction. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22694855,"author":"jcims","month":"2020-03","story":"Pangolins found to carry viruses related to Covid-19","stance":"reference","why":"Osterholm anticipated the pangolin finding in this episode","quote":"Michael Osterholm called this out during his episode on the Joe Rogan podcast back on 3/10","text":"Michael Osterholm called this out during his episode on the Joe Rogan podcast back on 3/10, presumably from prior research - [VIDEO]"},{"id":22717299,"author":"robocat","month":"2020-03","story":"Guidance against wearing masks for the coronavirus is wrong – cover your face","stance":"reference","why":"Distinguishes surgical masks from N95 and downplays handwashing at 43:20","quote":"Michael Osterholm (a very clued up epidemiologist) says","text":"That is pretty much correct from what I’ve read. But take care to differentiate between surgical masks (only help protect others), and N95 protects both ways (protects the wearer from others, protects others from the wearer). Go to 43:20 on [VIDEO] and Michael Osterholm (a very clued up epidemiologist) says: 1. Surgical masks help prevent transmission 2. N95 masks help prevent catching it Note also in that same section he says washing hands is probably a waste of time - because transmission vector is respiratory (duhhh). I think too much of our media and fear is concentrating hard on washing hands, with little science behind it for respitory viruses."}],"r":[["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["zmCTmgavkrQ",1],["Pld8wTa16Jk",1],["Qam5BkXIEhQ",1],["O5BJVO3PDeQ",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["rDPj5zI66LA",1],["U6qZPx4uD0g",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["pW-SOdj4Kkk",1],["f0gMdGrVteI",1]]},"eg3_kUaYFJA":{"wy":"Used as a joke or satire reference in moderation, free-speech and police threads.","q":{"x":"My all time favorite Trevor Moore sketch","i":28103885,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28103885,"author":"mabbo","month":"2021-08","story":"Trevor Moore has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite Trevor Moore sketch showing timing and delivery","quote":"My all time favorite Trevor Moore sketch","text":"My all time favorite Trevor Moore sketch: \"I want to kill the president\". [VIDEO] It showcases his wonderful way of speaking, his body language, the use of music to communicate the joke. The world is a less humorous place today."},{"id":38659212,"author":"googlryas","month":"2023-12","story":"Suspects can refuse to provide phone passcodes to police, court rules","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Your passphrase could be \"I want to kill the President of the United States of America\" USSS, please refer to: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33139234,"author":"paganel","month":"2022-10","story":"Why I think there's a one-in-six chance of an imminent global nuclear war","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant Whitest Kids U' Know sketch","text":"> Or “Russian oligarchs put plutonium in Putin’s coffee”. Saying that about the US president who was bankrolling a trillion dollars-plus invasion of Iraq and Afghanistan would have put one in jail, interesting to see that when applied to another country is now perfectly ok. Relevant Whitest Kids U' Know sketch [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25691011,"author":"lukifer","month":"2021-01","story":"Reddit bans subreddit group “r/DonaldTrump”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"My favorite satire on this dilemma","text":"I recommend digging back into TD.W posts from the 4th and 5th; while most content wouldn't pass a strict test of an explicit threat, their clear intent was not to protest or express dissent, but to prevent vote certification from taking place, along with the usual invectives about \"helicopter rides\" and \"Day of the Rope\", etc. The line is very tricky, as to what constitutes colorful metaphor, vs. explicit calls to violence. (My favorite satire on this dilemma: [VIDEO] ) Regardless, the emotional energy and goals of that community on Jan 6th was extremely clear: to alter the due process of law by threat of force. All that said, I'm quasi-absolutist on free speech issues, for the slippery-slope reasons you describe. Free speech doesn't end at 1A: it's a cultural value as well. Yet, it's also a tall order to enforce that value proactively: if a newspaper is not obligated to print every letter, should Twitter be obligated to let everyone have an account? There's a good argument for a \"digital commons\", and regulating tech giants as utilities; but the balance between an individual's freedom to share offensive views, and the freedom of service providers to choose to whom they offer service, is extremely murky."},{"id":19504512,"author":"lukifer","month":"2019-03","story":"Facebook to ban white nationalist content","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"My favorite edge case is this WKYK sketch, which is clearly satire","text":"> Let's start with curbing speech that encourages harming others. No doubt, explicit threats or advocacy/conspiracy to violence exist, and should not be tolerated. However, there are countless complicated grey areas subject to interpretation and potential bias. Was Kathy Griffin advocating violence by holding up a mock Trump severed head? Is “Abortion stops a beating heart” a coded message to inspire violence against abortion doctors? What about “Eat the rich” or “The only good billionaire is a dead billionaire”? My favorite edge case is this WKYK sketch, which is clearly satire, yet trivial to indict as an explicit call to unlawful violence: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4QXZ2RzN_Oo",1],["j3mhkYbznBk",1],["4jEz03Z8azc",1],["zFhYJRqz_xk",1],["2tdiKTSdE9Y",1],["HaEPXoXVf2k",1]]},"h_zgURwr6nA":{"wy":"HN links it to show the sheer complexity of EUV machines, especially when discussing whether competitors can challenge ASML.","q":{"x":"I recommend highly to everyone who thinks a startup can challenge ASML this 1.38min video","i":45953989,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45953989,"author":"ta12653421","month":"2025-11","story":"US startup Substrate announces chipmaking tool that it says will rival ASML","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short flight through an ASML High-NA machine showing why competing is hard.","quote":"I recommend highly to everyone who thinks a startup can challenge ASML this 1.38min video","text":"LOL, I recommend highly to everyone who thinks a startup can challenge ASML this 1.38min video: [VIDEO] Its an industrial flight video through a ASML TWIN Exe HighNAV. Here is also a presentation of ~45min at CCC / Germany (in English language) which I highly recommend to watch: [LINK] Especially the explanation how they use two milisecond laser shots to first molt the tin and then distribute it symetrically(!!!) with a second shot across the surface. There IS NO chance that this startup will be able to somehow compete, if even Chinese state actors cant do it (yet)."},{"id":42774503,"author":"danparsonson","month":"2025-01","story":"Dutch Align with US Export Controls on Some ASML Chip Tools","stance":"reference","why":"Promotional fly-through of ASML's machine.","quote":"Promotional fly through video from ASML here","text":"The rest of the machine is no less incredible - the EUV light is reflected off a series of mirrors (the largest weighing, IIRC, somewhere in the region of 300kg, and the largest that Zeiss have ever made) onto a mask of the target pattern, which is rapidly shuttling back and forth, onto another series of mirrors, and finally focused onto the wafer stage, which is also moving precisely in step with the mask, to expose the photoresist on a single die which is on the order of 10mm square. And each wafer goes through multiple cycles of this, so not only does the machine rapidly create features at the nanonmetre scale with incredible accuracy, but again and again in exactly the same place after removing and returning the wafer. Oh, and the wafer stage is pretty fun too; it uses an electrostatic chuck that induces a charge in the wafer and therefore holds it firmly in place without needing any kind of suction that could distort the wafer. Promotional fly through video from ASML here: [VIDEO] Asianometry video about the stage: [LINK] Another short video about the light source: [LINK]"},{"id":42277665,"author":"danparsonson","month":"2024-11","story":"ASML Twinscan EXE:5000 Lego Set","stance":"reference","why":"Promotional render of the real EUV machine.","quote":"Fly-through promotional render of the real thing","text":"Fly-through promotional render of the real thing: [VIDEO] Incredible technology - the light-source works by generating droplets of molten tin, firing them through a chamber at around (IIRC) 200mph, and hitting each droplet with two laser pulses... at 50kHz. How they achieve that using Lego is beyond me."},{"id":41470980,"author":"chasil","month":"2024-09","story":"China's chip capabilities just 3 years behind TSMC, teardown shows","stance":"reference","why":"Shows ASML's EUV droplets hit by two lasers.","quote":"I didn't realize that the droplets are hit twice by different lasers.","text":"I just found this one. [VIDEO] Edit: I didn't realize that the droplets are hit twice by different lasers. [LINK]"}],"r":[["NdppYYfQJgg",1]]},"ipAnwilMncI":{"wy":"Recommended as a well-produced investigation and cited as evidence of oligarch wealth around Putin.","q":{"x":"It's very well produced.","i":25885505,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25885505,"author":"tpmx","month":"2021-01","story":"Internet disrupted in Russia amid opposition protests","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Well-produced investigation into Putin's palace and the history of the bribe; English subtitles.","quote":"It's very well produced.","text":"So, Navalny produced this 1h52m Russian-language video that was published on Youtube 4-5 days ago, just after he returned to Russia and was arrested: [VIDEO] (it has English subtitles) Putin's palace. History of world's largest bribe It's very well produced. I saw all of it and it helped me understand a lot of the history of how the current gang of eh.. er, about how Putin got to where he is today. I really recommend watching it. After these 4-5 days, it has 74M views. The view count is at the moment still growing at ~0.7M views per hour - I just had to edit that view count. 70% of those views are from russian IPs, as per the Navalny people. Makes sense to me. Russia has a population of 144M people. I think it's only a matter of time until Putin/Russia bans Youtube."},{"id":43690297,"author":"shakna","month":"2025-04","story":"Harvard's response to federal government letter demanding changes","stance":"reference","why":"Putin's gold palace as example of billionaire hoarding.","quote":"Though Putin's gold palace did have to be stripped for fungal problems, later.","text":"> Nobody hoards wealth. They invest it. Nobody has a Smaug hoard. There are no Scrooge McDuck cash vaults. There are, unfortunately. [0] Though Putin's gold palace did have to be stripped for fungal problems, later. Musk does go around with a large amount of debt, such as the 13bil he currently owes. So he's less likely to have a prepper vault. That does not mean that human greed doesn't turn to cartoons for inspiration, at times. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26808253,"author":"sergeykish","month":"2021-04","story":"Is Russia going to war with Ukraine and other questions","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the palace investigation among Putin's internal problems.","quote":"","text":"Hello from Kharkiv, article is largely outdated. Russia invasion consolidated Ukrainians. Only NATO could bring peace to my country. Crimea with Russia returns to its native state, land without much water. That was a reason Crimea was handled to Ukraine, on its balance, it was not \"present\". Putin is trying to distract from internal problems — rising prices, peaceful meetings breakdown, his Palace [1], dying in prison opposition leader Navalny. And on top of it Baiden called Putin a killer. He is playing \"strong leader\" again. Full scale invasion is higly possible, but only because RF government is insane. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25933796,"author":"jjjeii3","month":"2021-01","story":"Peat fires continue to burn at air temperature of -50C in northeastern Yakutia","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for oligarch wealth and palaces versus wildfire funding.","quote":"Every single Putin's friend has become a dollar billionaire","text":"May be this is the reason: [VIDEO] Every single Putin's friend has become a dollar billionaire and they build multiple big palaces/resorts/etc. for him. But no money for fighting wildfires..."}],"r":[["wbSwFU6tY1c",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1]]},"-TKjwblp1XI":{"wy":"HN cites it as proof that infrastructure can be rebuilt very fast when incentives and urgency line up.","q":{"x":"The overpass collapsed on April 29th. They had it reopened to traffic 26 days later.","i":35253712,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35253712,"author":"viewtransform","month":"2023-03","story":"Why Construction Projects Always Go over Budget","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary of reopening a collapsed overpass in 26 days.","quote":"The overpass collapsed on April 29th. They had it reopened to traffic 26 days later.","text":"There's also the MacArthur Maze collapse in the Bay area 2007 [1] The overpass collapsed on April 29th. They had it reopened to traffic 26 days later. This was American leadership, project management and engineering at it's finest [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35254142,"author":"shagie","month":"2023-03","story":"Why Construction Projects Always Go over Budget","stance":"reference","why":"Counter example of rapid, incentivized freeway rebuild.","quote":"the rebuilding of the MacArthur Maze in 26 days back in 2007.","text":"For a counter example, the rebuilding of the MacArthur Maze in 26 days back in 2007. [LINK] > A contractor with a proven track record of rebuilding damaged freeways (most notably the Santa Monica Freeway after the 1994 Northridge earthquake) well ahead of schedule, C. C. Myers, Inc., submitted a winning bid of $876,075 to repair the damage to the I-580 connector. The bid was estimated to cover only one-third of the cost of the work, but the firm counted on making up the shortfall with an incentive of $200,000 per day if the work was completed before June 27, 2007. > On the evening of Thursday, May 24, the I-580 connector re-opened, just before the busy Memorial Day weekend. The deadline to finish the project was beaten by over a month, with the contractor earning the $5 million bonus for early completion. The entire reconstruction project was completed only 26 days after the original accident. Amazing: The Rebuilding the MacArthur Maze - [VIDEO]"},{"id":30735582,"author":"jldugger","month":"2022-03","story":"Why America can’t build quickly anymore","stance":"reference","why":"Example of fast highway repair.","quote":"Plenty of examples of massive speedups in repairs","text":"Plenty of examples of massive speedups in repairs: 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO] It's not that we can't \"build quickly,\" but that we can't build anything _new_ quickly."},{"id":25896473,"author":"jldugger","month":"2021-01","story":"Make Boring Plans","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"What developers think when they hear 'aggressive timeline': [LINK] What managers think: [VIDEO] Point being, nobody gets a documentary about themselves made for delivering against a boring deadline."}],"r":[["qSfFq02pK4s",1]]},"1BbMBdGPoHM":{"wy":"Pointed to as the authoritative history of FFS from one of its original creators.","q":{"x":"For more history, perhaps see \"A Brief History of the BSD Fast Filesystem by Marshall Kirk McKusick\"","i":35887199,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35887199,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-05","story":"50 years in filesystems: 1984 BSD FFS","stance":"rec","why":"McKusick's history of the BSD Fast Filesystem.","quote":"For more history, perhaps see \"A Brief History of the BSD Fast Filesystem by Marshall Kirk McKusick\"","text":"For more history, perhaps see \"A Brief History of the BSD Fast Filesystem by Marshall Kirk McKusick\": * [VIDEO] * [LINK] In the article the classic The Design and Implementation of the 4.4BSD Operating System is mentioned: * [LINK] See also the more recent The Design and Implementation of the FreeBSD Operating System : * [LINK]"},{"id":31270539,"author":"nix23","month":"2022-05","story":"Introduction to HardenedBSD World","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"exFAT has no journal, so you feel the difference when it's already to late ;) >I’ve never noticed any difference Well, i don't know what to say, but you can read some of them: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] UFS SoftUpdates are a bit like journals and UFS has something like policies so you can change the \"behavior\" of the filesystem on the fly. With SU, UFS gained the ability to make snapshots (a feature ~normally just COW filesystems support) >A Brief History of the BSD Fast Filesystem by Marshall Kirk McKusick [VIDEO] ZFS has just too many feature's to write them down, but data integrity, compression, snapshots and an integrated volume-manager are surely some high-lights. And speaking about Linux Filesystems, there are some points that RHEL uses XFS and not EXT3/4: [LINK]"},{"id":25178684,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-11","story":"The Birth of Unix with Brian Kernighan","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Marshall Kirk McKusick has done some talks on the subject.","text":"\"Design and Implementation of the FreeBSD Operating System, The, 2nd Edition\" [LINK] Marshall Kirk McKusick has done some talks on the subject. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1]]},"1FXuMs6YRCY":{"wy":"Cited in investing threads as a research-backed argument for diversifying beyond US equities, with sources in the description.","q":{"x":"Good video by Ben Felix citing a bunch of research papers (with DOIs in the description)","i":35887285,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35887285,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-05","story":"The four times the United States government defaulted on its debt","stance":"rec","why":"Research-backed case for international diversification with cited papers.","quote":"Good video by Ben Felix citing a bunch of research papers (with DOIs in the description)","text":"International diversification is generally a good idea. Good video by Ben Felix citing a bunch of research papers (with DOIs in the description): * [VIDEO]"},{"id":44975175,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2025-08","story":"Margin debt surges to record high","stance":"reference","why":"Supports diversification as a sensible approach.","quote":"","text":"> Index funds won't necessarily save you. So first off, picking individual winning stocks is hard because new information that determines pricing comes in randomly, so good luck getting information edge on your counter-party: * [LINK] Further, <5% of stocks actually make up the vast majority of earnings: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] Those winning stocks also change over time: what used to be a winning choice can become a losing choice, so it's not like you can really set and forget things. So index funds, buying all companies (especially if you go for more total market, like US Russell 3000), allow you to sidestep all of these risks. You are basically buying companies that service the entire economy, so as long as the economy is doing reasonably well the earnings of the companies will do reasonably well. So yes, the S&P 500 is highly concentrated, but that is not the only index. Diversification is generally not a bad idea: * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":43325327,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2025-03","story":"What We've Learned from 150 Years of Stock Market Crashes","stance":"reference","why":"Research suggesting international exposure helps even US investors.","quote":"Even for US investors the data/research suggests some international exposure leads to better results","text":"> Real interest rates in Japan were zero or negative for pretty much all of that same 35-year period, so bonds wouldn't have helped you either. The main thing that bonds would have helped with was in the 1980s: by regularly rebalancing to (say) 80/20 you would have taken profits off the table regularly. Further taking that (e.g.) 80% equities and putting and putting some in ex-JP/in-US would have further helped. Even for US investors the data/research suggests some international exposure leads to better results: * [VIDEO] Further, it looks like even in the US it hasn't been \"the US market\" that has done well, but rather tech specifically: > Looking at this data, there are two distinct periods of extended U.S. outperformance—the late 1990s and today. And what do these two periods have in common? The rise of U.S. technology stocks. Bespoke Investment Group recently created this chart illustrating this phenomenon: > Now that the U.S. technology sector makes up over 30% of the S&P 500 (as it did back in 2000), this begs the question: Is U.S. outperformance just a technological fad? * [LINK] So, slightly contra Buffett, you're not entirely \"betting on the US\" but rather \"betting on US tech\"."},{"id":40136265,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-04","story":"The death of the 60/40 portfolio","stance":"reference","why":"Cited sources supporting diversification even for American investors.","quote":"Diversification is important, even for Americans (cited sources in the description)","text":"> A slight over simplification, for example s&p was negative 1% per year from Jan 2000 to Dec 2009. Plus the cost of your leverage. For the 2000s, a US-only investor would only have been saved from the S&P500 by having at least 20% bonds and rebalancing: * [LINK] Of course if you were not US-only, but rather internationally diversified (and rebalanced), you would also have been fine. Diversification is important, even for Americans (cited sources in the description): * [VIDEO]"},{"id":40199711,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-04","story":"Most investments are bad. Here's why","stance":"reference","why":"Peer-reviewed citations for diversifying beyond the S&P 500.","quote":"Try some diversification (peer-reviewed citations in description)","text":"> it took 14 years for s&p 500 to break even if you bought it in 2000 Not if you had a modicum of bonds and rebalanced: * [LINK] Or if you had international stocks. This myopia of many Americans to only look at the S&P 500… words fail me. Sheesh. Try some diversification (peer-reviewed citations in description): * [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1],["NRoUC9P1PmA",1]]},"3vyNzgOP5yw":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a primary source from the architect of Zen, for example on how little the ISA matters compared to microarchitecture.","q":{"x":"Check out this interview with the actual architect of Zen, Mike Clark.","i":34912418,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34912418,"author":"paulmd","month":"2023-02","story":"Sam Zeloof and Jim Keller start a new semiconductor fab","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with Zen's architect on CPU development, Zen2 versus Zen3.","quote":"Check out this interview with the actual architect of Zen, Mike Clark.","text":"Check out this interview with the actual architect of Zen, Mike Clark. [VIDEO] Probably the most interesting nugget is that Zen2 was actually considered a tweak internally and not a full uarch revision, while Zen3 is actually the clean-sheet revision. But it's just a good summary of the general tone and tempo of CPU development I think."},{"id":45522725,"author":"stevefan1999","month":"2025-10","story":"AMD signs AI chip-supply deal with OpenAI, gives it option to take a 10% stake","stance":"reference","why":"Mike Clark as the father of Zen.","quote":"Actually, Mike Clark is the 'father of Zen'","text":"Actually, Mike Clark is the 'father of Zen': [VIDEO] . When Jim came along, Zen design was mostly finished already. Another honorable mention would be Suzanne Plummer"},{"id":34912514,"author":"paulmd","month":"2023-02","story":"Sam Zeloof and Jim Keller start a new semiconductor fab","stance":"reference","why":"Mike Clark executed Zen development.","quote":"in particular Mike Clark was really the guy who executed Zen development","text":"Jim Keller is tied to basically every ground-breaking architecture of the last 30 years. Not that everything he's worked on has been a hit, but, if something ended up being a huge success there's a high probability Jim Keller has been skulking around somewhere. Conroe/Sandy Bridge are the only ones that come to mind that haven't been him. His greatest hits: DEC Alpha, AMD K7 (Thunderbird/Athlon), AMD K8 (Athlon 64), Apple A5, AMD Zen... probably others I'm forgetting. Like god damn can someone else in the industry have some ideas of their own please, lol /s And again not that other people aren't involved either, in particular Mike Clark was really the guy who executed Zen development, but Keller was there at least as an advisor for a lot of the early development. He's one of Zen's uncles, Clark is the father. [VIDEO] I think it also says a lot that he completely fucking bailed from Intel after only being there a couple months... I think he saw they just weren't ready/willing to execute well and his time would be wasted there. He's the wandering silicon samurai who drops some golden nuggets of advice and wanders off into the sunset, not a babysitter while you purge middle-managers playing office politics."},{"id":31656607,"author":"lynguist","month":"2022-06","story":"Apple Unveils M2","stance":"reference","why":"Zen architect says ISA matters little versus microarchitecture.","quote":"Feel free to listen to the whole interview to get a feeling.","text":"Mike Clark, the architect of Zen says in [1] literally that ISA doesn’t matter and that it’s all about the microarchitecture. Quote: “Although I've worked on x86 obviously for 28 years, it's just an ISA, and you can build a low-power design or a high-performance out any ISA. I mean, ISA does matter, but it's not the main component - you can change the ISA if you need some special instructions to do stuff, but really the microarchitecture is in a lot of ways independent of the ISA. There are some interesting quirks in the different ISAs, but at the end of the day, it's really about microarchitecture.” In the end, ISA is such a small part that impacts the design of a modern high performance CPU that it is almost negligible. It is physically impacting only the decode unit, but the decode unit is only a few percent of die area. Feel free to listen to the whole interview to get a feeling. 1: [VIDEO]"}]},"Bw-opH4f8Qg":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as official evidence that the new simulator's flight model is serious and comparable to dedicated sims.","q":{"x":"they have an entire video on the aerodynamics work, it's beyond impressive","i":22238044,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22238044,"author":"eternalny1","month":"2020-02","story":"Flight Simulator 2020 – New Development Footage","stance":"rec","why":"Microsoft Flight Simulator's new aerodynamics work","quote":"they have an entire video on the aerodynamics work, it's beyond impressive","text":"Not this time around, they have an entire video on the aerodynamics work, it's beyond impressive and beyond X-Plane's \"blade element theory\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":26696678,"author":"mopsi","month":"2021-04","story":"How did Microsoft make Flight Simulator seem so real?","stance":"reference","why":"Official video showing new aerodynamics simulation, countering article","quote":"Microsoft has published two videos showcasing their new aerodynamics simulation","text":"The article was written by someone who didn't even bother to read/watch Microsoft's promo material. Lookup tables are a thing of the past. Microsoft has published two videos showcasing their new aerodynamics simulation[1][2]. There seems to be a lot of pushback from old-timers who have sunken thousands of $$$ into X-Plane and its expansions (from terrain packages to cloud visuals), many of which now look inferior to what Microsoft's new simulator offers out of the box. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23138982,"author":"Hawxy","month":"2020-05","story":"MS Flight Simulator 2020 vs. Real life","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence MSFS flight model matches X-Plane","quote":"The MSFS 2020 flight simulation is on par if not better than X-Plane","text":"> If you're looking for a more accurate, 'real' simulator it has a lot to offer. What do you mean by this? The MSFS 2020 flight simulation is on par if not better than X-Plane: [VIDEO] > but if you're interested in contributing to a product you can improve and have fun modelling/texturing additional planes, cities etc this is a project to get involved with. Again, MSFS 2020's SDK is already with all of the major third-party developers and will be publically available upon its release. A couple have even swapped to almost purely working on MSFS products instead of X-Plane or P3D."},{"id":22240020,"author":"MayeulC","month":"2020-02","story":"Flight Simulator 2020 – New Development Footage","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the aerodynamic simulation shown at","text":"Thank you, this is honestly really impressive! However, I must note that the aerodynamic simulation shown at [VIDEO] seems quite close to Xplane's :)"}]},"CLo3e1Pak-Y":{"wy":"Cited as a vivid demonstration of the scale of Chinese state surveillance, especially in TikTok and privacy debates.","q":{"x":"just watch the Vice doc on how China tracks people, it's on a whole another level","i":24012244,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24012032,"author":"fermienrico","month":"2020-07","story":"President Trump plans to sever TikTok from its Chinese owner, Bloomberg reports","stance":"rec","why":"Vice clip on mass facial recognition and tracking in China.","quote":"they need to first watch the Vice clip on how China tracks people","text":"For those who are unaware, India banned Tiktok recently: [LINK] I support this move - in addition to censorship, there are serious consequences of collecting facial features en-masse with no checks/balances, especially by the CCP who probably already has a massive database of non-Chinese nationals. This also applies to any company (Facebook/Apple/etc) but I am significantly more concerned about CCP having this data than any other government in the world. People who are about to present with a counter US whataboutism, they need to first watch the Vice clip on how China tracks people: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24012244,"author":"fermienrico","month":"2020-07","story":"Microsoft is reportedly in talks to buy TikTok's US operations","stance":"rec","why":"Vice documentary on Chinese state surveillance scale.","quote":"just watch the Vice doc on how China tracks people, it's on a whole another level","text":"This is far better than having facial recognition data in the hands of CCP. I am not a fan of US gov, but if I had the choice, I wouldn't side with CCP. The genie is already out and we've failed to put laws in place to stop all this data collection way back in 2004. Surprisingly, I feel comfortable with Microsoft having this data. What a world we've created. For those who are skeptical, just watch the Vice doc on how China tracks people, it's on a whole another level: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47858214,"author":"Cider9986","month":"2026-04","story":"I'm Sick of AI Everything","stance":"reference","why":"Shows AI-driven surveillance in China already in place since 2018.","quote":"This has been happening in China since 2018","text":"This has been happening in China since 2018[1]. North Korea is able to oppress its citizens just fine without AI. I don't think recent advances are going to revolutionize government oppression. [1] [VIDEO] youtube version: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24047212,"author":"fermienrico","month":"2020-08","story":"China will not accept U.S. 'theft' of TikTok: China Daily","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of China's overwhelming state surveillance.","quote":"Vice, How China Tracks Everyone","text":"While I don't condone President Trump's behavior and his approach towards China, and with US losing its place in the world; I find it equally amusing that we in democratic society are willing to kowtow to an authoritarian regime, despite of its overwhelming [1] state of surveillance, Han-supremacy motifs and funding it with more asymmetrical trade policies. Democracy is eroding in front of our eyes and no one seems to care. Hey, at least we can even protest in the west (while getting tear gassed, but not tanks and bullets causing a massacre like 1989). \"Take that, America!!!\" seems to get people entertained. Not defending America, there are plenty of Americans wanting to say \"Take that, China!!!\". EU folks are bashing US, US folks are speechless at what their nation is going through, India and China are at arms, Australia has Chinese infestation, Japan is the most Sinophobic country in the world, Taiwan is nervous, Hong Kong is decimated, Eastern EU is going authoritarian, UK is spiraling into isolation, etc. This is not going to be fun to watch. [1] Vice, How China Tracks Everyone: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24050817,"author":"actuator","month":"2020-08","story":"TikTok and the Sorting Hat","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here is the video if you want to see","text":"Yeah, I don't doubt it. It could very well have been edited to look like that, either for views or sensationalism. Here is the video if you want to see: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22979902,"author":"xiphias2","month":"2020-04","story":"Showdown looms between Silicon Valley, U.S. states over contact tracing apps","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I believe that Palantir is behind China in surveillence software (especially gathering data about people from public cameras, but of course using GPS data is much easier. Of course the funniest thing is that the name of the surveillence software is SkyNet, because the CEO of the company loves Terminator. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22810261,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2020-04","story":"Taiwan joins Canada in banning Zoom for government video conferencing","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Look at what we've created","text":"I would even say - the general public needs to boycott Zoom and shift to something else that's open. Large scale (never this large in the history of mankind) data collection of facial features across the globe along with other meta data such as name and email address, perhaps a phone number - this is unprecedented amount of data that any government agency would kill to have. Just in a couple of months, the world has given up privacy in a snap. I would say the same thing about Facebook. Look at what we've created: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PE4gwstWhmc",1]]},"ClfBxWPkBKU":{"wy":"Mentioned in threads about financial fraud as support for the claim that outright fraud, not just bad luck, caused the Great Recession.","q":{"x":"The far more significant cause of the Great Recession was outright fraud.","i":10720871,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10720871,"author":"pktgen","month":"2015-12","story":"The Fed’s imminent interest-rate decision – Is America ready for lift-off?","stance":"rec","why":"Argues outright fraud caused the Great Recession.","quote":"The far more significant cause of the Great Recession was outright fraud.","text":"The far more significant cause of the Great Recession was outright fraud. Watch [VIDEO] and other videos featuring William Black (a former bank regulator from the S&L crisis days)."},{"id":35322121,"author":"throwoutway","month":"2023-03","story":"Show HN: Notclick.in – summarize YouTube videos with ChatGPT","stance":"reference","why":"Used as a random test video for a summarizer.","quote":"","text":"Great idea! I tested on this random video from my bookmarks (one that I have not got around to watching): [VIDEO] The summary works great! But I was hoping for a bit more, perhaps if the speaker made 5 points, I'd like to know what those 5 points were. It would be great if your site could have a \"tell me more\" button to expand to include more detail each time you click it. Bookmarked this for the future, thanks!"},{"id":33617984,"author":"dools","month":"2022-11","story":"FTX tapped into customer accounts to fund risky bets, setting up its downfall","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It is indeed wrong, but nobody here has made such a statement Really? The very first comment I replied to was: \"banks at least tell you they are loaning your deposits out\" My response: \"Side not but that’s not really how banking works. Banks create deposits when they originate loans and separately look for the assets they need in order to satisfy any regulatory requirements and net flows of funds for inter bank settlements.\" Your initial response: \"No, when you take a loan out of a bank, the bank doesn't \"create deposits\" that it loans to you. The bank loans you existing deposits\" Are you saying there's a distinction between \"banks loan you existing deposits\" and \"banks lend out reserves\"? They seem to be the same statement to me ... > Now, a bank which has a high amount of capital can in practice issue more loans than a bank which has a low amount of capital. And one way of increasing the bank's capital (and thus improving the bank's ability to issue loans) is increasing the bank's reserves Yeah that's one way they can do it. Those reserves would then almost always be swapped for bonds unless there's more money to be made by loaning them to other banks (which is how the central bank sets and defends the interest rate). > So there's some kind of positive correlation between a bank's reserves and its ability to issue loans, right? You mentioned yourself that a bank has to have sufficient capital to survive short term liquidity events, and issuing a large amount of loans incr"}]},"KClAPipnKqw":{"wy":"HN refers to it in career and promotion discussions, including its warning about doing glue work too early in your career.","q":{"x":"Here is a talk which warns against doing this kind of glue work too early in your career","i":25467464,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25467464,"author":"st1x7","month":"2020-12","story":"John Carmack: Career Advice","stance":"rec","why":"Warns against doing glue work too early in your career.","quote":"Here is a talk which warns against doing this kind of glue work too early in your career","text":"> My suggestion is to find gaps that aren't integration efforts, if possible, that are instead extension efforts. Yes! This can go even further and the technical integration work can easily slip into soft/people/team/project/management integration. Here is a talk which warns against doing this kind of glue work too early in your career: Video: [VIDEO] Summary article: [LINK]"},{"id":30348058,"author":"csours","month":"2022-02","story":"You have two jobs (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Relates glue work to building culture.","quote":"Interesting to think of this in the context of Glue Work.","text":"Interesting to think of this in the context of Glue Work. [VIDEO] Building culture means handling these opposing forces in a productive manner."},{"id":25121686,"author":"BossingAround","month":"2020-11","story":"Being Glue","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on glue work and promotion, cited to discuss a case.","quote":"Based on the talk [0] where the OP suggested it number of times.","text":"Based on the talk [0] where the OP suggested it number of times. She mentions the engineer being rusty and having significantly fewer code contributions than her peers. In general, if the glue person has intermediate knowledge of the codebase, they'd get promoted without much controversy. In such a case, it should not be difficult for them to bang out a couple of features and ship it, for example. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["sD0NjbwqlYw",1]]},"Os7FE3J-U5Q":{"wy":"Cited alongside compiler-construction resources as the go-to talk on the nanopass approach used in Chez Scheme.","q":{"x":"Andrew Keep has a great talk on this approach [2].","i":16734815,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16734815,"author":"undecidabot","month":"2018-04","story":"MLton Compiler Overview","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on building compilers from many simple passes, as in Chez Scheme.","quote":"Andrew Keep has a great talk on this approach [2].","text":"Compilers come in all shapes and sizes. Some are single pass like Turbo Pascal. They directly generate machine code while parsing (no AST!). Niklaus Wirth (Pascal/Modula/Oberon guy) wrote a book following this approach [1]. Some have multiple passes like Chez Scheme. They have many simple passes, which they call a \"nanopass\". Andrew Keep has a great talk on this approach [2]. In practice, most compilers today are multi-pass, though probably not as many Chez. If we look at Rust, they go from AST -> HIR -> MIR -> LLVM IR -> machine code [3]. There are probably more things going on from LLVM to machine code, but I'm not knowledgeable enough to comment on it. I think the trade-off here is clear: less passes -> shorter compile times, more passes -> faster code generated, more modular compiler. Martin Odersky (Scala guy) has a paper attempting to get the best of both [4]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":47777286,"author":"LiamPowell","month":"2026-04","story":"Want to Write a Compiler? Just Read These Two Papers (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"Keep's Clojure/Conj 2013 talk on nanopass architecture.","quote":"See also, Andy Keep's dissertation [1] and his talk at Clojure/Conj 2013 [2].","text":"See also, Andy Keep's dissertation [1] and his talk at Clojure/Conj 2013 [2]. I think that the nanopass architecture is especially well suited for compilers implemented by LLMs as they're excellent at performing small and well defined pieces of work. I'd love to see Anthropic try their C compiler experiment again but with a Nanopass framework to build on. I've recently been looking in to adding Nanopass support to Langkit, which would allow for writing a Nanopass compiler in Ada, Java, Python, or a few other languages [3]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":15173234,"author":"vengarioth","month":"2017-09","story":"Ask HN: Resources for building a programming language?","stance":"reference","why":"Nanopass framework approach to compiler construction.","quote":"","text":"Many suggest resources for compiler implementations, i personally would start prototyping the language itself, think about it's type system and its runtime properties. There are many possibilities in terms of type systems, from static to dynamic and everything in between, concepts like linear types, algebraic types, etc. Runtime properties are things like it's memory model (garbage collection, yes or no). All of this depends on the purpose of your language. The second part would be choosing a platform to run on (so you only have to write a compiler frontend). There are many great technologies to host a language, but i'd advocate two specific ones here: * [LINK] (shoutout to @love2code) * [LINK] ZetaVM is a great target for dynamicly typed and/or jit compiled languages like python or javascript. LLVM is used in many low level / near metal languages like C, C++, rust and so on. None of both must be your final targets, your compiler frontend can be moved to target another platform like the JVM later. As a last part, implement your compiler frontend. This involves lexing/parsing source code, type checking if needed and generating your target's intermediate representation. I personally prefer hand-written recursive descent parsers using a parser combinator framework (like [LINK] ) over parser generators. For further processing like type checking, there is the common monolith aproach and the nanopass framework/approach [VIDEO] Also there's a great series on compiler frontends by Al"},{"id":13207733,"author":"616c","month":"2016-12","story":"An Incremental Approach to Compiler Construction (2006) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Is this preceding work to the tour de force that is the Chez Scheme incremental like 40+ Scheme compiler. I mean, Cisco bought it, probably to hide it from us all. It must be worth something. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["kTrOg19gzP4",1]]},"U3EEmVfbKNs":{"wy":"Pointed to as an interesting look inside how consumer DNA testing companies process samples.","q":{"x":"Smarter Every Day made a pretty interesting video about 23&Me.","i":21770453,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21770453,"author":"pel0","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Would you trust an ancestry DNA test?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting perspective on 23andMe from behind the scenes","quote":"Smarter Every Day made a pretty interesting video about 23&Me.","text":"Smarter Every Day made a pretty interesting video about 23&Me. [VIDEO] . I'm not pro/anti 23&Me or anything but it brings an interesting perspective."},{"id":19060641,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2019-02","story":"One of the Biggest At-Home DNA Testing Companies Is Working with the FBI","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how DNA testing companies process samples","quote":"","text":"Wait a second, I though these companies didn't sequence DNA. I thought most of them just ran your DNA against known patterns to see if they patch to give you a probably/percentage of ancestry, with all the original samples being destroyed: [VIDEO] Does ancestry actually sample and save entire DNA sequences (I guess they have to in order to do family tree matching)? Crazy."},{"id":17335878,"author":"propogandist","month":"2018-06","story":"Luxury hotels are being inundated with requests from self-described influencers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"influencers are very selective with their 'reviews' and may chose to leave out things like data-privacy policies, when reviewing a company that provides you DNA analysis -- which should make you question all the information you get from such sources [VIDEO]"}]},"XziuEdpVUe0":{"wy":"HN cites it as the example of oscilloscope music, where the song itself is the visual, in XY mode.","q":{"x":"these crazy fools are making oscilloscope music, where the song itself is the visual.","i":25803539,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25803539,"author":"filoeleven","month":"2021-01","story":"Encoding Data in Dubstep Drops","stance":"rec","why":"Songs whose waveforms themselves form the visuals on an oscilloscope.","quote":"these crazy fools are making oscilloscope music, where the song itself is the visual.","text":"Along the same lines, these crazy fools are making oscilloscope music, where the song itself is the visual. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23464713,"author":"solarkraft","month":"2020-06","story":"Toto’s “Africa” Played on Tesla Coils","stance":"reference","why":"Example of images created from two audio channels on an oscilloscope.","quote":"in which an image is made with 2 audio channels being shown on an oscilloscope","text":"Your description immediately made me think of oscilloscope music, in which an image is made with 2 audio channels being shown on an oscilloscope: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14624885,"author":"leni536","month":"2017-06","story":"After 3072 hours of manipulating BGP, a Nyancat was drawn on this RIPE interface","stance":"reference","why":"Music drawn as images by feeding stereo channels to an oscilloscope in XY mode.","quote":"The idea is that you display the left and right channels on an oscilloscope in XY mode.","text":"Oscilloscope music. The idea is that you display the left and right channels on an oscilloscope in XY mode. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aSEQfqNYNAc",1],["zAFcV7zuUDA",1],["OoJsPvmFixU",1],["AGUwcs9qJXY",1]]},"ZYD9yyMh9Hk":{"wy":"Cited as the definitive public account of Python at massive scale, especially in discussions of monorepos and bank Python.","q":{"x":"4500 developers making 20k commits a week. Codebase with 35m LOC.","i":23819270,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23819270,"author":"stevesimmons","month":"2020-07","story":"The most remarkable legacy system I have seen","stance":"rec","why":"Athena's origins, uses, architecture, infrastructure, dev tooling and culture.","quote":"4500 developers making 20k commits a week. Codebase with 35m LOC.","text":"As the comment by timsworkaccount says, this is definitely Athena at JPMorgan. For those interested, I gave a talk on Athena at PyData UK 2018 called \"Python at Massive Scale\". 4500 developers making 20k commits a week. Codebase with 35m LOC. The video is here: [VIDEO] It covers Athena's origins, what it is used for, application architecture, infrastructure, dev tooling and culture."},{"id":31276292,"author":"Barrin92","month":"2022-05","story":"Modern Python Performance Considerations","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on JPMorgan's 35 million LOC Python codebase.","quote":"Python is being used to built seriously large software nowadays","text":">Python shines beautifully as a (somewhat) cross platform scripting language Python is much more than just a scripting language. I remember attending this talk[1] a few years about JPMorgan's 35 million LOC Python codebase. Python is being used to built seriously large software nowadays and I don't think performance is ever a minor issue. It should always be in the top 3 for any general purpose language because it directly translates into development speed, time and money. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29815799,"author":"stevesimmons","month":"2022-01","story":"Problems once solved by a metaclass can be solved by __init_subclass__","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on JPMorgan's Athena trading and risk system.","quote":"Athena had 4,500 Python developers doing 20,000 commits a week.","text":"See [1] for a PyData London talk I did on \"Python at Massive Scale\", about JPMorgan's Athena trading and risk management system, used for FX, Commodities, Credit, Equities, etc trading. At that time, Athena had 4,500 Python developers doing 20,000 commits a week. Also see one recent HN discussion on \"An Oral History of Bank Python\" [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":29105146,"author":"stevesimmons","month":"2021-11","story":"An oral history of Bank Python","stance":"reference","why":"Author's talk on Python at JPMorgan.","quote":"For a view of Python at JPMorgan, I did a talk at PyData London","text":"Nice stuff! For a view of Python at JPMorgan, I did a talk at PyData London \"Python at Massive Scale\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":29105169,"author":"stevesimmons","month":"2021-11","story":"An oral history of Bank Python","stance":"reference","why":"Author's PyData talk on Athena for more details.","quote":"Seeing Python at the core of trading, risk and post-trade processing for Commodities, FX, Credit etc was such a great developer experience.","text":"I worked on Athena at JPMorgan for 8 years, and loved it. Seeing Python at the core of trading, risk and post-trade processing for Commodities, FX, Credit etc was such a great developer experience. By the time I left JPM, there were 4500 devs globally making 20k commits weekly into the Athena codebase. (I did a PyData presentation on this [1] for more details). The one downside was the delayed transition from Py2.7 to 3; I left just as that was getting underway. [1] [VIDEO] The"},{"id":20675459,"author":"Barrin92","month":"2019-08","story":"Python Is Eating the World","stance":"reference","why":"Large JP Morgan Python platform for trading and analytics.","quote":"JP Morgan operates a ~30 million LOC Python platform for trading and analytics.","text":">How stable is python to run a full trading / quantitative algorithm on? JP Morgan operates a ~30 million LOC Python platform for trading and analytics. (related talk: [VIDEO] ) Yes, there are very, very large working python codebases in fields out there that demand correctness. I'm honestly getting tired of the static typing circlejerk that has entered the industry."},{"id":18182950,"author":"mcintyre1994","month":"2018-10","story":"Python is becoming the world’s most popular programming language","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I've seen a struggle to manage a far smaller codebase than that in Python and really interested to see what you're doing differently","text":"If anyone else is looking: [VIDEO] :) I'm definitely interested in this - I've seen a struggle to manage a far smaller codebase than that in Python and really interested to see what you're doing differently to make it work."},{"id":17607376,"author":"stevesimmons","month":"2018-07","story":"Why Google Stores Billions of Lines of Code in a Single Repository (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on a 50m LOC Python monorepo and supporting devtools.","quote":"My company has a 50m LOC Python codebase in a monorepo. It works really well","text":"My company has a 50m LOC Python codebase in a monorepo. It works really well, given the rate of change of thousands of developers globally. That is only possible because of the significant investment in devtools, testing and the deployment infrastructure. Here is \"Python at Massive Scale\", my talk about it at PyData London earlier this year: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vI5pbDFDZyI",1]]},"ZYqG31V4qtA":{"wy":"HN people cite it as a review showing Zen 2's efficiency and performance advantage over Intel in laptops.","q":{"x":"This LTT review captures the massive advantage Zen 2 has in efficiency","i":22733027,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25289849,"author":"m12k","month":"2020-12","story":"AMD CPU sales skyrocket with Ryzen 5000 launch, leaving Intel in the dust","stance":"rec","why":"Linus was very impressed with the Zephyrus G14.","quote":"Linus (Tech Tips) seemed very impressed with it","text":"Have you considered an Asus Zephyrus G14? It comes with a Ryzen 9 4900HS and Linus (Tech Tips) seemed very impressed with it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22892703,"author":"TazeTSchnitzel","month":"2020-04","story":"10-core i9-10900F desktop CPU lags behind 8-core Ryzen 9 4900HS mobile APU","stance":"reference","why":"Gaming laptop with this APU outperformed Intel.","quote":"were super impressed with its Intel-scorching performance","text":"LinusTechTips recently took a look at a gaming laptop with the same APU and were super impressed with its Intel-scorching performance: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22925329,"author":"arcticbull","month":"2020-04","story":"Building a PC, Part IX: Downsizing","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I haven't found many machines built on those. It's a shame. The 4900HS just came out, so I assume we'll be seeing more of them! (LTT: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":22732374,"author":"piinbinary","month":"2020-03","story":"Ryzen 4000 Review","stance":"reference","why":"Review noting good battery life and power efficiency.","quote":"The LTT review [0] also had good things to say about the battery life.","text":"The LTT review [0] also had good things to say about the battery life. This CPU seems very power-efficient when compared to a 9980HK. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22733027,"author":"blattimwind","month":"2020-03","story":"Ryzen 4000 Review","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Zen 2 efficiency advantage over Intel.","quote":"This LTT review captures the massive advantage Zen 2 has in efficiency","text":"> Games are traditionally single threaded and hard to parallelize. On my 3900X I've seen even single player action titles utilize 16+ threads with overall CPU utilization reaching peaks of 80-90 %. Yes, it maybe be hard to parallelize game engines, but all major ones heavily use multiple cores. A bunch of years ago (4-5) people would recommend an i3-K dual core for gaming, because you could overclock it to 5+ GHz on air. That was sufficient for many titles back then. (And these i3s would sometimes give you higher FPS compared to the more expensive quad-core options). But a CPU like that is far too weak for newer titles. > You still can't beat intel at the high end. Both in terms of heat and performance. If you have a task that doesn't parallelize well, you can't beat intel. In terms of heat, that is, performance per Watt, Intel's high end is quite a bit worse than AMD's. It is true however that you can get slightly higher single-threaded performance out of Intel's highest-end CPUs, but not because they are more efficient (- AMD is better both perf/W and IPC-wise), but just by raw, unadulterated clock speed, pushing them to 5+ GHz. And that causes them to burn a lot of power. Zen 2 has consistently ~10-15 % higher performance clock-for-clock compared to Intel's best while achieving approximately 60-80 % higher perf/W. This LTT review captures the massive advantage Zen 2 has in efficiency: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HPqGaIMVuLs",1]]},"bwhU9goCiaI":{"wy":"HN points to it as an excellent history of how Japan entered chips, including government-coordinated cooperation like the VLSI project.","q":{"x":"There is an excellent video on this","i":31701618,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31701618,"author":"dijonman2","month":"2022-06","story":"Apple M2 Die Shot and Architecture Analysis – Big Cost Increase and A15 Based IP","stance":"rec","why":"How Japan entered the semiconductor market.","quote":"There is an excellent video on this","text":"Chip manufacturing is difficult, this reminds me of the Japanese entry into the semiconductor market. There is an excellent video on this for anyone interested in Japanese culture and the war against USA via semiconductors: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35506428,"author":"amrb","month":"2023-04","story":"A CERN for Open Source Large-Scale AI","stance":"reference","why":"Japan's VLSI project grouping its largest companies.","quote":"Japan did have the VLSI project a group of there largest companies","text":"Japan did have the VLSI project a group of there largest companies: [VIDEO] For Europe I could see a bigger group of companies, schools and researchers working towards open models with funding. This would allow EU companies to be competitive with the US while not duplicating effort, more eyes on the problem too. Maybe alignment work could be a goal, since the driver is not profit at any cost this time."},{"id":32314499,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2022-08","story":"Japan, U.S. to launch R&D for 2-nm chip mass production","stance":"reference","why":"Japan's government-coordinated cooperation among chip firms.","quote":"Japan's original chip industry lead was created by the government funding a bunch of also-rans to cooperate","text":"Japan's original chip industry lead was created by the government funding a bunch of also-rans to cooperate (and have each participant focus on one part of the job) instead of compete (where every team is spread too thin trying to do the whole hog) [VIDEO]"}]},"et8xNAc2ic8":{"wy":"Linked as a funny tour of JavaScript gotchas, usually to show the language has oddities of its own.","m":[{"id":5514813,"author":"aroman","month":"2013-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the best technical talk you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Longer version of the WTFJS talk at dotJS","quote":"See also a longer spin of of this talk at the more decent dotJS conference","text":"I'm glad that someone posted this :) See also a longer spin of of this talk at the more decent dotJS conference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40445426,"author":"TacticalCoder","month":"2024-05","story":"Try Clojure","stance":"reference","why":"A talk about the quirks of JavaScript","quote":"Here's a talk about the (in)sanity of JS","text":"That's JavaScript for you. Here's a talk about the (in)sanity of JS: [VIDEO] The same under Clojure (on top of the JVM) shall throw a class cast exception."},{"id":19002191,"author":"loeg","month":"2019-01","story":"Compiler Warnings","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence JavaScript equality has oddities too","quote":"Javascript doesn't really have any high ground here[0].","text":"Meh. They're different languages with different expectations. \"is\" is distinct from \"==\" in both, and Python gets \"==\" right (obviously). Javascript doesn't really have any high ground here[0]. (In contrast, Python:) >>> [] == False False >>> object() == False False >>> \"\" == False False >>> {} == False False They're just different. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9580737,"author":"MaulingMonkey","month":"2015-05","story":"Why should I have written ZeroMQ in C, not C++ (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for JavaScript gotchas that remain despite language size","quote":"","text":"> No, actually, it doesn't. Perhaps less in what features to avoid, but there's plenty of hidden gotchas to avoid in JavaScript still. [VIDEO] [LINK] > C++ is a huge language, javascript is a relativly small one. C++'s ISO/IEC 14882:2003 weighs in at 786 pages, compared to ECMA-262 (5.1 Edition)'s 258 pages (measuring by PDF reader). A ~3x difference, but not quite the order of magnitude I'd expect from huge vs small."},{"id":27509282,"author":"mrtksn","month":"2021-06","story":"80% of tech could be built outside IT by 2024, thanks to low-code tools","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I love JS but here it is 20min of programming jokes in the expense of JS","text":"I don’t see why you are offended. I love JS but here it is 20min of programming jokes in the expense of JS: [VIDEO] As it comes to PHP, I don’t say that you can do everything by a single PHP file in a web server folder(actually, you can but not comfortably). I’m trying to illustrate it’s low-code nature. There are no other languages that are that easy to start solving your problem with. Okay, maybe Python. The newer no-code or low-code tools have the same spirit. They are abstraction layers targeting a domain. These days there’s plenty you can do by programming visually without any knowledge of programming languages. Not everything needs to scale for throughput."}],"r":[["-2ZTmuX3cog",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1]]},"n-AbPav5E5M":{"wy":"HN links it as a vivid example of what bike-centred cities look like in debates on cars, helmets and urban design.","q":{"x":"Here is a nice example of a typical rush hour","i":12085744,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":14449666,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2017-05","story":"Electric Cars Soon Will Cost Less Than Gas Cars, Research Suggests","stance":"rec","why":"Vision of what American cities could be","quote":"American cities could look like this","text":"American attitudes to cycling are about 20 years behind the times. Cycling is a completely practical mode of transport for everyday journeys, even in low-density suburbs and moderately rural areas. Yes, you can fit all your shopping on a bicycle. You can even take your kids to school on one. [LINK] [LINK] Yes, your partner with a back injury can ride a bicycle. So can someone with coordination or balance problems, missing limbs or any number of other impairments. [LINK] [LINK] Yes, you can ride 20 miles to dinner without working up a sweat and you can do it in much less than 2 hours. [LINK] If there were the political will to make it happen, American cities could look like this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12085744,"author":"teekert","month":"2016-07","story":"Headphones Everywhere","stance":"reference","why":"Typical rush hour with separated bike lanes","quote":"Here is a nice example of a typical rush hour","text":"Well we do bike mostly on biking lanes here in the Netherlands (no chance of contact with a car) and we have a very biking oriented culture in general. Also here the cars are guilty (car driver can claim the damage with the bikers insurance afterwards though) unless proven that they had no responsibility what so ever (bikers and pedestrian are so called \"weaker traffic participants\"). It may make car drivers more cautious, or perhaps the fact that almost everybody is on a bike at some point makes them more careful. Also, on roundabouts (there are a quite some) bikes always have priority as well. In any case, I have never heard of any accidents. It is known though that here, when the schools start after a holiday there is an increase in accidents, this is probably related to car drivers needing to become used again of the high amount of bikers on the road. Here is a nice example of a typical rush hour, the red lane is only for bikes, of course everybody is very careful at intersections :) [VIDEO] (by the way the bus also has it's own lane in this example, and everybody knows: bus drivers don't mess around.)"},{"id":8441801,"author":"teekert","month":"2014-10","story":"Bicycle helmets do almost nothing to prevent concussions","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch rush hour with many cyclists","quote":"This is not at all uncommon during rush hour","text":"I'd be so annoyed by the helmet, it messes up my hair, makes my head sweaty, I have to put it somewhere or keep it with me the whole time. It would really make me bike less. Then again I hardly ever have to share the street with cars and trucks and if I do it is with a lot of other bikers. This is not at all uncommon during rush hour: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5212047,"author":"wybo","month":"2013-02","story":"Cars and Robust Cities Are Fundamentally Incompatible","stance":"reference","why":"Typical Dutch city bike traffic","quote":"The following is not an uncommon sight in your typical Dutch city","text":"There are many cities without a lot of cars that work very well (even if they have some). The large cities this is true for, that I have some personal experience with, are London and Amsterdam. The difference is that they are also swarming with public transport and/or bikes. The following is not an uncommon sight in your typical Dutch city: [VIDEO] And in London using the tube / subway is just the fastest way from any A to B anywhere in and around the center (unless you can't walk half a mile), and the most convenient and care-free one (no finding a parking spot, no road taxes, no insurance, no fueling it up, no having it repaired if it breaks down, no winter tires, no ice scraping, no cleaning it,...)."},{"id":3513701,"author":"lordlarm","month":"2012-01","story":"Cars kill cities","stance":"reference","why":"Amsterdam rush hour bikes and buses","quote":"It is really striking to see how many bicycles and buses there are.","text":"You'll find many videos of this on YouTube, but here is an example: Rush hour traffic in the center of Amsterdam: [VIDEO] It is really striking to see how many bicycles and buses there are. As I live in Norway I'm seeing a stronger focus on public transport here as well, but nothing compared to e.g. Amsterdam or Copenhagen in Denmark."},{"id":3313883,"author":"maurits","month":"2011-12","story":"The Future of Ground-Based Transportation Systems","stance":"reference","why":"Busy Dutch intersection without helmets or cars","quote":"here is a random busy intersection in a major city.","text":"Not having a shower hardly invalidates an entire transport system. Nor do I believe that much of the populace has a medical condition that makes it impossible to bike. After a couple of years in Netherlands I strongly believe that to make the bike a success you obviously need a bike-suitable commute in terms of distance, but also, it needs to be safe. And that boils down to - infrastructure that is designed for bikes and cars a like. Not some stupid painted lane as an after thought. - Stringent traffic laws that protect the weaker traffic participant. In Holland for example, the burden of proof is for the more dangerous vehicle in a collision. For this reason I am not a fan of bike-rental programs. I feel they are easy to implement (dump 20 bikes at train station) and get some political brownie points (see, we do bikes) yet change very little. Redesigning an intersection for safer passage is a whole different matter. Inspiration? Have a look: [LINK] Yes, that is government promo, so here is a random busy intersection in a major city. Note, no helmets, no high vis gear, and all ages are represented. Also, no cars, no door-zone... [VIDEO]"},{"id":2699935,"author":"StephenFalken","month":"2011-06","story":"The American suburbs are a giant Ponzi scheme","stance":"reference","why":"Healthier city living by bicycle","quote":"","text":"I guess some people have found more sustainable healthier ways of living inside a city: \"Bicycle Rush Hour Utrecht (Netherlands) III\" -> [VIDEO]"},{"id":2055183,"author":"NiekvdMaas","month":"2010-12","story":"Bike Commuting help Dutch meet physical activity guideline","stance":"reference","why":"Utrecht bike rush hour","quote":"Here's a sample video shot in the city where I live (Utrecht)","text":"Here's a sample video shot in the city where I live (Utrecht): [VIDEO] At times (e.g. 6PM workdays) certain routes can be even busier. If you're interested, here are a lot more videos on this subject: [LINK]"}],"r":[["yvFKh_3evC8",1],["FACK2knC08E",1],["ckBfbvOXDvU",1],["HCUct4NlxE0",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["3kEfedtQVOY",1]]},"nLv_INgaLq8":{"wy":"Cited as an example of using modern C++ on 8-bit hardware.","m":[{"id":20420222,"author":"MereInterest","month":"2019-07","story":"Super Mario 64 has been decompiled","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on compiler optimizations explaining why recompilation loses information.","quote":"I'd recommend watching this CppCon talk [1] about compiler optioning.","text":"I'd recommend watching this CppCon talk [1] about compiler optioning. It isn't just that recompilation is hard, or that it is difficult for humans to do. At a fundamental level, the information is not there at all, because the compiler can make very impressive optimizations. Machine learning can deal with weak signals, but it can't deal with no signal. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43463355,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-03","story":"Mruby/C and picoruby: high level langs in low-level devices?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of using C++ on 8-bit hardware.","quote":"C++14 For The Commodore 64","text":"It is a return to the age when we had BASIC and Z80/6502 Assembly, and yes doing memcpy/strcpy all over the place instead of having proper arrays and strings is that bad. At very least, C++. \"C++14 For The Commodore 64\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":12640510,"author":"voltagex_","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"reference","why":"C++ code targeting the Commodore 64.","quote":"which definitely builds on some of Matt's work","text":"Your second link reminded me of [VIDEO] (which definitely builds on some of Matt's work). [LINK] - C++17 for the Commodore 64. Probable language warnings for my other suggestions: * Rescuing Prince of Persia from the Sands of Time [LINK] (was this talk ever given elsewhere?) * And You Shall Know Me By My Trail of Documentation - [LINK] * The History and Evolution of Computer Viruses - [LINK]"}],"r":[["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1]]},"xQx-tuW9A4Q":{"wy":"Recommended for hearing Engelbart's vision of collaborative hypertext systems straight from him, including his view of Wikipedia.","q":{"x":"Inspirational ideas (unfortunately displayed with slides instead of Hyperdoc demos) and perplexed reactions in the ending Q&A session.","i":17123527,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17123527,"author":"8bitsrule","month":"2018-05","story":"Doug Engelbart's advice to a young software developer [video]","stance":"rec","why":"56-minute Google visit with Doug Engelbart presenting inspirational ideas, ending in perplexed Q&A.","quote":"Inspirational ideas (unfortunately displayed with slides instead of Hyperdoc demos) and perplexed reactions in the ending Q&A session.","text":"One video on the /islandweller channel is this: [VIDEO] a 56-minute visit / presentation with Doug and several others at Google. Inspirational ideas (unfortunately displayed with slides instead of Hyperdoc demos) and perplexed reactions in the ending Q&A session. These ideas are so different; ordinary presentations are clearly incapable of selling them. (As Nelson, too, found out repeatedly.) Looks like job#1 is bootstrapping Hyperdoc presentation methods. Guess the 'Mother of all Demos' needs a 'Mother-Squared'. Another reaction: the video I saw had very low resolution, and the audio was recorded in a terribly noisy environment. I'm completely unable to process how this could -ever- have happened. TV did better in the Ted Mac era."},{"id":17696413,"author":"icc97","month":"2018-08","story":"Freeing the Web from the Browser","stance":"reference","why":"Engelbart politely calls Wikipedia a good start for his hypertext vision.","quote":"I watched a Google interview with Douglas Engelbart [0] where people asked him a couple of times if Wikipedia was what he'd envisioned for hypertext","text":"Thanks for the reply! It's a really interesting piece of research. Yes, you're quite right, the browser alone can't interact between all the programs, I was purely thinking of low hanging fruit for the browser plugin. I watched a Google interview with Douglas Engelbart [0] where people asked him a couple of times if Wikipedia was what he'd envisioned for hypertext, he was very polite about it and said it \"was a good start\", but he'd clearly wished that we'd got much further by now. What you're suggesting is definitely a step forward. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8025239,"author":"Detrus","month":"2014-07","story":"This is a motherfucking website. And it’s fucking not completely perfect","stance":"reference","why":"Engelbart on complex-topic discussion systems and powerful, learnable interfaces.","quote":"","text":"Sure but you can't re-allocate workers in a capitalist economy with a paper idea and a signature. You have to convince thousands of people. You have to convince investors to start new companies where new types of workers are doing something new on the web. You probably can't re-educate millions of the current designers, code monkeys, content farmers and clients to do new types of work that has nothing to do with their old work. There are plenty of these smartest and best people trying startups and they're not really measuring up to what computer pioneers imagined. Seems the best we can do for Engelbart's complex topic discussion systems are Wikipedia, Quora, Reddit and HN. HN's designer Paul Graham subscribes to Job's idea of simple, minimalist interfaces because they're easy to learn and get users fast. The old school of thought was programs and interfaces would do a lot, took a while to learn, but were worth it in the end. Today we assume only things that are worth learning on a computer are existing job skills. It's a Catch-22 and a hard sell. For reference [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7583173,"author":"Detrus","month":"2014-04","story":"Ask HN: Idea Sunday","stance":"reference","why":"Engelbart discusses computer systems enabling complex, structured discussion beyond print-world imitation.","quote":"Doug Engelbart the creator of NLS and famous for Mother of All Demos talks about such systems","text":"This is along the lines of structured arguments [LINK] Doug Engelbart the creator of NLS and famous for Mother of All Demos talks about such systems [VIDEO] and [LINK] etc. The general principle is we won't make much progress discussing complex issues by simply recreating the print world with increased convenience of a computer like we do now. The discussions have to take a fundamentally different form, possible only on computers, such as semantic argument maps, etc. Of course his perspective comes from 1950's when educated people had civil discussions that solved problems, or at least that's the modern nostalgia. He imagined a new class of \"knowledge workers\" moderating information. Instead we have voting rings and content farms. If anything, a structured argument map could shut down much of the attention seeking drivel surrounding every political/complex system issue."}],"r":[["f5SE47Xjx2Q",1],["oaDTiWaYfcM",1]]},"xgKY9hmbfgo":{"wy":"Pointed to in threads about Nintendo lawsuits and takedowns as an explanation of how the company behaves.","q":{"x":"These videos talk about it a little","i":39789872,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39789872,"author":"subjectsigma","month":"2024-03","story":"Gitlab confirms it's removed Suyu, a fork of Nintendo Switch emulator Yuzu","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Nintendo's brand-protection strategy and aggressive litigation.","quote":"These videos talk about it a little","text":"These videos talk about it a little: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] TL;DR: forming a hydra-summary of the three: Nintendo’s strategy has been to position themselves as a premium video game brand since the 1980’s. They use high pricing, exclusivity, and close integration with hardware to distance themselves from other companies. Because they want to position themselves as the one and only Nintendo, knockoffs and copycats represent an existential threat to their brand. They also want parents to have absolute trust in the family friendliness of their games and knockoffs could diminish that as well. Because of this they do literally every scummy thing in their power to aggressively pursue and litigate anything from piracy to fan projects. Control of their image is their biggest asset. As someone who didn’t really grow up playing Nintendo games I have a slight fascination with how people worship this company and continually make excuses for them, so I really like these videos, even if they’re probably not 100% accurate."},{"id":45871936,"author":"indrora","month":"2025-11","story":"Sued by Nintendo","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"See Also: Sacrifices to the Church of Nintendo [1], \"Currently at Nintendo\" [2], Attorney to Nintendo: WTF? [3], Nintendo tries a DMCA and fails [4] as well as the various deluges of Switch 2 bans for used games[5]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":43901062,"author":"DrillShopper","month":"2025-05","story":"Bethesda Thinks Fan Remaster of Oblivion Is 'Very Special' and Supports It","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Nintendo doesn't litigate if they believe the community project won't impact their sales/profits. lmao [VIDEO]"}]},"y7h4OtFDnYE":{"wy":"HN people link it as Feynman's charming and clear explanation of why train wheels are tapered, whenever trains, bicycles or steering come up.","m":[{"id":32893258,"author":"adolph","month":"2022-09","story":"How mechanics keep 50-year-old BART trains running: Windows 98, eBay, and scraps","stance":"rec","why":"Lovely Feynman description of why train wheels are tapered.","quote":"","text":"That doesn’t seem to be a charitable reading and in any case it has to do with wheel taper, more like cone than cylinder. More specifically the nature of the solution was getting two parts of the org, track and train, to work together. While that may seem elementary stuff, anyone at a big org can attest to how often it is tremendously difficult to do sometimes. I write this not as an apologist for commuter rail, which I find excessively expensive and inflexible outside of very circumstances, but as an excuse to link to the lovely Feynman description of why train wheels are tapered: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27935079,"author":"jameshart","month":"2021-07","story":"If Richard Feynman applied for a job at Microsoft (2002)","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman repeating the train-on-track puzzle, enjoying its geometric answer.","quote":"","text":"Indeed. Feynman talked in ‘surely you’re joking..’ about his affection for MIT students in his fraternity setting one another puzzles, and he talked about the question of how trains go round corners being one he found particularly interesting. In fact he repeats it in this interview: [VIDEO] He doesn’t answer the question ‘how does a train stay on the track’ with ‘some trains don’t’; he doesn’t switch to talking about rollercoaster trains and insist that the way they stay on the track is with horizontal wheels. He finds the logical, geometrically satisfying answer quite delightful. And in fact, in many cases the flanges which he says are just for safety and aren’t supposed to impact the track because otherwise they make a terrible sound… are expected to hit the track to help the train make it round particularly tight turns. Coming from New York and having ridden the subways you’d think Feynman would know there are some corners subway trains take where the flanges rub all the way round, and the lovely conical wheels aren’t what keeps the train on the track at all. So I definitely find this a weird characterization of a Feynman-ish approach to this kind of problem. I think he’d probably have delighted in the geometric neatness of why circular or triangular manhole covers can’t fall down their holes, and been happy to ignore the practical fact of square ones for the sake of a good puzzle story."},{"id":9589602,"author":"ars","month":"2015-05","story":"New Greaseless Bearings Spin with 10 Times Less Friction","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman explains slanted wheels steering trains back onto the track.","quote":"","text":"> doesn't a speed change imply a force on the bearing .... imply friction Not necessarily. A speed change could also be because the track is longer. That's how trains stay on the tracks, the wheels are slanted, and if they turn off the track the length of the wheel changes, which changes the speed (relative to the wheel on the other side) and steers it back onto the track. Feynman explains it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6167624,"author":"arh68","month":"2013-08","story":"We still don't really know how bicycles work","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman's explanation of how trains stay on track, analogous to bicycles.","quote":"","text":"If Feynman were still around, he'd set things straight. I don't think a bicycle staying upright is much different from a train keeping course [1], but I can't describe why. Instead of the rail veering off and the wheel adapting, it's like the bike does what it wants and the ground shifts beneath it. If you've read about Einstein's pail-of-milk-on-a-lazy-susan, it's a similarly unintuitive frame of reference. Also, if the experts haven't figured it out: I don't know what I'm talking about. I find it fascinating that we as a species can observe, define, and exploit Maxwell's law, neutrino physics, etc, but we can't clearly explain bicycles. Or why wings provide lift. EDIT: forgot the Feynman video [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["klaJqofCsu4",1],["aY985qzn7oI",1],["YdOXS_9_P4U",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["MvgN5gCuLac",1]]},"z9UjeTMb3Yk":{"wy":"Cited as the original Inbox Zero talk, and for Mann's point that optimizing how you organize tasks is no substitute for doing them.","q":{"x":"lifehacking is similar to optimizing the process of taking orders and organizing them, but at some point those orders actually need to be fulfilled","i":6122109,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":22260424,"author":"johnkarahalis","month":"2020-02","story":"Basecamp to launch email competitor","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the Inbox Zero method for taming email.","quote":"It really does help.","text":"For years, I have been using the Inbox Zero method ( [VIDEO] ) to stay sane. It really does help. I regularly empty my inbox while others seem stuck in the hell that is 9,000 unread emails. I have known people to declare email bankruptcy. Why should anyone have to declare defeat to a productivity tool, let alone one that didn't exist when my parents were born? Insanity! Even with my successes reigning in email, I realize that I have it backwards. Technology should adapt to us, not the other way around. I'm interested to see what Hey is all about."},{"id":38812599,"author":"neilv","month":"2023-12","story":"Show HN: Inbox Zero – open-source email assistant","stance":"reference","why":"Original Inbox Zero talk by Merlin Mann at Google.","quote":"Google TechTalks, 2007-07-23","text":"How does this software relate to the popular email inbox management method of the same name? Merlin Mann, \"Inbox Zero\", Google TechTalks, 2007-07-23. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6122109,"author":"enraged_camel","month":"2013-07","story":"Down with lifehacking","stance":"reference","why":"Merlin Mann's burger joint analogy: optimizing orders is pointless unless you fulfill them.","quote":"lifehacking is similar to optimizing the process of taking orders and organizing them, but at some point those orders actually need to be fulfilled","text":"One thing I remember from watching the Inbox Zero presentation [1] by Merlin Mann a couple of years ago was the analogy he used involving a burger joint. Paraphrasing, he said that lifehacking is similar to optimizing the process of taking orders and organizing them, but at some point those orders actually need to be fulfilled. Otherwise you'll end up with a lot of angry customers, who don't care about what awesome optimizations you're doing behind the scenes! This is not to say that lifehacking is useless. Rather, the emphasis is that ultimately, you have to get things done. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13253369,"author":"bootload","month":"2016-12","story":"Why time management is ruining our lives","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Merlin Mann totally misses this fact.","text":"\"he would stand in front of management and say \"no, fuck that, that's useless\". He was quite successful and could \"get away\" with this behaviour, though I suspect it was this very behaviour which had something to do with his success.\" Your manager gets what Merlin Mann could not, the most important tasks are the ones you need to do ^right now^, to completion. It's interesting that Bethlehem Steel is mentioned. Charles R. Schwab, the Bethlehem Steel boss, someone who Thomas Edison described as a ^hustler^, engaged Ivy Ledbetter Lee to improve productivity at Bethlehem for managers. [1] What is not mentioned is the fact Lee was a business owner of an early PR company and approached Bethlehem for business. His hack to Bethlehem management, set aside 15 minutes at the end of a day and specify six tasks you need done tomorrow; * Prioritise them, one to six; * Do each task, in order, till finished; * Work your arse off; * Left-over tasks are added to tomorrows list; * Repeat; I coin this, JIT task task management [3] and that simple behaviour your manager shows reflects this simple idea. I Merlin Mann totally misses this fact. [4] I was so impressed with Lees idea I wrote an article and some software to explore this idea. References [1] James Clear, \"The Ivy Lee Method\" ~ [LINK] [2] \"Zero Tasks: Maximum Six. Add new tasks to leftovers. Prioritise. Kill one task at a time. Repeat.\", [LINK] [3] A month ago: \" To-Do Lists Are Not the Answer to Getting Things Done\" ~ [LINK] [4] Merlin "}]},"3mnG_Gbxf_w":{"wy":"HN cites it mainly critically, as one of several tests used to question Tesla's collision avoidance compared with lidar systems.","m":[{"id":33485104,"author":"Veserv","month":"2022-11","story":"Fifteen New Tests Reveal Tesla FSD Still Runs Down Child-Sized Mannequins","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a different test about Tesla AEB.","quote":"This is unrelated to the Dawn Project tests which evaluate the ability to avoid child pedestrians without intervention as seen in the videos.","text":"What lidar product are you referring to? The Dawn Project and founder are unaffiliated with any lidar company. Are you perhaps referencing the unrelated test [1] by a unrelated lidar or maybe AEB company that was making the rounds a few months ago? That test was demonstrating the Tesla AEB system and whether it would override the human pressing on the accelerator like the other car. This is unrelated to the Dawn Project tests which evaluate the ability to avoid child pedestrians without intervention as seen in the videos. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34823358,"author":"rvnx","month":"2023-02","story":"Tesla recalls 362,758 vehicles, says full self-driving beta may cause crashes","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"And random public tests too","text":"There are many of such tests in the open. There is even a former Tesla AI engineer that throws objects in front of the car on YouTube, as a demonstration. The results are not glorious at all :| (trying to find the channel back if someone knows). And random public tests too: [VIDEO] This is a basic safety auto-braking. Just feels very wrong to even accept it goes into release."},{"id":33985931,"author":"westpfelia","month":"2022-12","story":"Tesla Full Self-Driving data looks awful","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Would it have though?","text":"> It would've still stopped Would it have though? [VIDEO] At this point I can't imagine buying anything Tesla related. Or anything related to Elon Musk for that matter. He's a grifter who has managed to stand on the shoulders of giants and call himself tall."},{"id":33989634,"author":"vgt","month":"2022-12","story":"Waymo's collision avoidance testing","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Compare Waymo[0] with Tesla [1].","text":"Compare Waymo[0] with Tesla [1].. These are easily searchable which leads me to question your sincerity in feinting ignorance. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"9IEFD_JVYd0":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a reference point or negative example in debates about campus politics and online codes of conduct.","m":[{"id":10655538,"author":"Tenhundfeld","month":"2015-12","story":"This Is Not a Day Care. It’s a University","stance":"reference","why":"Video of the Yale Halloween costume protest.","quote":"I think this is the video","text":"I think this is the video: [VIDEO] Along with some NYT coverage on this general topic: [LINK] This issue of students complaining about trigger warnings and the need for safe spaces certainly seems to be increasingly prevalent, but that could just be due to the media giving it more attention."},{"id":12956316,"author":"throwaway4891a","month":"2016-11","story":"Trump Won Because Leftist Political Correctness Inspired a Terrifying Backlash","stance":"critical","why":"Cited as example of campus SJW bikeshedding.","quote":"","text":"Gad Saad and Jonathan Haidt bring sensibility to causes and effects of the Karpman drama triangle that displaced actual, past revolutionary action on college campuses / social sciences with mostly an imposter, going through the motions but stuck bikeshedding (i.e., microaggressions, SJWing) rather than attacking major social problems. There's also helicopter parenting, lack of movement leadership, fall of SFDS, extended adolescence, for-profit universities catering to student whims and other aspects which compound the situation. Critical thinking skills, respectful debate without onerous preconditions (i.e., demanding apologies/subservience) and proportionality of response could be emphasized more in education. See also: - \"ABOLISH ALL SCIENCE 'COS ITS RACIST! -SJW University Student\" [LINK] - \"Yale University Students Protest Halloween Costume Email (VIDEO 3)\" [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK]"},{"id":11000287,"author":"cat-dev-null","month":"2016-01","story":"Matz: I cannot accept “the CoC” for the Ruby community","stance":"critical","why":"Used as example of browbeating drama behind amorphous bureaucracy.","quote":"browbeating others into bending to their will a-la Yale screaming girl","text":"Yeah, me too. -1. There is legitimate trolling, harassment, drama and d!ck moves that need to be handled with decency, common-sense and least bad measures... in open societies in real life, specific laws and enforcement haven't yet been able to eliminate #sshole behavior... it's usually handled socially. Sure it's different, but there's got to be some common sense and due-process, rather than instahellban of a core dev because someone new makes a bogus accusation. 0. Folks should try to calmly stand up to bullyvictim behavior and excessive microagression drama. 1. On large projects facing mostly public / non-enterprise / younger users, there could be need for formal training of project leaders or designated third-parties in conflict resolution / mediation to make peace, where possible. (Maybe open source conflict resolution as a service would be a viable startup? IDK, or a pockethotline perhaps?) 2. 37signals showed less anonymous participation increased kindness and reduced toxic behavior... perhaps projects should link accounts to G+ / Facebook / Github? 3. A risk of blindly adopting CoCs is that a new, amorphous bureaucracy of unknown persons/governance itself gains a foothold into many projects, and may come back and start asking for compliance reports, more control and more changes. Some of this might be good, but it could become more about browbeating others into bending to their will a-la Yale screaming girl [VIDEO] 4. Popularity of a behavior or trend is not necessari"}]},"D2fYguIX17Q":{"wy":"HN posts it as a joke in threads about shifting nutrition and health science advice.","m":[{"id":40612918,"author":"alamortsubite","month":"2024-06","story":"The flip-flop on whether alcohol is good for you (2023)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"You'll probably enjoy this clip from the Woody Allen movie Sleeper","text":"You'll probably enjoy this clip from the Woody Allen movie Sleeper, where his character is revived 200 years in the future after being cryogenically frozen in 1973. Just watch the first minute or so. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21119948,"author":"NeedMoreTea","month":"2019-09","story":"New research questions the evidence for health benefits of eating less red meat","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"He wakes up hundreds of years in the future, and starts asking for health foods.","text":"Sleeper. He wakes up hundreds of years in the future, and starts asking for health foods. From the early 70s. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16719382,"author":"delbel","month":"2018-03","story":"Nicotinamide riboside effects on aging similar to caloric restriction","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Who knew the movie Sleeper had it right, keto diet and tabacco.","text":"Smoking cigarettes would do it. Nicotine + Oxygen right? Who knew the movie Sleeper had it right, keto diet and tabacco. [VIDEO]"}]},"DAcjV60RnRw":{"wy":"HN uses it as the reference for counting how many songs or melodies are possible from a finite set of notes.","q":{"x":"As for melodies - we won't run out of them anytime soon.","i":19142400,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21982881,"author":"grenoire","month":"2020-01","story":"How much innate knowledge can the genome encode?","stance":"reference","why":"Analogy for counting possible songs from the chromatic scale","quote":"how many individual songs you can write with the chromatic scale","text":"This is likely akin to the question of how many individual songs you can write with the chromatic scale ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":19142400,"author":"throwmeback","month":"2019-02","story":"Millennials still recognise songs from music’s 1960s-90s golden age, study finds","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that we will not run out of melodies","quote":"As for melodies - we won't run out of them anytime soon.","text":"I don't have much time because I'm at work, so I won't write everything I have on my mind. I've been in your spot before, I also have perfect pitch (which I'd argue is irrelevant to the discussion) and I severely disagree. There's more music being produced than ever before. There is a vast amount of music you didn't listen to yet - it's humanly impossible to sift through it all. There's obviously a lot of cheap, quickly produced music, but IMHO the way you describe it makes it feel like \"music died 15 years ago\". It didn't. E.g. we still have readily identifiable trends in music (eg. indie, vaporwave, chillwave, trap beats). Filling up concert halls isn't a viable way of measuring an artist's achievement in the internet age for me. People go to concert just because they know the band's name, because someone bought them a ticket or because the media built up the hype. As for melodies - we won't run out of them anytime soon. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10709217,"author":"cleansy","month":"2015-12","story":"Show HN: Jukedeck – create unique, royalty-free music for your videos using A.I.","stance":"reference","why":"Vsauce on whether we will run out of new music","quote":"Michael from Vsauce talked about","text":"This could replace my spotify subscription at all. It's exciting to hear a song that I and a software have made for the first time and maybe I am the only one who ever listens to it*. Michael from Vsauce talked about 'will we ever run out of new music?' [VIDEO]"}]},"Dek5HtNdIHY":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up as a joke or shorthand in threads about diversity hiring and women in tech.","m":[{"id":26394683,"author":"henry_bone","month":"2021-03","story":"Four times I felt discriminated against for being a female developer","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This SV clip says it for me.","text":"This SV clip says it for me. In the words of Guilfoyle \"... we should hire the best person for the job. Period.\" [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15948802,"author":"kelukelugames","month":"2017-12","story":"I don’t want to be the pink elephant in the room","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Has anyone seen the scene from SV where Jared is excited after they hired a woman engineer?","text":"Has anyone seen the scene from SV where Jared is excited after they hired a woman engineer? And Jared expects her to be pals with Monica because both are female? I feel some of us men behave that way. :( link to clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42955446,"author":"paxys","month":"2025-02","story":"Google kills diversity hiring targets","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Still the perfect representation of the entire effort.","text":"All the talk about \"diversity\" in tech companies always reminds me of this Silicon Valley scene - [VIDEO] . Still the perfect representation of the entire effort."},{"id":18187390,"author":"thoughtexplorer","month":"2018-10","story":"Amazon scraps secret AI recruiting tool that showed bias against women","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Silicon Valley did a good bit on this","text":"Yes. There is a bonus to being a woman in this situation, all other things being equal. Silicon Valley did a good bit on this [VIDEO] It's funny because it's true."}]},"F_7IPm7f1vI":{"wy":"Used as a data point on how capable humanoid robots are at practical tasks, with commenters noting how far they are from household chores.","m":[{"id":43435497,"author":"Symmetry","month":"2025-03","story":"Boston Dynamics shows off another major leap in humanoid mobility","stance":"reference","why":"Atlas doing practical tasks, less impressively than its acrobatics.","quote":"But you can still find videos of Atlas doing practical tasks, just not as impressively as it jumps around.","text":"When a robot is moving itself it has a very good idea of what each part of itself weighs, how big it is, and has sensors to tell it what angle all its joints are at. These are all much more difficult with an object the robot is holding in its hand. But you can still find videos of Atlas doing practical tasks, just not as impressively as it jumps around. [VIDEO] A mentor of mine in robotics talks about how the people at his research group would watch a video from an old Julia Childs TV show of her chopping vegetables and doing other cooking tasks and they would try to figure out how long until a robot could do those things."},{"id":42845931,"author":"hnuser123456","month":"2025-01","story":"The impact of competition and DeepSeek on Nvidia","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Atlas manipulating objects, which still looks years from folding laundry.","quote":"Folding, hanging, and dresser drawer operation appears to be a few years out still.","text":"2 months ago, Boston Dynamics' Atlas was barely able to put solid objects in open cubbies. [1] Folding, hanging, and dresser drawer operation appears to be a few years out still. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43118776,"author":"mikehollinger","month":"2025-02","story":"Helix: A vision-language-action model for generalist humanoid control","stance":"critical","why":"Points to a moment where Atlas moves dangerously fast.","quote":"You can see it bump and then move VERY quickly","text":"From a different robot (Boston Dynamics' new Atlas) - the system moves at a \"reasonable\" speed. But watch at 1m20s in this video[1]. You can see it bump and then move VERY quickly -- with speed that would certainly damage something, or hurt someone. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"H4ThZcq1oJQ":{"wy":"Cited as the moment Trump supposedly broke the Bush dynasty's hold on the GOP by making the Iraq War a wedge issue.","q":{"x":"Trump overthrew the Bush dynasty by eviscerating Jeb over the Iraq War","i":43253394,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46485616,"author":"0xDEAFBEAD","month":"2026-01","story":"Loud noises heard in Venezuela capital, southern area without electricity","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Iraq was a key wedge issue which allowed Trump to gain mindshare within the GOP","text":"People forget that Iraq was a key wedge issue which allowed Trump to gain mindshare within the GOP: [VIDEO] A fairly optimistic way to spin this situation is as follows. Either it somehow works out for Venezuela, in which case we effectively helped millions of people Homer Simpson style. Or, more likely, failure disgraces Trump the same way it disgraced GWB. Then (fingers crossed) we elect a humbler, more realistic leader who works to rebuild the country we wrecked, and we can move on from the Trump era."},{"id":44909522,"author":"WillPostForFood","month":"2025-08","story":"What does Palantir actually do?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"And in the 2015 Debate attack Jeb.","text":"Trump launched MAGA by attacking the Iraq war, and the Bush family. This is 2008: “When she first got in and was named speaker, I met her. And I’m very impressed by her. I think she’s a very impressive person. I like her a lot. But I was surprised that she didn’t do more in terms of Bush and going after Bush. It was almost – it just seemed like she was going to really look to impeach Bush and get him out of office, which personally I think would’ve been a wonderful thing,” Trump told Blitzer in the interview. “Impeaching him?” Blitzer asked. “Absolutely. For the war. For the war,” said Trump, referring to the 2003 invasion of Iraq. “Well, he lied. He got us into the war with lies, and I mean, look at the trouble Bill Clinton got into with something that was totally unimportant and they tried to impeach him, which was nonsense. And yet Bush got us into this horrible war with lies. By lying. By saying they had weapons of mass destruction. By saying all sorts of things that turned out not to be true.” And in the 2015 Debate attack Jeb. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43865526,"author":"rayiner","month":"2025-05","story":"Retailers will soon have only about 7 weeks of full inventories left","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"He won the primary because he curb-stomped Jeb Bush on live TV","text":"> The USA’s global influence and entanglements goes all the way back to the Monroe Doctrine and grew substantially after WWII The U.S. had virtually no engagement with the world outside the western hemisphere between the GOP’s formation in 1854 and World War I. Between Lincoln’s election in 1861 to World War I tariff rates exceeded 40%: [LINK] > It still doesn’t explain how he won the GOP primary the first time and continues to maintain a cult-like following. He won the primary because he curb-stomped Jeb Bush on live TV and called for immigration bans when Rubio was proposing amnesty. [VIDEO] . He maintains a cult-like following because he’s the first republican in recent memory to actually deliver conservative victories instead of blowing all their political capital on wars. Since he took office in 2016, conservatives have ended affirmative action, overturned Roe , cut border crossings nearly to zero, established a legal framework for prosecuting DEI under the civil rights laws, ended disparate impact, shut down entire federal agencies, raised tariffs for the first time since the 1960s. We might get a $45 billion budget for ICE! I’m not even covering everything in the EOs, which have gone under the radar because everyone is flipping out over tariffs. Trump makes clear that Bush/Cheney republicans weren’t even trying. > If the poll numbers are to be believed, isolationists and nationalists never made up more than a slim percentage of the American electorate until very recent"},{"id":43253161,"author":"rayiner","month":"2025-03","story":"U.S. pauses all military aid to Ukraine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"overthrew the GOP leadership by destroying Jeb Bush over the Iraq War","text":"There’s no “propaganda network.” America has a president that overthrew the GOP leadership by destroying Jeb Bush over the Iraq War: [VIDEO] . After the mistakes of the last two decades, there is little appetite in America to do anything in foreign countries americans can’t find on a map beyond empty virtue signaling."},{"id":43253394,"author":"rayiner","month":"2025-03","story":"U.S. pauses all military aid to Ukraine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Trump overthrew the Bush dynasty by eviscerating Jeb over the Iraq War","text":"You think random factoids are driving Trump’s viewpoint? Trump overthrew the Bush dynasty by eviscerating Jeb over the Iraq War: [VIDEO] . Now, neocons want to chalk that up as bad execution of an otherwise sound ideology. But you don’t think that Trump’s supporters could genuinely disagree with that assessment—think that the underlying problem is that america is too willing to intervene militarily in places that don’t directly affect the U.S.? You think that view is driven by Russian propaganda? The way I see it, Ukraine is the test case for the foreign policy populist Democrats have long supported. The Cold War is over. The likelihood that Putin will invade Germany or France next is low. Regional wars are just that, and we should let them play out without getting involved. Otherwise you’re still subscribing to Reaganite neoconservatism—you’re just quibbling about the details of whether to involve the american military empire in any particular skirmish."},{"id":43261764,"author":"rayiner","month":"2025-03","story":"U.S. pauses all military aid to Ukraine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"And then I saw the US make a $6 trillion mistake with the Iraq War.","text":"By the time I was growing up in the 1990s, Korea and Vietnam were widely regarded as mistakes. And then I saw the US make a $6 trillion mistake with the Iraq War. So when Trump called out America’s latest “big fat mistake,” that’s basically what he was running on: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43261801,"author":"rayiner","month":"2025-03","story":"U.S. pauses all military aid to Ukraine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"captured the GOP by basically beheading the next-in-line of the Bush dynasty","text":"I don’t know if you’ve been following American politics. But the guy who is currently President captured the GOP by basically beheading the next-in-line of the Bush dynasty that was responsible for those “past 20 years.” [VIDEO] The criticism of decades of American foreign policy was finally vindicated. So there is very little appetite to give up that achievement by getting embroiled in yet another war that’s billed as “easy” and “just” this time (they all were—remember, “we will be greeted as liberators?”)"},{"id":43110917,"author":"rayiner","month":"2025-02","story":"Palantir Drops 10% on Report of Pentagon Slashing Budget","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Trump destroyed the Bush dynasty in one debate and took over the party","text":"Yes, and then Trump destroyed the Bush dynasty in one debate and took over the party: [VIDEO] . To the point where Jeb’s son couldn’t even win the nomination for Texas AG, and Cheney’s daughter was thrown out of Congress."}]},"JYqfVE-fykk":{"wy":"HN only uses it as a joke reference in unrelated threads.","m":[{"id":48160966,"author":"georgemcbay","month":"2026-05","story":"Points are a weird and inconsistent unit of measure","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Real question is why US just don't move to metric system? Because we live in a land of liberty! [VIDEO]"},{"id":47747645,"author":"jmalicki","month":"2026-04","story":"Uncharted island soon to appear on nautical charts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"In the words of the great General Washington, we use meters and kilometers for certain unpopular sports like swimming. So for measuring an island, use of swimming units seems appropriate. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45798125,"author":"thelastgallon","month":"2025-11","story":"Tiny electric motor can produce more than 1,000 horsepower","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It helps to understand that the only freedom Americans only cared for (and the only freedom they have left from the looks of it) is the freedom to choose standards of measurement and vocabulary. This will provide historical context: [VIDEO] (Washington's Dream - SNL)"},{"id":42834796,"author":"cocacola1","month":"2025-01","story":"Quiet Quitting: Why Employees Are Demanding Fairness and Boundaries","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Not all solutions require monetary compensation. Employers often overlook what employees find rewarding. This is where a culture of curiosity becomes invaluable—simply asking employees what motivates and empowers them can reveal unexpected insights. Acts like acknowledging sacrifices, allowing flexibility for parenting emergencies, or offering an afternoon off after a rough day can build goodwill and strengthen the employer-employee relationship. This reminds me of that recent SNL skit where Nate Bargatze plays George Washington and when Keenan Thompson asks him about the slaves, he changes the topic hastily: [VIDEO] What employees want is money . All that extra stuff is nice, in addition to more money for their work."},{"id":41733333,"author":"jajko","month":"2024-10","story":"Gamma radiation is produced in large tropical thunderstorms","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes, but that's only because societies basically gave up on trying to get better in such ways and laid back very deep in their 'comfort zone' for the lack of better words, laziness and fear of challenge may be another. You know, the place where ossification happens, be it in individuals or in millions. Sweden switched to right hand driving in 1967, I don't think if they postponed it till today they would still dare (not bashing swedes in any way, happy to be wrong here). Now that's a brave move, and everybody gained from it. Imagine US switching say to left hand side if whole world would be driving like that... nope. It doesn't matter if metric (of Celzius based on freakin' H2O states for fucks sake) system is vastly simpler, especially the less intelligent/educated one is (since we have 10 fingers in our face 24/7 and we learn counting on them), ego is too big, thus you guys measure in washing machines, football fields etc. You guys even fail to realize that whole US population would benefit, scientists don't care they will use whatever suits them and they are smart enough to not be slowed down by units used or their conversion. Nice summary via great Nate Bargatze in this SNL sketch [1]. Its fun to make fun of but its also sad, because it really is pure ego game, nothing more and those are always childish and immature at their core, throttling the potential for greatness in hard to measure ways. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40980491,"author":"breck","month":"2024-07","story":"Show HN: Contacts: A Microlang for Managing Contacts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This documentary might change your mind: [VIDEO] > There is _one_ correct way to write dates But seriously what if a comet hits earth at the perfect vector to reduce it's spin rate so that's it's slower than it's orbit rate?"}]},"Q1YqgPAtzho":{"wy":"Cited as a goofy layman's introduction to the double slit experiment in quantum physics threads.","m":[{"id":21363266,"author":"jchook","month":"2019-10","story":"Double Slit Experiment No Mystery","stance":"reference","why":"Goofy layman explanation stressing observation effects on electrons.","quote":"When I watched a goofy layman’s explanation","text":"When I watched a goofy layman’s explanation[1] about the double slit, it stressed the importance of the effects of observation on the behavior or nature of the electrons. When observed to see “which” slit the light passed through, they no longer created the interference pattern described by Duane’s law. And when left unobserved (at the slits) the interference pattern emerged even when shooting “one electron at a time” at the slits. Does this article or Duane’s Law cover or appreciate this? It seems like it only describes the interference pattern. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11801006,"author":"studentrob","month":"2016-05","story":"What should we learn from past AI forecasts?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Only if you start by assuming that \"free\" will must, necessarily, be supernatural. But of course, if you start that way, you've started everything in bad faith, because you're going to reject any possible view of the world that relies on science instead of magic. I'm willing to admit there are parts of science we don't understand, and that people often label this as \"magic\". In my opinion, sometimes humans call it religion and use it as a form of hope as we continue in the quest to know the unknown. Admittedly, any tool can be used to do harm too, and clearly in 2016, many of us can see how many religions have been perverted. Yet at the same time I think religious folks tend to levy the same criticism of strict scientists. Anyway, I have no beef with either side, and I know I won't be able to convince anyone here that science can also be viewed as a religion with its own biases. Plus I believe that most of the time science is great. I just don't know about this quest for true AI. It feels religion-y to me. > No sensible definition of free will says that you can will yourself to desire slood (a substance you've never heard of because it doesn't actually exist and has no properties even as a hypothetical) or will yourself out of the influence of gravity. Right, no, I didn't mean it like that. The thing I wanted to share is that free will and determinism cannot co-exist, in my opinion, for the reason stated in my last comment. Say I have a choice - pizza or pasta. If I choose "},{"id":30576145,"author":"russellbeattie","month":"2022-03","story":"A wave function does not collapse upon detection?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Watch this video if you're unclear what I'm asking","text":"Does anyone have a link to a real world example of the double slit experiment where the photons are observed at the slits , causing the wave function of the light to collapse so the light acts as a particle beyond the slit, ending up in two piles on the detector screen, rather than the wave pattern. Every time I ask this, some do-gooder will not read a word I wrote and send me a video of the basic slit experiment and think they've somehow educated me. Every time. It's amazing. What I'm looking for is a photo or video of a real experiment showing just two groupings. There a million photos/videos of the wave pattern. I'm looking for an example with exactly two clumps of photons because the light is observed as it passes each slit. In real life, not a computer simulation. Watch this video if you're unclear what I'm asking: [VIDEO]"}]},"UFcb-XF1RPQ":{"wy":"HN quotes the scene when a better product loses to a more pragmatic or dominant competitor.","q":{"x":"You don't get it Steve, that doesn't matter.","i":40359103,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40359103,"author":"urda","month":"2024-05","story":"Meta Is Shuttering Workplace, Its Enterprise Version of Facebook","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"You don't get it Steve, that doesn't matter.","text":"> It isn't. Slack is better. \"You don't get it Steve, that doesn't matter.\" [1] That actually does not matter if Teams is making larger progress than Slack. I prefer Slack over Teams too, but to disregard the market force and market position of Teams is naive. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39873146,"author":"ksec","month":"2024-03","story":"Can Demis Hassabis save Google?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Steve Jobs said they have the better products.","text":"Off Topic: >Google’s core business is thriving, but that almost seems beside the point. I have vidid memory of a scene in Private of the Silicon Valley, where Microsoft is winning, and Apple is losing. But Steve Jobs said they have the better products. And Bill Gate replied \"It doesn't matter.\" The movie was 25 years ago at the time it finally strike me what I was so irritated about. No one gives a damn about quality, they only cares about the money. 25 Years later ( Well I thought Google were bad for more than a decade but I guess it is more accepted now. ) Google are making all the money but their products are absolutely crap. While not crap but degradation of quality is happening to Apple too. As many comments have already pointed out, AI wont save Google. Google has a leadership and culture problem along with Management issues. It is a bit like 2012 - 2017 when people were so certain Intel will continue to lead in SemiConductor with healthy profits and business. Until 2020 they realise Intel finally did fall behind and the culture and management couldn't turns things around. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38008736,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2023-10","story":"Microsoft now demands to know why you won't use Edge when you download Chrome","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Using Microsoft products is for people that did not read the fable of the Scorpion and the Frog. [LINK] The character of Microsoft as a company is well known since their early days. It is not wise to trust them. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32614434,"author":"insightcheck","month":"2022-08","story":"Slack’s free plan change is causing an exodus","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This Pirates of Silicon Valley scene comes to mind","text":"Microsoft is in a tough place in that respect, because its user base is so broad by design (as a default program in Windows 11 now). It can never be fully optimized for professionals in formal environments, nor professionals who prefer more casual environments. This Pirates of Silicon Valley scene comes to mind [0]. Teams may not be the most optimized software for formal versus informal users, but that doesn't matter because it's trying to have a broad appeal. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43980458,"author":"amy_petrik","month":"2025-05","story":"The world could run on older hardware if software optimization was a priority","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant apocrypha","text":"> We were sure that a better product would win people over and lead to viral success. It didn’t. Things grew, but so slowly that we ran out of money after a few years before reaching break even. Relevant apocrypha: [VIDEO]"}]},"UYHFrf5ci_g":{"wy":"HN mentions are thin and mostly cite it in Lightning Network discussions, as a reference point for that critical viewpoint rather than a recommendation.","m":[{"id":31390718,"author":"cowtools","month":"2022-05","story":"The problem with Bitcoin miners","stance":"reference","why":"Cited regarding LN relay node operators and services","quote":"","text":"As someone who is more familiar with BCH rhetoric, I can tell you that what the parent comment is referring to is not mining companies but rather LN relay node operators and other \"services\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":26209153,"author":"hggh","month":"2021-02","story":"Nano is what Bitcoin hoped to be","stance":"reference","why":"Lightning network for faster and cheaper transactions","quote":"use lightning network, it's functional and growing in adoption.","text":"> If you want faster and cheaper transactions, use lightning network, it's functional and growing in adoption. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17091659,"author":"coyote-w-912","month":"2018-05","story":"Too Clever by Half","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The author describes how coyotes win the \"skirmish game\" when they casually walk into suburban yards and demonstrate that they are not afraid of the humans when they do things to scare them away such as make loud noises at them. However, they lose the \"meta-game\" when animal control shows up and puts them down because they won't go away. The author uses this analogy to compare coyotes to financial innovators. His argument is that every time financial innovation gets out of control and the economy at large is impacted, larger powers step in and squash it. He argues tha this is what happened in the 2008 financial crisis with mortage backed securities, and that this is the same thing happening now with Bitcoin and cryptocurrencies. I disagree with the parallel he draws to cryptocurrencies. While I don't necessarily think cryptocurrencies will dominate the world, and Governments may even succeed in reigning them in, they aren't in the same category as other financial innovation. Mortgage backed securites were the coyotes stalking across the laws of suburban moms, hoping animal control wouldn't interfere. Cryptocurrencies are a coordinated attack by a pack of coyotes on animal control headquarters. They were designed specifically to be as government-resistant as possible. Now, animal control has a lot of firepower and they can call in reinforcements. Governments have a lot of resources and powers they can draw on to stop and regulate cryptocurrencies. However, these coyotes are we"}]},"VWDFgzAjr1k":{"wy":"Used as a concrete example of what a car-free neighbourhood looks like and of the demand for them.","q":{"x":"","i":38271468,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39622986,"author":"mitthrowaway2","month":"2024-03","story":"The built environment and the determination of fault in urban pedestrian crashes","stance":"reference","why":"Car-free neighbourhoods are effectively illegal to build today","quote":"","text":"> Cities are welcome to erect whatever barriers they like to private cars. [VIDEO] \"The real problem is that we couldn’t build another place like this if we wanted to. It’s not allowed. It’s literally illegal in Canada, to build something like this today.\""},{"id":38271468,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-11","story":"Vehicles with higher, more vertical front ends pose greater risk to pedestrians","stance":"reference","why":"Shows what a car-free island looks like","quote":"Here's what a 100% car-free island looks like","text":"You're comparing living in a car dependant city vs living in car dependant suburbs. It's not going to be meaningfully different. You mention the constant noise, but what do you think causes it? To see what everyone means by car dependency, you'd need to see the few places that aren't. Here's what a 100% car-free island looks like: [VIDEO] Here are some progressive European cities that have started to undo the damage that's been done by cars: • Freiburg, Germany: [LINK] • Haarlem, Netherlands: [LINK] • Paris, France: [LINK] Check out these background noise levels: • A very busy mixed-use street in Amsterdam: [LINK] • Exiting the train station in Delft, Netherlands: [LINK] • Cycling streets in the Netherlands: [LINK] . You can even hear birds chirping at 4:29."},{"id":32295974,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2022-07","story":"When cities treated cars as dangerous intruders","stance":"reference","why":"Example of high demand for car-free neighbourhoods","quote":"","text":"> I’m not convinced that will ever change unless sprawl becomes unimaginably expensive. Right, but that's the core of my point: It is unimaginably expensive, it's just getting subsidized to ridiculous extents. (For the readers unaware of this: [LINK] ) And saying the \"vast majority\" prefer it is wrong. They prefer it over the alternatives they have within the United States , not over the alternative possibilities . I'm absolutely not dismissing that some people prefer the suburbian lifestyle over EU-style walkable cities, but saying the majority of Americans do when so few even know what's possible is misplaced in the best possible read of it. The demand for car-free neighbourhoods is also obvious if you look at what exists, and how exorbitant the prices are due to the high demand and low offer. Another example: [VIDEO] This song-and-dance of \"Everyone likes suburbia because everyone wants to move there, it's illegal to build anything else because it would then be something else than suburbia, nobody wants the alternative anyway because it's too expensive, it's too expensive because it's in high demand, and it's in high demand because it's illegal to build something else\" is like the definition of catch-22."}]},"YlGklt4BSQ8":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke or reference whenever advertising intrudes into a new medium, since the show predicted it.","m":[{"id":34919514,"author":"BLKNSLVR","month":"2023-02","story":"The FBI now recommends using an ad blocker when searching the web","stance":"reference","why":"Futurama predicted pervasive internet advertising decades ago.","quote":"It's only been predictable for the last 20 years... (first aired in March 2000)","text":"Google and Meta seem to have no idea what's going on within their own advertising networks. Similarly to all the stories (with two currently in the front page of HN, eBay and PayPal) about algorithms that are just insufficient for the range of realistic scenarios these companies must deal with on a regular basis. It's merely the equation of profit outweighing customer service. Admittedly, they're working on a scale that's difficult to comprehend, but that shouldn't absolve them of aiding and abetting criminal use of their systems. Google's and Meta's profit motives are the base cause of this continuing escalation of the ubiquity and user-hostility (to put it mildly) of internet advertising. It's only been predictable for the last 20 years... [VIDEO] (first aired in March 2000) [LINK] (April 1999)"},{"id":41654685,"author":"BLKNSLVR","month":"2024-09","story":"Orion, our first true augmented reality glasses","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> inject this crap into our dreams Well I just have to reference Futurama now: [LINK] Which is incomplete without: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27937142,"author":"BLKNSLVR","month":"2021-07","story":"Mark Zuckerberg says Facebook will turn into a ‘metaverse’","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Behold, the Facebook Metaverse: [VIDEO] “WE WILL EFFECTIVELY TRANSITION FROM PEOPLE SEEING US AS PRIMARILY BEING A SOCIAL MEDIA COMPANY TO BEING A METAVERSE COMPANY” But, Facebook already transitioned to being an advertising company. The social media aspect became a funnel to direct members towards advertising, changes to Facebook are not about the social aspect, they're about eyeballs on ads and more page views, which are actually distractions away from the social network roots of Facebook. Mark goes on about building an economy within this metaverse: \"And this is something that I hope eventually millions of people will be working in and creating content for — whether it’s experiences, or spaces, or virtual goods, or virtual clothing, or doing work helping to curate and introduce people to spaces and keep it safe. I just think this is going to be a huge economy and frankly, I think that that needs to exist. This needs to be a rising tide that lifts a lot of boats. We can’t just think about this as a product that we’re building.\" I'm obviously very cynical, but this feels like Facebook's app store play: 30% please creators. In addition to the advertising dollars. What I hope comes out of this is an advancement of the technology for building a \"metaverse\" and that open source alternatives can keep up, or at least nip at the heels constantly."},{"id":27539884,"author":"corobo","month":"2021-06","story":"Virtual reality ads make their way to Facebook's Oculus headsets","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It used to be entirely up to the users whether they wanted a Facebook account with it too My bet's on menu ads being first Oculus 2025: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14555342,"author":"logandavis","month":"2017-06","story":"Susan Fowler's Uber Expose Should Win a Pulitzer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"An interesting idea. Fowler certainly deserves credit for her work. As the author correctly notes, it was not only a courageous thing to publish, but also an impressive piece of writing. On an unrelated note, the experience of reading this article for me was absolutely destroyed by the Forbes ad-block-blocker and the ad-first design of their article view. There were a couple of autoplaying video ads and one particularly pernicious scroll-locking ad on the sidebar to the right. Forbes is one of the very worst offenders in web monetization, as they demand you turn off AdBlock and then serve you a garbage reading experience (usually of a poorly written article by an unpaid or low-paid \"contributor\"). Every time I visit their website, I feel a sudden urge to start espousing Ev William's gospel that Something Must Be Done about content monetization on the internet, before Futurama becomes reality: [VIDEO]"}]},"_OwG_rQ_Hqw":{"wy":"HN people point to it as a primary-source look at Star's interface design, especially its philosophy of fewer commands without fewer functions.","q":{"x":"Star had many fewer commands than today's systems, and it didn't do it by having fewer functions; it just had fewer commands .","i":10448265,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":41249003,"author":"AlbertCory","month":"2024-08","story":"Larry Tesler pioneered cut-and-paste, the one-button mouse, WYSIWYG (2005)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows David Canfield Smith involved with the Star","quote":"(he IS a little stiff in this)","text":"> So why haven’t you heard of him? Because you've had your head under a rock? It was headline news when he died (which was after this was published). > “And the question I remember most was from Steve Jobs. He said, ’You guys are sitting on a gold mine here. Why aren’t you making this a product?’” Xerox WAS making it into a product (the Star). Of course Larry couldn't tell him about that. It failed, just like the Lisa did. > As one of Tesler’s first tasks at PARC, he and a co-worker wrote a paper on the future of interactive computing, which for the first time talked about cut-and-paste as a way of moving blocks of text, images, and the like. It also described representing documents and other office objects stored on the computer as tiny images—icons—instead of as a list of names [see photo, ]. The \"co-worker\" was David Canfield Smith, who was directly involved in the Star, unlike Larry. [LINK] . (he IS a little stiff in this) [VIDEO]"},{"id":23078814,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-05","story":"Advanced Programming Languages (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"One of several PARC-era workstation demo videos","quote":"Here are some video sessions.","text":"Anything based on the workstation concepts explored at Xerox PARC, and further pursued at ETHZ, TI and Genera. Bitsavers has all the necessary papers and manuals, and computer museum as well. Here are some video sessions. \"Yesterday's Computer of Tomorrow: The Xerox Alto │Smalltalk-76 Demo\" [LINK] \"Alto System Project: Dan Ingalls demonstrates Smalltalk\" [LINK] \"Eric Bier Demonstrates Cedar\" [LINK] \"The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer\" [VIDEO] The end of the road from Plan 9, Inferno. [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":21398682,"author":"kps","month":"2019-10","story":"Simple Does Not Mean Ugly","stance":"reference","why":"Simple design need not remove functionality","quote":"Star had many fewer commands than today’s systems, and it didn’t do it by having fewer functions.","text":"> I've watched both sandbag good design because there was no percieved business value more than once. Riffing on the article title: Simple Does Not Mean Deficient. “Star had many fewer commands than today’s systems, and it didn’t do it by having fewer functions. It just had fewer commands.” — Dave Smith at The Final Demonstration of the Xerox ‘Star’ Computer (video: [VIDEO] text: [LINK] )"},{"id":15357200,"author":"kps","month":"2017-09","story":"Refresh Is Sacred","stance":"reference","why":"Star designer on fewer commands while keeping functionality","quote":"Star had many fewer commands than today's systems, and it didn't do it by having fewer functions","text":"> The easiest way to achieve that is simply to remove functionality The easiest way, yes. I am reminded again of the point made by Dave Smith, one of the Xerox Star human factors designers, at the ‘Final Demo’¹: “Star had many fewer commands than today's systems, and it didn't do it by having fewer functions — it just had fewer commands .” PARC weren't trying to make things easy on themselves . Genericity is hard. Orthogonality is hard. Design is hard. (It's unfortunate that Steve Jobs either didn't understand what he saw, or didn't care, and popularized a Potemkin village imitation.) ¹ [VIDEO] (The particular session starts around 54:30.)"},{"id":14545535,"author":"kps","month":"2017-06","story":"Is the keyboard faster than the mouse?","stance":"reference","why":"Star's two-handed mouse and function key model","quote":"the mouse and the left-side function key block worked together","text":"> you have two hands. Every watch ‘the mother of all demos’⁰? Engelbart's system was designed to be used with one hand on the mouse and the other on the chordset. Xerox followed this with the Alto, and on to the Star¹, eventually dropping the chordset but with the same model: the mouse and the left-side function key block worked together.² ⁰ [LINK] ¹ [LINK] ² [VIDEO]"},{"id":11877865,"author":"kps","month":"2016-06","story":"Too Many Knobs","stance":"reference","why":"Star designer on fewer commands without fewer functions","quote":"Star had many fewer commands than today’s systems, and it didn’t do it by having fewer functions. It just had fewer commands.","text":"“Star had many fewer commands than today’s systems, and it didn’t do it by having fewer functions. It just had fewer commands.” — Dave Smith¹ at The Final Demonstration of the Xerox ‘Star’ Computer² Most UX designers are not as good as David Smith; they worship Apple/Jobs' “function follows form” misunderstanding of the Star GUI, and think you need to remove functionality in order to remove complexity. ¹ [LINK] ² video: [VIDEO] text: [LINK]"},{"id":10448265,"author":"kps","month":"2015-10","story":"Engineers Build Ugly Products","stance":"reference","why":"Star designer on having fewer commands, not fewer functions","quote":"Star had many fewer commands than today's systems, and it didn't do it by having fewer functions; it just had fewer commands .","text":"And current designers and product managers build crippled products. “Star had many fewer commands than today's systems, and it didn't do it by having fewer functions; it just had fewer commands .” — Dave Smith, Xerox Star human factors designer, in the ‘Final Demo’ [VIDEO]"},{"id":7920840,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2014-06","story":"Web Starter Kit","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among historic workstation and UI demos","quote":"The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer","text":"Self [LINK] Xerox Star User Interface [LINK] [LINK] Tektronix 4404 Smalltalk [LINK] The Smalltalk-80 Programming System [LINK] The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer [VIDEO] Symbolics Hypertext Document [LINK] Warren Teitelman work at Xerox PARC [LINK] The Mesa/Cedar programming environment [LINK] Steve Jobs internal demo of NeXTSTEP 3, [LINK] Alan Kay on Xerox PARC work [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":7565751,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2014-04","story":"Shadow DOM","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among historic workstation and UI demos","quote":"The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer","text":"Mainly videos Self [LINK] Xerox Star User Interface [LINK] [LINK] Tektronix 4404 Smalltalk [LINK] The Smalltalk-80 Programming System [LINK] The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer [VIDEO] Symbolics Hypertext Document [LINK]"}]},"__CvmS6uw7E":{"wy":"HN mentions it mainly critically, as an example of Rogan spreading a claim later debunked.","m":[{"id":28307858,"author":"mhh__","month":"2021-08","story":"Joe Rogan, confined to Spotify, is losing influence","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The thing of Joe Rogan shouting at the primatologist about the \"new\" chimpanzee is genuinely hilarious [VIDEO]"},{"id":36433508,"author":"suzzer99","month":"2023-06","story":"Turning Joe Rogan down was the right thing to do","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Literally all RFK Jr. has to do is throw endless nonsense against the wall, doesn’t have to be consistent with itself, doesn’t have to come from real sources, and just trip up the PhD on one single issue. Then he can spike the football and claim victory. It’s an impossible setup. [LINK] Refute all 30 of these, NOW, and if you can’t then clearly you don’t know anything and RFK Jr. wins. When you’re done we got 30 more and basically an endless supply. Doesn’t matter if you prove 99 of them wrong. If you can’t prove one wrong during a live debate we claim victory. We don’t care if you show that our arguments are inconsistent with each other, and totally impugn one of our sources. We still expect you to refute endless nonsense from that same source. Because we’re debating in bad faith. You don’t ever get to grill JFK Jr., because he’s not the scientist, you are. It’s not a debate it’s an interrogation. Joe Rogan berated a primatologist for telling him that no, there is not a race of 6’ tall chimps who walk upright and sleep on the ground. These are not serious people. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32362558,"author":"cma","month":"2022-08","story":"Lex Fridman Podcast #309 – John Carmack","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sometimes he's like that. Other times with ivermectin etc. he slips back into this mode: [VIDEO] More and more since joining Spotify."},{"id":30217131,"author":"inferiorhuman","month":"2022-02","story":"Spotify deletes 70 Joe Rogan episodes","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Oh I liked the Rogaine + Burr episode where he shouted down a primatologist for being stupid and not checking the internet for wisdom first. He closed by mocking her for having a vagina. He's really an impressively free thinker. [VIDEO] \"You're a fucking idiot go online and look it up.\" \"Go online and look it up.\" \"Yeah you've learned from shit from college. When was the last time you got online and researched primates.\" \"Stupid\" \"Stupid\" \"Stupid\" … \"I have a vagina .\""},{"id":30150390,"author":"shepardrtc","month":"2022-01","story":"Joe Rogan Apologizes, Spotify Publishes Content Policy in Response to Neil Young","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he discusses the now-debunked bondo ape","text":"> alternative views I think you mean crackpot views. > carefully curated information which is incomplete Its curated because people attempt to confirm and verify the information. Nonsense like Joe Rogan has no verification - instead it has him Googling the subject for a few hours while doing a lot of drugs. To wit, here is a call of his where he discusses the now-debunked bondo ape: [VIDEO] . During the call, an expert phones in and he ridicules her without even listening to anything she has to say."}]},"a0BpfwazhUA":{"wy":"HN invokes it as a joke about infighting among groups with nearly identical aims.","m":[{"id":30068014,"author":"akkartik","month":"2022-01","story":"Gemini is Solutionism","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm not yet sure how I feel about Gemini (I like inline links), but this post makes me think it's starting to gain some traction. It's past the \"first they ignore it\" and \"then they laugh at it\" phases and now into the \"then they fight it\" phase. Given that the mainstream stack we all are dependent on has foundational problems as OP acknowledges, language like \"solutionism at its worst\" feels counter-productive. Surely anything Gemini does or doesn't isn't as damaging as status quo. So why not let a thousand flowers bloom? Criticism like this feels like the People's Front of Judea ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":26076054,"author":"guytv","month":"2021-02","story":"A Statement on Recent Events Between Signal and the Anti-Censorship Community","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Censorship and privacy are important issues. So is civilised online debate, and communities learning to work together in a nice way. I admire the people that put in time and energy to create a safer future for us all. Hope that this is not going to be taken the wrong way, but whenever I read such threads (and again - I respect all the people involved, their efforts and the importance of this issues) - I can't help but being reminded with this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24588118,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2020-09","story":"Democratic regression in comparative perspective: scope, methods, and causes","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Unless someone shows up who'll defend BenOp, I'm not looking further into it. As far as cursory surfing reveals (I have only been able to find a table of contents online whose chapter headings are not helpful) not only does Dreher not bother with the separatism of plain religions in the US, nor the experiences of other more heavily persecuted, more melaninly enabled, christian churches in the US, his doctrine doesn't accord with any of his[1] religions, nor does it, according to secondary criticism, seem to have been informed much by actual Benedictines[2]. Indeed, he's even been accused of rooting the whole project in reaction to Obergefell v. Hodges, 576 U.S. 644 (2015)[3]. [1] protestant, catholic, and orthodox, in that order. One is reminded of the person who is constantly looking for the New Shiny Library/Language instead of writing their app with the ecosystem they have at hand. (cue GKC quote on difficulties untried, Benedict on Sarabaites) For more similarities to open source schisms, consider [LINK] and replace the christian references with geeky projects such as \"building a modern operating system\", \"immanentising the crypto eschaton\", \"welcoming our new GPT-N overlords\", etc. [VIDEO] [2] who are still around to ask. There's a non-benedictine abbey near me which celebrated its 1'500th birthday a while ago, and at least a handful of benedictine which are probably not many centuries younger. TIL Benedictines are more Marxist than Marx. From each according to their abi"}]},"b2F-DItXtZs":{"wy":"Posted as a classic meme whenever MongoDB, NoSQL hype or tech fads come up.","m":[{"id":44176009,"author":"wpietri","month":"2025-06","story":"My AI skeptic friends are all nuts","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an example of a past 'world is about to change' hype","quote":"","text":"I think \"the reality\", at least as something involving a new paradigm, has yet to be established. I'll note that I heard plenty of similar talk about how developers just couldn't adapt six months or more ago. Promoters now can admit those tools were in fact pretty bad, because they now have something else to promote, but at the time those not rawdogging LLMs were dinosaurs under a big meteor. I do of course agree that some people are just refusing to \"wrap their minds around the changing world\". But anybody with enough experience in tech can count a lot more instances of \"the world is about to change\" than \"the world really changed\". The most recent obvious example being cryptocurrencies, but there are plenty of others. [1] So I think there's plenty of room here for legitimate skepticism. And for just waiting until things settle down to see where we ended up. [1] E.g. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29197551,"author":"arkush","month":"2021-11","story":"The Rust compiler has gotten faster again","stance":"reference","why":"Tangential link in a long rebuttal about Rust","quote":"","text":"The hvasilev's comment got flagged and I could not reply to it anymore, so I'll reply here (sorry) and copy-paste the hvasilev's comment verbatim below, for the sake of commenting on it's claims: --- Reality is not on the side of this language. 11 year old, has a very low adoption with virtually no jobs associated. ( [LINK] ) On the other hand if you search for \"Rust\" in the latest \"Who wants to be hired?\" thread, you will see it is quite popular with unemployed people. The reality is that the language has a lot of friction, the ergonomics are bad, the syntax is heavy and some poor decision making has been made there for a systems-level programming language. There are a lot of ideological traps in this industry and many people that fall for them. Why people are interested in ideologies and cults is beyond me. [ A screenshot: [LINK] ] --- <rant> Now my comments on the issues mentioned in the hvasilev's comment. > Reality is not on the side of this language. 11 year old, has a very low adoption with virtually no jobs associated. ( [LINK] ) Tiobe index is shit. The most flattering thing I've read about it states that it (poorly) depicts quantity of educational materials available online for particular programming language. Unfortunate naming of programming languages after letters of alphabet, symbols (++, #) and real-life stuff (like islands) doesn't help this rating either. That said, Rust isn't that popular and isn't growing much according to other better language ratings: 1. "},{"id":47220111,"author":"mikey_p","month":"2026-03","story":"OpenClaw Surpasses React to Become the Most-Starred Software Project on GitHub","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Who cares about stars on Github??? \"If dev null is fast and webscale I will use it\" \"Does dev null support sharding\" Who remembers [VIDEO]"},{"id":47280929,"author":"gavinray","month":"2026-03","story":"We Might All Be AI Engineers Now","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm reminded of the \"MongoDB is WebScale\" video: as of this moment I officially resigned from my job as software engineer and will take up work on the farm shoveling pig shit and administering anal suppositories to sick horses because that will be a thousand times more tolerable than being in the same industry as dipshits like you [VIDEO]"},{"id":45530473,"author":"thewebguyd","month":"2025-10","story":"Why Self-Host?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Majority of projects don't need the web scale or whatever. Truth. All the major cloud platform marketing is YAGNI but for infrastructure instead of libraries/code. As someone who works in ops and has since starting as a sysadmin in the early 00s, it's been entertaining to say the least to watch everyone rediscover hosting your own stuff as if it's some new innovation and wasn't ever possible before. It's like that old MongoDB is web scale video ( [VIDEO] ) Watching devs discover Docker was similarly entertaining back then when us in ops have been using LXC and BSD jails, etc. to containerize code pre-DevOps. Anyway, all that to say - go buy your gray beard sysadmins a coffee let them help you. We would all be thrilled to bring stuff back on-prem or go back to self hosting and running infra again and probably have a few tricks to teach you."},{"id":44834380,"author":"theshrike79","month":"2025-08","story":"GPT-5 for Developers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"But with a hour long video, how do you know if the content is any good? With text I can skim around the headings and images and see at a glance how deep the author is going into the subject. In that specific video the first 30 minutes is related to everything but the new Web Scale[0] LLM native database the author is \"moving to\" from SQL. Meanwhile Postgresql is just chugging along and over-performing all of them. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44164674,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2025-06","story":"Conformance checking at MongoDB: Testing that our code matches our TLA+ specs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"About 15 years ago or so, webscale was everything and MongoDB was webscale[1][2] hence being used everywhere. Has it become a legacy product, or are there still good reasons for using it in a new project in 2025? [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":43440507,"author":"Kilenaitor","month":"2025-03","story":"Tech terms I was pronouncing wrong","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I used to mispronounce Redis too and I fully fault the \"MongoDB is web scale\"[0] video for that. Glad I'm not alone heh [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40728003,"author":"bitwize","month":"2024-06","story":"Neofetch developer archives all his repositories: \"Have taken up farming\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Every time I hear a programmer has taken up farming I think of this moment from \"MongoDB Is Web Scale\": [VIDEO] Not a bad idea, actually."},{"id":38947559,"author":"annexrichmond","month":"2024-01","story":"How to get coworkers to stop giving me ChatGPT-generated suggestions?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm not looking forward to having to argue with a coworker that thinks MongoDB is web scale because ChatGPT told them so. It will be a hell of a lot more annoying than[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36389587,"author":"wasimanitoba","month":"2023-06","story":"Imaginary Problems Are the Root of Bad Software","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I wonder if you've ever seen this classic video mocking such marketing claims which is called \"MongoDB is Web Scale\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":33676575,"author":"MauranKilom","month":"2022-11","story":"Inside boost::unordered_flat_map","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I understand “web scale” in the marketing term Mongo sense That's the joke. (And for anyone who hasn't seen it: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":32261034,"author":"Banana699","month":"2022-07","story":"SQLite Internals: Pages and B-trees","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Mongo DB is a web scale DB and doesn't use SQL or Joins so it's high-performance. Everybody knows that relational DBs don't scale because they use Joins and Write-To-Disk.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":29736914,"author":"xcambar","month":"2021-12","story":"Consider SQLite","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Glad you liked it! The reference to this series of videos was intended of course. The specific video I had in mind was actually [VIDEO] Enjoy!"},{"id":24379755,"author":"slyall","month":"2020-09","story":"MongoDB History","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The \"Mongo DB is Web Scale\" video just turned 10 years old. I'm pretty sure that was a big factor in MongoDB not being taken seriously by many people. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23285542,"author":"senko","month":"2020-05","story":"Jepsen Disputes MongoDB's Data Consistency Claims","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Some readers might not be familiar with that particular meme","text":"Some readers might not be familiar with that particular meme: [VIDEO] IMHO it perfectly describes the hype-reality disconnect at the early days of MongoDB. Yeah it was that bad. Mongo has improved since, the hype has toned down and the NoSQL space is more crowded these days."},{"id":15135480,"author":"SkyMarshal","month":"2017-08","story":"The Marketing Behind MongoDB","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"From what I've observed, the \"crowd\" is easily seduced by performance above all other concerns - correctness, security, science, etc. NoSQL was invented and gained popularity b/c it originally was easier to scale via sharding, and that was seductive enough [1] to give it industry momentum. While performance is a feature, it seemed many folks originally advocating NoSQL did not understand or appreciate the mathematical foundations of the relational model. A decade of experience seems to be driving zeitgeist back to scientific fundamentals. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11685343,"author":"abraae","month":"2016-05","story":"SQL vs. NoSQL Databases: What’s the Difference?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of this classic","text":"And on that theme, then get ready for when your boss gets to the part about how NoSQL gives you: \"Rapid development. If you’re developing within two-week Agile sprints, cranking out quick iterations, or needing to make frequent updates to the data structure without a lot of downtime between versions, a relational database will slow you down.\" Reminds me of this classic [VIDEO]"},{"id":10099409,"author":"smoyer","month":"2015-08","story":"The Handmade Manifesto","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Learn X in Y minutes\" is an awesome concept! Let me know if you ever decide to run with it. I guess I don't fit the CoffeeScript is a hipster language profile very well but I still enjoy writing code in it ;) I basically agree with everything you've said (in both posts) ... you can be successful while wasting cycles. And if you're working on a low-volume and/or internal only application, you'll probably never face the limits of a modern server. If you need to operate \"at web scale\" [1], or run into an uncommon (or common) bug, you'll need to know more about the frameworks and systems you're code relies on (e.g. MongoDB configuration for systems over 2GB [2]). Blog posts like the one referenced are completely unfair to those that developed MongoDB - read the manual and understand how MongoDB works OR use it at your own risk. So I'll switch arguments and help you make your point. We have an application written in Oracle's Application Express - while we have extensive expertise in Oracle's database software, we have this one system which was completed for expediency's sake. It's kind of horrific but (mostly) works at the scale required. It would be financially foolish to dig into more deeply into ApEx for this one dead-end application. Everyone is happy. [1] [VIDEO] (audio NSFW) [2] [LINK]"},{"id":6674551,"author":"afandian","month":"2013-11","story":"OpenNote – an open web-based alternative to Microsoft OneNote and EverNote","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't like MySQL either. But it's probably the perfect choice. Or perhaps you think something webscale[1] is required? [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"bO4jHXjCXw8":{"wy":"Cited, usually critically, as a window into how payment companies view cryptocurrency and cash.","q":{"x":"These statements from an executive at MasterCard are a great window into the company's designs on cash and its electronic corollaries","i":14819602,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":26803085,"author":"CryptoPunk","month":"2021-04","story":"Update on beta testing payments in Signal","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Credit card company representative's statement on it in 2014","text":">>tax evasion and illegality hiding (which it isn't even good at). This is just a Big Brother smear against privacy, with a socialist twist emanating from those with a financial stake in empowering the state and its dependent industries over the private citizen. Exactly your arguments are made in the campaign to criminalize the use of cash: [LINK] Credit card company representative's statement on it in 2014: >There's huge interest in cryptocurrencies and what perhaps they can create in the market place. Now we at MasterCard are not completely comfortable with the idea of cryptocurrencies largely because they go against the whole principle that we've established our business on which is really moving to a world beyond cash and ensuring greater transparency.. If you think about it, cash is a problem for a number of countries. Cash really facilitates anonymity, it facilitates illegal activity, it facilitates tax avoidance and a range of other things that aren't going to drive efficiency in an economy - [VIDEO] >If it's an anonymous transaction, that sounds like a suspicious transaction. Why does somebody need to be anonymous? - [VIDEO]"},{"id":21383619,"author":"CryptoPunk","month":"2019-10","story":"Tether: The Story So Far","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The President of Mastercard South East Asia sums it up","text":"The article takes it is a priori that laws that institute warrantless mass surveillance of financial transactions, aka AML/KYC, are good. This is an opinion held mostly by those in the elite: people living in wealthy/politically-powerful countries, and or those with lucrative jobs in government or government-protected industries like banking. The President of Mastercard South East Asia sums it up: [VIDEO] \"If it's an anonymous transaction, that sounds like a suspicious transaction. Why does somebody need to be anonymous?\" AML/KYC laws create global second class citizens and massively centralize power in the hands of whichever state has the critical mass of financial power, which right now is the US and which one day could be another state that authors like this wouldn't be so enthusiastic about. A great write-up on what KYC/AML laws, which are euphemisms for laws criminalizing financial privacy, mean for those outside of the elite circles: [LINK] Another relevant article: the War on Cash [LINK] >>The proclaimed Death of Cash is thus an episode in the broader drama that is the Death of Privacy, the death of breathing room, and the death of informal, non-measured, unaccounted-for behaviour."},{"id":14678301,"author":"Jabanga","month":"2017-07","story":"SEC Files Fraud Charges in Bitcoin and Office Space Investment Schemes","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"MasterCard SE Asia President on cryptocurrencies and cash","text":">why not blacklist coins that have been through a mixer? If you can't mix the coins, you can't launder them. It won't be possible to use Bitcoin for these activities anymore. This would totally eliminate privacy. It would be no different than making HTTPS illegal. It's not just criminals that use privacy technology. Eliminating privacy in money for the sake of eliminating some crime at the margins is giving up the farm to gain a horse. It's making a situation where everyone is subjected to mass-surveillance, and power concentrates to a small politically connected elite that can monitor all private interactions, exponentially more likely to be realized. It's very dangerous to society. [LINK] >There's huge interest in cryptocurrencies and what perhaps they can create in the market place. Now we at MasterCard are not completely comfortable with the idea of cryptocurrencies largely because they go against the whole principle that we've established our business on which is really moving to a world beyond cash and ensuring greater transparency.. If you think about it, cash is a problem for a number of countries. Cash really facilitates anonymity, it facilitates illegal activity, it facilitates tax avoidance and a range of other things that aren't going to drive efficiency in an economy - [VIDEO] >If it's an anonymous transaction, that sounds like a suspicious transaction. Why does somebody need to be anonymous? - [VIDEO] MasterCard SE Asia President on cryptocurrencies and cash"},{"id":14819602,"author":"Jabanga","month":"2017-07","story":"A hacker stole $31M of Ether – how it happened, and what it means for Ethereum","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"These statements from an executive at MasterCard are a great window into the company's designs on cash and its electronic corollaries","text":"Cryptocurrencies, growing pains aside, will arrest the trend toward economic centralization. They are not just more powerful tools. They are tools that shift the control over money from large trusted third parties to individuals. They literally allow an individual to be their own bank. They are a counter-force against the trend toward eliminating cash, and with it, financial privacy [1]: >Any future cashless bank-payments society will be the outcome of a deliberate war on cash waged by an alliance of three elite groups with deep interests in seeing it emerge. These statements from an executive at MasterCard are a great window into the company's designs on cash and its electronic corollaries: >There's huge interest in cryptocurrencies and what perhaps they can create in the market place. Now we at MasterCard are not completely comfortable with the idea of cryptocurrencies largely because they go against the whole principle that we've established our business on which is really moving to a world beyond cash and ensuring greater transparency.. If you think about it, cash is a problem for a number of countries. Cash really facilitates anonymity, it facilitates illegal activity, it facilitates tax avoidance and a range of other things that aren't going to drive efficiency in an economy - [VIDEO] >If it's an anonymous transaction, that sounds like a suspicious transaction. Why does somebody need to be anonymous? - [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":12326204,"author":"aminok","month":"2016-08","story":"The War on Cash","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"MasterCard SE Asia President on cryptocurrencies and cash","text":"\"There's huge interest in cryptocurrencies and what perhaps they can create in the market place. Now we at MasterCard are not completely comfortable with the idea of cryptocurrencies largely because they go against the whole principle that we've established our business on which is really moving to a world beyond cash and ensuring greater transparency.. If you think about it, cash is a problem for a number of countries. Cash really facilitates anonymity, it facilitates illegal activity, it facilitates tax avoidance and a range of other things that aren't going to drive efficiency in an economy\" - [VIDEO] \"If it's an anonymous transaction, that sounds like a suspicious transaction. Why does somebody need to be anonymous?\" - [VIDEO] MasterCard SE Asia President on cryptocurrencies and cash"}]},"ebjkD1Om4uw":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a primary source for Altman's statements, such as releasing AI more slowly, detection of LLM output being futile, and dismissing the GPT-4 parameter-count rumor.","m":[{"id":36894221,"author":"letmevoteplease","month":"2023-07","story":"How to scale LLMs better with an alternative to transformers","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Altman denying the parameter-count rumor","quote":"In an interview, Sam Altman mentions he saw the tweet","text":"Here is the timeline again: 2022/11/11: A viral tweet claims GPT-4 will have \"100 trillion parameters.\"[1] At this point, there were no rumors about mixture of experts. 2023/01/16: In an interview, Sam Altman mentions he saw the tweet and it was \"complete bullshit.\"[2] 2023/06/20: geohotz and the lead of PyTorch, two people who would be expected to have relevant connections, claim that GPT-4 is an 8 x 220B mixture of experts model.[3] These are two separate, unconnected rumors. One was denied by Sam Altman and was never plausible in the first place. The other was never denied and is highly plausible. You are conflating them by claiming, without any source, that there was \"a clear denial from OpenAI's CEO\" that \"GPT4 is a trillion parameter mixture of experts model.\" [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":35273460,"author":"Sunhold","month":"2023-03","story":"What Will Transformers Transform?","stance":"reference","why":"Altman dismisses 100 trillion parameter rumor","quote":"This article repeats the rumor that GPT4 has 100 trillion parameters","text":"This article repeats the rumor that GPT4 has 100 trillion parameters, which Sam Altman has called \"complete bullshit.\"[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34656278,"author":"calny","month":"2023-02","story":"Open Assistant – project meant to give everyone access to a great chat based LLM","stance":"reference","why":"Altman on releasing AI more slowly","quote":"Sam A said OpenAI will be releasing","text":"> they shouldn't all be walled off inside of companies Strong agree. This is becoming a bigger concern than people realize too. Sam A said OpenAI will be releasing \"much more slowly than people would like\" and would \"sit on\" their tech for a long time going forward.[0] And Deepmind's founder said that \"the AI industry's culture of publishing its findings openly may soon need to end.\"[1] This sounds like Google and MSFT won't even be shipping their best AI to people via API's. They'll just keep that tech in-house to power their own services. That underscores the need for open, distributed models. And like you say, there's room for both. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":34858807,"author":"wand3r","month":"2023-02","story":"GPTZero case study discovers it's only accurate on less than 50% of text","stance":"reference","why":"Altman on futility of detecting LLM output","quote":"I saw this[1] interview with Sam Altman touching on interim AI impact.","text":"I saw this[1] interview with Sam Altman touching on interim AI impact. I really agree with his point that basically detecting output from LLMs is basically going to be futile and only really relevant in the near term. Accuracy is obviously going to improve in models and detection isnt that difficult now but will be in the future, especially if output is modified or an attempt to obfuscate origin is made. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"i5f8bqYYwps":{"wy":"Commenters cite timestamps from it as a primary source for Jobs' views on specific topics.","q":{"x":"Privacy means people know what they are signing up for in plain English and repeatedly.","i":23827283,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":28581207,"author":"shrimpx","month":"2021-09","story":"Steve Jobs: Let's force Amazon to use our payment system (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Source for the quoted conversation, around the 30:15 mark.","quote":"The particular conversation is from this video","text":"Sorry, I had to dig. The particular conversation is from this video: [VIDEO] Around the 30:15 mark. Apologies for my incorrect quoting, I was paraphrasing from memory."},{"id":23827283,"author":"shaabanban","month":"2020-07","story":"A Steve Jobs masterclass from a decade ago","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on privacy meaning people know what they sign up for, around 1:10.","quote":"Privacy means people know what they are signing up for in plain English and repeatedly.","text":"I have to say he was spot on about user privacy 10 years ago and it shows in the platforms today. Starts at around 1:10. > We do a lot of things like that to ensure that people understand what these apps are doing > Privacy means people know what they are signing up for in plain English and repeatedly. Edit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23658720,"author":"maram","month":"2020-06","story":"“Adobe Flash is no longer supported in Safari”","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs explains why he wrote Thoughts on Flash, around 2:00.","quote":"He answered Mossberg and Swisher here at 2:00 on why he wrote Thoughts on Flash.","text":">Is he really whining about something being closed and proprietary? Can't fucking believe it. \"whining?\" Steve Jobs didn't whine ...He built the greatest products in our digital era. He answered Mossberg and Swisher here at 2:00 on why he wrote Thoughts on Flash. [VIDEO] \">Mossberg: you published thoughts on flash, is it fair to be abrupt? Steve: Apple is a company that doesn’t have the most resources of everybody in the world and the way we’ve succeeded is by choosing what horses to ride carefully technically. We try to look for these technical vectors that have a future and that are headed up and you technology..different pieces of technology go in cycles. They have their Springs and summers and autumns and then they you know go to the graveyard of technology. So we try to pick things that are in there Springs and if you choose wisely you can save yourself an enormous amount of work versus trying to do everything and you can really put energy into making those new emerging technologies be great on your platform rather than just okay because you’re spreading yourself too thin. So we have a history of doing that that. As an example, we went from the 5 inch floppy disk the 3.5 inch floppy disk with the Mac. >Mossberg: before other people right? Steve: We were the first to do that we made the 3.5 floppy disk this popular, Sony invented it and we put it in the first products, and there some good reasons we did that, we got rid of the floppy disk altogether in 1998, with the firs"},{"id":23095970,"author":"maram","month":"2020-05","story":"I got my first $50/mo customer","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on why market valuation is not what matters, at 1:02.","quote":"It doesn’t matter. It’s not what’s important. It’s not what makes you come in the morning.","text":"> From now on, I have no goals. Nothing. I don't care about anything. I don't care about followers. I don't care about revenue. I don't care about anything. I just want to enjoy my every day life. Enjoy the process. Nothing brings joy to entrepreneurs more than the “process of building” and crafting products. Your paragraph reminded me of Steve Jobs reply to Kara Swisher when she asked for his thoughts on surpassing Microsoft in market valuation. He said: “It doesn’t matter. It’s not what’s important. It’s not what makes you come in the morning. Not why any of our customers buy our products. It’s good to keep that in mind and remember what we are doing and why” 01:02 [VIDEO]"},{"id":21375172,"author":"acqq","month":"2019-10","story":"Playboy Interview: Steve Jobs (1985)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a coda to Jobs' thoughts on death and obsolescence.","quote":"","text":"So it's right there in 1985 (emphasis mine): \"Jobs: That’s inevitably what happens. That’s why I think death is the most wonderful invention of life. It purges the system of these old models that are obsolete. \" (He was speaking about the computer hardware). 20 years later \"2005 Stanford Commencement Address\": [LINK] \"About a year ago I was diagnosed with cancer. (...) Having lived through it, I can now say this to you with a bit more certainty than when death was a useful but purely intellectual concept: No one wants to die. Even people who want to go to heaven don’t want to die to get there. And yet death is the destination we all share. No one has ever escaped it. And that is as it should be, because Death is very likely the single best invention of Life. It is Life’s change agent. It clears out the old to make way for the new. Right now the new is you, but someday not too long from now, you will gradually become the old and be cleared away. Sorry to be so dramatic, but it is quite true. Your time is limited, so don’t waste it living someone else’s life. Don’t be trapped by dogma — which is living with the results of other people’s thinking. Don’t let the noise of others’ opinions drown out your own inner voice. And most important, have the courage to follow your heart and intuition. They somehow already know what you truly want to become. Everything else is secondary.\" There is also a coda: in 2010, at D8 conference (Jobs died 2011), Jobs got a question: \"Q: A few years a"},{"id":16835420,"author":"slap_shot","month":"2018-04","story":"What did Alan Kay and Steve Jobs talk about at the 2007 iPhone keynote?","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs describes the multitouch idea starting with a tablet, around 37:00.","quote":"He \"had an idea of being able to type on a multi touch glass display","text":"Steve revealed at D10 that the iPad actually did get prototyped first. IIRC, he tasked a team with creating a keyless keyboard tablet thing (forgive my terrible paraphrasing). They came back with a tablet. When Steve scrolled up and saw the rubber band effect, he said \"oh my god we can make a phone with this\" and they went the phone first. Edit: Below is the interview. He \"had an idea of being able to type on a multi touch glass display\" [VIDEO] @ 37:00"},{"id":43504035,"author":"rickdeckard","month":"2025-03","story":"Apple needs a Snow Sequoia","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"people pay us to make that decision for them","text":"But the opposite is assuming that Apple has a \"responsibility\" towards its existing users and has to acknowledge their expectations from them. A sentiment which famously led Steve Jobs to respond that he doesn't understand this, because \"people pay us to make that decision for them\" and \"If people like our products they will buy them; if they don't, they won't\" [0] So according to Steve Jobs himself, the only Apple-acknowledged way to disagree with Apple is to NOT buy their products, and by extend into the services-world of today it means STOP USING their products. Now Steve Jobs doesn't officially run this company anymore, but I don't see any indication that this philosophy has changed in any way. [0]: [VIDEO]"}]},"jJT_CACIZqs":{"wy":"HN cites it as real evidence for whether a non-pilot can land a plane, whenever the topic comes up.","q":{"x":"this actually happened and you can see a video of it here","i":11001395,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11001395,"author":"myztic","month":"2016-01","story":"Flying a plane with flight simulator experience only","stance":"reference","why":"Video of the actual event.","quote":"this actually happened and you can see a video of it here","text":"You might not want to skim through the forums (though I find the discussion interesting). In the end, this actually happened and you can see a video of it here [VIDEO] EDIT: and rdancer also linked to the final report, see his post, thanks :)"},{"id":7269493,"author":"lisper","month":"2014-02","story":"Questions People Ask When I Tell Them I Own an Airplane","stance":"reference","why":"Someone actually landed a plane with only sim experience.","quote":"Someone actually did this experiment.","text":"The answer is yes. Someone actually did this experiment. [VIDEO] But it's pretty challenging. Today's simulators are pretty good, but there's something about having your life actually on the line that can't be captured by a sim."},{"id":5153790,"author":"revelation","month":"2013-02","story":"How to land an airplane if you are not a pilot","stance":"reference","why":"Video of the actual flight.","quote":"The trip report will spoil the outcome, so the video is somewhat more nerve-wrecking.","text":"And here is the video: [VIDEO] The trip report will spoil the outcome, so the video is somewhat more nerve-wrecking."}]},"kNWfqVWC2KI":{"wy":"Cited as a favorite flying oddity and as a joke about projects that get airborne through sheer thrust.","m":[{"id":7362021,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2014-03","story":"Did an F-15 airplane successfully land with just one wing?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an RC lawnmower flying on sheer thrust","quote":"if you put ridiculous amounts of thrust on something, you can make anything fly.","text":"RC plane builders have known for a long time, that if you put ridiculous amounts of thrust on something, you can make anything fly. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3785085,"author":"georgieporgie","month":"2012-04","story":"R/C SR-71","stance":"reference","why":"RC flying lawnmower as a favorite flying oddity","quote":"My personal favorite flying oddities are the lawnmowers","text":"My personal favorite flying oddities are the lawnmowers: [VIDEO] The reason that the SR-71 surprised me is that it seems like it doesn't have that much wing area (which is probably somewhat counterintuitive given the length), and it wasn't flying at a significant upward angle like those foamies."},{"id":697941,"author":"tom_rath","month":"2009-07","story":"Youtube remains unprofitable 3 years after Google acquisition","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Google's many projects remind me of the Flying Lawnmower","text":"Until GOOG approaches MSFT's revenues, I don't think Microsoft will worry. If there's a 'war' between those two companies, Microsoft is probably handling it as one of attrition. Google has one strong source of revenue (search advertising) and has been thrashing about for years in an attempt to build at least a second source without notable success. Microsoft has many different sources of revenue and continues to build on each. To take out Google, all they have to do is have Bing, Yahoo and the rest patiently chip away at GOOG's search advertising dominance by a few percentage points each year and that revenue will dry up. Then, without that revenue, all of Google's vanity projects will collapse in a heap. Google's many projects remind me of the Flying Lawnmower: [VIDEO] You can make anything fly if you have a strong enough engine shoving it and Google's engine is its search advertising revenue. Once that revenue decreases enough, their lawnmower will fall to earth, and Microsoft is surely happy to see them tack on new expensive doodads like YouTube and Chrome O/S to hurry that day forward."}]},"mKHY69AFstE":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of fear-based public safety messaging that sticks in memory.","q":{"x":"our infamous safety adverts that you never quite forget","i":46374674,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48282984,"author":"iamalizard","month":"2026-05","story":"The Cost of Safetyism","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a graphic PSA","quote":"","text":"Having soon a lot of real gore from a relatively young age, I may be calloused but in a good way - when something really fucked up happens that actually involves gore, death, violence or really scary, unpredictable or urgent things, I am quite calm and am able to handle situation much better than people who feel like vomiting at the sight of blood or a worm on an infection. Sure, some of the stuff I saw was in my mind for days, but I got used to it. Not to say I'm desensitized to it in a way that makes me not care about it. I'm still very empathetical to anyone who's suffered even the smallest of wounds or hardships. I was exposed to violence first from movies (my parents let me watch anything and I thank them for that), then from shock sites, 4chan, liveleak, /r/watchpeopledie and so on. I think I've really internalized how dangerous the road is, how dangerous machines are, how horrible war really is, how you can get killed by just saying the wrong thing, how there's always a chance you can die from anything at any moment. I still sometimes cross on reds and mouth off at someone who might be dangerous - I'm not scared of anything to an unnecessary degree, although being \"scared\" in a rational way is good IMO. I think I have a better way to assess risk than the average person, though. I found some of the PSAs and they involve mild injuries or death without the injury itself: [VIDEO] [LINK] I bet if you show kids actual footage of road accidents, it will burn into their memory"},{"id":46374674,"author":"xnorswap","month":"2025-12","story":"Autonomously navigating the real world: lessons from the PG&E outage","stance":"reference","why":"Infamous UK road safety advert","quote":"our infamous safety adverts that you never quite forget","text":"I thought the UK ranked well, I didn't realise it ranked that well. Maybe there's something to be said for left-hand driving, I see Japan ranks very highly too. ;) The real reason is I guess we take road safety seriously, we have strict drink-driving laws, and our driving test is genuinely difficult to pass. I seem to remember road safety also featuring prominently throughout the primary national curriculum. And of course, our infamous safety adverts that you never quite forget, such as: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41764152,"author":"djtango","month":"2024-10","story":"Congress fights to keep AM radio in cars","stance":"reference","why":"UK campaign about rear passengers endangering front passengers","quote":"","text":"I'm trying to read your comments charitably and made in good faith but I'll admit it is proving to be a challenge here. In the UK they ran a campaign to raise awareness that during an accident a passenger riding in the back can injure the passenger in front if they don't wear a seatbelt. That seems like a serious externality to me. I don't know if you just hate seatbelts or love arguing but there are absolutely reasons that people should wear seatbelts and I'm glad most people have accepted that the inconvenience of wearing them is pretty negligible now. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":37164487,"author":"dspillett","month":"2023-08","story":"Straightforward Thai Railway Safety Posters","stance":"reference","why":"Memorable UK seatbelt public information film","quote":"The seatbelt and in-town speed limit ones stick in the mind too","text":"I assume [LINK] is the one you are thinking of (there was a shorter version that aired in TV ad breaks). The Think! campaign was always pretty on-the-nose, as you need to be sometimes to get these messages across. The seatbelt and in-town speed limit ones stick in the mind too: [VIDEO] and [LINK] respectively. The memorably stark tone and the frequency they were on TV made these spots ripe for parody, for example this one from Monkey Dust: [LINK]"},{"id":29210419,"author":"ghoward","month":"2021-11","story":"Latvia bans unvaccinated lawmakers from voting, docks pay","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I think you are misrepresenting my statement. I said \"without any speakable side effects\". > You always have to compare potential side effects of a measure against the effects of the things the measure should protect against. Safety belts might be detrimental to saving you life in traffic accidents in a not insignificant percentage of cases (e.g. being trapped in a burning car) – however if you compare it to the likelyhood of dying because you are not using a safety belt the whole thing is miniscule. And there are still ways to reduce the risk of dying from a seat belt even further. I have a seat belt cutter in my car for a reason. There is no such mitigation for vaccines. > Many people say they don't want to get vaccinated because they don't know about the long term effects of such a vaccination. But if they were honest they would need to admit that they don't know about the long term effects of getting the virus as well. Not to speak of the real risk to die or destroy your respiratory apartus in the process, in which case worrying about purely hypothetical long term effects seems to be a little delusional. Except that viruses tend to get less deadly over time, which we are seeing with Delta. So I can take precautions to reduce the chances of catching COVID, which indirectly reduces my chances of having a long-term side effect of COVID. > The one minor difference to the safety belt example is that your choice also affects the risk for the rest of society. If you dont wear "},{"id":22943350,"author":"helsinkiandrew","month":"2020-04","story":"Don't Mess with Texas","stance":"reference","why":"Fear-based UK seatbelt public safety advert","quote":"The UK used fear to spread the message, with great effect.","text":"The UK used fear to spread the message, with great effect. The thought of being in a car without a seat belt terrifies me: [VIDEO]"}]},"me4BZBsHwZs":{"wy":"HN invokes it as a joke whenever someone complains about a miraculous technology not working perfectly.","m":[{"id":48494928,"author":"nsagent","month":"2026-06","story":"Anthropic apologizes for invisible Claude Fable guardrails","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"reminds me of an old Louis CK bit about airplane wifi","text":"I know this isn't going to be a popular take, but here goes anyway... The complaints that Anthropic are routing your requests to a different model reminds me of an old Louis CK bit about airplane wifi. Clearly Anthropic was too aggressive with whatever guardrails they put in, but the response seems overly entitled to a model people didn't even know existed not that long ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43946490,"author":"steveBK123","month":"2025-05","story":"The cult of doing business","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Louis CK did a bit ~15 years back about someone throwing a tantrum on a plane","text":"> Appreciating the magic of living in a thriving economy (true for most of the world for decades now) is partly attitude. Yes. Louis CK did a bit ~15 years back about someone throwing a tantrum on a plane when the (then brand new tech) WiFi broke down. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42486192,"author":"diego_sandoval","month":"2024-12","story":"GPT-5 is behind schedule","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"how quickly does the world owe him something that he knew existed only 10 seconds ago?","text":"Reminds me of this Louis CK joke: I was on an airplane and there was high-speed Internet on the airplane. That's the newest thing that I know exists. And I'm sitting on the plane and they go, open up your laptop, you can go on the Internet. And it's fast, and I'm watching YouTube clips. It's amazing. I'm on an airplane! And then it breaks down. And they apologize, the Internet's not working. And the guy next to me goes, 'This is bullshit.' I mean, how quickly does the world owe him something that he knew existed only 10 seconds ago?\" [VIDEO]"}]},"rbHxeOQA1Mc":{"wy":"HN commenters link it during 2020 coronavirus threads as supposed evidence about exotic animal markets in China, rather than recommending it.","m":[{"id":24092036,"author":"abc-xyz","month":"2020-08","story":"How Chinese Officials Hijacked My Company","stance":"reference","why":"Creator addresses fear of publishing footage while in China.","quote":"","text":"It’s worth pointing out that it was dangerous for him to highlight the bad parts of China while he was still living there. In one of his videos he mentioned that during his time in China he had recorded a lot of footage but he was understandably afraid to publish it (I believe he partly addresses it in this video unless my memory is failing me: [VIDEO] ). I personally like this video a lot: [LINK] . It talks a bit about why the rest of the world had no masks or other medical equipment when the Wuhan Virus spread outside of China. There’s also another foreigner who was semi-famous in China that’s now making funny videos about China (e.g. [LINK] )."},{"id":22765183,"author":"kyuudou","month":"2020-04","story":"Shenzhen becomes first Chinese city to ban eating cats and dogs","stance":"reference","why":"Former expat's experiences about animal eating in China.","quote":"former expat in China, youtuber serpentza, finally unleashed the hounds with his experiences about animal eating over there","text":"former expat in China, youtuber serpentza, finally unleashed the hounds with his experiences about animal eating over there: [VIDEO] Hard to argue with. Totally gross, too. But I love eating cows, chickens and fish and see dogs as companions so whatever."},{"id":22518302,"author":"oDot","month":"2020-03","story":"Milton Friedman: Is Capitalism Humane? (1977) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as a difficult-to-watch example about China.","quote":"Warning, difficult to watch.","text":"You say my iPhone example is nonsense yet you speak about wealth accumulation in a way that supports my example. You ignore other countries falling the exact same faults as you say Capitalism You seem to support a democracy where a majority could vote to oppress a minority You blame me for suggesting a world \"without government\" which is extreme intellectual dishonesty, especially when I mention limited _government_. Then you, once again, point at the problems with the US that are Cronyism, not Capitalism. Living in the USSR is nothing more than an anecdote, the same way Mainland Chinese claim wrongs about China, probably without even knowing[0]. I'm sorry, but this is the point where I leave this discussion, as it seems to go nowhere. I suggest you read a little about the quality of life in the 1950s in the US, before Cronyism caught up to it, and compare it to the quality of life in the USSR at the time. [0]: Warning, difficult to watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22398851,"author":"zed88","month":"2020-02","story":"Decoding evolution and transmissions of novel pneumonia coronavirus","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as support for exotic animal eating claim.","quote":"","text":"Chinese have been known to eat all kind of exotic wild animals for medicinal and recreational reasons. The government may be trying to curb this, but a large portion of Chinese are still rural and food superstitions are rife, the regulation is not that easy I suppose. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22398886,"author":"zed88","month":"2020-02","story":"Decoding evolution and transmissions of novel pneumonia coronavirus","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as proof of claims about China.","quote":"And here is a video to prove it","text":"Totally agree. And here is a video to prove it [VIDEO] CCP is also lying on the numbers of deaths and treatment of people in quarantine. Where their apartments are sealed from outside and they are left to die. I saw several videos from Wuhan where seemingly normal people are collapsing in the street and dying."}]}});
